1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,125
You wrote a skill for a coding agent, and
it works when you try it.

2
00:00:04,625 --> 00:00:08,894
But you can't tell whether it works on
another model, on the fifth run, or after

3
00:00:08,894 --> 00:00:10,975
your next edit, or what each run costs.

4
00:00:11,475 --> 00:00:15,622
This course is for anyone who writes
skills, or builds an agent into an app,

5
00:00:15,622 --> 00:00:17,750
and wants evidence in place of a hunch.

6
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:23,207
We take one short skill, which writes
changelog entries, and measure it with

7
00:00:23,207 --> 00:00:23,542
Vally.

8
00:00:24,042 --> 00:00:27,792
First we measure the skill, and read what
the agent really did.

9
00:00:28,292 --> 00:00:32,642
Then we build graders we can trust, and
ask whether the skill earns its place.

10
00:00:33,142 --> 00:00:37,743
We add a judge for what code can't check,
and learn what a result does and doesn't

11
00:00:37,743 --> 00:00:38,192
support.

12
00:00:38,692 --> 00:00:42,992
We work out what a passing trial costs,
and turn the checks into a routine.

13
00:00:43,492 --> 00:00:46,892
Finally, we evaluate the same skill inside
an app.

14
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:52,283
By the end, you can change the skill, or
the model under it, and say with evidence

15
00:00:52,283 --> 00:00:54,375
whether it got better, and what it costs.

16
00:00:54,875 --> 00:00:56,250
Let's begin.

17
00:00:57,467 --> 00:01:01,792
Imagine you wrote a skill, tried it once,
and the agent did what you wanted.

18
00:01:02,292 --> 00:01:06,614
Then a teammate runs it on another model
and gets something different, and neither

19
00:01:06,614 --> 00:01:08,617
of you can say which result is normal.

20
00:01:09,117 --> 00:01:13,606
Here, you'll run an experiment on two
models, and find the differences in their

21
00:01:13,606 --> 00:01:14,117
behavior.

22
00:01:15,334 --> 00:01:17,559
Let's start with what a skill is.

23
00:01:18,059 --> 00:01:23,121
It's a folder with a file called SKILL dot
M D in it: a name, a one-line description,

24
00:01:23,121 --> 00:01:24,134
and instructions.

25
00:01:24,634 --> 00:01:29,612
An agent is a program that uses a language
model to do a task with tools: it reads

26
00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:31,859
files, edits them, and runs commands.

27
00:01:32,359 --> 00:01:36,731
The model is the part that decides what to
do, and the same agent can run on

28
00:01:36,731 --> 00:01:37,709
different models.

29
00:01:38,209 --> 00:01:42,034
So a skill isn't code that runs the same
way every time.

30
00:01:42,534 --> 00:01:45,859
It's advice, and each model reads it its
own way.

31
00:01:47,067 --> 00:01:51,617
The skill this course improves is about
one small chore: keeping a changelog.

32
00:01:52,117 --> 00:01:56,719
A changelog lists what changed in each
version of a tool, for the people who use

33
00:01:56,719 --> 00:01:56,892
it.

34
00:01:57,392 --> 00:02:01,417
Changes that haven't shipped yet go under
a heading called Unreleased.

35
00:02:01,917 --> 00:02:06,442
They're grouped into categories such as
Added and Fixed, one line per entry.

36
00:02:06,942 --> 00:02:11,061
And an entry often ends with a reference
like this one: the number of the pull

37
00:02:11,061 --> 00:02:12,592
request that made the change.

38
00:02:13,092 --> 00:02:17,317
A pull request is a numbered proposal to
change a project's code.

39
00:02:18,534 --> 00:02:20,109
Here's the skill.

40
00:02:20,609 --> 00:02:25,022
It tells an agent how to add an entry to a
changelog, and it's a plain first draft,

41
00:02:25,022 --> 00:02:26,884
the kind you write in five minutes.

42
00:02:27,384 --> 00:02:30,084
Let's read it the way a model has to.

43
00:02:30,584 --> 00:02:33,284
Add the entry in the right category.

44
00:02:33,784 --> 00:02:35,884
Which categories exist?

45
00:02:36,384 --> 00:02:38,534
Reference the pull request.

46
00:02:39,034 --> 00:02:41,784
In what form, and what if there isn't one?

47
00:02:42,284 --> 00:02:46,359
The skill doesn't say, so each model fills
the gap with its own guess.

48
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:51,367
When you try the skill by hand, you see
one model's guess, once.

49
00:02:51,867 --> 00:02:56,372
That's one observation, and it tells you
little about the next model, or the next

50
00:02:56,372 --> 00:02:56,817
request.

51
00:02:57,317 --> 00:03:01,699
To see more than that, you need a tool
that runs the agent for you and keeps a

52
00:03:01,699 --> 00:03:02,092
record.

53
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:04,925
That's the problem.

54
00:03:05,425 --> 00:03:07,575
Now here's how Vally works.

55
00:03:08,075 --> 00:03:12,690
Vally is a command-line tool that runs an
agent in a controlled way and keeps a

56
00:03:12,690 --> 00:03:13,975
record of what it did.

57
00:03:14,475 --> 00:03:18,876
The agent it runs is the one that comes
with GitHub Copilot, which is why this

58
00:03:18,876 --> 00:03:20,625
course needs a Copilot account.

59
00:03:21,125 --> 00:03:25,834
What it runs is an eval: a repeatable test
of an agent, with the same request, in the

60
00:03:25,834 --> 00:03:28,825
same starting folder, checked the same way
every time.

61
00:03:30,034 --> 00:03:34,318
First, Vally makes a fresh scratch folder,
called the workspace, and copies in the

62
00:03:34,318 --> 00:03:35,834
starting files and the skill.

63
00:03:36,334 --> 00:03:40,634
Second, it starts an agent on the model
you named, and gives it the request.

64
00:03:41,134 --> 00:03:45,484
Third, when the agent stops, it saves a
record of what the agent changed.

65
00:03:45,984 --> 00:03:49,534
And fourth, it runs the check, and prints
a tick or a cross.

66
00:03:50,034 --> 00:03:54,684
Every run gets a new workspace, so no run
can see what another one did.

67
00:03:55,184 --> 00:03:58,109
What's inside the check, we'll explain
later.

68
00:03:59,334 --> 00:04:03,584
The command that does all this is vally
eval, and we're going to start by giving

69
00:04:03,584 --> 00:04:04,434
it three things.

70
00:04:04,934 --> 00:04:07,984
Dash e, followed by the eval file to run.

71
00:04:08,484 --> 00:04:11,834
The course comes with one, holding a
single request.

72
00:04:12,334 --> 00:04:15,984
It says: I fixed the typo in the help text
for notes add.

73
00:04:16,484 --> 00:04:18,609
Put that in the changelog.

74
00:04:19,109 --> 00:04:23,984
Dash dash model, followed by one model
name, or several separated by commas.

75
00:04:24,484 --> 00:04:27,734
With several, Vally does the whole run
once for each.

76
00:04:28,234 --> 00:04:32,709
And dash dash output dir, followed by the
folder to save the results in.

77
00:04:33,934 --> 00:04:38,609
It writes only inside that results folder,
but each run is one short session with the

78
00:04:38,609 --> 00:04:41,634
agent, and that spends requests from your
Copilot plan.

79
00:04:42,134 --> 00:04:45,734
If the connection drops, Vally prints
ERROR for that model.

80
00:04:46,234 --> 00:04:50,284
That says nothing about the skill, so
you'd just run the command again.

81
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,663
One more thing we'll use in a moment:
model names differ from account to

82
00:04:55,663 --> 00:04:56,125
account.

83
00:04:56,625 --> 00:05:00,806
If you name a model your account doesn't
have, Vally stops before starting any

84
00:05:00,806 --> 00:05:02,950
agent, and prints the names you do have.

85
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:06,625
So a made-up name is a free way to see
your list.

86
00:05:07,834 --> 00:05:12,184
Step three, the record of what the agent
changed, is a patch: a text file that

87
00:05:12,184 --> 00:05:15,809
records a change to other files as lines
added and lines removed.

88
00:05:16,309 --> 00:05:20,384
Vally writes one for every run, and names
it workspace dot patch.

89
00:05:20,884 --> 00:05:25,266
It saves each command's results in a
folder named with the time the command

90
00:05:25,266 --> 00:05:25,734
started.

91
00:05:26,234 --> 00:05:30,404
Inside that, the patch sits under the
eval's name, then the request's name, then

92
00:05:30,404 --> 00:05:32,959
the model, then a run number that starts
at zero.

93
00:05:34,167 --> 00:05:37,317
Here's a real one, from the run you're
about to see.

94
00:05:37,817 --> 00:05:40,742
The first line names the file that
changed.

95
00:05:41,242 --> 00:05:44,892
The next three repeat it in other forms,
and you can skip them.

96
00:05:45,392 --> 00:05:49,117
This line says where the change is:
starting at line seven.

97
00:05:49,617 --> 00:05:52,092
The rest of that line you can skip.

98
00:05:52,592 --> 00:05:56,742
A line that begins with a space was
already there, and hasn't changed.

99
00:05:57,242 --> 00:06:00,442
It's context, so you can see where the
change sits.

100
00:06:00,942 --> 00:06:04,592
A line that begins with a plus was added
by the agent.

101
00:06:05,092 --> 00:06:08,667
A minus would mean one it removed, and
this patch has none.

102
00:06:09,167 --> 00:06:13,611
So this model added a Fixed heading and
one entry under Unreleased, and touched

103
00:06:13,611 --> 00:06:14,342
nothing else.

104
00:06:14,842 --> 00:06:19,317
You can read exactly what an agent did to
a file without opening the file.

105
00:06:20,534 --> 00:06:22,609
That's everything you need.

106
00:06:23,109 --> 00:06:25,984
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

107
00:06:26,484 --> 00:06:30,837
Both models get the same skill, the same
changelog, and the same request, and the

108
00:06:30,837 --> 00:06:32,934
request doesn't mention a pull request.

109
00:06:33,434 --> 00:06:37,784
So what would you expect each model to do
about reference the pull request?

110
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:45,691
First, we check the tools: node twenty-two
point fifteen or later, and Vally zero

111
00:06:45,691 --> 00:06:46,675
point twenty-one.

112
00:06:50,460 --> 00:06:55,013
Then we copy the starter that ships with
the course into a new practice folder, and

113
00:06:55,013 --> 00:06:55,835
change into it.

114
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:03,070
Every checkpoint in the course starts
there.

115
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,250
Next, we list the models, using the
made-up name.

116
00:07:09,750 --> 00:07:12,575
It starts no agent and spends nothing.

117
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:20,200
There's the list, inside the error.

118
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:22,425
Yours will differ.

119
00:07:22,925 --> 00:07:27,525
We'll use claude haiku four point five and
G P T five point six terra.

120
00:07:30,434 --> 00:07:33,109
Now the real run, on both models.

121
00:07:33,609 --> 00:07:36,984
You'd put your own two model names in
place of these.

122
00:07:37,484 --> 00:07:40,959
This one spends two short agent runs from
your plan.

123
00:07:41,459 --> 00:07:44,934
We're watching for a tick or a cross next
to each model.

124
00:07:47,247 --> 00:07:50,697
About twenty seconds later, there's a tick
for each model.

125
00:07:51,197 --> 00:07:54,622
The Saved line at the bottom says where
the results went.

126
00:07:58,122 --> 00:08:00,797
So let's look in that results folder.

127
00:08:02,567 --> 00:08:05,992
There's one folder in it, named with the
time of the run.

128
00:08:06,492 --> 00:08:10,871
We change into it, then into the folders
for the eval and the request, and list

129
00:08:10,871 --> 00:08:11,592
what's there.

130
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:20,585
One folder for each model.

131
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:25,975
Here's the patch from the first model.

132
00:08:26,475 --> 00:08:29,250
The zero in the path is its first run.

133
00:08:29,750 --> 00:08:32,975
The lines that begin with a plus are what
it added.

134
00:08:36,475 --> 00:08:39,075
And here's the patch from the second
model.

135
00:08:41,707 --> 00:08:44,882
Its entry ends differently from the first
model's.

136
00:08:49,382 --> 00:08:52,282
Then we change back up to the practice
folder.

137
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:00,075
So what did that show?

138
00:09:00,575 --> 00:09:02,575
Both models got a tick.

139
00:09:03,075 --> 00:09:06,450
One wrote an entry with no pull request
number.

140
00:09:06,950 --> 00:09:10,550
The other ended its entry with pull
request forty-two.

141
00:09:11,050 --> 00:09:14,025
Nobody mentioned a pull request forty-two.

142
00:09:14,525 --> 00:09:18,700
The entry above it is forty-one, and the
model made up the next number.

143
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:23,650
In our runs, terra usually made up a
number, and haiku usually didn't.

144
00:09:24,150 --> 00:09:27,600
Your two models may differ in another way,
or agree.

145
00:09:28,100 --> 00:09:32,573
Either way, it's evidence about your skill
that would be difficult to discover for

146
00:09:32,573 --> 00:09:34,700
every model if you were trying by hand.

147
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:39,675
Both models got a green tick, yet they did
different things.

148
00:09:40,175 --> 00:09:42,650
So what did that tick actually check?

149
00:09:43,867 --> 00:09:48,167
Two models got the same request, and wrote
two different changelog entries.

150
00:09:48,667 --> 00:09:53,292
One entry cites a pull request that
doesn't exist, and both got a green tick.

151
00:09:53,792 --> 00:09:57,687
Here, you'll find out what that tick
checked, and where Vally wrote down what

152
00:09:57,687 --> 00:09:58,142
happened.

153
00:09:59,367 --> 00:10:04,250
Let's start with the file that told Vally
what to do: the eval file we ran on both

154
00:10:04,250 --> 00:10:04,667
models.

155
00:10:05,167 --> 00:10:09,997
It opens with the eval's name, which Vally
uses as a folder name when it saves

156
00:10:09,997 --> 00:10:10,492
results.

157
00:10:10,992 --> 00:10:15,385
Under defaults are settings for the whole
file, and this one gives the agent three

158
00:10:15,385 --> 00:10:16,992
minutes before Vally stops it.

159
00:10:17,492 --> 00:10:21,317
Next comes the agent environment: what the
agent starts with.

160
00:10:21,817 --> 00:10:26,135
Everything listed here is copied into the
workspace, the fresh scratch folder the

161
00:10:26,135 --> 00:10:28,267
agent works in, before the agent begins.

162
00:10:28,767 --> 00:10:33,367
There's one skill folder to install, given
as a path relative to this file.

163
00:10:33,867 --> 00:10:38,127
And there's one file to copy in: the
starting changelog, and the name it gets

164
00:10:38,127 --> 00:10:39,067
in the workspace.

165
00:10:40,267 --> 00:10:43,742
Then comes a list called stimuli: the
things to try.

166
00:10:44,242 --> 00:10:49,148
One entry in it is a stimulus: a request,
together with the graders that check the

167
00:10:49,148 --> 00:10:49,567
result.

168
00:10:50,067 --> 00:10:53,742
This file has a single stimulus, named
typo fix.

169
00:10:54,242 --> 00:10:58,417
Its prompt is the request, word for word
as the agent receives it.

170
00:10:58,917 --> 00:11:01,267
Under the prompt are the graders.

171
00:11:01,767 --> 00:11:04,517
A grader is what we've been calling the
check.

172
00:11:05,017 --> 00:11:09,525
Vally runs it after the agent has stopped,
and it reports pass or fail, with a line

173
00:11:09,525 --> 00:11:11,317
of evidence saying what it found.

174
00:11:11,817 --> 00:11:14,917
The type picks one of Vally's built-in
graders.

175
00:11:15,417 --> 00:11:17,917
This one is called file contains.

176
00:11:18,417 --> 00:11:22,497
It opens one file in the finished
workspace, and passes if that file

177
00:11:22,497 --> 00:11:24,117
contains the text in value.

178
00:11:24,617 --> 00:11:28,692
So the whole check is: does the changelog
contain the words notes add?

179
00:11:29,192 --> 00:11:32,717
The grader's name is ours, and it's what
the results show.

180
00:11:33,934 --> 00:11:37,759
One more command belongs with the eval
file: vally lint.

181
00:11:38,259 --> 00:11:42,909
It reports mistakes in an eval file, such
as a misspelt key or a missing file,

182
00:11:42,909 --> 00:11:44,459
without starting an agent.

183
00:11:44,959 --> 00:11:50,034
Lint takes dash e and the file's path, the
same as vally eval, and it spends nothing.

184
00:11:51,234 --> 00:11:55,159
That's the file that went in; now let's
see what a run leaves behind.

185
00:11:55,659 --> 00:12:00,724
First, one more of Vally's words: a trial
is one attempt at one stimulus by one

186
00:12:00,724 --> 00:12:01,109
model.

187
00:12:01,609 --> 00:12:05,934
Our command had one stimulus and two
models, so it made two trials.

188
00:12:06,434 --> 00:12:11,210
From here on, a run is one vally eval
command and everything it saves, and a

189
00:12:11,210 --> 00:12:13,159
trial is one attempt inside it.

190
00:12:14,367 --> 00:12:19,349
Each run writes one folder, named with the
time it started, and this is everything in

191
00:12:19,349 --> 00:12:19,642
ours.

192
00:12:20,142 --> 00:12:25,242
At the top, eval results dot M D is the
report: tables for people to read.

193
00:12:25,742 --> 00:12:31,167
Results dot jsonl holds the same results
for programs, one record on each line.

194
00:12:31,667 --> 00:12:36,367
The third file is timing data for tracing
tools, and this course doesn't use it.

195
00:12:36,867 --> 00:12:41,206
Below those are nested folders that lead
to each trial: the eval's name, then the

196
00:12:41,206 --> 00:12:44,742
stimulus, then the model, then a trial
number that starts at zero.

197
00:12:45,242 --> 00:12:48,017
Each trial folder holds three files.

198
00:12:48,517 --> 00:12:52,865
Workspace dot patch is the one you've
seen: the lines the agent added and

199
00:12:52,865 --> 00:12:53,342
removed.

200
00:12:53,842 --> 00:12:59,016
Events dot jsonl is the agent's own log of
the session, with every message and every

201
00:12:59,016 --> 00:12:59,817
tool it used.

202
00:13:00,317 --> 00:13:04,644
And metadata dot json holds a few facts
about the trial: which model, when it

203
00:13:04,644 --> 00:13:07,117
started and ended, and whether it
completed.

204
00:13:08,334 --> 00:13:11,484
Here's the report, with one table for each
model.

205
00:13:11,984 --> 00:13:15,709
The graders column lists each grader by
the name we gave it.

206
00:13:16,209 --> 00:13:20,909
Pass rate is the trials that passed out of
the trials that ran: here, one of one.

207
00:13:21,409 --> 00:13:26,585
Tool calls is how many times the agent
used a tool, such as opening or editing a

208
00:13:26,585 --> 00:13:26,909
file.

209
00:13:27,409 --> 00:13:32,226
And the verdict is the result: a trial
passes only if every one of its graders

210
00:13:32,226 --> 00:13:32,659
passes.

211
00:13:33,159 --> 00:13:35,884
The tokens column, we'll come to shortly.

212
00:13:37,100 --> 00:13:41,775
Results dot jsonl holds the detail behind
each row of that table.

213
00:13:42,275 --> 00:13:47,050
Each record is one very long line, so
here's one trial's record spread out.

214
00:13:47,550 --> 00:13:51,350
The type says what kind of record this is:
one trial's result.

215
00:13:51,850 --> 00:13:56,282
Status says whether the agent finished:
it's about the trial completing, not about

216
00:13:56,282 --> 00:13:57,525
the answer being right.

217
00:13:58,734 --> 00:14:02,659
Further down is grade result, the part
that explains the tick.

218
00:14:03,159 --> 00:14:06,034
Passed is true: that's the trial's
verdict.

219
00:14:06,534 --> 00:14:10,609
Details has one entry for each grader, and
this trial had one.

220
00:14:11,109 --> 00:14:15,845
Here's that grader's evidence, in its own
words: it found the words notes add in the

221
00:14:15,845 --> 00:14:16,409
changelog.

222
00:14:16,909 --> 00:14:21,451
The record ends with the trajectory, which
is Vally's name for the full record of

223
00:14:21,451 --> 00:14:23,134
what the agent did in a trial.

224
00:14:24,334 --> 00:14:28,209
Inside the trajectory is a record of what
the trial used.

225
00:14:28,709 --> 00:14:33,296
Models take in and write text in pieces
called tokens, each a word or part of a

226
00:14:33,296 --> 00:14:35,909
word, and model services charge by the
token.

227
00:14:36,409 --> 00:14:39,184
Every trial saves its token usage.

228
00:14:39,684 --> 00:14:44,328
Input tokens are everything the model was
given: the request, the skill, the files

229
00:14:44,328 --> 00:14:46,934
the agent opened, and the conversation so
far.

230
00:14:47,434 --> 00:14:51,434
Output tokens are everything it wrote, and
there are far fewer of them.

231
00:14:51,934 --> 00:14:56,312
The call count explains why input is so
much larger: the agent called the model

232
00:14:56,312 --> 00:14:59,859
six times, and each call takes in the
conversation so far again.

233
00:15:00,359 --> 00:15:04,352
Cache read tokens are the part of the
input the service had already processed

234
00:15:04,352 --> 00:15:07,309
earlier in the session, which costs less
the second time.

235
00:15:07,809 --> 00:15:10,884
And total tokens is input plus output.

236
00:15:12,100 --> 00:15:17,075
The token usage ends with what the trial
cost, as GitHub Copilot reported it.

237
00:15:17,575 --> 00:15:23,050
Its unit is the nano-AIU: a billionth of
an AIU, which is the unit Copilot reports

238
00:15:23,050 --> 00:15:23,650
usage in.

239
00:15:24,150 --> 00:15:28,775
One AIU is one GitHub AI Credit, the unit
Copilot bills in.

240
00:15:29,275 --> 00:15:32,050
And one AI Credit is one cent.

241
00:15:32,550 --> 00:15:37,083
So this trial's three point three six
billion nano-AIU come to about three

242
00:15:37,083 --> 00:15:37,450
cents.

243
00:15:37,950 --> 00:15:42,499
Copilot counts usage against your plan's
monthly allowance first, so a trial may

244
00:15:42,499 --> 00:15:45,000
cost you nothing extra until that's used
up.

245
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:50,750
That total is the number in the report's
tokens column, on this trial's row.

246
00:15:51,250 --> 00:15:55,773
When a stimulus has several trials, the
column shows their median, the middle

247
00:15:55,773 --> 00:15:56,125
value.

248
00:15:56,625 --> 00:16:00,975
We'll build on these numbers later, to
work out what a passing trial costs.

249
00:16:02,200 --> 00:16:04,600
So why did both models pass?

250
00:16:05,100 --> 00:16:07,450
Let's put the two halves together.

251
00:16:07,950 --> 00:16:12,732
The eval file's only grader asks one
question: does the changelog contain the

252
00:16:12,732 --> 00:16:13,725
words notes add?

253
00:16:14,225 --> 00:16:18,350
And the evidence line says what it found:
those words, in the changelog.

254
00:16:18,850 --> 00:16:23,125
Both models wrote an entry that mentions
notes add, so both passed.

255
00:16:23,625 --> 00:16:26,875
Nothing looked at the made-up pull request
forty-two.

256
00:16:28,100 --> 00:16:31,725
Here's the thing to hold on to about any
eval result.

257
00:16:32,225 --> 00:16:36,175
Graders only raise an objection to what
you told them to look for.

258
00:16:36,675 --> 00:16:41,046
A pass means none of your graders
objected; it doesn't mean the result is

259
00:16:41,046 --> 00:16:41,525
correct.

260
00:16:42,025 --> 00:16:45,725
So a tick tells you only as much as the
graders behind it.

261
00:16:46,225 --> 00:16:50,549
And so far there's one grader, written
before anyone had looked at what the

262
00:16:50,549 --> 00:16:51,125
models do.

263
00:16:52,334 --> 00:16:56,584
It's tempting to add a grader for made-up
pull request numbers straight away.

264
00:16:57,084 --> 00:16:59,284
But we've seen two trials.

265
00:16:59,784 --> 00:17:04,009
We don't yet know whether that model
always does this, or what else goes wrong

266
00:17:04,009 --> 00:17:04,984
on other requests.

267
00:17:05,484 --> 00:17:08,884
So the graders can wait until we've looked
at more runs.

268
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:13,925
That's the idea; now let's find it in our
own results folder.

269
00:17:14,425 --> 00:17:17,300
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

270
00:17:17,800 --> 00:17:20,075
Nothing here starts an agent.

271
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:23,725
First, we lint the eval file.

272
00:17:27,087 --> 00:17:29,937
A tick: the file has no mistakes in it.

273
00:17:31,437 --> 00:17:34,687
Next, let's find the folder our run was
saved in.

274
00:17:36,480 --> 00:17:39,655
There's one, named with the time the run
started.

275
00:17:42,860 --> 00:17:46,310
We change into it, and list what the run
left behind.

276
00:17:47,107 --> 00:17:52,532
The report, results dot jsonl, the timing
data, and a folder named after the eval.

277
00:17:55,032 --> 00:17:57,182
Now down to one trial.

278
00:17:59,234 --> 00:18:04,145
We change into the eval's folder, then the
stimulus, and list the first model's trial

279
00:18:04,145 --> 00:18:04,434
zero.

280
00:18:06,294 --> 00:18:09,994
Three files: the agent's events, the
metadata, and the patch.

281
00:18:13,200 --> 00:18:16,075
Here's the metadata file from that folder.

282
00:18:16,575 --> 00:18:19,750
Model says which model the trial ran on.

283
00:18:20,250 --> 00:18:23,425
And status says success: the agent
finished.

284
00:18:23,925 --> 00:18:27,150
The rest is identifiers, times, and paths.

285
00:18:28,367 --> 00:18:31,267
Now let's check that we can explain the
tick.

286
00:18:31,767 --> 00:18:35,142
Which file did the grader open, and what
did it look for?

287
00:18:37,642 --> 00:18:42,026
It opened the changelog in the finished
workspace, and looked for the words notes

288
00:18:42,026 --> 00:18:42,242
add.

289
00:18:42,742 --> 00:18:46,586
What if a model had put its entry under
the released one point two section

290
00:18:46,586 --> 00:18:48,767
instead: would this grader have passed it?

291
00:18:51,267 --> 00:18:55,593
Yes: the entry would still contain those
words, and the grader looks at nothing

292
00:18:55,593 --> 00:18:55,867
else.

293
00:18:56,367 --> 00:18:59,517
And what if a model had changed nothing at
all?

294
00:19:02,017 --> 00:19:06,118
Then the trial fails: the starting
changelog doesn't contain notes add, so

295
00:19:06,118 --> 00:19:08,667
the grader reports that the text wasn't
found.

296
00:19:09,867 --> 00:19:13,767
One last question: which model used more
tokens in our run?

297
00:19:16,267 --> 00:19:21,117
The report's tokens column has the answer:
haiku, by about a thousand tokens.

298
00:19:21,617 --> 00:19:23,717
Your own run will differ.

299
00:19:24,934 --> 00:19:28,259
We can now read everything a run left
behind.

300
00:19:28,759 --> 00:19:32,859
But each model ran only once: would it do
the same thing next time?

301
00:19:34,067 --> 00:19:37,942
One model made up a pull request number,
and the other didn't.

302
00:19:38,442 --> 00:19:42,567
It's tempting to decide which model is the
careless one, and move on.

303
00:19:43,067 --> 00:19:45,517
But we've watched each model once.

304
00:19:46,017 --> 00:19:50,340
Here, you'll run the same request five
times on each model, and count how often

305
00:19:50,340 --> 00:19:51,817
each one makes up a number.

306
00:19:53,034 --> 00:19:55,684
Let's start with why once isn't enough.

307
00:19:56,184 --> 00:19:59,609
A language model doesn't compute one fixed
answer.

308
00:20:00,109 --> 00:20:04,625
It produces one of many plausible answers,
and which one you get varies from run to

309
00:20:04,625 --> 00:20:06,584
run, even when nothing else changes.

310
00:20:07,084 --> 00:20:11,484
An agent adds to that, because each step
it takes depends on the step before.

311
00:20:11,984 --> 00:20:16,059
So a single trial tells you that something
can happen, and nothing more.

312
00:20:17,267 --> 00:20:21,216
If a colleague made a slip the one time
you watched, you wouldn't know whether

313
00:20:21,216 --> 00:20:23,292
they slip every time, or one time in ten.

314
00:20:23,792 --> 00:20:26,167
Those call for different responses.

315
00:20:26,667 --> 00:20:30,972
For any skill, the questions that matter
are how often it goes wrong, and whether

316
00:20:30,972 --> 00:20:32,567
that's the same on each model.

317
00:20:33,067 --> 00:20:37,125
Those are counts, and a count needs
several trials of the same stimulus: the

318
00:20:37,125 --> 00:20:39,367
same request, checked by the same graders.

319
00:20:40,567 --> 00:20:43,617
That variation shows up in measurements,
too.

320
00:20:44,117 --> 00:20:48,733
In a paper from twenty twenty-six, not yet
reviewed by other researchers, the same

321
00:20:48,733 --> 00:20:51,717
agent ran the same set of coding tasks
several times.

322
00:20:52,217 --> 00:20:56,707
From one identical run to the next, its
score, the share of tasks it completed,

323
00:20:56,707 --> 00:20:58,867
moved by two to six percentage points.

324
00:21:00,067 --> 00:21:02,892
That's the reason; here's how Vally does
it.

325
00:21:03,392 --> 00:21:08,513
Adding dash dash runs five to vally eval
repeats every stimulus five times, on

326
00:21:08,513 --> 00:21:09,892
every model you name.

327
00:21:10,392 --> 00:21:13,792
Each one is a trial of its own, in a fresh
workspace.

328
00:21:14,292 --> 00:21:18,990
And each trial gets its own numbered
folder, zero to four, with its own patch

329
00:21:18,990 --> 00:21:19,417
inside.

330
00:21:19,917 --> 00:21:23,742
With one stimulus and two models, that
makes ten trials.

331
00:21:24,967 --> 00:21:29,192
With several trials, a stimulus no longer
just passes or fails.

332
00:21:29,692 --> 00:21:34,292
For each model, Vally sums up the
stimulus's trials with one of four words.

333
00:21:34,792 --> 00:21:37,292
Pass means every trial passed.

334
00:21:37,792 --> 00:21:40,992
Flaky means some trials passed and some
failed.

335
00:21:41,492 --> 00:21:43,892
Fail means no trial passed.

336
00:21:44,392 --> 00:21:48,756
Finally, error means at least one trial
didn't finish, so the stimulus has no

337
00:21:48,756 --> 00:21:50,967
verdict, whatever the other trials did.

338
00:21:52,167 --> 00:21:54,517
Error is not a kind of failure.

339
00:21:55,017 --> 00:21:59,620
It means the agent gave no answer to
judge, for example because its connection

340
00:21:59,620 --> 00:22:00,092
dropped.

341
00:22:00,592 --> 00:22:04,546
When that happens, we run the command
again, or leave that trial out of the

342
00:22:04,546 --> 00:22:05,917
count and say that we did.

343
00:22:07,134 --> 00:22:11,781
One more thing is saved for every trial,
and it matters when a patch comes back

344
00:22:11,781 --> 00:22:12,134
empty.

345
00:22:12,634 --> 00:22:15,884
An empty patch means the agent changed no
file.

346
00:22:16,384 --> 00:22:21,778
Each trial's record in results dot jsonl
keeps the agent's final reply, in a field

347
00:22:21,778 --> 00:22:22,634
named output.

348
00:22:23,134 --> 00:22:25,609
That reply usually says why.

349
00:22:26,834 --> 00:22:29,959
Before we run ten trials, one more flag.

350
00:22:30,459 --> 00:22:33,634
Vally doesn't run trials one after
another.

351
00:22:34,134 --> 00:22:38,925
It runs several agent sessions at once,
and dash dash workers sets how many; the

352
00:22:38,925 --> 00:22:39,884
default is five.

353
00:22:40,384 --> 00:22:44,692
More at once is faster, but each session
holds an open connection to the model

354
00:22:44,692 --> 00:22:47,509
service, and those connections can drop
under load.

355
00:22:48,009 --> 00:22:52,467
When we ran ten trials with five workers,
three of them ended in error, with the

356
00:22:52,467 --> 00:22:54,084
message session disconnected.

357
00:22:54,584 --> 00:22:57,209
With two workers, all ten finished.

358
00:22:57,709 --> 00:23:02,045
So we use two whenever a command starts
more than a couple of trials, and accept

359
00:23:02,045 --> 00:23:03,184
that it takes longer.

360
00:23:03,684 --> 00:23:07,934
Even at two, a session drops now and then,
so an error is still possible.

361
00:23:09,134 --> 00:23:11,609
That's how it works; now let's count.

362
00:23:12,109 --> 00:23:16,802
First, we'll run the same stimulus five
times on each model, then open the ten

363
00:23:16,802 --> 00:23:17,284
patches.

364
00:23:17,784 --> 00:23:22,305
Each trial goes in one of three columns:
an entry with no number, an entry with a

365
00:23:22,305 --> 00:23:24,259
made-up number, or no entry at all.

366
00:23:24,759 --> 00:23:27,209
What would you expect from each model?

367
00:23:29,709 --> 00:23:32,584
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

368
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:37,625
Here's the run: the command from before,
with the two new flags.

369
00:23:38,125 --> 00:23:40,775
It spends ten short agent runs.

370
00:23:41,275 --> 00:23:45,522
We're watching the summary at the end,
where a model gets a tick only when none

371
00:23:45,522 --> 00:23:46,650
of its trials failed.

372
00:23:49,467 --> 00:23:54,005
About a minute later, there's a tick for
each model: on both, the stimulus is a

373
00:23:54,005 --> 00:23:54,292
pass.

374
00:23:54,792 --> 00:23:59,159
Flaky, fail or error would show up as a
line of its own above the summary, and

375
00:23:59,159 --> 00:24:00,167
here there's none.

376
00:24:03,667 --> 00:24:06,142
So let's look in the results folder.

377
00:24:07,927 --> 00:24:11,052
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run.

378
00:24:16,167 --> 00:24:20,342
We change into it, then into the folders
for the eval and the stimulus.

379
00:24:22,347 --> 00:24:24,922
Then we list one model's trials.

380
00:24:26,567 --> 00:24:29,167
Five folders, numbered from zero.

381
00:24:30,667 --> 00:24:33,667
Here's the patch from that model's trial
zero.

382
00:24:36,307 --> 00:24:40,382
Its entry ends with a pull request number
that nobody gave it.

383
00:24:48,600 --> 00:24:53,375
Reading the other nine the same way gives
us ten entries, five from each model.

384
00:24:53,875 --> 00:24:56,300
Here's the line each trial added.

385
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:03,775
Terra ended every one of its five entries
with a made-up number.

386
00:25:04,275 --> 00:25:07,250
Haiku didn't add a number in any of its
five.

387
00:25:10,467 --> 00:25:14,812
That fills in the tally: five with no
number for haiku, five made up for terra,

388
00:25:14,812 --> 00:25:16,517
and nothing in the last column.

389
00:25:20,734 --> 00:25:25,034
The final replies in results dot jsonl
tell the same story.

390
00:25:25,534 --> 00:25:29,784
In our run no patch was empty, so every
reply reports an edit.

391
00:25:30,284 --> 00:25:33,884
And terra's replies name the made-up
number outright.

392
00:25:38,100 --> 00:25:40,200
So what does the tally say?

393
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:43,600
We now have counts in place of an
impression.

394
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:49,100
On haiku, the skill led to a made-up pull
request number in none of its five trials.

395
00:25:49,600 --> 00:25:52,225
On terra, it did in five of five.

396
00:25:52,725 --> 00:25:57,034
That's how the skill behaves on each
model, as far as five trials can show, and

397
00:25:57,034 --> 00:25:58,725
it's worth fixing in the skill.

398
00:25:59,225 --> 00:26:03,630
Counts like these are also what we'll
compare against, once the skill has been

399
00:26:03,630 --> 00:26:04,025
edited.

400
00:26:05,234 --> 00:26:08,009
Every trial that made up a number passed.

401
00:26:08,509 --> 00:26:13,192
This grader looks only for the words notes
add, so it passes an entry whether or not

402
00:26:13,192 --> 00:26:14,084
it has a number.

403
00:26:14,584 --> 00:26:18,853
The careful answer would be to change
nothing, and ask which pull request to

404
00:26:18,853 --> 00:26:19,134
cite.

405
00:26:19,634 --> 00:26:23,534
That's the one kind of answer this grader
would mark as a failure.

406
00:26:24,034 --> 00:26:27,534
Later in the course, one of our trials
does exactly that.

407
00:26:28,733 --> 00:26:33,783
So far we've counted one problem, on one
tidy request, by reading ten patches.

408
00:26:34,283 --> 00:26:37,458
What else goes wrong when the requests get
messier?

409
00:26:38,667 --> 00:26:43,067
So far, we know about one problem with our
skill, because we happened to look.

410
00:26:43,567 --> 00:26:48,192
That was on one request, and it's one of
the tidier things a person might ask for.

411
00:26:48,692 --> 00:26:53,528
Real requests mention changes that users
can't see, bundle two changes together, or

412
00:26:53,528 --> 00:26:55,567
describe a fix by how it was coded.

413
00:26:56,067 --> 00:27:00,449
Here, you'll run six ordinary requests on
two models, read what each one did, and

414
00:27:00,449 --> 00:27:02,992
come away with a short list of what goes
wrong.

415
00:27:04,200 --> 00:27:07,750
Let's start with why reading comes before
writing graders.

416
00:27:08,250 --> 00:27:12,606
It's natural to sit down and list what a
good changelog entry looks like, then

417
00:27:12,606 --> 00:27:14,225
write a grader for each item.

418
00:27:14,725 --> 00:27:19,200
But that list comes from your imagination,
and the failures come from the models.

419
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:23,775
You probably wouldn't have thought to
check for a made-up pull request number

420
00:27:23,775 --> 00:27:25,150
until a model made one up.

421
00:27:26,367 --> 00:27:30,950
People who do this for a living put it as
a rule: write evaluators for errors you

422
00:27:30,950 --> 00:27:32,817
discover, not errors you imagine.

423
00:27:33,317 --> 00:27:36,267
Evaluators is their word for graders.

424
00:27:36,767 --> 00:27:38,867
Their process is plain.

425
00:27:39,367 --> 00:27:43,353
You run realistic requests, go through
what came back, write a short note on

426
00:27:43,353 --> 00:27:44,717
each, and group the notes.

427
00:27:45,217 --> 00:27:47,467
They call it error analysis.

428
00:27:47,967 --> 00:27:52,117
Graders come after the last step, and only
for failures you've seen.

429
00:27:53,334 --> 00:27:57,209
Each group of notes gets a name, and that
makes it a failure mode.

430
00:27:57,709 --> 00:28:02,190
By failure mode, we mean a named way in
which the skill's output goes wrong, seen

431
00:28:02,190 --> 00:28:03,684
in at least one real trial.

432
00:28:04,184 --> 00:28:08,084
Recording a change that users can't see is
a failure mode.

433
00:28:08,584 --> 00:28:12,334
Saying only that trial three looked odd is
not one yet.

434
00:28:13,534 --> 00:28:17,859
Reading does something else that a list
can't: it makes you decide what you want.

435
00:28:18,359 --> 00:28:21,509
Is a security fix filed under Fixed wrong?

436
00:28:22,009 --> 00:28:26,475
You may not have an opinion until you see
one, and once you do, that opinion is a

437
00:28:26,475 --> 00:28:27,909
rule the skill is missing.

438
00:28:28,409 --> 00:28:32,437
Researchers who studied this found that
people's criteria shift while they're

439
00:28:32,437 --> 00:28:35,734
reading outputs, so criteria written
beforehand are incomplete.

440
00:28:36,934 --> 00:28:39,034
How many runs are enough?

441
00:28:39,534 --> 00:28:43,897
Those same practitioners suggest reading
at least a hundred for a real product, and

442
00:28:43,897 --> 00:28:46,684
stopping when new ones stop showing you
anything new.

443
00:28:47,184 --> 00:28:49,184
Here, we'll read twelve.

444
00:28:49,684 --> 00:28:54,102
That's enough to find the main failure
modes of a five-line skill, and too few to

445
00:28:54,102 --> 00:28:55,684
be sure we've found them all.

446
00:28:56,900 --> 00:29:00,200
That's the reasoning; here's the eval file
we'll run.

447
00:29:00,700 --> 00:29:05,275
It's a second eval file from the starter
folder, and it holds six stimuli.

448
00:29:05,775 --> 00:29:10,515
One eval file can hold as many as you
like, and Vally runs each of them on each

449
00:29:10,515 --> 00:29:10,875
model.

450
00:29:11,375 --> 00:29:16,072
Stimulus one is the request we already
know, so this run can be compared with the

451
00:29:16,072 --> 00:29:16,825
earlier ones.

452
00:29:17,325 --> 00:29:21,365
Each stimulus can have an agent
environment of its own, and here, each one

453
00:29:21,365 --> 00:29:23,275
names the changelog it starts from.

454
00:29:23,775 --> 00:29:27,125
Our skill is listed once, at the top, for
all of them.

455
00:29:27,625 --> 00:29:31,636
Down at the end, the last stimulus starts
from a different changelog: one where

456
00:29:31,636 --> 00:29:35,697
version one point three has just been
released, so there's no Unreleased section

457
00:29:35,697 --> 00:29:35,900
yet.

458
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:39,950
Nowhere in this file is there a grader,
and that's allowed.

459
00:29:41,167 --> 00:29:43,667
Between those two sit four more.

460
00:29:44,167 --> 00:29:48,617
First, a refactor: a tidying of the code
that changes nothing for users.

461
00:29:49,117 --> 00:29:52,517
Next, one pull request that contains two
changes.

462
00:29:53,017 --> 00:29:55,017
Then a security fix.

463
00:29:55,517 --> 00:29:58,542
Last, a fix described by how it was coded.

464
00:29:59,042 --> 00:30:02,967
Each one leaves the skill something to
decide that it doesn't mention.

465
00:30:04,167 --> 00:30:07,892
To judge the results, we need one fact
about this changelog.

466
00:30:08,392 --> 00:30:11,667
It follows a public format called Keep a
Changelog.

467
00:30:12,167 --> 00:30:16,774
That format has six categories, in this
order: Added, Changed, Deprecated,

468
00:30:16,774 --> 00:30:18,517
Removed, Fixed and Security.

469
00:30:19,017 --> 00:30:23,417
It writes the heading for unshipped
changes as Unreleased, inside square

470
00:30:23,417 --> 00:30:23,967
brackets.

471
00:30:24,467 --> 00:30:26,967
Our skill mentions none of this.

472
00:30:28,167 --> 00:30:32,958
Because the purpose of this run is to
read, the command adds one flag: dash dash

473
00:30:32,958 --> 00:30:33,617
skip grade.

474
00:30:34,117 --> 00:30:39,042
With it, Vally runs the agents and saves
everything as usual, and skips grading.

475
00:30:39,542 --> 00:30:43,910
This file has no graders, so here the flag
changes nothing: it states the purpose,

476
00:30:43,910 --> 00:30:46,467
and it's what you'd use on a file that has
some.

477
00:30:46,967 --> 00:30:49,517
Vally's summary still prints ticks.

478
00:30:50,017 --> 00:30:53,642
With skip grade, a tick means only that
the trial finished.

479
00:30:54,867 --> 00:30:58,392
That's the file and the flag; now let's
run it and read.

480
00:30:58,892 --> 00:31:03,743
Each of the six stimuli runs once on each
model, which gives us twelve patches to

481
00:31:03,743 --> 00:31:04,042
read.

482
00:31:04,542 --> 00:31:09,167
For every trial we'll write one note, and
then group the notes into failure modes.

483
00:31:09,667 --> 00:31:12,292
Here are the skill's four rules again.

484
00:31:12,792 --> 00:31:16,789
Which of these would you expect it to
handle badly: the refactor users can't

485
00:31:16,789 --> 00:31:20,997
see, the two changes in one pull request,
the security fix, the fix described in

486
00:31:20,997 --> 00:31:23,942
code terms, or the changelog with no
Unreleased section?

487
00:31:26,442 --> 00:31:29,317
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

488
00:31:30,534 --> 00:31:34,859
First, we check the eval file with vally
lint, which starts no agent.

489
00:31:38,220 --> 00:31:40,645
Lint says the file is valid.

490
00:31:41,145 --> 00:31:46,032
Here's the run: six stimuli on two models,
with no grading, and two workers as

491
00:31:46,032 --> 00:31:46,470
before.

492
00:31:46,970 --> 00:31:49,670
It spends twelve short agent runs.

493
00:31:50,170 --> 00:31:53,795
In the summary, we're looking for six of
six under each model.

494
00:31:58,554 --> 00:32:03,263
About a minute later, both models show six
of six: all twelve trials finished, and

495
00:32:03,263 --> 00:32:04,354
nothing was graded.

496
00:32:07,854 --> 00:32:10,329
So let's look in the results folder.

497
00:32:12,074 --> 00:32:15,199
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run.

498
00:32:18,100 --> 00:32:22,875
We change into that folder, and then into
the one for the eval, which is named six.

499
00:32:23,674 --> 00:32:26,549
Inside is a folder for each stimulus.

500
00:32:29,049 --> 00:32:33,205
Our notes go in a file of their own, named
failures, with one line per trial: the

501
00:32:33,205 --> 00:32:36,899
stimulus, the model, what the agent did,
and anything we think is wrong.

502
00:32:37,399 --> 00:32:40,874
Here's the first patch: the refactor, on
the haiku model.

503
00:32:44,200 --> 00:32:48,950
One question decides the note: would we be
happy to see this in our own changelog?

504
00:32:53,450 --> 00:32:58,313
This trial added an entry under Changed,
for a change the request said users can't

505
00:32:58,313 --> 00:32:58,550
see.

506
00:33:02,767 --> 00:33:05,867
Terra's patch for the refactor does the
same thing.

507
00:33:06,367 --> 00:33:10,717
So that's one note for each model: it
records a change users can't see.

508
00:33:14,934 --> 00:33:17,859
Let's go through the other ten the same
way.

509
00:33:18,359 --> 00:33:22,987
On the request we already know, haiku's
entry has no pull request number, and

510
00:33:22,987 --> 00:33:23,709
that's fine.

511
00:33:24,209 --> 00:33:27,409
Terra's ends with a number that nobody
gave it.

512
00:33:30,634 --> 00:33:34,884
Now the request that looked hardest: two
changes in one pull request.

513
00:33:35,384 --> 00:33:40,089
Both models split it into two entries, one
under Added and one under Fixed, and both

514
00:33:40,089 --> 00:33:41,434
cite pull request sixty.

515
00:33:41,934 --> 00:33:43,534
Those are fine.

516
00:33:46,734 --> 00:33:50,084
Both models filed the security fix under
Fixed.

517
00:33:50,584 --> 00:33:55,359
But the format has a Security category of
its own, so each of these gets a note.

518
00:33:58,567 --> 00:34:03,313
For the out-of-memory fix, haiku's entry
ends by naming the streaming parser, which

519
00:34:03,313 --> 00:34:04,742
is how the fix was coded.

520
00:34:05,242 --> 00:34:08,292
Terra's entry says only what users notice.

521
00:34:11,500 --> 00:34:14,725
Last, the changelog with no Unreleased
section.

522
00:34:15,225 --> 00:34:18,000
Both models added the heading themselves.

523
00:34:18,500 --> 00:34:22,984
Here, haiku wrote it inside square
brackets, like every other heading in that

524
00:34:22,984 --> 00:34:23,275
file.

525
00:34:23,775 --> 00:34:25,875
Terra left the brackets off.

526
00:34:29,100 --> 00:34:33,574
Grouping the notes that describe the same
kind of problem gives us a short list, at

527
00:34:33,574 --> 00:34:34,975
the top of the notes file.

528
00:34:35,475 --> 00:34:39,200
It has five failure modes, each with a
trial that shows it.

529
00:34:39,700 --> 00:34:44,425
One: there's no rule for a missing pull
request number, so a model invents one.

530
00:34:44,925 --> 00:34:47,950
Two: it records a change users can't see.

531
00:34:48,450 --> 00:34:51,475
Three: it files a security fix under
Fixed.

532
00:34:51,975 --> 00:34:54,900
Four: it describes how the fix was coded.

533
00:34:55,400 --> 00:34:59,750
Five: a new Unreleased heading doesn't
match the other headings in the file.

534
00:35:02,250 --> 00:35:06,481
Looking back at our run, the request that
looked hardest was handled well by both

535
00:35:06,481 --> 00:35:09,875
models, and the plain-looking refactor was
handled badly by both.

536
00:35:11,100 --> 00:35:13,475
So where do these five come from?

537
00:35:13,975 --> 00:35:16,975
In every one, the skill left a question
open.

538
00:35:17,475 --> 00:35:20,300
What to do without a pull request number.

539
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:24,500
Which changes count, and what it means to
write for the tool's users.

540
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:28,600
Which categories exist, and what a new
heading looks like.

541
00:35:29,100 --> 00:35:31,650
Neither model disobeyed the skill.

542
00:35:32,150 --> 00:35:35,325
Each one filled a gap, and they filled it
differently.

543
00:35:35,825 --> 00:35:39,700
That's good news, because a gap in a skill
is something we can fix.

544
00:35:40,900 --> 00:35:45,469
Your list won't match ours exactly, and
some entries on it will be matters of

545
00:35:45,469 --> 00:35:45,825
taste.

546
00:35:46,325 --> 00:35:49,675
Deciding which ones you care about is part
of the work.

547
00:35:50,175 --> 00:35:54,536
For two of ours, the project's format
settles it: a security fix has its own

548
00:35:54,536 --> 00:35:57,175
category, and the heading has square
brackets.

549
00:36:00,400 --> 00:36:02,900
We found those failures by reading.

550
00:36:03,400 --> 00:36:07,892
After every edit to the skill, we'd have
to read them all again, so can code do the

551
00:36:07,892 --> 00:36:08,325
reading?

552
00:36:09,533 --> 00:36:13,913
By now, we have a list of ways this skill
goes wrong, and we found every one by

553
00:36:13,913 --> 00:36:15,133
going through patches.

554
00:36:15,633 --> 00:36:19,733
We could fix the skill now: add a rule,
try it, and look at the result.

555
00:36:20,233 --> 00:36:24,521
But looking at the result means going
through patches again, on both models,

556
00:36:24,521 --> 00:36:26,608
several times each, after every edit.

557
00:36:27,108 --> 00:36:29,508
Hardly anybody keeps that up.

558
00:36:30,008 --> 00:36:34,396
Here, you'll hand that work to code: one
small grader for each failure, so that one

559
00:36:34,396 --> 00:36:37,833
command counts how often it happens,
before an edit and after it.

560
00:36:39,033 --> 00:36:42,808
Let's start with what makes a grader worth
having: three habits.

561
00:36:43,308 --> 00:36:46,108
First, we grade the result, not the route.

562
00:36:46,608 --> 00:36:51,333
An agent can reach a correct changelog by
many routes: opening the file or searching

563
00:36:51,333 --> 00:36:52,458
it, one edit or two.

564
00:36:52,958 --> 00:36:57,708
Any grader that insists on one route fails
every good route we didn't think of.

565
00:36:58,208 --> 00:37:00,933
So we look at what the agent left behind.

566
00:37:02,133 --> 00:37:06,318
Second, we write one grader for each
failure mode, and name it after the

567
00:37:06,318 --> 00:37:06,783
failure.

568
00:37:07,283 --> 00:37:11,563
If a grader called no made-up number
fails, you know what went wrong without

569
00:37:11,563 --> 00:37:12,408
opening a file.

570
00:37:12,908 --> 00:37:17,683
One grader that checks five things at once
only tells you that something went wrong.

571
00:37:18,900 --> 00:37:21,775
Third, a grader has to be able to fail.

572
00:37:22,275 --> 00:37:26,883
So far we've had one grader, and it passed
every trial, including the trials that

573
00:37:26,883 --> 00:37:27,850
made up a number.

574
00:37:28,350 --> 00:37:31,800
A grader that has never failed might be
checking nothing.

575
00:37:32,300 --> 00:37:36,741
So the first time we run a new grader, we
run it where we already know the failure

576
00:37:36,741 --> 00:37:37,175
happens.

577
00:37:37,675 --> 00:37:40,425
If it passes there, the grader is wrong.

578
00:37:41,633 --> 00:37:46,183
One more habit goes with those three: code
does everything that code can decide.

579
00:37:46,683 --> 00:37:51,208
A pattern costs nothing to run, and it
gives the same answer every time.

580
00:37:51,708 --> 00:37:56,054
Some qualities are beyond a pattern, such
as whether an entry is written for the

581
00:37:56,054 --> 00:37:57,358
people who use the tool.

582
00:37:57,858 --> 00:38:00,008
Those wait until later.

583
00:38:01,233 --> 00:38:04,808
Those are the habits; here's what Vally
gives us to follow them.

584
00:38:05,308 --> 00:38:09,277
Three of the failure modes on our list are
about what the agent added to the

585
00:38:09,277 --> 00:38:11,783
changelog, or whether adding anything was
right.

586
00:38:12,283 --> 00:38:16,983
The patch already holds exactly that, and
Vally has three graders that look at it.

587
00:38:17,483 --> 00:38:21,608
Vally calls the patch the diff, which is
another word for the same thing.

588
00:38:22,108 --> 00:38:26,558
Diff contains passes when the patch
contains the text or pattern we give it.

589
00:38:27,058 --> 00:38:30,658
Diff not contains passes when the patch
doesn't contain it.

590
00:38:31,158 --> 00:38:34,983
Finally, diff empty passes when the agent
changed nothing at all.

591
00:38:36,200 --> 00:38:40,700
One thing to know before using them: these
graders see the whole patch, including the

592
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:42,500
unchanged lines around the change.

593
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:46,000
Here's the patch from a trial that made up
a number.

594
00:38:46,500 --> 00:38:50,800
This unchanged line happens to hold a pull
request number, forty-one.

595
00:38:51,300 --> 00:38:55,818
It's in the patch, so a grader that looked
for any number in brackets would find one

596
00:38:55,818 --> 00:38:56,625
in every trial.

597
00:38:57,125 --> 00:39:00,550
What matters is this line, the one the
agent added.

598
00:39:01,050 --> 00:39:06,025
So a grader for made-up numbers has to
look only at lines that begin with a plus.

599
00:39:07,233 --> 00:39:11,962
Each of these graders takes a value, which
is exact text, or a pattern, which is a

600
00:39:11,962 --> 00:39:13,058
regular expression.

601
00:39:13,558 --> 00:39:17,708
Here's the eval file we're going to run:
three of the six stimuli we went through

602
00:39:17,708 --> 00:39:19,808
earlier, with graders at the end of each.

603
00:39:20,308 --> 00:39:24,258
Its first stimulus is the typo fix, and it
gets two graders.

604
00:39:24,758 --> 00:39:28,308
The first asks whether the agent added an
entry at all.

605
00:39:28,808 --> 00:39:33,321
Nothing in the starting changelog mentions
notes add, so that text can only be in the

606
00:39:33,321 --> 00:39:34,808
patch if the agent wrote it.

607
00:39:35,308 --> 00:39:39,639
The second is the grader for made-up
numbers, and it passes only if its pattern

608
00:39:39,639 --> 00:39:41,283
is found nowhere in the patch.

609
00:39:42,500 --> 00:39:45,100
Here's that pattern, a piece at a time.

610
00:39:45,600 --> 00:39:50,418
A caret and an escaped plus mean a line
that starts with a plus: a line the agent

611
00:39:50,418 --> 00:39:50,775
added.

612
00:39:51,275 --> 00:39:55,226
The switch at the front makes the caret
match at the start of every line, where it

613
00:39:55,226 --> 00:39:57,925
would otherwise match only the start of
the whole patch.

614
00:39:58,425 --> 00:40:02,500
Then comes anything at all, and then a
hash and digits in brackets.

615
00:40:03,000 --> 00:40:06,900
In words: an added line that contains a
pull request number.

616
00:40:08,100 --> 00:40:11,225
The other two stimuli get one grader each.

617
00:40:11,725 --> 00:40:16,451
The refactor changes nothing for users, so
the right result is no change to the

618
00:40:16,451 --> 00:40:17,050
changelog.

619
00:40:17,550 --> 00:40:21,325
Diff empty checks exactly that, and it
takes no settings.

620
00:40:21,825 --> 00:40:26,458
For the security fix, the grader looks for
an added line that starts with a Security

621
00:40:26,458 --> 00:40:26,900
heading.

622
00:40:27,400 --> 00:40:31,275
It passes when the agent has created a
Security category.

623
00:40:32,500 --> 00:40:37,544
Once a stimulus has more than one grader,
a trial passes only if every one of them

624
00:40:37,544 --> 00:40:37,975
passes.

625
00:40:38,475 --> 00:40:43,493
Vally lint prints a warning, named scoring
defaults applied, to say that this rule is

626
00:40:43,493 --> 00:40:44,025
in force.

627
00:40:44,525 --> 00:40:47,575
It's the rule we want, so we expect the
warning.

628
00:40:48,800 --> 00:40:52,050
That's how the graders work; now let's
count with them.

629
00:40:52,550 --> 00:40:57,275
First, we run these three stimuli three
times on each model, with the skill as it

630
00:40:57,275 --> 00:40:57,450
is.

631
00:40:57,950 --> 00:41:01,825
Then we add three rules to the skill, and
run the same command again.

632
00:41:02,325 --> 00:41:06,714
Each time, we count how many of a
stimulus's three trials passed, on each

633
00:41:06,714 --> 00:41:07,075
model.

634
00:41:07,575 --> 00:41:10,975
What would you expect the edit to change,
on each model?

635
00:41:13,475 --> 00:41:16,350
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

636
00:41:17,566 --> 00:41:22,191
The graders are now in the eval file, so
we lint it first, which costs nothing.

637
00:41:25,273 --> 00:41:28,598
One warning, the one we expected, and no
errors.

638
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:37,082
Now we measure the skill as it is: the
command from before, with three runs of

639
00:41:37,082 --> 00:41:38,675
each stimulus, two at a time.

640
00:41:39,175 --> 00:41:41,975
It spends eighteen short agent runs.

641
00:41:42,475 --> 00:41:46,665
We're after the table at the end, which
says, for each model, how many of the

642
00:41:46,665 --> 00:41:48,625
three stimuli passed in every trial.

643
00:41:50,946 --> 00:41:55,546
About three minutes later, it says one of
the three on haiku, and none on terra.

644
00:41:59,046 --> 00:42:03,342
Above that table, Vally lists each
stimulus that didn't pass, with the grader

645
00:42:03,342 --> 00:42:05,071
that objected and its evidence.

646
00:42:11,300 --> 00:42:15,637
The same output says how many trials of
each stimulus passed, and that fills in

647
00:42:15,637 --> 00:42:17,175
the first half of the table.

648
00:42:17,675 --> 00:42:21,400
On the typo fix, haiku passed three of
three, and terra none.

649
00:42:21,900 --> 00:42:26,150
Neither model passed the refactor or the
security fix, in any trial.

650
00:42:29,366 --> 00:42:31,741
Next comes the edit to the skill.

651
00:42:32,241 --> 00:42:36,577
Each failure is a case the skill left
open, so we add three rules at the end,

652
00:42:36,577 --> 00:42:38,041
one for each failure mode.

653
00:42:38,541 --> 00:42:42,825
The first covers a missing pull request
number: the agent leaves the reference

654
00:42:42,825 --> 00:42:45,516
off, never makes one up, and doesn't stop
to ask.

655
00:42:46,016 --> 00:42:50,816
In a Vally run nobody is there to answer a
question, so we chose to leave it off.

656
00:42:51,316 --> 00:42:56,066
The second says that a change which makes
no difference to users gets no entry.

657
00:42:56,566 --> 00:43:01,466
The third lists the categories in order,
and puts a security fix under Security.

658
00:43:02,666 --> 00:43:07,296
With the rules in the skill, we measure
again, the same way, into a second results

659
00:43:07,296 --> 00:43:07,691
folder.

660
00:43:08,191 --> 00:43:10,941
That's another eighteen agent runs.

661
00:43:19,773 --> 00:43:24,148
About three minutes later, the table says
three of three, on both models.

662
00:43:27,648 --> 00:43:32,598
This time, no failures are listed above
it: every trial of every stimulus passed.

663
00:43:37,800 --> 00:43:40,400
Here are both counts, side by side.

664
00:43:40,900 --> 00:43:44,950
The made-up number is gone on terra, the
model that produced it.

665
00:43:45,450 --> 00:43:49,075
Neither model adds an entry for the
refactor any more.

666
00:43:49,575 --> 00:43:53,175
The security rule worked every time, on
both models.

667
00:43:57,400 --> 00:43:59,375
So what do the counts show?

668
00:43:59,875 --> 00:44:04,475
One edit added three rules, and in our
run, all three worked on both models.

669
00:44:04,975 --> 00:44:09,097
An earlier run of the same edit came out
differently: haiku still filed one

670
00:44:09,097 --> 00:44:11,900
security fix under Fixed, and passed eight
of nine.

671
00:44:12,400 --> 00:44:16,891
So the same words can have a different
effect, on another model or on another

672
00:44:16,891 --> 00:44:17,125
run.

673
00:44:17,625 --> 00:44:22,038
Going through a couple of patches by hand
might well have shown us only the trials

674
00:44:22,038 --> 00:44:22,900
where it worked.

675
00:44:23,400 --> 00:44:27,925
A count shows all of them, and one command
will count again after the next edit.

676
00:44:29,133 --> 00:44:32,958
It's also worth a look at what the graders
did in the first run.

677
00:44:33,458 --> 00:44:38,183
Every one of them failed somewhere, on the
kinds of trial we already knew to be bad.

678
00:44:38,683 --> 00:44:42,183
That's a first piece of evidence that they
check what we meant.

679
00:44:42,683 --> 00:44:45,458
Later, we'll run that test on purpose.

680
00:44:46,667 --> 00:44:49,567
Patterns now catch the failures we named.

681
00:44:50,067 --> 00:44:54,184
But checking that nothing else in the file
changed is not a pattern: it needs a

682
00:44:54,184 --> 00:44:56,842
script, kept where the agent can't read or
edit it.

683
00:44:58,067 --> 00:45:02,619
With a pattern, a grader can say that a
patch contains an entry citing a pull

684
00:45:02,619 --> 00:45:03,092
request.

685
00:45:03,592 --> 00:45:08,015
It can't say that the entry sits under
Fixed, inside Unreleased, and not inside a

686
00:45:08,015 --> 00:45:09,817
version that has already shipped.

687
00:45:10,317 --> 00:45:14,838
Checking position takes a few lines of
code to read the file, and that makes the

688
00:45:14,838 --> 00:45:15,742
grader a script.

689
00:45:16,242 --> 00:45:20,617
But a script is a file, and the agent
works in a folder full of files.

690
00:45:21,117 --> 00:45:25,622
Here, you'll add a grading script that the
agent never sees, and find out what it

691
00:45:25,622 --> 00:45:26,067
catches.

692
00:45:27,267 --> 00:45:30,567
Let's start with why the agent mustn't see
the grader.

693
00:45:31,067 --> 00:45:34,717
An agent can read and edit everything in
its workspace.

694
00:45:35,217 --> 00:45:39,188
If the grading script were there, the
agent could read it, and learn exactly

695
00:45:39,188 --> 00:45:40,442
what's being looked for.

696
00:45:40,942 --> 00:45:44,267
Worse, it could edit the script so that it
always passes.

697
00:45:44,767 --> 00:45:49,643
Or, most likely, it could be distracted by
a file it didn't expect, and start fixing

698
00:45:49,643 --> 00:45:49,817
it.

699
00:45:51,034 --> 00:45:53,234
This has happened in practice.

700
00:45:53,734 --> 00:45:58,434
Benchmarks are public sets of tasks with
graders, made for comparing agents.

701
00:45:58,934 --> 00:46:03,139
In April twenty twenty-six, researchers at
Berkeley took eight well-known benchmarks,

702
00:46:03,139 --> 00:46:07,197
and scored between seventy-three and one
hundred percent on them, with agents that

703
00:46:07,197 --> 00:46:08,434
solved none of the tasks.

704
00:46:08,934 --> 00:46:13,091
One way was reading answers that the
graders had left where an agent could

705
00:46:13,091 --> 00:46:13,709
reach them.

706
00:46:14,209 --> 00:46:18,990
Two months later, the makers of the Cursor
editor reported on one model's passing

707
00:46:18,990 --> 00:46:20,584
runs on a coding benchmark.

708
00:46:21,084 --> 00:46:25,759
In sixty-three percent of them, the agent
had found the published fix and copied it.

709
00:46:26,967 --> 00:46:30,342
Our changelog skill doesn't need a
security review.

710
00:46:30,842 --> 00:46:32,992
It needs two plain rules.

711
00:46:33,492 --> 00:46:37,801
First, grading scripts and expected
answers stay where the agent can't see

712
00:46:37,801 --> 00:46:38,092
them.

713
00:46:38,592 --> 00:46:43,217
Second, no grader depends on a file the
agent could have altered to make it pass.

714
00:46:44,434 --> 00:46:48,109
Those are the rules; here's what an agent
is actually given.

715
00:46:48,609 --> 00:46:53,187
In an eval file, the agent environment
lists the skills and files copied into the

716
00:46:53,187 --> 00:46:55,109
workspace before the agent starts.

717
00:46:55,609 --> 00:46:57,809
Two details matter now.

718
00:46:58,309 --> 00:47:02,513
Each skill is copied as a whole folder:
everything inside it goes into the

719
00:47:02,513 --> 00:47:05,184
workspace, and not only the SKILL dot M D
file.

720
00:47:05,684 --> 00:47:10,184
Each listed file arrives under the
destination name on the line below it.

721
00:47:11,400 --> 00:47:13,675
We can check this for ourselves.

722
00:47:14,175 --> 00:47:18,456
Adding dash dash workspace and a folder
name to vally eval tells Vally to keep

723
00:47:18,456 --> 00:47:22,626
each trial's workspace in that folder,
instead of deleting it when the trial

724
00:47:22,626 --> 00:47:22,900
ends.

725
00:47:23,400 --> 00:47:27,766
Inside the folder we name, there's a
folder that Vally calls main, and inside

726
00:47:27,766 --> 00:47:29,750
that, one folder for each stimulus.

727
00:47:30,250 --> 00:47:33,825
Dash dash workspace works with one model
at a time.

728
00:47:34,325 --> 00:47:38,710
The kept path has no model name in it, so
with several models, Vally stops before

729
00:47:38,710 --> 00:47:41,525
running anything, and reports a workspace
collision.

730
00:47:42,734 --> 00:47:47,665
So anything stored inside a skill's folder
is handed to the agent: eval files,

731
00:47:47,665 --> 00:47:49,309
graders, expected answers.

732
00:47:49,809 --> 00:47:54,484
So the evals folder sits beside the skills
folder, and never inside a skill.

733
00:47:55,700 --> 00:47:58,125
So where can a grading script go?

734
00:47:58,625 --> 00:48:03,275
Vally has a second environment for exactly
this, named the grading environment.

735
00:48:03,775 --> 00:48:07,995
It lists files that Vally adds after the
agent has stopped, to a copy of the

736
00:48:07,995 --> 00:48:09,050
finished workspace.

737
00:48:09,550 --> 00:48:13,825
Only graders run in that copy, and it's
thrown away when grading ends.

738
00:48:14,325 --> 00:48:18,957
So the script joins the files once the
agent has finished, in a folder the agent

739
00:48:18,957 --> 00:48:19,825
never works in.

740
00:48:21,034 --> 00:48:25,159
Here's a stimulus that uses one, in our
changelog eval file.

741
00:48:25,659 --> 00:48:30,259
Its grading environment is written like
the files part of the agent environment.

742
00:48:30,759 --> 00:48:35,610
This source line names our script, in a
grading folder under evals, which no agent

743
00:48:35,610 --> 00:48:36,734
environment copies.

744
00:48:37,234 --> 00:48:41,868
Its destination is a folder in the grading
copy whose name starts with a dot, which

745
00:48:41,868 --> 00:48:43,934
keeps it away from the agent's files.

746
00:48:44,434 --> 00:48:47,534
Then comes the grader, and its type is
program.

747
00:48:48,034 --> 00:48:52,759
It runs one program inside the grading
copy, and takes the verdict from what that

748
00:48:52,759 --> 00:48:53,634
program prints.

749
00:48:54,134 --> 00:48:58,230
Here the program is node, and its
arguments are the script to run, and then

750
00:48:58,230 --> 00:48:59,159
what to look for.

751
00:49:00,367 --> 00:49:05,264
From here on, a program grader that runs a
script is a script grader, and this is our

752
00:49:05,264 --> 00:49:05,667
script.

753
00:49:06,167 --> 00:49:09,392
It's plain JavaScript, with nothing to
install.

754
00:49:09,892 --> 00:49:14,040
Its opening comment says what it checks:
each argument is a category and a pull

755
00:49:14,040 --> 00:49:18,345
request number, and the Unreleased section
must have an entry under that category,

756
00:49:18,345 --> 00:49:19,342
citing that number.

757
00:49:19,842 --> 00:49:22,492
Its last lines say how it reports.

758
00:49:22,992 --> 00:49:27,819
It prints the result as one line of JSON
with five fields, and Vally needs all of

759
00:49:27,819 --> 00:49:28,117
them.

760
00:49:28,617 --> 00:49:32,467
Its name and kind say which check this is,
and that code made it.

761
00:49:32,967 --> 00:49:37,292
Passed is the verdict, and score is one
for a pass, and zero for a fail.

762
00:49:37,792 --> 00:49:42,061
Evidence is a sentence saying what the
script found, and Vally saves it in the

763
00:49:42,061 --> 00:49:43,867
results as the grader's evidence.

764
00:49:44,367 --> 00:49:48,854
Finally, the script exits with code zero
whether the entry is there or not, because

765
00:49:48,854 --> 00:49:50,692
the verdict is in what it printed.

766
00:49:51,192 --> 00:49:55,092
Any other exit code would mean that the
script itself had broken.

767
00:49:56,300 --> 00:49:58,925
Two new stimuli use the script.

768
00:49:59,425 --> 00:50:04,175
In the first, named two-changes, one pull
request adds a flag and fixes a bug.

769
00:50:04,675 --> 00:50:09,014
Its arguments, Added equals sixty and
Fixed equals sixty, mean that Unreleased

770
00:50:09,014 --> 00:50:13,575
must have an entry under Added citing pull
request sixty, and another under Fixed.

771
00:50:14,075 --> 00:50:18,349
An entry in a released version doesn't
count, and the heading must be written

772
00:50:18,349 --> 00:50:20,625
exactly as Unreleased in square brackets.

773
00:50:21,125 --> 00:50:25,919
Our second stimulus, named after-release,
starts from a changelog with no Unreleased

774
00:50:25,919 --> 00:50:26,375
section.

775
00:50:26,875 --> 00:50:31,550
It asks the script for one entry, under
Added, citing pull request fifty-eight.

776
00:50:32,767 --> 00:50:35,667
Two safeguards are worth knowing in
advance.

777
00:50:36,167 --> 00:50:40,469
Vally lint refuses a grading file whose
source the agent could see, such as a

778
00:50:40,469 --> 00:50:42,592
script stored inside the skill folder.

779
00:50:43,092 --> 00:50:47,374
If a script prints a result with a field
missing, Vally fails the trial, and says

780
00:50:47,374 --> 00:50:50,017
that the grader's output did not match the
schema.

781
00:50:50,517 --> 00:50:53,892
That's a mistake in the script, and not in
the agent's work.

782
00:50:55,100 --> 00:50:59,568
Vally has a second grader that runs a
script, named run command, which passes

783
00:50:59,568 --> 00:51:01,425
when the script exits with zero.

784
00:51:01,925 --> 00:51:06,101
That grader is simpler to write, and it
tells you less: when it fails, the

785
00:51:06,101 --> 00:51:07,850
evidence is only the exit code.

786
00:51:08,350 --> 00:51:11,350
We use program, so that the results say
why.

787
00:51:12,567 --> 00:51:15,092
That's how it works; now let's use it.

788
00:51:15,592 --> 00:51:19,992
First, we'll keep one workspace and list
it, to see what an agent is given.

789
00:51:20,492 --> 00:51:25,071
Then we'll add the two stimuli that use
the script, and run all five stimuli on

790
00:51:25,071 --> 00:51:25,767
both models.

791
00:51:26,267 --> 00:51:30,667
Whatever the script fails, we'll answer
with a rule in the skill, and run again.

792
00:51:31,167 --> 00:51:34,742
Which files would you expect to find in
the kept workspace?

793
00:51:37,242 --> 00:51:40,117
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

794
00:51:41,334 --> 00:51:46,240
Here's the run that keeps a workspace: the
one-request eval file, on one model, with

795
00:51:46,240 --> 00:51:47,409
dash dash workspace.

796
00:51:47,909 --> 00:51:50,284
It spends one agent run.

797
00:51:58,514 --> 00:52:03,484
Moments later, the stimulus is a pass, on
a single summary line, because there's one

798
00:52:03,484 --> 00:52:03,839
model.

799
00:52:06,339 --> 00:52:09,414
Now we list every file under the kept
folder.

800
00:52:11,100 --> 00:52:15,727
There's the skill folder with its one
file, and the changelog, as the agent left

801
00:52:15,727 --> 00:52:15,900
it.

802
00:52:18,400 --> 00:52:22,875
That's everything the agent had, and
nothing from the evals folder is in it.

803
00:52:26,100 --> 00:52:30,360
Next, we paste the two stimuli onto the
end of the changelog eval file; the

804
00:52:30,360 --> 00:52:33,200
starter folder has them ready, in a
snippets file.

805
00:52:33,700 --> 00:52:38,063
That makes five stimuli: three with
pattern graders, and two graded by the

806
00:52:38,063 --> 00:52:38,475
script.

807
00:52:39,700 --> 00:52:44,225
Lint comes first, because it's free, and
catches mistakes before we spend anything.

808
00:52:47,427 --> 00:52:52,552
It gives the one warning this eval file
already had, about scoring, and no errors.

809
00:52:55,052 --> 00:52:59,402
Now the run: all five stimuli, once on
each model, with two workers.

810
00:52:59,902 --> 00:53:02,352
That spends ten agent runs.

811
00:53:02,852 --> 00:53:07,335
With one trial per stimulus, each failure
is listed with the grader that failed, and

812
00:53:07,335 --> 00:53:10,377
a script grader's line carries the
script's own sentence.

813
00:53:12,667 --> 00:53:16,725
About a minute later, the after-release
stimulus has failed on terra, and the

814
00:53:16,725 --> 00:53:20,467
evidence is the script's own: no
Unreleased heading in square brackets.

815
00:53:24,967 --> 00:53:29,292
Meanwhile, haiku passed all five, so its
new heading had the brackets.

816
00:53:31,792 --> 00:53:36,167
At the bottom, the table counts the
stimuli each model passed, out of five.

817
00:53:40,367 --> 00:53:44,596
That evidence says what the script didn't
find; the trial's patch shows what the

818
00:53:44,596 --> 00:53:45,917
agent wrote in its place.

819
00:53:48,367 --> 00:53:52,092
We change into the run's folder, and then
into the eval's.

820
00:53:53,440 --> 00:53:56,465
Here's terra's patch for the after-release
stimulus.

821
00:54:02,580 --> 00:54:06,920
Its new heading says Unreleased without
the brackets, so the script found no

822
00:54:06,920 --> 00:54:08,005
Unreleased section.

823
00:54:15,234 --> 00:54:19,084
That's a question the skill left open, so
we answer it in the skill.

824
00:54:19,584 --> 00:54:23,754
One more rule goes at the end: if the
changelog has no Unreleased section, add

825
00:54:23,754 --> 00:54:27,977
one above the newest version, with its
heading written exactly, square brackets

826
00:54:27,977 --> 00:54:28,459
included.

827
00:54:29,667 --> 00:54:33,492
Then the same five stimuli run again,
saved to a new folder.

828
00:54:33,992 --> 00:54:36,492
That's another ten agent runs.

829
00:54:45,054 --> 00:54:49,829
This time, both models passed all five, so
terra's new heading has its brackets.

830
00:54:53,329 --> 00:54:57,379
With nothing failing, there are no
failures to list above the summary.

831
00:55:01,600 --> 00:55:03,650
So what did the script catch?

832
00:55:04,150 --> 00:55:08,294
In the first run, terra wrote the new
heading without brackets, and haiku wrote

833
00:55:08,294 --> 00:55:08,975
it with them.

834
00:55:09,475 --> 00:55:13,840
With one trial for each model, that shows
the failure can happen, and not which

835
00:55:13,840 --> 00:55:15,000
model is prone to it.

836
00:55:15,500 --> 00:55:19,600
Once the rule was in the skill, both
models wrote the heading correctly.

837
00:55:20,800 --> 00:55:25,409
In our two runs, the script passed all
four two-changes trials, and all four were

838
00:55:25,409 --> 00:55:25,750
right.

839
00:55:26,250 --> 00:55:30,834
An earlier run of these same stimuli went
differently: the script passed both of its

840
00:55:30,834 --> 00:55:33,125
two-changes trials, and haiku's was wrong.

841
00:55:33,625 --> 00:55:35,625
Here's part of that patch.

842
00:55:36,125 --> 00:55:40,646
That agent wrote its Fixed entry where the
heading of version one point two had been,

843
00:55:40,646 --> 00:55:41,975
and deleted that heading.

844
00:55:42,475 --> 00:55:46,929
That version has been released, and its
entries now sit under Unreleased, as if

845
00:55:46,929 --> 00:55:48,225
they had never shipped.

846
00:55:52,434 --> 00:55:54,459
Why did the script pass it?

847
00:55:54,959 --> 00:55:59,358
It finds the Unreleased heading, and takes
everything down to the next version

848
00:55:59,358 --> 00:55:59,809
heading.

849
00:56:00,309 --> 00:56:04,525
With that heading gone, the next version
heading was further down, so the script

850
00:56:04,525 --> 00:56:06,634
found every entry it wanted, and passed.

851
00:56:07,134 --> 00:56:11,461
When a grader passes a wrong answer, it's
worse than having none, because its tick

852
00:56:11,461 --> 00:56:12,834
tells you to stop looking.

853
00:56:13,334 --> 00:56:17,529
Our skill already says to leave released
versions alone, so the gap is in the

854
00:56:17,529 --> 00:56:19,109
grader, and not in the skill.

855
00:56:20,334 --> 00:56:23,059
One more thing happened, and it's quieter.

856
00:56:23,559 --> 00:56:27,820
In an earlier run, with the security rule
already in the skill, haiku filed the

857
00:56:27,820 --> 00:56:30,409
security fix under Fixed in one of three
trials.

858
00:56:30,909 --> 00:56:35,604
Across our runs with that rule, it has
filed it under Security every time: five

859
00:56:35,604 --> 00:56:36,734
trials out of five.

860
00:56:37,234 --> 00:56:39,734
Nothing was changed to cause that.

861
00:56:40,234 --> 00:56:44,838
It's the variation from run to run, and a
reminder that a few trials are a glimpse,

862
00:56:44,838 --> 00:56:46,059
and not a measurement.

863
00:56:47,267 --> 00:56:52,167
A hidden script now decides pass and fail,
and only we can see whether it's right.

864
00:56:52,667 --> 00:56:55,017
So how do we test a grader?

865
00:56:56,233 --> 00:57:00,740
Five stimuli now have graders, and those
graders decide whether the skill is

866
00:57:00,740 --> 00:57:01,333
improving.

867
00:57:01,833 --> 00:57:05,608
But a grader is a small program, and
programs have bugs.

868
00:57:06,108 --> 00:57:09,233
If one is wrong, we might fix things that
were fine.

869
00:57:09,733 --> 00:57:14,158
Or we might believe a failure is gone,
when the grader simply can't see it.

870
00:57:14,658 --> 00:57:18,908
Here, you'll test your graders against a
correct answer and against an untouched

871
00:57:18,908 --> 00:57:20,608
file, without starting an agent.

872
00:57:21,833 --> 00:57:24,683
Let's start with how a grader can be
wrong.

873
00:57:25,183 --> 00:57:28,333
It gives one of two answers, so there are
two ways.

874
00:57:28,833 --> 00:57:33,008
First, it can fail correct work: the agent
did the right thing, and the grader

875
00:57:33,008 --> 00:57:36,433
objects anyway, perhaps because it insists
on one exact wording.

876
00:57:36,933 --> 00:57:39,583
Then we chase a problem that isn't there.

877
00:57:40,083 --> 00:57:44,494
Second, it can pass work that was never
done: it's so loose, or so broken, that

878
00:57:44,494 --> 00:57:46,783
anything satisfies it, including nothing.

879
00:57:47,283 --> 00:57:50,208
Then we stop looking at a problem that's
still there.

880
00:57:51,433 --> 00:57:54,508
To test for each, we need the matching
example.

881
00:57:55,008 --> 00:57:59,406
For the first, that's a result we know is
correct: if the grader fails it, the

882
00:57:59,406 --> 00:58:00,308
grader is wrong.

883
00:58:00,808 --> 00:58:05,325
For the second, it's a result where we
know no work was done: the starting files,

884
00:58:05,325 --> 00:58:05,883
untouched.

885
00:58:06,383 --> 00:58:09,533
If the grader passes that, it isn't
checking the work.

886
00:58:10,033 --> 00:58:14,250
You've seen a rough form of the second
test already, when new graders ran against

887
00:58:14,250 --> 00:58:16,958
the skill before it was edited, and each
one failed.

888
00:58:17,458 --> 00:58:20,583
Both tests can be made exact, and
repeatable.

889
00:58:21,800 --> 00:58:26,250
That's the idea; here's the first example,
the result known to be correct.

890
00:58:26,750 --> 00:58:31,200
You already own several: every trial whose
patch you've read and judged right.

891
00:58:31,700 --> 00:58:35,925
One of those, copied to a place where you
keep it, is called a golden patch.

892
00:58:36,425 --> 00:58:40,643
That file holds exactly the changes a
correct answer would make to the starting

893
00:58:40,643 --> 00:58:45,075
files, and golden is the usual word for a
reference answer that's known to be good.

894
00:58:45,575 --> 00:58:49,914
Here's the one we're about to keep, for
the typo fix stimulus: the patch from one

895
00:58:49,914 --> 00:58:51,575
of our saved trials, unchanged.

896
00:58:52,075 --> 00:58:55,950
It adds a Fixed category under Unreleased,
in the right place.

897
00:58:56,450 --> 00:59:01,368
Its one entry mentions the command and
cites no pull request, because the request

898
00:59:01,368 --> 00:59:01,975
gave none.

899
00:59:05,200 --> 00:59:09,544
The eval file names the golden patch
inside the stimulus it belongs to, and

900
00:59:09,544 --> 00:59:11,050
here's how that will look.

901
00:59:11,550 --> 00:59:15,750
Its key is golden underscore patch, and it
sits beside graders.

902
00:59:16,250 --> 00:59:20,852
The path on the next line is relative to
the eval file, and must sit in the eval

903
00:59:20,852 --> 00:59:22,175
file's folder or below.

904
00:59:22,675 --> 00:59:25,325
Not every stimulus can have one.

905
00:59:25,825 --> 00:59:30,170
For the refactor stimulus, the correct
answer is to change nothing, and there's

906
00:59:30,170 --> 00:59:31,325
no patch for nothing.

907
00:59:32,533 --> 00:59:34,858
Now for the tool that uses it.

908
00:59:35,358 --> 00:59:40,333
Vally oracle runs your graders against a
known answer, with no agent involved.

909
00:59:40,833 --> 00:59:45,317
The word comes from testing, where an
oracle is a source of answers known to be

910
00:59:45,317 --> 00:59:45,658
right.

911
00:59:46,158 --> 00:59:50,958
Plain vally oracle builds the workspace
exactly as it would for a real trial.

912
00:59:51,458 --> 00:59:55,358
Where an agent would have worked, it
applies the golden patch instead.

913
00:59:55,858 --> 01:00:00,600
Applying a patch means making the changes
it records, so its plus lines are added to

914
01:00:00,600 --> 01:00:01,108
the file.

915
01:00:01,608 --> 01:00:05,930
Then it runs the graders, and a grader
that fails here is failing a correct

916
01:00:05,930 --> 01:00:06,333
answer.

917
01:00:07,533 --> 01:00:12,242
Adding dash dash no golden input does the
other test: the same workspace, with

918
01:00:12,242 --> 01:00:13,208
nothing applied.

919
01:00:13,708 --> 01:00:17,183
Vally's name for that untouched state is
the baseline.

920
01:00:17,683 --> 01:00:21,987
So on one side the oracle plays an agent
that got everything right, and on the

921
01:00:21,987 --> 01:00:23,808
other, an agent that did nothing.

922
01:00:25,033 --> 01:00:29,904
On the baseline, a grader ought to fail,
and the oracle reports that as good news:

923
01:00:29,904 --> 01:00:31,033
failed as expected.

924
01:00:31,533 --> 01:00:34,383
That grader depends on the work being
done.

925
01:00:34,883 --> 01:00:37,358
There are two other things it can say.

926
01:00:37,858 --> 01:00:42,839
Next, N/A: that's for a grader that checks
something is absent, such as diff not

927
01:00:42,839 --> 01:00:44,333
contains, or diff empty.

928
01:00:44,833 --> 01:00:49,389
With nothing done, nothing unwanted is
there, so that grader passes honestly, and

929
01:00:49,389 --> 01:00:51,358
the test tells us nothing about it.

930
01:00:51,858 --> 01:00:56,500
Last, trivially passing: the grader passed
the untouched workspace, and it isn't an

931
01:00:56,500 --> 01:00:57,283
absence check.

932
01:00:57,783 --> 01:01:02,203
It doesn't depend on the work at all, so
the oracle prints baseline problem, and

933
01:01:02,203 --> 01:01:03,308
exits with an error.

934
01:01:04,533 --> 01:01:08,808
The command takes dash e and the eval
file, as vally eval does.

935
01:01:09,308 --> 01:01:13,558
Dash dash stimulus, with a name, limits
the oracle to one stimulus.

936
01:01:14,058 --> 01:01:18,683
Dash dash verbose prints each grader's
evidence: what it found, or didn't find.

937
01:01:19,183 --> 01:01:24,058
Without dash dash stimulus, the oracle
covers every stimulus that has a golden

938
01:01:24,058 --> 01:01:24,433
patch.

939
01:01:25,633 --> 01:01:28,633
That's how it works; now let's prove our
graders.

940
01:01:29,133 --> 01:01:33,975
First, we'll keep one correct trial as a
golden patch, and run the oracle both ways

941
01:01:33,975 --> 01:01:34,908
on its stimulus.

942
01:01:35,408 --> 01:01:38,258
Then we'll do the same for the other
stimuli.

943
01:01:38,758 --> 01:01:42,308
No step starts an agent, so none of it
costs anything.

944
01:01:42,808 --> 01:01:47,008
Which of these six graders would you
expect to fail an untouched file?

945
01:01:49,508 --> 01:01:52,383
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

946
01:01:53,600 --> 01:01:58,571
First, we find our most recent run of the
changelog eval, saved after the last edit

947
01:01:58,571 --> 01:01:59,350
to the skill.

948
01:02:01,480 --> 01:02:04,605
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run.

949
01:02:06,105 --> 01:02:09,680
We change into it, and then into the
folder for the eval.

950
01:02:14,500 --> 01:02:18,150
The typo fix trial whose patch we just
read is in here.

951
01:02:18,650 --> 01:02:23,592
So we make a folder named golden beside
the eval file, and copy that trial's patch

952
01:02:23,592 --> 01:02:24,075
into it.

953
01:02:33,360 --> 01:02:35,960
Then we go back up to the practice folder.

954
01:02:41,733 --> 01:02:46,812
Next, we add those two lines to the typo
fix stimulus in the eval file, just above

955
01:02:46,812 --> 01:02:47,308
graders.

956
01:02:48,533 --> 01:02:53,408
Now the first test: the oracle on that one
stimulus, with the golden patch applied.

957
01:02:53,908 --> 01:02:56,958
What matters is the mark beside each
grader.

958
01:02:59,193 --> 01:03:03,924
Both graders pass the correct answer, each
with its evidence, and the last line says

959
01:03:03,924 --> 01:03:04,318
passed.

960
01:03:08,818 --> 01:03:13,845
On the stimulus's line, the word oracle in
brackets marks a run with the golden patch

961
01:03:13,845 --> 01:03:14,318
applied.

962
01:03:15,818 --> 01:03:20,567
The warning on the first line is about
scoring, and only says that every grader

963
01:03:20,567 --> 01:03:21,168
must pass.

964
01:03:22,668 --> 01:03:27,393
Next, the second test: the same command,
plus dash dash no golden input.

965
01:03:27,893 --> 01:03:32,018
If the graders are sound, its last line
begins baseline OK.

966
01:03:34,160 --> 01:03:38,980
Adds an entry failed as expected: with
nothing done, there's no entry for it to

967
01:03:38,980 --> 01:03:39,285
find.

968
01:03:42,785 --> 01:03:47,860
No made up number is marked N/A, because
it checks that something is absent.

969
01:03:50,360 --> 01:03:53,835
Finally, the last line does begin baseline
OK.

970
01:03:57,066 --> 01:04:01,609
Here's what a useless grader looks like,
added on purpose, so we can watch the

971
01:04:01,609 --> 01:04:02,541
oracle catch it.

972
01:04:03,041 --> 01:04:07,747
This one is a file contains grader named
mentions unreleased, and it looks for the

973
01:04:07,747 --> 01:04:09,641
word Unreleased in the changelog.

974
01:04:10,141 --> 01:04:15,166
The starting changelog already contains
that word, so this grader can never fail.

975
01:04:16,366 --> 01:04:19,991
With that grader in place, we run the
second test again.

976
01:04:26,833 --> 01:04:30,665
This time there's a cross: mentions
unreleased passed without the golden

977
01:04:30,665 --> 01:04:33,433
solution, and the oracle calls it
trivially passing.

978
01:04:37,933 --> 01:04:41,058
The last line now begins baseline problem.

979
01:04:43,558 --> 01:04:47,633
Having seen it caught, we delete those
five lines from the eval file.

980
01:04:50,533 --> 01:04:54,033
The other stimuli get golden patches the
same way.

981
01:04:54,533 --> 01:04:58,660
For security, two changes, and after
release, we pick a trial, copy its patch

982
01:04:58,660 --> 01:05:01,983
into the golden folder, and add the two
lines to its stimulus.

983
01:05:02,483 --> 01:05:06,752
Each time, it's a trial whose patch we've
read and judged correct, not one that

984
01:05:06,752 --> 01:05:07,508
merely passed.

985
01:05:08,008 --> 01:05:10,408
Refactor stays without one.

986
01:05:11,633 --> 01:05:15,958
Now both tests on everything, starting
with the golden patches applied.

987
01:05:20,200 --> 01:05:23,525
Each of the four stimuli it graded ends in
passed.

988
01:05:29,025 --> 01:05:33,225
Refactor isn't among them, because it has
no golden patch to apply.

989
01:05:34,725 --> 01:05:38,700
Then the same with nothing applied, so
refactor takes part too.

990
01:05:44,866 --> 01:05:49,668
This report is longer than the screen, so
here's its end; all five of its stimuli

991
01:05:49,668 --> 01:05:51,091
finish with baseline OK.

992
01:05:56,300 --> 01:05:59,275
Here are both reports, grader by grader.

993
01:06:02,775 --> 01:06:06,875
With its golden patch applied, every
grader that ran has passed.

994
01:06:07,375 --> 01:06:12,100
Without it, four graders failed as
expected, and two are marked N/A.

995
01:06:15,300 --> 01:06:18,825
So which graders have now been tested in
both directions?

996
01:06:19,325 --> 01:06:23,225
Four have: they pass a correct answer, and
fail an empty one.

997
01:06:23,725 --> 01:06:27,000
Two haven't: no made up number, and adds
nothing.

998
01:06:27,500 --> 01:06:32,089
Both check that something is absent, and
by marking them N/A, the oracle is saying

999
01:06:32,089 --> 01:06:34,775
honestly that an untouched file can't test
them.

1000
01:06:35,275 --> 01:06:39,992
Nothing so far has shown that the grader
named no made up number would object when

1001
01:06:39,992 --> 01:06:41,200
a number is invented.

1002
01:06:42,400 --> 01:06:44,275
There's a second limit.

1003
01:06:44,775 --> 01:06:49,475
The oracle tested each grader against one
correct answer, the one we chose.

1004
01:06:49,975 --> 01:06:53,875
Another correct answer, worded
differently, hasn't been tried.

1005
01:06:55,100 --> 01:06:59,202
That test tried one correct answer for
each grader, and it couldn't test the

1006
01:06:59,202 --> 01:07:00,875
graders that check for absence.

1007
01:07:01,375 --> 01:07:06,134
But we have dozens of real answers saved,
good and bad: can the graders run on

1008
01:07:06,134 --> 01:07:06,500
those?

1009
01:07:07,700 --> 01:07:11,859
Among our graders, no made-up number is
the one we've leaned on most, and the

1010
01:07:11,859 --> 01:07:13,425
oracle couldn't vouch for it.

1011
01:07:13,925 --> 01:07:18,425
An untouched changelog has no made-up
number in it, so the grader passes it, and

1012
01:07:18,425 --> 01:07:19,550
that proves nothing.

1013
01:07:20,050 --> 01:07:24,350
What would prove something is a changelog
where a model did make up a number.

1014
01:07:24,850 --> 01:07:28,875
Trials like that are already saved, from a
run we paid for earlier.

1015
01:07:29,375 --> 01:07:33,837
Here, you'll run your graders over trials
you've already saved, and catch a grader

1016
01:07:33,837 --> 01:07:34,925
that can never fail.

1017
01:07:36,133 --> 01:07:39,358
Let's start with why saved trials are
worth keeping.

1018
01:07:39,858 --> 01:07:44,147
Every trial you've read is an example with
a known answer: you know whether it's

1019
01:07:44,147 --> 01:07:45,433
right or wrong, and why.

1020
01:07:45,933 --> 01:07:50,730
That makes saved trials the best material
we have for testing graders, in two ways

1021
01:07:50,730 --> 01:07:52,133
the oracle can't manage.

1022
01:07:52,633 --> 01:07:57,144
Any trial you know is bad tests a grader
that checks for absence, because the

1023
01:07:57,144 --> 01:07:58,433
grader has to fail it.

1024
01:07:58,933 --> 01:08:03,171
Any trial you know is good, but worded
differently from the golden patch, tests

1025
01:08:03,171 --> 01:08:04,833
whether a grader is too strict.

1026
01:08:05,333 --> 01:08:09,776
The oracle tried one correct answer for
each stimulus, while our saved runs hold

1027
01:08:09,776 --> 01:08:12,108
several, each worded a little differently.

1028
01:08:12,608 --> 01:08:16,958
When a grader fails one of them, it's
objecting to wording, not to a fault.

1029
01:08:18,167 --> 01:08:22,676
The example here is no made-up number,
which passes when its pattern is absent

1030
01:08:22,676 --> 01:08:24,642
from the patch: a must-not grader.

1031
01:08:25,142 --> 01:08:29,622
If it doesn't fail a trial where the model
wrote a number nobody gave it, the grader

1032
01:08:29,622 --> 01:08:31,542
is broken, whatever the oracle said.

1033
01:08:32,042 --> 01:08:36,522
That gives us a simple rule: every
must-not grader needs at least one saved

1034
01:08:36,522 --> 01:08:37,717
trial that it fails.

1035
01:08:38,217 --> 01:08:42,437
And when a grader and our own reading
disagree, we don't assume either is right:

1036
01:08:42,437 --> 01:08:44,917
we open the evidence and the patch, and
decide.

1037
01:08:46,133 --> 01:08:49,033
That's the idea; here's the command that
does it.

1038
01:08:49,533 --> 01:08:53,558
Vally grade runs graders over trials that
are already saved.

1039
01:08:54,058 --> 01:08:56,833
No agent starts, so it costs nothing.

1040
01:08:57,333 --> 01:08:59,808
Dash e names the eval file.

1041
01:09:00,308 --> 01:09:04,972
Its graders are applied as they are in the
file now, matched to each saved trial by

1042
01:09:04,972 --> 01:09:05,758
stimulus name.

1043
01:09:06,258 --> 01:09:10,283
So when we change a grader, the next
re-grade uses the changed one.

1044
01:09:10,783 --> 01:09:16,233
The saved trials are the run's results dot
jsonl, given to the command as its input.

1045
01:09:16,733 --> 01:09:21,275
And dash dash run dir names the run's
folder, because one more piece is kept

1046
01:09:21,275 --> 01:09:21,633
there.

1047
01:09:22,833 --> 01:09:26,308
Each trial's folder has a workspace patch
for us to read.

1048
01:09:26,808 --> 01:09:30,990
If the eval file has a diff grader,
meaning one of the three graders that work

1049
01:09:30,990 --> 01:09:35,387
from the patch, the run also keeps a copy
of every trial's diff, in a folder named

1050
01:09:35,387 --> 01:09:35,708
diffs.

1051
01:09:36,208 --> 01:09:40,983
Those copies are what a re-grade reads,
and run dir tells it which run to look in.

1052
01:09:41,483 --> 01:09:44,083
There are two limits, and here's the
first.

1053
01:09:44,583 --> 01:09:49,578
Runs made before the eval file had a diff
grader kept no copies, so they give a diff

1054
01:09:49,578 --> 01:09:51,183
grader nothing to re-grade.

1055
01:09:52,400 --> 01:09:55,225
The second limit is about script graders.

1056
01:09:55,725 --> 01:10:00,200
Diff graders work from the saved diff, so
their results in a re-grade hold.

1057
01:10:00,700 --> 01:10:05,083
Script graders need the workspace, and a
saved run keeps the diff, not the

1058
01:10:05,083 --> 01:10:05,675
workspace.

1059
01:10:06,175 --> 01:10:11,298
So in a re-grade, a script grader fails on
every trial, including trials that really

1060
01:10:11,298 --> 01:10:11,725
passed.

1061
01:10:12,225 --> 01:10:15,575
Ours even says why: there's no changelog
to check.

1062
01:10:16,075 --> 01:10:20,400
In a re-grade we ignore script graders,
and test those with the oracle.

1063
01:10:21,600 --> 01:10:26,230
The saved trials are about to catch a real
mistake, one we made while writing this

1064
01:10:26,230 --> 01:10:26,625
course.

1065
01:10:27,125 --> 01:10:30,825
When we first wrote no made-up number,
this was its pattern.

1066
01:10:31,325 --> 01:10:35,675
The eval file's pattern is the same, plus
four characters at the front.

1067
01:10:36,175 --> 01:10:40,855
And it reads correctly: a line that starts
with a plus sign, and contains a number in

1068
01:10:40,855 --> 01:10:41,350
brackets.

1069
01:10:41,850 --> 01:10:43,450
It's wrong, though.

1070
01:10:44,667 --> 01:10:47,617
Here's why, on the patch from a saved
trial.

1071
01:10:48,117 --> 01:10:52,584
Those four characters are a switch that
makes the caret mean the start of every

1072
01:10:52,584 --> 01:10:52,867
line.

1073
01:10:53,367 --> 01:10:57,417
Without the switch, the caret means only
the very start of the whole patch.

1074
01:10:57,917 --> 01:11:02,610
But a patch never starts with a plus sign;
it starts with a header line like this

1075
01:11:02,610 --> 01:11:02,842
one.

1076
01:11:03,342 --> 01:11:07,967
So the pattern can never be found, however
many made-up numbers sit further down.

1077
01:11:08,467 --> 01:11:13,192
Any grader that passes when its pattern
isn't found will then pass everything.

1078
01:11:14,400 --> 01:11:16,950
That's how it works; now let's catch it.

1079
01:11:17,450 --> 01:11:21,928
First, we'll re-grade a saved run where we
know failures happened, with the graders

1080
01:11:21,928 --> 01:11:22,575
as they are.

1081
01:11:23,075 --> 01:11:27,511
Then the flawed pattern goes in, and we
ask the oracle and the saved trials about

1082
01:11:27,511 --> 01:11:28,825
it, one after the other.

1083
01:11:29,325 --> 01:11:31,775
Last, the right pattern goes back.

1084
01:11:32,275 --> 01:11:36,600
What would you expect the oracle to say
about a grader that can never fail?

1085
01:11:39,100 --> 01:11:41,975
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

1086
01:11:43,200 --> 01:11:47,575
For this, the run we want is the first one
we graded with diff graders, made before

1087
01:11:47,575 --> 01:11:51,950
the skill had any rule about numbers, and
before the eval file had a script grader.

1088
01:11:54,160 --> 01:11:57,285
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run.

1089
01:11:57,785 --> 01:12:00,685
We change into it, so the paths stay
short.

1090
01:12:03,480 --> 01:12:08,049
Now the re-grade: the eval file is three
folders up, the run dir is this folder,

1091
01:12:08,049 --> 01:12:10,105
and the saved results are the input.

1092
01:12:10,605 --> 01:12:14,604
Vally prints one block for each saved
trial: the stimulus name, a tick or a

1093
01:12:14,604 --> 01:12:18,230
cross, and each failing grader with its
evidence, but not the model.

1094
01:12:19,333 --> 01:12:23,539
Eighteen blocks come back, for three
stimuli run three times on two models,

1095
01:12:23,539 --> 01:12:25,558
which is more than fits on a screen.

1096
01:12:29,767 --> 01:12:34,604
Here are the six blocks for the typo fix
stimulus: three trials on each of the two

1097
01:12:34,604 --> 01:12:35,017
models.

1098
01:12:35,517 --> 01:12:39,867
Three passed and three failed, the same
count as when the run was first graded.

1099
01:12:40,367 --> 01:12:44,974
Two of the failures name no made-up
number: those are the trials where a model

1100
01:12:44,974 --> 01:12:45,742
invented one.

1101
01:12:46,242 --> 01:12:50,774
The third names adds an entry: in that
trial the model asked for the number, and

1102
01:12:50,774 --> 01:12:51,567
wrote nothing.

1103
01:12:54,767 --> 01:12:57,292
Next, the flawed pattern goes in.

1104
01:12:57,792 --> 01:13:01,582
In the eval file, under the no made-up
number grader, we delete the four

1105
01:13:01,582 --> 01:13:04,267
characters from the front of the pattern,
and save.

1106
01:13:04,767 --> 01:13:07,292
Nothing else in the file changes.

1107
01:13:08,500 --> 01:13:12,725
Back in the practice folder, we ask the
oracle about the changed grader.

1108
01:13:14,087 --> 01:13:18,935
First with the golden patch applied, for
the typo fix stimulus alone, printing each

1109
01:13:18,935 --> 01:13:19,987
grader's evidence.

1110
01:13:25,733 --> 01:13:29,683
It passed: with a correct answer in place,
the pattern isn't found.

1111
01:13:33,183 --> 01:13:36,408
Then without the golden patch, on the
untouched file.

1112
01:13:43,367 --> 01:13:48,128
Adds an entry failed as expected, no
made-up number is marked not applicable,

1113
01:13:48,128 --> 01:13:50,292
and the last line says baseline OK.

1114
01:13:54,792 --> 01:13:57,167
The oracle has no complaint.

1115
01:14:00,067 --> 01:14:04,931
So let's ask the saved trials: back into
the run's folder, and the same re-grade as

1116
01:14:04,931 --> 01:14:05,342
before.

1117
01:14:08,127 --> 01:14:11,202
The only thing that has changed is the
pattern.

1118
01:14:15,007 --> 01:14:17,057
Eighteen blocks again.

1119
01:14:20,267 --> 01:14:25,046
Side by side, for the six typo fix trials:
with the right pattern, two failed no

1120
01:14:25,046 --> 01:14:25,942
made-up number.

1121
01:14:26,442 --> 01:14:30,901
With the flawed pattern, none did, not
even the trials where we know a number was

1122
01:14:30,901 --> 01:14:31,342
made up.

1123
01:14:31,842 --> 01:14:36,136
Five of the six now pass, and the sixth
still fails adds an entry, because it

1124
01:14:36,136 --> 01:14:36,917
wrote nothing.

1125
01:14:37,417 --> 01:14:42,192
So the oracle accepted this grader, and
the saved trials show that it cannot fail.

1126
01:14:45,400 --> 01:14:49,757
Finally, the four characters go back into
the eval file, and we run the re-grade

1127
01:14:49,757 --> 01:14:51,500
once more from the run's folder.

1128
01:14:58,053 --> 01:15:03,053
These blocks match the first re-grade: no
made-up number fails the trials it should.

1129
01:15:07,267 --> 01:15:09,442
So what should we make of that?

1130
01:15:09,942 --> 01:15:14,058
Four characters were the difference
between a grader that works and one that

1131
01:15:14,058 --> 01:15:15,142
approves everything.

1132
01:15:15,642 --> 01:15:18,567
Our oracle runs couldn't tell the two
apart.

1133
01:15:19,067 --> 01:15:22,667
Saved trials could, because some of them
were known to be bad.

1134
01:15:23,867 --> 01:15:28,592
The same re-grade settled our other
must-not grader, with no extra work.

1135
01:15:29,092 --> 01:15:33,830
Adds nothing failed all six refactor
trials, as it should, because both models

1136
01:15:33,830 --> 01:15:35,167
recorded the refactor.

1137
01:15:35,667 --> 01:15:39,642
So both must-not graders now have saved
trials that they fail.

1138
01:15:40,867 --> 01:15:44,967
This is worth making a habit, whenever we
write or change a grader.

1139
01:15:45,467 --> 01:15:47,842
First the oracle, which is free.

1140
01:15:48,342 --> 01:15:53,145
Then a re-grade of a saved run where the
failure is known to occur, which is also

1141
01:15:53,145 --> 01:15:53,442
free.

1142
01:15:53,942 --> 01:15:56,667
Only then do we spend agent runs.

1143
01:15:57,866 --> 01:16:02,758
Our graders are now tested against correct
answers, untouched files, and real bad

1144
01:16:02,758 --> 01:16:03,241
answers.

1145
01:16:03,741 --> 01:16:08,196
In every trial so far the skill was there
to use: will it load for other wordings,

1146
01:16:08,196 --> 01:16:10,641
and stay out of requests that aren't its
job?

1147
01:16:11,867 --> 01:16:16,392
Every rule we've added to the skill does
nothing unless the agent reads it.

1148
01:16:16,892 --> 01:16:19,817
Agents don't read every skill they have.

1149
01:16:20,317 --> 01:16:24,431
Instead, an agent reads a one-line
description of each, and opens a skill

1150
01:16:24,431 --> 01:16:26,967
only when that line seems to fit the
request.

1151
01:16:27,467 --> 01:16:31,592
Here, you'll test whether an agent opens
the skill for the requests it's meant for

1152
01:16:31,592 --> 01:16:35,315
and leaves it alone for the others, and
see what rewriting its description

1153
01:16:35,315 --> 01:16:35,717
changes.

1154
01:16:36,934 --> 01:16:39,959
Let's start with how an agent chooses a
skill.

1155
01:16:40,459 --> 01:16:44,663
When it starts, it's given the name and
description of every installed skill, and

1156
01:16:44,663 --> 01:16:45,909
nothing else about them.

1157
01:16:46,409 --> 01:16:50,759
When a request arrives, the model decides
whether any description fits.

1158
01:16:51,259 --> 01:16:56,059
If one does, the agent loads that skill,
which means it reads the rest of the file.

1159
01:16:56,559 --> 01:16:59,859
If none does, those instructions are never
seen.

1160
01:17:00,359 --> 01:17:04,159
Our skill's description is still the one
line from the first draft.

1161
01:17:05,367 --> 01:17:09,851
That gives a skill two new ways to fail,
and the graders we've written can't see

1162
01:17:09,851 --> 01:17:10,467
either one.

1163
01:17:10,967 --> 01:17:13,442
First, it doesn't load when it should.

1164
01:17:13,942 --> 01:17:18,581
Then the agent does the task anyway, from
its own habits, without our rules, and the

1165
01:17:18,581 --> 01:17:20,017
result may even look fine.

1166
01:17:20,517 --> 01:17:24,783
Second, it loads when it shouldn't, and
the agent applies changelog rules to a

1167
01:17:24,783 --> 01:17:26,642
task that isn't a changelog entry.

1168
01:17:27,867 --> 01:17:30,242
Not loading is the common one.

1169
01:17:30,742 --> 01:17:34,738
In one vendor's report from twenty
twenty-six, a skill went unused in

1170
01:17:34,738 --> 01:17:37,517
fifty-six percent of the cases it was
meant for.

1171
01:17:38,017 --> 01:17:42,624
That figure is the vendor's own, and the
number of runs behind it wasn't published,

1172
01:17:42,624 --> 01:17:44,567
but the pattern is widely reported.

1173
01:17:45,767 --> 01:17:49,267
Obvious requests tell us little about
either failure.

1174
01:17:49,767 --> 01:17:54,325
Any request to update the changelog will
load a changelog skill with almost any

1175
01:17:54,325 --> 01:17:55,017
description.

1176
01:17:55,517 --> 01:17:59,667
What tells us something is a request near
the edge of the skill's job.

1177
01:18:00,167 --> 01:18:04,866
So here's a term: a near-miss request
shares words or a subject with the skill's

1178
01:18:04,866 --> 01:18:06,217
job, but isn't its job.

1179
01:18:06,717 --> 01:18:10,996
Draft the release notes from the changelog
mentions the changelog, and it isn't

1180
01:18:10,996 --> 01:18:12,567
asking for a changelog entry.

1181
01:18:13,767 --> 01:18:16,842
So a loading test needs two groups of
requests.

1182
01:18:17,342 --> 01:18:22,128
Some should load the skill, including ones
that never use its obvious word, which for

1183
01:18:22,128 --> 01:18:23,142
ours is changelog.

1184
01:18:23,642 --> 01:18:27,142
The rest are near-miss requests, which
should not load it.

1185
01:18:27,642 --> 01:18:31,841
And the test has to observe what the agent
did, because asking a model whether it

1186
01:18:31,841 --> 01:18:34,692
would use a skill tests what it says, not
what it does.

1187
01:18:35,900 --> 01:18:40,350
When a test shows the description is at
fault, a better one says three things.

1188
01:18:40,850 --> 01:18:43,050
It says what the skill does.

1189
01:18:43,550 --> 01:18:48,550
It says when to use it, described as a
situation, and not as a word to look for.

1190
01:18:49,050 --> 01:18:51,650
And it says what the skill is not for.

1191
01:18:52,867 --> 01:18:55,217
How big should such a test be?

1192
01:18:55,717 --> 01:19:00,063
For a skill you depend on, the usual
guidance is about twenty requests, with

1193
01:19:00,063 --> 01:19:01,092
three trials each.

1194
01:19:01,592 --> 01:19:05,473
Some of those requests are kept aside and
never looked at while rewording the

1195
01:19:05,473 --> 01:19:07,942
description, so it isn't just fitted to
the test.

1196
01:19:08,442 --> 01:19:13,242
Those numbers are conventions; nobody has
published a calculation behind them.

1197
01:19:13,742 --> 01:19:18,492
Our test has six requests and two trials
each, which is enough to show the method.

1198
01:19:19,700 --> 01:19:22,950
That's the idea; here's where a trial
records it.

1199
01:19:23,450 --> 01:19:28,253
Each trial's record in results dot jsonl
holds its trajectory, the full record of

1200
01:19:28,253 --> 01:19:30,625
what the agent did, as a list of events.

1201
01:19:31,125 --> 01:19:35,775
When the agent loads a skill, Vally adds
an event of the type skill activation.

1202
01:19:36,275 --> 01:19:39,000
Its data names the skill that loaded.

1203
01:19:39,500 --> 01:19:44,295
Its turn field says which request of a
conversation that was, counting from zero;

1204
01:19:44,295 --> 01:19:46,425
every trial so far has had just one.

1205
01:19:46,925 --> 01:19:51,150
Vally calls this activation, and it means
what we've been calling loading.

1206
01:19:52,367 --> 01:19:55,592
You rarely need to open a record to find
that out.

1207
01:19:56,092 --> 01:19:59,017
Every report has a column named Skills.

1208
01:19:59,517 --> 01:20:04,017
For each stimulus, it lists the skills
that loaded in its trials, with a count.

1209
01:20:04,517 --> 01:20:08,828
Here it is in the report from our
five-trial run: the changelog entry skill

1210
01:20:08,828 --> 01:20:10,667
loaded five times on each model.

1211
01:20:11,167 --> 01:20:14,217
When no skill loaded, the column shows a
dash.

1212
01:20:15,434 --> 01:20:19,980
Events like that are read by a grader
named skill invocation, which is one more

1213
01:20:19,980 --> 01:20:20,959
word for loading.

1214
01:20:21,459 --> 01:20:25,659
The starter folder has an eval file that
uses it, named loading.

1215
01:20:26,159 --> 01:20:30,909
Its agent environment installs three
skills, so the agent has a real choice.

1216
01:20:31,409 --> 01:20:35,803
Beside ours, one writes commit messages,
and one drafts release notes; we'll call

1217
01:20:35,803 --> 01:20:37,159
those two the neighbours.

1218
01:20:37,659 --> 01:20:42,198
This stimulus, load no keyword, should
load the skill, and its request never says

1219
01:20:42,198 --> 01:20:42,759
changelog.

1220
01:20:43,259 --> 01:20:48,209
Its grader lists our skill under required,
so it passes only if that skill loaded.

1221
01:20:49,434 --> 01:20:53,520
Here's the other kind: a near-miss
request, which mentions the changelog, and

1222
01:20:53,520 --> 01:20:55,484
is a job for the release notes skill.

1223
01:20:55,984 --> 01:21:00,172
Its grader lists our skill under
disallowed, so it passes only if that

1224
01:21:00,172 --> 01:21:01,309
skill did not load.

1225
01:21:01,809 --> 01:21:05,684
Which other skill loaded, if any, doesn't
matter to this grader.

1226
01:21:06,900 --> 01:21:10,125
That file has six stimuli, in those two
shapes.

1227
01:21:10,625 --> 01:21:14,175
Three should load the skill, and their
names begin with load.

1228
01:21:14,675 --> 01:21:19,473
Only load plain says changelog; the other
two ask to note a change down, and to make

1229
01:21:19,473 --> 01:21:20,900
sure users can see a fix.

1230
01:21:21,400 --> 01:21:25,591
The other three are near-miss requests,
named skip: one wants a commit message,

1231
01:21:25,591 --> 01:21:29,250
one wants release notes, and one asks what
changed in a past version.

1232
01:21:29,750 --> 01:21:34,187
These graders have no name of their own,
so in its output Vally labels each with

1233
01:21:34,187 --> 01:21:35,850
its type and what it requires.

1234
01:21:36,350 --> 01:21:40,612
Loading varies from trial to trial like
everything else, so we'll run each

1235
01:21:40,612 --> 01:21:41,475
stimulus twice.

1236
01:21:42,700 --> 01:21:45,975
That's how loading is recorded; now let's
test it.

1237
01:21:46,475 --> 01:21:50,325
First, we'll read the two neighbouring
skills and check the eval file.

1238
01:21:50,825 --> 01:21:55,600
Then we'll run the six requests on both
models, with the description as it stands.

1239
01:21:56,100 --> 01:21:59,500
After that, we'll rewrite the description
and run them again.

1240
01:22:00,000 --> 01:22:03,525
What would you expect a one-line
description to get wrong?

1241
01:22:06,025 --> 01:22:08,900
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

1242
01:22:10,100 --> 01:22:13,775
Here are the two neighbours, starting with
the commit message skill.

1243
01:22:16,440 --> 01:22:21,152
It has a one-line description of its own,
and it tells the agent to reply without

1244
01:22:21,152 --> 01:22:22,140
editing any file.

1245
01:22:26,640 --> 01:22:28,890
Then the release notes skill.

1246
01:22:31,434 --> 01:22:35,459
It drafts an announcement from the
changelog, and doesn't edit it either.

1247
01:22:39,959 --> 01:22:44,009
Lint checks the eval file for mistakes
without starting an agent.

1248
01:22:47,280 --> 01:22:48,580
It's valid.

1249
01:22:52,100 --> 01:22:55,600
Now the first measurement, with the
description as it stands.

1250
01:22:56,100 --> 01:23:01,011
Six stimuli, two models and two trials
each: it spends twenty-four short agent

1251
01:23:01,011 --> 01:23:01,325
runs.

1252
01:23:01,825 --> 01:23:05,850
We're watching the list of failures at the
end, and the summary under it.

1253
01:23:08,167 --> 01:23:12,646
About two minutes later, that list has a
single line: an error on terra, where a

1254
01:23:12,646 --> 01:23:13,542
session dropped.

1255
01:23:14,042 --> 01:23:17,867
That trial has no verdict, and it says
nothing about loading.

1256
01:23:18,367 --> 01:23:22,576
A loading failure would appear here with
evidence that a required skill was not

1257
01:23:22,576 --> 01:23:25,667
called, Vally's third word for loading;
this run has none.

1258
01:23:26,167 --> 01:23:30,977
So the table shows six of six stimuli
passing on haiku, and five of six on

1259
01:23:30,977 --> 01:23:31,367
terra.

1260
01:23:35,867 --> 01:23:38,492
Vally saved the run under results.

1261
01:23:40,647 --> 01:23:42,897
That folder holds the report.

1262
01:23:45,800 --> 01:23:50,300
Here's the Skills column from that report,
beside the pass rate, for each model.

1263
01:23:50,800 --> 01:23:55,575
For the three skip stimuli, it names a
neighbour or shows a dash, on both models.

1264
01:23:59,075 --> 01:24:02,350
So our skill stayed out of every near-miss
request.

1265
01:24:02,850 --> 01:24:07,641
And for the three load stimuli, it shows
our skill loading in both trials, on both

1266
01:24:07,641 --> 01:24:08,050
models.

1267
01:24:12,267 --> 01:24:17,043
An earlier run of this same test, with the
same one-line description, did find a

1268
01:24:17,043 --> 01:24:17,342
miss.

1269
01:24:17,842 --> 01:24:22,317
On haiku, load no keyword loaded the skill
in only one of its two trials.

1270
01:24:22,817 --> 01:24:26,692
In the other, the evidence says the
required skill was not called.

1271
01:24:27,192 --> 01:24:31,274
So the one-line description can miss a
request that never says changelog, even

1272
01:24:31,274 --> 01:24:33,367
though our run didn't catch it doing so.

1273
01:24:36,567 --> 01:24:40,317
That one line says what the skill does,
and nothing about when.

1274
01:24:40,817 --> 01:24:44,017
Here's the rewrite, which replaces that
one line.

1275
01:24:44,517 --> 01:24:47,642
Its first sentence still says what the
skill does.

1276
01:24:48,142 --> 01:24:52,726
Then comes the situation: a change, fix or
merged pull request to note for the next

1277
01:24:52,726 --> 01:24:55,542
release, even if the request doesn't say
changelog.

1278
01:24:56,042 --> 01:25:00,281
And last, what it's not for: commit
messages, release notes, or questions

1279
01:25:00,281 --> 01:25:01,442
about past versions.

1280
01:25:01,942 --> 01:25:04,442
Nothing else in the skill changes.

1281
01:25:05,667 --> 01:25:10,003
With the new description in place, we run
the same command, saving to a second

1282
01:25:10,003 --> 01:25:10,392
folder.

1283
01:25:10,892 --> 01:25:13,717
That's another twenty-four agent runs.

1284
01:25:22,354 --> 01:25:26,954
This time every trial finished, and the
list has one line of a different kind.

1285
01:25:27,454 --> 01:25:32,203
On terra, skip question failed the grader
that forbids our skill, in one of its two

1286
01:25:32,203 --> 01:25:32,604
trials.

1287
01:25:33,104 --> 01:25:37,679
So the skill loaded for a near-miss
request, which is the second way to fail.

1288
01:25:42,900 --> 01:25:47,663
Now the comparison, stimulus by stimulus:
the trials that passed before the rewrite,

1289
01:25:47,663 --> 01:25:48,400
and after it.

1290
01:25:48,900 --> 01:25:52,600
On terra, skip question went from two of
two to one of two.

1291
01:25:53,100 --> 01:25:57,350
In every other cell, each trial that
finished passed, before and after.

1292
01:26:02,567 --> 01:26:04,967
So what did the loading test show?

1293
01:26:05,467 --> 01:26:09,680
In our run, the one-line description did
its job in every trial that finished: the

1294
01:26:09,680 --> 01:26:12,917
skill loaded when it should, and stayed
out of every near-miss.

1295
01:26:13,417 --> 01:26:18,167
An earlier run caught it missing once, on
a request that never says changelog.

1296
01:26:18,667 --> 01:26:22,942
After the rewrite, the skill still loaded
in every trial that should load it.

1297
01:26:23,442 --> 01:26:27,898
But on terra, it also loaded once for a
question about a past version, which the

1298
01:26:27,898 --> 01:26:29,792
new description says it's not for.

1299
01:26:31,000 --> 01:26:35,133
One failure in two trials is a small
sample, too small to blame the rewrite,

1300
01:26:35,133 --> 01:26:38,450
and we'll get more careful about samples
that small later on.

1301
01:26:38,950 --> 01:26:41,975
What we have for certain is the test
itself.

1302
01:26:42,475 --> 01:26:47,224
Real projects install dozens of skills,
descriptions get edited, and neighbours

1303
01:26:47,224 --> 01:26:47,825
get added.

1304
01:26:48,325 --> 01:26:52,882
Each of those can change which requests
load the skill, and now one command shows

1305
01:26:52,882 --> 01:26:53,725
whether it did.

1306
01:26:54,933 --> 01:26:57,033
We know when the skill loads.

1307
01:26:57,533 --> 01:27:01,583
But is the result any better with the
skill than with no skill at all?

1308
01:27:02,800 --> 01:27:05,600
Models already know what a changelog is.

1309
01:27:06,100 --> 01:27:11,050
So perhaps they'd pass our graders with no
skill at all, and the skill is decoration.

1310
01:27:11,550 --> 01:27:14,475
Or perhaps the skill matters a great deal.

1311
01:27:14,975 --> 01:27:19,327
So far, we've measured the skill against
earlier versions of itself, but not

1312
01:27:19,327 --> 01:27:20,300
against no skill.

1313
01:27:20,800 --> 01:27:24,920
Here, you'll run the same stimuli with the
skill and without it, and say where the

1314
01:27:24,920 --> 01:27:26,025
skill earns its place.

1315
01:27:27,233 --> 01:27:29,733
Let's start with what a skill costs.

1316
01:27:30,233 --> 01:27:33,858
It adds to the text the model reads on
every request.

1317
01:27:34,358 --> 01:27:37,333
It has to be maintained, and it can be
wrong.

1318
01:27:37,833 --> 01:27:42,101
So it earns its place only on requests
where the agent does better with it than

1319
01:27:42,101 --> 01:27:42,533
without.

1320
01:27:43,733 --> 01:27:45,858
We can't assume that it does.

1321
01:27:46,358 --> 01:27:51,290
One study from twenty twenty-six ran the
same eighty-seven tasks with skills and

1322
01:27:51,290 --> 01:27:51,783
without.

1323
01:27:52,283 --> 01:27:56,813
Skills written with care raised the share
of tasks completed by about seventeen

1324
01:27:56,813 --> 01:27:58,533
percentage points, on average.

1325
01:27:59,033 --> 01:28:02,408
Yet they made thirteen of the eighty-seven
tasks worse.

1326
01:28:02,908 --> 01:28:07,266
And skills that the models wrote for
themselves completed fewer tasks than no

1327
01:28:07,266 --> 01:28:08,058
skills at all.

1328
01:28:09,267 --> 01:28:12,217
The way to find out is a fair comparison.

1329
01:28:12,717 --> 01:28:16,787
It keeps the same stimuli, the same
graders and the same model, and changes

1330
01:28:16,787 --> 01:28:19,067
one thing: whether the skill is installed.

1331
01:28:19,567 --> 01:28:24,066
Here the baseline, the thing a result is
compared against, is the run with no

1332
01:28:24,066 --> 01:28:24,417
skill.

1333
01:28:25,633 --> 01:28:29,383
For each stimulus, the comparison gives
one of three answers.

1334
01:28:29,883 --> 01:28:33,758
The skill helps: the stimulus fails
without it, and passes with it.

1335
01:28:34,258 --> 01:28:38,712
The skill makes no difference: the model
manages either way, so the skill isn't

1336
01:28:38,712 --> 01:28:40,008
earning anything there.

1337
01:28:40,508 --> 01:28:44,508
Or the skill hurts: the stimulus passes
without it, and fails with it.

1338
01:28:45,008 --> 01:28:47,133
All three are worth knowing.

1339
01:28:48,333 --> 01:28:51,558
That's the idea; here's the file that
expresses it.

1340
01:28:52,058 --> 01:28:56,733
An experiment file lists variants of an
eval, and one command runs them all.

1341
01:28:57,233 --> 01:29:02,025
Each variant is one version of the set-up
being compared: the same eval, run with

1342
01:29:02,025 --> 01:29:03,208
one setting changed.

1343
01:29:03,708 --> 01:29:07,833
Our practice folder came with one, for
comparing with and without the skill.

1344
01:29:08,333 --> 01:29:13,156
Its evals line names the eval file to run:
the changelog eval, whose stimuli and

1345
01:29:13,156 --> 01:29:15,808
graders are used unchanged in every
variant.

1346
01:29:17,033 --> 01:29:21,408
Next, the vary line lists the settings
that variants are allowed to differ in.

1347
01:29:21,908 --> 01:29:26,103
Each is written as a path into the eval's
settings: one for the list of skills in

1348
01:29:26,103 --> 01:29:28,433
the agent environment, and one for the
model.

1349
01:29:28,933 --> 01:29:33,377
If a variant differed in anything else,
Vally would refuse to run, and report

1350
01:29:33,377 --> 01:29:34,358
experiment drift.

1351
01:29:34,858 --> 01:29:37,383
That's what keeps the comparison fair.

1352
01:29:37,883 --> 01:29:42,108
Then the baseline line names the variant
that the others are compared with.

1353
01:29:43,333 --> 01:29:46,933
After that come the variants, each under a
name we choose.

1354
01:29:47,433 --> 01:29:52,393
The first, haiku no skill, sets its model
under overrides, and overrides replace the

1355
01:29:52,393 --> 01:29:53,633
eval file's defaults.

1356
01:29:54,133 --> 01:29:57,933
Its list of skills is empty, so no skill
is installed.

1357
01:29:58,433 --> 01:30:03,512
Haiku with skill keeps the same model, and
installs the changelog entry skill, as the

1358
01:30:03,512 --> 01:30:04,408
eval file does.

1359
01:30:04,908 --> 01:30:07,708
Two more variants do the same for terra.

1360
01:30:08,208 --> 01:30:12,927
So an experiment sets the model in the
file, and not with the dash dash model

1361
01:30:12,927 --> 01:30:13,233
flag.

1362
01:30:14,433 --> 01:30:18,783
The command is vally experiment run,
followed by the experiment file.

1363
01:30:19,283 --> 01:30:23,936
Adding dash dash dry run makes it print
the plan, one block per variant, without

1364
01:30:23,936 --> 01:30:24,983
starting an agent.

1365
01:30:25,483 --> 01:30:28,158
That's the experiment's version of lint.

1366
01:30:28,658 --> 01:30:33,629
For the real run, it takes dash dash
workers and dash dash output dir, as vally

1367
01:30:33,629 --> 01:30:34,258
eval does.

1368
01:30:35,467 --> 01:30:39,658
When the run finishes, it prints a table
with one column per variant, and the

1369
01:30:39,658 --> 01:30:41,292
baseline's column is labelled.

1370
01:30:41,792 --> 01:30:44,842
Its first row counts the stimuli that
passed.

1371
01:30:45,342 --> 01:30:49,879
Beneath that are each variant's total
tokens over all its trials, and its trial

1372
01:30:49,879 --> 01:30:50,167
time.

1373
01:30:50,667 --> 01:30:54,767
Above the table, it prints a block for
each stimulus that failed, naming its

1374
01:30:54,767 --> 01:30:56,817
variant, the stimulus, and the grader.

1375
01:30:58,033 --> 01:31:02,433
The run's folder gets one subfolder per
variant, named after it.

1376
01:31:02,933 --> 01:31:07,844
Each has its own results dot jsonl, and a
folder for the eval, laid out like the

1377
01:31:07,844 --> 01:31:09,133
ones we already know.

1378
01:31:09,633 --> 01:31:14,150
There's also one report for the whole
experiment, report dot md, with a heading

1379
01:31:14,150 --> 01:31:15,808
and a table for each variant.

1380
01:31:16,308 --> 01:31:20,283
An experiment writes it in place of a
single eval's report.

1381
01:31:21,500 --> 01:31:24,100
That's how it works; now let's run it.

1382
01:31:24,600 --> 01:31:29,198
The changelog eval's stimuli are the typo
fix with no pull request number, the

1383
01:31:29,198 --> 01:31:31,025
refactor, and the security fix.

1384
01:31:31,525 --> 01:31:35,778
Then come the two changes in one pull
request, and the changelog with no

1385
01:31:35,778 --> 01:31:36,900
Unreleased section.

1386
01:31:37,400 --> 01:31:41,625
Which of them would you expect a model to
get right with no skill at all?

1387
01:31:44,125 --> 01:31:47,000
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

1388
01:31:48,200 --> 01:31:53,350
Our experiment file already names the two
models we use, so nothing needs editing.

1389
01:31:53,850 --> 01:31:58,025
First comes the dry run, which checks the
plan and starts no agent.

1390
01:31:58,760 --> 01:32:02,899
It prints a block for each of the four
variants, naming its model; the screen

1391
01:32:02,899 --> 01:32:04,135
holds the last of them.

1392
01:32:09,367 --> 01:32:13,217
Now the real run, with two workers and a
folder for the results.

1393
01:32:13,717 --> 01:32:18,689
It spends twenty short agent runs: each of
the four variants runs the five stimuli

1394
01:32:18,689 --> 01:32:18,992
once.

1395
01:32:19,492 --> 01:32:22,217
What we're after is the table at the end.

1396
01:32:24,480 --> 01:32:28,789
About a minute and a half later, the table
is there, with the last of the failed

1397
01:32:28,789 --> 01:32:29,705
stimuli above it.

1398
01:32:34,933 --> 01:32:37,533
Here's that table again, as a slide.

1399
01:32:40,033 --> 01:32:44,708
With no skill, haiku passes two of the
five stimuli, and terra passes three.

1400
01:32:45,208 --> 01:32:47,908
With the skill, each passes all five.

1401
01:32:51,133 --> 01:32:53,608
Now for the blocks above the table.

1402
01:32:54,108 --> 01:32:58,133
Our run printed five, and every one is in
a no-skill variant.

1403
01:33:01,633 --> 01:33:05,711
Without the skill, both models wrote an
entry for the refactor, where nothing

1404
01:33:05,711 --> 01:33:06,558
should be added.

1405
01:33:07,058 --> 01:33:11,958
Both also filed the security fix under
Fixed, when it belongs under Security.

1406
01:33:12,458 --> 01:33:16,933
Haiku, with no skill, also failed the
changelog with no Unreleased section.

1407
01:33:20,133 --> 01:33:24,735
The typo fix is where a model once made up
a pull request number, so let's read what

1408
01:33:24,735 --> 01:33:26,433
one wrote for it with no skill.

1409
01:33:28,267 --> 01:33:32,942
As before, the run's folder is named with
its time, and we change into it.

1410
01:33:35,340 --> 01:33:39,861
Each variant's folder is named after the
variant, so we go into terra no skill,

1411
01:33:39,861 --> 01:33:42,265
then the eval, then the typo fix stimulus.

1412
01:33:45,973 --> 01:33:48,923
Here's the patch from that model's one
trial.

1413
01:33:54,240 --> 01:33:59,040
It adds an entry for the typo, and the
entry ends with no pull request number.

1414
01:34:06,267 --> 01:34:08,892
So, are the results better with the skill?

1415
01:34:09,392 --> 01:34:13,788
In our run, yes: five of five with the
skill on both models, against two of five

1416
01:34:13,788 --> 01:34:16,317
for haiku and three of five for terra
without.

1417
01:34:16,817 --> 01:34:20,642
The skill earns its place on the refactor
and the security fix.

1418
01:34:21,142 --> 01:34:25,392
On the typo fix and the two changes, both
models managed without it.

1419
01:34:25,892 --> 01:34:30,623
The changelog with no Unreleased section
split them: terra handled it alone, and

1420
01:34:30,623 --> 01:34:31,392
haiku didn't.

1421
01:34:34,600 --> 01:34:37,500
Now, back to that patch of the typo fix.

1422
01:34:38,000 --> 01:34:42,486
With the first-draft skill, this same
model ended its entry with a made-up pull

1423
01:34:42,486 --> 01:34:44,700
request number, in five trials of five.

1424
01:34:45,200 --> 01:34:49,685
With no skill installed, it made nothing
up in its one trial, and neither did the

1425
01:34:49,685 --> 01:34:50,350
other model.

1426
01:34:50,850 --> 01:34:54,500
So the made-up number wasn't that model's
own weakness.

1427
01:34:55,000 --> 01:34:57,175
Our first draft caused it.

1428
01:34:57,675 --> 01:35:01,826
It said to reference the pull request,
with no word on what to do when there is

1429
01:35:01,826 --> 01:35:04,400
none, and that pushed a model into making
one up.

1430
01:35:05,600 --> 01:35:10,381
That's the third kind of answer: the first
draft made this result worse than no skill

1431
01:35:10,381 --> 01:35:10,775
at all.

1432
01:35:11,275 --> 01:35:14,325
We fixed it earlier, without knowing its
cause.

1433
01:35:14,825 --> 01:35:18,400
It took a comparison with no skill to show
that cause.

1434
01:35:18,900 --> 01:35:23,633
That's a good reason to run one before
trusting any skill, and again after any

1435
01:35:23,633 --> 01:35:24,300
large edit.

1436
01:35:25,500 --> 01:35:27,575
Then there are the tokens.

1437
01:35:28,075 --> 01:35:31,000
A skill adds text for the model to read.

1438
01:35:31,500 --> 01:35:35,625
Yet in our run, both models used fewer
tokens with it than without.

1439
01:35:36,125 --> 01:35:40,166
On haiku, that's about three hundred
thirty thousand with the skill, against

1440
01:35:40,166 --> 01:35:42,825
about three hundred seventy-five thousand
without.

1441
01:35:43,325 --> 01:35:47,417
On terra, it's about two hundred
eighty-two thousand, against about two

1442
01:35:47,417 --> 01:35:48,800
hundred ninety thousand.

1443
01:35:49,300 --> 01:35:54,126
Each figure covers five trials: too few to
say the skill saves tokens, but enough to

1444
01:35:54,126 --> 01:35:55,850
see that it doesn't cost many.

1445
01:35:57,067 --> 01:36:01,841
One caution about those ticks and crosses:
each is one trial of one stimulus, so

1446
01:36:01,841 --> 01:36:03,692
small differences deserve care.

1447
01:36:04,192 --> 01:36:08,917
When a stimulus fails without the skill on
every model, that's solid evidence.

1448
01:36:09,417 --> 01:36:13,764
When a single cell differs, as haiku's did
on the changelog with no Unreleased

1449
01:36:13,764 --> 01:36:16,217
section, that's a reason to run more
trials.

1450
01:36:17,433 --> 01:36:21,033
Every stimulus so far has been a single
request.

1451
01:36:21,533 --> 01:36:24,858
Real use goes on: someone asks for a
follow-up.

1452
01:36:26,067 --> 01:36:29,467
Nobody uses an agent one request at a
time.

1453
01:36:29,967 --> 01:36:34,367
You ask for a changelog entry, then
remember a second change, then a third.

1454
01:36:34,867 --> 01:36:39,792
Yet every stimulus we've written starts a
fresh conversation, with a single request.

1455
01:36:40,292 --> 01:36:44,655
Here, you'll test a conversation of three
requests, and find a mistake that comes

1456
01:36:44,655 --> 01:36:46,217
from the conversation itself.

1457
01:36:47,434 --> 01:36:51,084
Let's start with what's different when a
conversation goes on.

1458
01:36:51,584 --> 01:36:54,859
Each request in a conversation is called a
turn.

1459
01:36:55,359 --> 01:36:58,184
Between turns, the agent keeps two things.

1460
01:36:58,684 --> 01:37:02,459
One is the workspace, exactly as the last
turn left it.

1461
01:37:02,959 --> 01:37:05,759
Its memory of what was said is the other.

1462
01:37:06,259 --> 01:37:09,034
That memory is a new source of mistakes.

1463
01:37:10,234 --> 01:37:14,234
Three things can now go wrong that can't
go wrong in a single request.

1464
01:37:14,734 --> 01:37:16,759
First, a rule fades.

1465
01:37:17,259 --> 01:37:21,472
The skill was loaded at the start, and
several turns later the model follows the

1466
01:37:21,472 --> 01:37:24,159
pattern of its own recent work more than
the rules.

1467
01:37:24,659 --> 01:37:29,420
Second, an earlier turn leaks into a later
one: a detail from the previous request is

1468
01:37:29,420 --> 01:37:32,109
carried into a change it has nothing to do
with.

1469
01:37:32,609 --> 01:37:37,373
Third, a later turn disturbs earlier work:
while adding the third entry, the agent

1470
01:37:37,373 --> 01:37:38,884
alters or drops the first.

1471
01:37:40,100 --> 01:37:42,200
So where should a grader look?

1472
01:37:42,700 --> 01:37:47,349
One that reads only the final file can't
tell you which turn went wrong, and it can

1473
01:37:47,349 --> 01:37:48,525
miss a leak entirely.

1474
01:37:49,025 --> 01:37:51,950
So we grade a conversation turn by turn.

1475
01:37:52,450 --> 01:37:56,918
For each request, the question is: what
did the agent change in response to that

1476
01:37:56,918 --> 01:37:57,700
request alone?

1477
01:37:58,900 --> 01:38:02,575
There's one limit to know before designing
a conversation.

1478
01:38:03,075 --> 01:38:07,407
Vally sends the follow-up requests you
wrote, in order, whatever the agent

1479
01:38:07,407 --> 01:38:07,875
replied.

1480
01:38:08,375 --> 01:38:13,182
It doesn't play a user who reacts, and its
documentation describes no way to make it

1481
01:38:13,182 --> 01:38:13,525
do so.

1482
01:38:14,025 --> 01:38:18,427
So we can test how the skill copes with a
script of requests, but not with a user

1483
01:38:18,427 --> 01:38:19,350
who answers back.

1484
01:38:20,567 --> 01:38:24,117
That's the idea; here's how an eval file
expresses it.

1485
01:38:24,617 --> 01:38:29,292
A stimulus can have turns in place of
prompt, and turns is a list of requests.

1486
01:38:29,792 --> 01:38:34,375
Vally sends the first, waits for the agent
to finish, then sends the second to the

1487
01:38:34,375 --> 01:38:35,717
same session, and so on.

1488
01:38:36,217 --> 01:38:40,242
However many turns it has, one
conversation is one trial.

1489
01:38:41,467 --> 01:38:45,319
This conversation is already in our
practice folder, and each of its three

1490
01:38:45,319 --> 01:38:47,817
requests leans on a different rule of the
skill.

1491
01:38:48,317 --> 01:38:52,920
Its first request names a merged pull
request, sixty-four, so the entry should

1492
01:38:52,920 --> 01:38:53,392
cite it.

1493
01:38:53,892 --> 01:38:58,566
Next comes an internal change, and by the
skill's rule the right response is to add

1494
01:38:58,566 --> 01:38:59,017
nothing.

1495
01:38:59,517 --> 01:39:04,815
Last is a fix with no pull request number,
arriving just after two requests that had

1496
01:39:04,815 --> 01:39:05,067
one.

1497
01:39:06,267 --> 01:39:07,917
Next, the graders.

1498
01:39:08,417 --> 01:39:12,367
You can limit a grader to one turn by
adding turn and a number.

1499
01:39:12,867 --> 01:39:16,717
Vally's documentation calls such a grader
scoped to a turn.

1500
01:39:17,217 --> 01:39:19,567
Turns are numbered from zero.

1501
01:39:20,067 --> 01:39:24,681
So a diff grader with turn two sees only
what the agent changed during the third

1502
01:39:24,681 --> 01:39:25,142
request.

1503
01:39:25,642 --> 01:39:29,692
With no turn, a grader sees the whole
conversation, as before.

1504
01:39:30,900 --> 01:39:33,900
Not every grader can be limited this way.

1505
01:39:34,400 --> 01:39:36,750
All three diff graders can.

1506
01:39:37,250 --> 01:39:41,450
So can graders that look at the agent's
events, such as skill invocation.

1507
01:39:41,950 --> 01:39:46,742
But a script grader can't, because it sees
only the workspace as it is at the very

1508
01:39:46,742 --> 01:39:46,975
end.

1509
01:39:48,200 --> 01:39:51,075
Here are the graders we'll add under the
turns.

1510
01:39:51,575 --> 01:39:55,925
Each turn key sits beside type and name,
not nested with the pattern.

1511
01:39:56,425 --> 01:40:01,125
In turn zero, the agent must add a line
that cites pull request sixty-four.

1512
01:40:01,625 --> 01:40:05,750
In turn one, it must change nothing, so
that grader is diff empty.

1513
01:40:06,967 --> 01:40:09,542
There are two graders on turn two.

1514
01:40:10,042 --> 01:40:14,442
One says the agent must add an entry that
mentions notes export.

1515
01:40:14,942 --> 01:40:19,407
Its partner says the agent must add no
line that cites any pull request, with the

1516
01:40:19,407 --> 01:40:21,942
pattern that caught the made-up number
before.

1517
01:40:22,442 --> 01:40:26,960
Our last grader has no turn, so it reads
the whole conversation: by the end, the

1518
01:40:26,960 --> 01:40:28,767
first entry must still be there.

1519
01:40:29,967 --> 01:40:32,442
One more thing before we run it.

1520
01:40:32,942 --> 01:40:38,070
Vally saves a diff for every turn of every
trial, in a folder named diffs inside the

1521
01:40:38,070 --> 01:40:38,742
run folder.

1522
01:40:39,242 --> 01:40:44,092
Each file's name ends with its trial and
turn numbers, both counted from zero.

1523
01:40:45,300 --> 01:40:47,900
That's how it works; now let's run it.

1524
01:40:48,400 --> 01:40:52,756
First we add the graders, then run the
conversation three times on each model,

1525
01:40:52,756 --> 01:40:55,325
and then open the diff of any turn that
fails.

1526
01:40:55,825 --> 01:40:59,025
Which of the three turns would you expect
to go wrong?

1527
01:41:01,525 --> 01:41:04,400
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

1528
01:41:05,600 --> 01:41:10,450
With the five graders in the eval file, we
lint it first, since that costs nothing.

1529
01:41:13,740 --> 01:41:18,115
One warning, the reminder that every
grader must pass, and no errors.

1530
01:41:20,615 --> 01:41:25,115
Now the run: the conversation, three times
on each model, with two workers.

1531
01:41:25,615 --> 01:41:29,240
This run spends six agent runs of three
requests each.

1532
01:41:29,740 --> 01:41:33,931
We're watching for a block above the
summary, which appears for any model that

1533
01:41:33,931 --> 01:41:35,865
didn't pass all three conversations.

1534
01:41:38,134 --> 01:41:42,511
About a minute later, there is one, for
haiku: one of its three conversations

1535
01:41:42,511 --> 01:41:42,909
failed.

1536
01:41:43,409 --> 01:41:47,646
Its failing grader, third no made-up
number, names the turn and the problem,

1537
01:41:47,646 --> 01:41:48,984
and points at trial two.

1538
01:41:53,484 --> 01:41:58,484
Every other grader passed on both models,
so terra passed all three conversations.

1539
01:42:01,700 --> 01:42:05,775
So let's read what that turn changed,
starting in the results folder.

1540
01:42:07,600 --> 01:42:10,750
There's one run folder, and we change into
it.

1541
01:42:13,147 --> 01:42:17,965
That summary counted from one and file
names count from zero, so its trial two is

1542
01:42:17,965 --> 01:42:20,047
the file named trial one, turn two.

1543
01:42:20,547 --> 01:42:23,897
That star stands for the start of the
file's long name.

1544
01:42:25,667 --> 01:42:29,342
It's a short patch, holding only what that
one turn changed.

1545
01:42:32,842 --> 01:42:37,422
It adds one entry under a Fixed heading,
and the entry ends with pull request

1546
01:42:37,422 --> 01:42:38,017
sixty-six.

1547
01:42:42,517 --> 01:42:47,336
But the third request gave no number: the
two requests before it gave sixty-four and

1548
01:42:47,336 --> 01:42:47,967
sixty-five.

1549
01:42:51,167 --> 01:42:54,442
That gap is in the skill, so that's where
we fix it.

1550
01:42:54,942 --> 01:42:59,092
We add one more rule at the end: treat
every request on its own.

1551
01:42:59,592 --> 01:43:03,585
By this rule, a pull request number
belongs only to the change it was given

1552
01:43:03,585 --> 01:43:06,567
with, and is never carried over from an
earlier request.

1553
01:43:07,767 --> 01:43:12,042
Then we run the same conversations again,
saving to a second folder.

1554
01:43:12,542 --> 01:43:15,117
That's another six agent runs.

1555
01:43:24,147 --> 01:43:28,642
This time there's a tick for each model,
and no block above the summary: all six

1556
01:43:28,642 --> 01:43:29,822
conversations passed.

1557
01:43:34,034 --> 01:43:36,384
So what did the conversation show?

1558
01:43:36,884 --> 01:43:41,334
Here's the line that the failing turn
added, under the two requests before it.

1559
01:43:41,834 --> 01:43:46,859
Sixty-six is a number nobody gave: it
simply follows sixty-four and sixty-five.

1560
01:43:47,359 --> 01:43:51,807
In an earlier run of this same
conversation, that turn cited sixty-five

1561
01:43:51,807 --> 01:43:52,309
instead.

1562
01:43:52,809 --> 01:43:57,466
Sixty-five is a real number: it belongs to
the internal rename, which the agent had

1563
01:43:57,466 --> 01:44:00,384
correctly left out of the changelog a
moment before.

1564
01:44:00,884 --> 01:44:05,087
So there, the number wasn't invented: it
leaked from one turn into the next, and

1565
01:44:05,087 --> 01:44:07,084
attached itself to a different change.

1566
01:44:08,300 --> 01:44:12,496
This happened on haiku, which hadn't made
up a number in any of our single-request

1567
01:44:12,496 --> 01:44:15,975
runs; a test of those alone would have
called it safe on this point.

1568
01:44:16,475 --> 01:44:20,972
Reading only the final file, a grader
would have seen this: a tidy, well-formed

1569
01:44:20,972 --> 01:44:22,850
entry with a plausible reference.

1570
01:44:26,067 --> 01:44:30,784
Before the new rule, one of haiku's three
conversations failed; after it, all six

1571
01:44:30,784 --> 01:44:31,192
passed.

1572
01:44:31,692 --> 01:44:36,567
One failure in three becoming none in
three is, once again, a small sample.

1573
01:44:37,767 --> 01:44:41,567
There's something these graders didn't
check, and we're leaving it alone.

1574
01:44:42,067 --> 01:44:46,217
None of them asks whether an entry is
filed under the right category.

1575
01:44:46,717 --> 01:44:49,017
That's a judgement about meaning.

1576
01:44:49,517 --> 01:44:54,130
So is a question we left open earlier: is
an entry written for the people who use

1577
01:44:54,130 --> 01:44:54,642
the tool?

1578
01:44:55,142 --> 01:44:57,642
No pattern can decide either one.

1579
01:44:58,866 --> 01:45:02,841
Everything we've graded so far is
structure that code can see.

1580
01:45:03,341 --> 01:45:07,788
Whether an entry is written for the people
who use the tool is not a pattern, so it

1581
01:45:07,788 --> 01:45:09,341
needs another kind of grader.

1582
01:45:10,567 --> 01:45:14,517
Our skill says each entry should be
written for people who use the tool.

1583
01:45:15,017 --> 01:45:19,114
Yet when the fix was a switch to a
streaming parser, one model's entry told

1584
01:45:19,114 --> 01:45:20,917
users about the streaming parser.

1585
01:45:21,417 --> 01:45:25,142
None of our graders checks for that,
because no pattern can.

1586
01:45:25,642 --> 01:45:30,061
Here, you'll write a grader that asks
another model one yes-or-no question, run

1587
01:45:30,061 --> 01:45:32,467
it over saved trials, and read its
reasons.

1588
01:45:33,667 --> 01:45:36,517
Let's start with why code can't check
this.

1589
01:45:37,017 --> 01:45:41,883
Every grader so far is code: a pattern, an
empty-diff check, a script, or a check of

1590
01:45:41,883 --> 01:45:43,042
which skills loaded.

1591
01:45:43,542 --> 01:45:46,292
Each one checks something code can see.

1592
01:45:46,792 --> 01:45:50,898
Whether an entry says how a fix was coded
isn't something a pattern can match,

1593
01:45:50,898 --> 01:45:53,267
because the giveaway words could be
anything.

1594
01:45:53,767 --> 01:45:58,485
Here they're streaming parser, when all a
user cares about is that big imports no

1595
01:45:58,485 --> 01:45:59,242
longer crash.

1596
01:45:59,742 --> 01:46:04,117
Checking that takes something that can
read, and that something is another model.

1597
01:46:05,334 --> 01:46:10,492
That kind of grader is called an LLM
judge, where LLM stands for large language

1598
01:46:10,492 --> 01:46:10,884
model.

1599
01:46:11,384 --> 01:46:15,966
It asks a language model to read what the
agent did, and decide whether it meets a

1600
01:46:15,966 --> 01:46:17,084
criterion you wrote.

1601
01:46:17,584 --> 01:46:21,334
Such a judge can decide things code can't,
but it has costs.

1602
01:46:21,834 --> 01:46:26,259
It's slower than code, and every trial it
judges costs a call to a model.

1603
01:46:26,759 --> 01:46:30,927
It can also be wrong in ways a pattern
can't: swayed by confident wording, or

1604
01:46:30,927 --> 01:46:33,309
reading your criterion differently from
you.

1605
01:46:33,809 --> 01:46:38,976
So we use a judge only for what code can't
decide, and everything graded so far stays

1606
01:46:38,976 --> 01:46:39,584
with code.

1607
01:46:40,800 --> 01:46:44,475
When a judge is needed, three habits make
its verdicts usable.

1608
01:46:44,975 --> 01:46:47,375
First, one question per judge.

1609
01:46:47,875 --> 01:46:51,883
Asked whether an entry is good, a judge
weighs several things at once, and you

1610
01:46:51,883 --> 01:46:53,475
can't tell which one tipped it.

1611
01:46:53,975 --> 01:46:56,600
Second, the answer is yes or no.

1612
01:46:57,100 --> 01:47:01,328
With a score from one to five, it's hard
for anyone, the judge included, to say

1613
01:47:01,328 --> 01:47:03,200
what separates a three from a four.

1614
01:47:03,700 --> 01:47:06,950
Yes or no forces the criterion to be
clear.

1615
01:47:08,167 --> 01:47:11,017
Third, we decide our own answers first.

1616
01:47:11,517 --> 01:47:16,086
Before writing the criterion, we go
through real examples, and say yes or no

1617
01:47:16,086 --> 01:47:16,567
to each.

1618
01:47:17,067 --> 01:47:21,196
If we hesitate over one, the criterion
isn't clear yet, and the judge would

1619
01:47:21,196 --> 01:47:22,242
inherit the muddle.

1620
01:47:22,742 --> 01:47:26,918
Each answer we write down is called a
label, and our labels are what the judge

1621
01:47:26,918 --> 01:47:28,042
gets checked against.

1622
01:47:29,267 --> 01:47:32,817
That's when to use one; here's how Vally's
judge is written.

1623
01:47:33,317 --> 01:47:37,580
This eval file ships in the practice
folder, and we're looking at it with a

1624
01:47:37,580 --> 01:47:38,717
judge already added.

1625
01:47:39,217 --> 01:47:42,092
It takes three additions, in three places.

1626
01:47:42,592 --> 01:47:46,817
First, under defaults, judge model names
the model that does the judging.

1627
01:47:47,317 --> 01:47:51,766
Without that line Vally picks one, and its
choice can change from one version of

1628
01:47:51,766 --> 01:47:52,767
Vally to the next.

1629
01:47:53,267 --> 01:47:57,998
We name a model that isn't one of the two
being tested, so that no model marks its

1630
01:47:57,998 --> 01:47:58,517
own work.

1631
01:47:59,734 --> 01:48:04,244
Each stimulus in this file is a request
worded in coding terms, like this one

1632
01:48:04,244 --> 01:48:05,709
about a streaming parser.

1633
01:48:06,209 --> 01:48:11,213
The second addition is the rubric: a list
of criteria for this stimulus, in plain

1634
01:48:11,213 --> 01:48:11,584
words.

1635
01:48:12,084 --> 01:48:14,584
Ours has a single criterion.

1636
01:48:15,084 --> 01:48:19,188
It asks that the new entry says what
changes for people who use the tool, and

1637
01:48:19,188 --> 01:48:20,734
doesn't say how it was coded.

1638
01:48:21,934 --> 01:48:26,009
The third addition is the judge itself: a
grader of type prompt.

1639
01:48:26,509 --> 01:48:30,932
Its name is unlucky, because it's
unrelated to the prompt field that holds

1640
01:48:30,932 --> 01:48:31,709
each request.

1641
01:48:32,209 --> 01:48:35,959
This grader reads the rubric of the
stimulus it sits in.

1642
01:48:36,459 --> 01:48:39,684
Scoring binary makes its answer yes or no.

1643
01:48:40,184 --> 01:48:44,827
On the next line is an extra instruction,
telling the judge to start each reason by

1644
01:48:44,827 --> 01:48:45,834
quoting the entry.

1645
01:48:46,334 --> 01:48:49,884
That way, each reason shows what the judge
was looking at.

1646
01:48:50,384 --> 01:48:54,786
Below it is the grader that was there
already, a pattern that checks the entry

1647
01:48:54,786 --> 01:48:56,084
cites its pull request.

1648
01:48:57,300 --> 01:49:01,385
When the judge runs, Vally gives the judge
model three things: the request, the

1649
01:49:01,385 --> 01:49:04,900
rubric, and files holding what the agent
said and did in that trial.

1650
01:49:05,400 --> 01:49:09,025
The judge reads them, and returns a
verdict and a reason.

1651
01:49:09,525 --> 01:49:14,459
Its verdict becomes the grader's pass or
fail, and its reason becomes the grader's

1652
01:49:14,459 --> 01:49:15,000
evidence.

1653
01:49:15,500 --> 01:49:20,425
Each trial gets one overall verdict,
however many criteria the rubric lists.

1654
01:49:20,925 --> 01:49:24,800
With one criterion, that verdict answers
our one question.

1655
01:49:25,300 --> 01:49:30,000
With several, it would be a blend, which
is another reason to ask about one thing.

1656
01:49:31,200 --> 01:49:34,725
Here's what one judged trial prints, taken
from our run.

1657
01:49:35,225 --> 01:49:38,875
Each trial gets a block, headed by the
name of its stimulus.

1658
01:49:39,375 --> 01:49:44,044
Inside, the line that begins with prompt
in square brackets is the judge: a tick or

1659
01:49:44,044 --> 01:49:45,450
a cross, then its reason.

1660
01:49:45,950 --> 01:49:49,977
This reason starts by quoting the entry,
and then objects that it leaks

1661
01:49:49,977 --> 01:49:51,225
implementation detail.

1662
01:49:54,725 --> 01:49:57,775
Under it is the pattern grader, which
passed.

1663
01:49:58,275 --> 01:50:01,750
Since every grader has to pass, this trial
fails.

1664
01:50:02,967 --> 01:50:07,537
Because a judge is a grader, vally grade
can run it over saved trials, without

1665
01:50:07,537 --> 01:50:08,592
starting an agent.

1666
01:50:09,092 --> 01:50:13,974
We run it from inside the run's folder, so
run dir is a dot, and the saved trials

1667
01:50:13,974 --> 01:50:15,842
come in from results dot jsonl.

1668
01:50:16,342 --> 01:50:20,859
As with the oracle, dash dash verbose
prints every grader's evidence, which for

1669
01:50:20,859 --> 01:50:22,117
a judge is its reason.

1670
01:50:22,617 --> 01:50:27,286
So we can reword the rubric and judge the
same trials again, without paying for the

1671
01:50:27,286 --> 01:50:28,017
agents twice.

1672
01:50:28,517 --> 01:50:33,588
Each judged trial is one call to the judge
model, charged to the Copilot plan the way

1673
01:50:33,588 --> 01:50:34,542
an agent run is.

1674
01:50:35,767 --> 01:50:38,292
That's how it works; now let's use it.

1675
01:50:38,792 --> 01:50:43,271
As it ships, the eval file has three
stimuli, each describing a merged change

1676
01:50:43,271 --> 01:50:44,842
the way a programmer would.

1677
01:50:45,342 --> 01:50:50,092
One swapped in a streaming parser, so that
big imports stop running out of memory.

1678
01:50:50,592 --> 01:50:54,959
Another put a cache in front of the tag
index, which makes one command much

1679
01:50:54,959 --> 01:50:55,367
faster.

1680
01:50:55,867 --> 01:51:00,584
The last rewrote the date parser as a
small tokenizer, so single-digit months

1681
01:51:00,584 --> 01:51:01,442
and days work.

1682
01:51:01,942 --> 01:51:06,792
Each request runs twice on both models,
which gives us twelve entries to judge.

1683
01:51:07,292 --> 01:51:10,767
Which model would you expect to name the
code more often?

1684
01:51:13,267 --> 01:51:16,142
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

1685
01:51:17,367 --> 01:51:22,216
Here's the run: vally eval on the wording
file, with two trials of each stimulus on

1686
01:51:22,216 --> 01:51:22,917
both models.

1687
01:51:23,417 --> 01:51:26,117
It spends twelve short agent runs.

1688
01:51:26,617 --> 01:51:31,292
So far, the file's only graders check that
each entry cites its pull request.

1689
01:51:34,487 --> 01:51:37,612
About a minute later, there's a tick for
each model.

1690
01:51:41,112 --> 01:51:43,487
Let's look in the results folder.

1691
01:51:45,274 --> 01:51:48,574
It holds one folder, named with the time
of the run.

1692
01:51:53,700 --> 01:51:57,125
We change into it, then into the folder
for the eval.

1693
01:51:58,394 --> 01:52:00,419
Then we list what's there.

1694
01:52:01,214 --> 01:52:05,014
There's one folder per stimulus: cache,
dates and parser.

1695
01:52:06,514 --> 01:52:11,069
Each one holds a folder per model and
trial, with that trial's patch, and here's

1696
01:52:11,069 --> 01:52:11,639
the first.

1697
01:52:14,880 --> 01:52:19,303
Haiku's entry fixes the out-of-memory
errors, and then says how: by using a

1698
01:52:19,303 --> 01:52:20,305
streaming parser.

1699
01:52:27,534 --> 01:52:32,031
Reading all twelve patches that way gives
us twelve entries, and we answer one

1700
01:52:32,031 --> 01:52:33,184
question about each.

1701
01:52:33,684 --> 01:52:38,098
Does it say what changes for people who
use the tool, without saying how it was

1702
01:52:38,098 --> 01:52:38,434
coded?

1703
01:52:38,934 --> 01:52:43,341
We don't need to know what a streaming
parser is, only whether the entry mentions

1704
01:52:43,341 --> 01:52:43,559
one.

1705
01:52:44,059 --> 01:52:48,384
Haiku's first parser entry names the
streaming parser, so we say no.

1706
01:52:48,884 --> 01:52:53,590
Its second, and both of terra's, only say
that large imports no longer run out of

1707
01:52:53,590 --> 01:52:55,159
memory, so those get a yes.

1708
01:52:58,367 --> 01:53:03,097
On the cache request, haiku's two entries
name the kind of cache outright, so again

1709
01:53:03,097 --> 01:53:03,667
we say no.

1710
01:53:04,167 --> 01:53:08,767
Terra's two are harder: they lead with the
speed-up, and then add, by caching the tag

1711
01:53:08,767 --> 01:53:09,092
index.

1712
01:53:09,592 --> 01:53:13,827
That's the benefit plus a hint of the
method, and we decided that a hint still

1713
01:53:13,827 --> 01:53:14,967
counts as saying how.

1714
01:53:15,467 --> 01:53:19,902
Hesitating like that is worth noticing,
because it marks an edge of the criterion

1715
01:53:19,902 --> 01:53:20,942
that isn't settled.

1716
01:53:24,167 --> 01:53:28,395
All four date entries say that
single-digit months and days now work, and

1717
01:53:28,395 --> 01:53:30,017
none mentions the tokenizer.

1718
01:53:30,517 --> 01:53:32,642
So those four get a yes.

1719
01:53:33,142 --> 01:53:35,742
That makes seven yes and five no.

1720
01:53:36,242 --> 01:53:40,327
We write the twelve answers in a notes
file, and we won't change them once the

1721
01:53:40,327 --> 01:53:41,217
judge has spoken.

1722
01:53:44,434 --> 01:53:48,484
Now the judge goes into the eval file,
just as we walked through it.

1723
01:53:48,984 --> 01:53:51,984
That's the judge model line under
defaults.

1724
01:53:52,484 --> 01:53:56,984
Then, in each of the three stimuli, the
same rubric and the same prompt grader.

1725
01:54:02,440 --> 01:54:06,944
Back up in the practice folder, lint
checks the edited file before anything is

1726
01:54:06,944 --> 01:54:07,290
spent.

1727
01:54:10,607 --> 01:54:14,007
It reports no errors, and the usual one
warning.

1728
01:54:14,507 --> 01:54:19,057
That warning says that with several
graders and no scoring block, every grader

1729
01:54:19,057 --> 01:54:19,757
has to pass.

1730
01:54:23,967 --> 01:54:27,292
For the judging, we change back into the
run's folder.

1731
01:54:29,734 --> 01:54:33,809
From here, vally grade runs the judge over
the twelve saved trials.

1732
01:54:34,309 --> 01:54:38,234
It starts no agent, and spends twelve
calls to the judge model.

1733
01:54:41,814 --> 01:54:46,189
A minute or two later, twelve blocks have
scrolled past, one for each trial.

1734
01:54:46,689 --> 01:54:50,748
Still on screen are the last two, each
with its prompt line: here a tick, and

1735
01:54:50,748 --> 01:54:52,014
then the judge's reason.

1736
01:54:59,234 --> 01:55:02,159
Here are two of the twelve reasons in
full.

1737
01:55:02,659 --> 01:55:07,285
To this parser entry the judge said yes:
it's user-focused, and doesn't describe

1738
01:55:07,285 --> 01:55:08,384
the implementation.

1739
01:55:10,884 --> 01:55:15,707
To this one it said no: the entry states
the benefit, but includes implementation

1740
01:55:15,707 --> 01:55:16,184
details.

1741
01:55:18,684 --> 01:55:23,162
Each reason was asked to start with its
entry, so that a verdict can be matched to

1742
01:55:23,162 --> 01:55:23,709
its trial.

1743
01:55:24,209 --> 01:55:28,338
Eleven of the twelve did; this second one
didn't, and only its mention of the

1744
01:55:28,338 --> 01:55:30,859
streaming parser tells us which entry it
means.

1745
01:55:32,067 --> 01:55:36,259
We write each verdict beside our own
answer, and leave our answers as they

1746
01:55:36,259 --> 01:55:36,542
were.

1747
01:55:37,042 --> 01:55:41,142
Across the twelve entries, the judge said
yes to eight and no to four.

1748
01:55:41,642 --> 01:55:45,017
Eleven of its verdicts match ours, and one
doesn't.

1749
01:55:49,234 --> 01:55:51,309
So what did the judge find?

1750
01:55:51,809 --> 01:55:56,027
We now have a grader for a line of the
skill that nothing checked before, and it

1751
01:55:56,027 --> 01:55:57,609
has already told us something.

1752
01:55:58,109 --> 01:56:02,216
Haiku got a no for three of its six
entries: it named the cache both times,

1753
01:56:02,216 --> 01:56:03,859
and the streaming parser once.

1754
01:56:04,359 --> 01:56:08,809
Terra got a no for one, a cache entry that
adds, by caching the tag index.

1755
01:56:09,309 --> 01:56:13,661
So the rule about writing for people who
use the tool is followed unevenly, and

1756
01:56:13,661 --> 01:56:15,534
until now nothing could show that.

1757
01:56:16,734 --> 01:56:19,509
It's tempting to act on that straight
away.

1758
01:56:20,009 --> 01:56:24,421
But every grader before this one was code,
which we tested with the oracle and

1759
01:56:24,421 --> 01:56:25,609
against saved trials.

1760
01:56:26,109 --> 01:56:28,534
This one is a model's opinion.

1761
01:56:29,034 --> 01:56:33,735
In our run it said no to one cache entry,
and yes to another that differs by a

1762
01:56:33,735 --> 01:56:34,459
single word.

1763
01:56:34,959 --> 01:56:39,216
Its reasons are well written, but good
writing doesn't show that a verdict is

1764
01:56:39,216 --> 01:56:39,659
correct.

1765
01:56:40,866 --> 01:56:44,391
The judge said yes for some trials and no
for others.

1766
01:56:44,891 --> 01:56:47,366
It's a model too, so is it right?

1767
01:56:48,567 --> 01:56:52,976
Our judge gave a yes or a no to twelve
changelog entries, with a well-written

1768
01:56:52,976 --> 01:56:53,892
reason for each.

1769
01:56:54,392 --> 01:56:58,574
But the judge is a model, and a
well-written reason isn't evidence of a

1770
01:56:58,574 --> 01:56:59,517
correct verdict.

1771
01:57:00,017 --> 01:57:04,378
Code graders could be tested against a
golden patch, and there's no golden patch

1772
01:57:04,378 --> 01:57:06,667
for whether an entry reads well to a user.

1773
01:57:07,167 --> 01:57:11,422
What we do have is our own yes or no for
the same twelve entries, written down

1774
01:57:11,422 --> 01:57:12,567
before the judge ran.

1775
01:57:13,067 --> 01:57:17,377
Here, you'll measure the judge against
those labels as two numbers, and decide

1776
01:57:17,377 --> 01:57:18,592
whether to rely on it.

1777
01:57:19,800 --> 01:57:22,575
Let's start with how a judge can be wrong.

1778
01:57:23,075 --> 01:57:26,250
Like any grader, it can be wrong in two
directions.

1779
01:57:26,750 --> 01:57:31,183
It can say yes to an entry we'd reject: a
failure slips through, and we believe the

1780
01:57:31,183 --> 01:57:32,625
skill is better than it is.

1781
01:57:33,125 --> 01:57:37,640
Or it can say no to an entry we'd accept:
a false alarm, and we go hunting for a

1782
01:57:37,640 --> 01:57:39,050
problem that isn't there.

1783
01:57:40,267 --> 01:57:44,722
The obvious measure is agreement: on what
share of the entries did the judge and we

1784
01:57:44,722 --> 01:57:45,742
say the same thing?

1785
01:57:46,242 --> 01:57:50,667
That makes it a poor measure, because it
hides which direction the errors run in.

1786
01:57:51,167 --> 01:57:54,792
Let's say we label twenty entries, and
eighteen are fine.

1787
01:57:55,292 --> 01:57:59,636
Even a judge that says yes to everything
agrees with us eighteen times out of

1788
01:57:59,636 --> 01:58:01,442
twenty, which is ninety percent.

1789
01:58:01,942 --> 01:58:04,967
Yet it has caught neither of the two bad
entries.

1790
01:58:05,467 --> 01:58:08,492
It's useless, and its agreement looks
excellent.

1791
01:58:09,700 --> 01:58:12,475
So we count the two directions separately.

1792
01:58:12,975 --> 01:58:17,525
Of the entries we said yes to, how many
did the judge pass, by saying yes too?

1793
01:58:18,025 --> 01:58:21,575
Of the entries we said no to, how many did
it fail?

1794
01:58:22,075 --> 01:58:25,775
That makes two numbers, and a judge has to
do well on both.

1795
01:58:26,275 --> 01:58:28,300
How well is well enough?

1796
01:58:28,800 --> 01:58:33,148
Practitioners this course leans on suggest
aiming above ninety percent on each

1797
01:58:33,148 --> 01:58:37,608
number, and labelling a hundred or more
examples for a judge we intend to depend

1798
01:58:37,608 --> 01:58:37,775
on.

1799
01:58:38,275 --> 01:58:42,650
Twelve labels, which is what we have, can
show that a judge is badly wrong.

1800
01:58:43,150 --> 01:58:45,875
They can't show that it's reliably right.

1801
01:58:47,100 --> 01:58:51,147
One caution before the arithmetic: a
measurement like this holds for one

1802
01:58:51,147 --> 01:58:53,900
rubric, one judge model, and one kind of
request.

1803
01:58:54,400 --> 01:58:58,586
Changing the rubric's wording, changing
the judge model, or pointing the judge at

1804
01:58:58,586 --> 01:59:00,550
a different kind of request undoes it.

1805
01:59:01,050 --> 01:59:03,650
Each of them means labelling again.

1806
01:59:04,150 --> 01:59:08,166
When we reword a rubric to fix a
disagreement, we set some labelled entries

1807
01:59:08,166 --> 01:59:10,950
aside first, and don't look at them while
rewording.

1808
01:59:11,450 --> 01:59:15,575
Otherwise, we'd be fitting the rubric to
the examples we're testing it on.

1809
01:59:16,800 --> 01:59:20,708
That's the idea; here's how the two
numbers are worked out, starting with

1810
01:59:20,708 --> 01:59:21,350
their names.

1811
01:59:21,850 --> 01:59:26,884
First, the true positive rate, or TPR: of
the trials we labelled yes, the share the

1812
01:59:26,884 --> 01:59:27,975
judge also passed.

1813
01:59:28,475 --> 01:59:31,475
Positive is the statistician's word for
yes.

1814
01:59:31,975 --> 01:59:36,927
Second, the true negative rate, or TNR: of
the trials we labelled no, the share the

1815
01:59:36,927 --> 01:59:38,000
judge also failed.

1816
01:59:38,500 --> 01:59:40,825
Negative is the word for no.

1817
01:59:42,034 --> 01:59:46,580
To work them out, every trial goes into
one of four boxes, by what the judge said

1818
01:59:46,580 --> 01:59:47,534
and what we said.

1819
01:59:48,034 --> 01:59:51,209
Here's an invented judge, to show the
arithmetic.

1820
01:59:51,709 --> 01:59:55,184
We labelled twenty entries: twelve yes,
and eight no.

1821
01:59:55,684 --> 02:00:00,034
This judge passed eleven of our twelve,
and it also passed three of our eight.

1822
02:00:00,534 --> 02:00:04,384
So it failed one of our yes entries, and
five of our no entries.

1823
02:00:05,600 --> 02:00:08,700
Each rate uses one column, read downwards.

1824
02:00:09,200 --> 02:00:13,900
On the left is TPR: eleven out of twelve,
which is ninety-two percent.

1825
02:00:14,400 --> 02:00:18,525
On the right is TNR: five out of eight,
which is sixty-three percent.

1826
02:00:19,025 --> 02:00:23,500
Agreement is sixteen out of twenty, eighty
percent, which sounds respectable.

1827
02:00:24,000 --> 02:00:28,818
TNR tells the real story: of the entries
we'd reject, this judge lets more than a

1828
02:00:28,818 --> 02:00:29,650
third through.

1829
02:00:30,867 --> 02:00:34,017
Each number, when it's low, says something
different.

1830
02:00:34,517 --> 02:00:38,792
Low TPR means false alarms: the judge is
stricter than we are.

1831
02:00:39,292 --> 02:00:43,742
Low TNR means missed failures: the judge
is more lenient than we are.

1832
02:00:44,242 --> 02:00:48,286
Which matters more depends on what the
judge is for, but a judge with either

1833
02:00:48,286 --> 02:00:50,467
number low isn't measuring our criterion.

1834
02:00:51,667 --> 02:00:54,892
That's how the numbers work; now let's
count ours.

1835
02:00:55,392 --> 02:00:59,942
First we'll sort our twelve trials into
the four boxes, and work out both rates.

1836
02:01:00,442 --> 02:01:04,368
Then we'll look closely at the one entry
where the judge and we differ, and write

1837
02:01:04,368 --> 02:01:06,792
down what we will and won't rely on the
judge for.

1838
02:01:07,292 --> 02:01:10,017
None of it runs an agent or a judge.

1839
02:01:10,517 --> 02:01:15,164
On that entry we said no and the judge
said yes: which of the two rates will it

1840
02:01:15,164 --> 02:01:15,517
lower?

1841
02:01:18,017 --> 02:01:21,367
If you did the same count yourself, here's
what you'd see.

1842
02:01:22,567 --> 02:01:26,767
Both lists are in the notes file from the
judging, named wording labels.

1843
02:01:27,267 --> 02:01:31,567
Each of the twelve entries has our yes or
no, with the judge's beside it.

1844
02:01:32,067 --> 02:01:35,417
Our answers haven't been changed since the
judge ran.

1845
02:01:39,634 --> 02:01:42,934
Sorting takes one look at each row's two
answers.

1846
02:01:43,434 --> 02:01:47,834
Seven entries got a yes from both of us,
and four got a no from both.

1847
02:01:50,334 --> 02:01:55,234
One entry got a yes from the judge and a
no from us, and the last box is empty.

1848
02:01:58,434 --> 02:02:01,084
Now the two rates, one column each.

1849
02:02:01,584 --> 02:02:05,684
On the left, TPR is seven out of seven: a
hundred percent.

1850
02:02:06,184 --> 02:02:09,734
On the right, TNR is four out of five:
eighty percent.

1851
02:02:10,234 --> 02:02:14,333
Agreement is eleven out of twelve,
ninety-two percent, and it wouldn't say

1852
02:02:14,333 --> 02:02:15,884
which column the miss is in.

1853
02:02:16,384 --> 02:02:19,434
With only twelve labels, both rates are
rough.

1854
02:02:22,634 --> 02:02:25,659
Next, every disagreement gets a second
look.

1855
02:02:26,159 --> 02:02:30,697
For each one, we'd read the entry and the
judge's reason again, and decide who is

1856
02:02:30,697 --> 02:02:31,034
right.

1857
02:02:31,534 --> 02:02:35,009
If we still disagree, that's one error of
the judge's.

1858
02:02:35,509 --> 02:02:39,609
If the judge has persuaded us, our
criterion wasn't what we thought.

1859
02:02:40,109 --> 02:02:44,646
Even then, the label stays as we first
wrote it, and the entry still counts as a

1860
02:02:44,646 --> 02:02:45,384
disagreement.

1861
02:02:45,884 --> 02:02:49,709
Changing labels to match a judge would
make any judge look good.

1862
02:02:50,209 --> 02:02:54,927
For a criterion that turned out unclear,
the remedy is to reword it, then measure

1863
02:02:54,927 --> 02:02:56,209
again on fresh labels.

1864
02:02:57,434 --> 02:03:00,059
In our run, there's one to examine.

1865
02:03:00,559 --> 02:03:04,909
It's one of two entries from the same
model, which differ by a single word.

1866
02:03:05,409 --> 02:03:10,309
Both say a command got faster by caching
the tag index: the benefit, with a hint of

1867
02:03:10,309 --> 02:03:10,959
the method.

1868
02:03:11,459 --> 02:03:15,334
We hesitated over both when labelling, and
said no to both.

1869
02:03:15,834 --> 02:03:20,184
To the first, the judge said no as well,
because it states the implementation.

1870
02:03:20,684 --> 02:03:25,009
To the second, it said yes, calling the
same mention a minor blemish.

1871
02:03:30,234 --> 02:03:31,884
So who is right?

1872
02:03:32,384 --> 02:03:36,822
Our criterion says an entry doesn't say
how the change was coded, and the phrase

1873
02:03:36,822 --> 02:03:38,709
by caching the tag index says how.

1874
02:03:39,209 --> 02:03:42,934
In its reason for the first entry, the
judge says the same.

1875
02:03:43,434 --> 02:03:48,322
So we still disagree, and count it as one
error of the judge's: it was inconsistent

1876
02:03:48,322 --> 02:03:50,384
on two entries a single word apart.

1877
02:03:50,884 --> 02:03:55,346
It's also the wording we hesitated over
ourselves, which suggests our criterion

1878
02:03:55,346 --> 02:03:56,759
isn't clear at this edge.

1879
02:03:57,259 --> 02:03:59,659
Our label stays as we wrote it.

1880
02:04:00,867 --> 02:04:04,817
Last, we write the decision down, at the
end of the same notes file.

1881
02:04:05,317 --> 02:04:09,742
On twelve labels, seven yes and five no,
the judge matched eleven.

1882
02:04:10,242 --> 02:04:15,000
Its one miss is an entry we hesitated over
ourselves, which it failed in one trial

1883
02:04:15,000 --> 02:04:16,392
and passed in the other.

1884
02:04:16,892 --> 02:04:20,890
That's enough to use the judge for
comparing versions of the skill, knowing

1885
02:04:20,890 --> 02:04:22,542
it may pass a borderline entry.

1886
02:04:23,042 --> 02:04:27,192
It's not enough to trust a single verdict
without reading its reason.

1887
02:04:27,692 --> 02:04:31,742
Whenever the rubric or the judge model
changes, we'll label more.

1888
02:04:35,967 --> 02:04:38,267
So what does that result show?

1889
02:04:38,767 --> 02:04:43,460
On these twelve entries, this judge model
applied this criterion the way we do

1890
02:04:43,460 --> 02:04:44,242
eleven times.

1891
02:04:44,742 --> 02:04:48,917
Its TNR came out at eighty percent, under
the ninety we aim for.

1892
02:04:49,417 --> 02:04:53,946
But with five no labels, one entry is the
whole distance between eighty and a

1893
02:04:53,946 --> 02:04:54,417
hundred.

1894
02:04:54,917 --> 02:04:59,170
Twelve labels are too few to tell a judge
that's right ninety-nine percent of the

1895
02:04:59,170 --> 02:05:02,267
time from one that's right eighty-five
percent of the time.

1896
02:05:03,467 --> 02:05:05,492
It's still a real result.

1897
02:05:05,992 --> 02:05:10,654
Had the judge passed everything, the same
twelve labels would have put its TNR at

1898
02:05:10,654 --> 02:05:10,942
zero.

1899
02:05:11,442 --> 02:05:16,074
Likewise, a judge that failed entries for
reasons unrelated to the criterion would

1900
02:05:16,074 --> 02:05:17,317
have shown up at once.

1901
02:05:17,817 --> 02:05:21,167
Those are the most common ways for a judge
to go wrong.

1902
02:05:21,667 --> 02:05:26,063
Ours did neither: it caught four of our
five no entries, and wavered on one

1903
02:05:26,063 --> 02:05:26,942
borderline one.

1904
02:05:28,166 --> 02:05:31,166
We've measured the judge against our own
labels.

1905
02:05:31,666 --> 02:05:35,877
But some entries it refused were nearly
right and some were far off, and a yes or

1906
02:05:35,877 --> 02:05:39,775
no can't tell them apart: can a grader
give partial credit without hiding a

1907
02:05:39,775 --> 02:05:40,191
failure?

1908
02:05:41,400 --> 02:05:45,800
Our judge said no to four of the twelve
entries, and we said no to five.

1909
02:05:46,300 --> 02:05:48,050
Here are two of them.

1910
02:05:48,550 --> 02:05:51,575
One says outright how the change was
coded.

1911
02:05:52,075 --> 02:05:56,100
The other starts with what users notice,
and only hints at how.

1912
02:05:56,600 --> 02:06:00,400
Both got the same no, and a yes or no
can't tell them apart.

1913
02:06:00,900 --> 02:06:05,375
Here, you'll give the judge a scale, and
see how an average can let a failure pass.

1914
02:06:06,600 --> 02:06:09,500
Let's start with when a score is worth
having.

1915
02:06:10,000 --> 02:06:14,232
We asked the judge for a yes or no on
purpose: a yes-or-no criterion has to be

1916
02:06:14,232 --> 02:06:17,650
clear, and the difference between a three
and a four rarely is.

1917
02:06:18,150 --> 02:06:21,050
That still holds for the question, did it
pass?

1918
02:06:21,550 --> 02:06:25,626
A scale is worth adding when the work
really can be partly right, and you want

1919
02:06:25,626 --> 02:06:27,350
to know how far off a failure is.

1920
02:06:27,850 --> 02:06:32,147
Wording is like that: an entry can be
mostly for users, with one word of

1921
02:06:32,147 --> 02:06:33,400
implementation in it.

1922
02:06:34,600 --> 02:06:36,675
Other checks aren't like that.

1923
02:06:37,175 --> 02:06:41,990
An entry either cites its pull request or
it doesn't, and a security fix is either

1924
02:06:41,990 --> 02:06:43,575
under Security or it isn't.

1925
02:06:44,075 --> 02:06:48,449
For these there's no nearly: a skill that
gets one wrong has failed, however good

1926
02:06:48,449 --> 02:06:49,475
everything else is.

1927
02:06:49,975 --> 02:06:54,191
Vally's docs draw the same line: a
threshold, meaning a pass mark on a score,

1928
02:06:54,191 --> 02:06:57,750
is for work that's genuinely graded, not
for pass-or-fail checks.

1929
02:06:58,250 --> 02:07:02,525
So we keep checks that must all hold out
of any score that gets averaged.

1930
02:07:03,733 --> 02:07:06,908
The sources we rely on differ about
scales.

1931
02:07:07,408 --> 02:07:11,392
One argues for yes-or-no judges
throughout, and another treats partial

1932
02:07:11,392 --> 02:07:13,383
success as a range worth recording.

1933
02:07:13,883 --> 02:07:18,086
They agree on the part that matters here:
whatever the scale, you still decide pass

1934
02:07:18,086 --> 02:07:21,883
or fail with a clear line, and you check
that line against your own labels.

1935
02:07:23,100 --> 02:07:28,100
That's when a score helps; now, here's how
Vally turns grader results into a verdict.

1936
02:07:28,600 --> 02:07:33,525
Every grader gives a score between zero
and one, as well as a pass or a fail.

1937
02:07:34,025 --> 02:07:38,453
A pass-or-fail grader, such as diff
contains, scores one when it passes and

1938
02:07:38,453 --> 02:07:39,575
zero when it fails.

1939
02:07:40,075 --> 02:07:44,275
For each trial, Vally combines its
graders' scores into one score.

1940
02:07:44,775 --> 02:07:49,181
What decides the trial's verdict depends
on one optional part of the eval file,

1941
02:07:49,181 --> 02:07:50,575
called the scoring block.

1942
02:07:51,800 --> 02:07:56,807
With no scoring block, which is what every
eval file so far has had, every grader

1943
02:07:56,807 --> 02:07:57,425
must pass.

1944
02:07:57,925 --> 02:08:02,374
Vally still works out a score, the plain
average of the graders' scores, and prints

1945
02:08:02,374 --> 02:08:04,250
it, but that score decides nothing.

1946
02:08:04,750 --> 02:08:08,125
Lint's familiar warning is saying exactly
that.

1947
02:08:09,333 --> 02:08:13,646
Here's the scoring block we'll add later,
at the end of a copy of our judge's eval

1948
02:08:13,646 --> 02:08:16,433
file, where it applies to every stimulus
in the file.

1949
02:08:16,933 --> 02:08:20,858
Weights say how much each grader type
counts towards the trial's score.

1950
02:08:21,358 --> 02:08:25,408
They're keyed by the grader's type, not
its name: prompt is the judge, and diff

1951
02:08:25,408 --> 02:08:28,483
contains is the check that the entry cites
its pull request.

1952
02:08:28,983 --> 02:08:31,183
Here, the two count equally.

1953
02:08:31,683 --> 02:08:36,458
The threshold is the score, from zero to
one, that a trial must reach to pass.

1954
02:08:36,958 --> 02:08:41,408
With this block, a trial passes when its
weighted score reaches the threshold,

1955
02:08:41,408 --> 02:08:43,233
whatever any single grader said.

1956
02:08:43,733 --> 02:08:48,345
Sixty percent sounds like a fair bar;
we're using it to show what goes wrong,

1957
02:08:48,345 --> 02:08:49,183
not as advice.

1958
02:08:50,400 --> 02:08:54,900
Two rules follow from the arithmetic, and
each lets an average hide a failure.

1959
02:08:55,400 --> 02:08:59,625
First, a strong score on one grader can
carry a failure on another.

1960
02:09:00,125 --> 02:09:04,218
In this trial, the judge fails with a
score of a quarter, and the pull request

1961
02:09:04,218 --> 02:09:05,950
check passes with a score of one.

1962
02:09:06,450 --> 02:09:11,306
The trial's score comes to zero point six
two five, which reaches the threshold, so

1963
02:09:11,306 --> 02:09:12,300
the trial passes.

1964
02:09:12,800 --> 02:09:17,482
The judge's failure is still printed with
a cross; it just no longer decides the

1965
02:09:17,482 --> 02:09:17,950
verdict.

1966
02:09:19,167 --> 02:09:23,886
Second, a grader type that's left out of
the weights stops counting: its weight is

1967
02:09:23,886 --> 02:09:24,692
taken as zero.

1968
02:09:25,192 --> 02:09:29,159
With diff contains alone in the weights,
the judge would still be called and paid

1969
02:09:29,159 --> 02:09:31,217
for, and its verdict would change nothing.

1970
02:09:31,717 --> 02:09:35,192
The docs put it plainly: omitting a type
silences it.

1971
02:09:35,692 --> 02:09:40,242
A threshold with no weights at all takes
the plain average of every grader.

1972
02:09:40,742 --> 02:09:45,117
Here's a stimulus with three graders: a
yes-or-no judge, and two checks.

1973
02:09:45,617 --> 02:09:50,205
The judge fails, which for a yes-or-no
judge is a score of zero, and both checks

1974
02:09:50,205 --> 02:09:50,492
pass.

1975
02:09:50,992 --> 02:09:55,948
The average is zero point six seven, which
reaches zero point six, so the judge is

1976
02:09:55,948 --> 02:09:56,492
outvoted.

1977
02:09:57,700 --> 02:10:02,475
That's the scoring block; next, here's a
judge that can score between zero and one.

1978
02:10:02,975 --> 02:10:07,758
The judge is the prompt grader, and its
config has a scoring setting, which so far

1979
02:10:07,758 --> 02:10:09,800
we've set to binary: a yes or a no.

1980
02:10:10,300 --> 02:10:14,668
That setting has two other values: scale
one to five, which is also what a judge

1981
02:10:14,668 --> 02:10:17,725
does when the setting is left out, and
scale one to ten.

1982
02:10:18,225 --> 02:10:20,800
We'll give ours scale one to five.

1983
02:10:21,300 --> 02:10:25,194
It's a different thing from the scoring
block: this setting sits inside the

1984
02:10:25,194 --> 02:10:28,050
judge's config, and changes only how the
judge answers.

1985
02:10:28,550 --> 02:10:31,625
Beside it goes a threshold of the judge's
own.

1986
02:10:32,833 --> 02:10:37,733
On this scale, the judge gives each rubric
criterion a whole number from one to five.

1987
02:10:38,233 --> 02:10:42,809
Vally rescales that number to the range
zero to one, so it can be combined with

1988
02:10:42,809 --> 02:10:44,083
other graders' scores.

1989
02:10:44,583 --> 02:10:48,808
A one becomes zero, a five becomes one,
and each step is a quarter.

1990
02:10:49,308 --> 02:10:53,483
The judge passes when its rescaled score
reaches its own threshold.

1991
02:10:53,983 --> 02:10:57,833
Left out, that threshold is a half, which
means a three passes.

1992
02:10:58,333 --> 02:11:02,883
Ours is zero point seven five, so the
judge has to give at least a four.

1993
02:11:04,100 --> 02:11:07,825
So there are two thresholds, and it
matters which one decides.

1994
02:11:08,325 --> 02:11:12,624
With no scoring block, the judge's own
threshold decides: it sets the judge's

1995
02:11:12,624 --> 02:11:15,025
tick or cross, and a cross fails the
trial.

1996
02:11:15,525 --> 02:11:19,855
With a scoring block, only the judge's
score goes into the trial's score, and the

1997
02:11:19,855 --> 02:11:22,475
judge's own threshold stops deciding the
verdict.

1998
02:11:23,700 --> 02:11:26,975
There's one thing to know before the
output appears.

1999
02:11:27,475 --> 02:11:31,891
Vally grade prints a reason and a tick or
cross for the judge, and a score line for

2000
02:11:31,891 --> 02:11:34,125
the trial, but not the judge's own number.

2001
02:11:34,625 --> 02:11:38,869
In our trials, every pull request check
passes and scores one, so the trial's

2002
02:11:38,869 --> 02:11:41,625
score is the average of one and the
judge's score.

2003
02:11:42,125 --> 02:11:46,262
That means the judge's answer can be
worked out from the score line: a hundred

2004
02:11:46,262 --> 02:11:50,770
percent is a five, eighty-seven point five
is a four, and so on down to fifty percent

2005
02:11:50,770 --> 02:11:51,300
for a one.

2006
02:11:51,800 --> 02:11:55,200
If a trial's check failed, this table
wouldn't apply.

2007
02:11:56,400 --> 02:12:00,325
That's how the scale works; now let's
score our twelve entries with it.

2008
02:12:00,825 --> 02:12:05,103
We'll copy the judge's eval file, put the
judge on the scale, and re-grade the

2009
02:12:05,103 --> 02:12:06,200
twelve saved trials.

2010
02:12:06,700 --> 02:12:10,075
No agent starts; the cost is twelve judge
calls.

2011
02:12:10,575 --> 02:12:14,425
The yes-or-no judge agreed with eleven of
our twelve labels.

2012
02:12:14,925 --> 02:12:19,163
Which of these verdicts would you expect
to change, with a judge that has to give

2013
02:12:19,163 --> 02:12:20,000
at least a four?

2014
02:12:22,500 --> 02:12:25,375
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

2015
02:12:26,600 --> 02:12:31,378
First, we copy the judge's eval file, so
that the original stays as it is for

2016
02:12:31,378 --> 02:12:31,750
later.

2017
02:12:36,280 --> 02:12:40,379
In the copy, the name becomes wording
scaled, and each of the three judges gets

2018
02:12:40,379 --> 02:12:44,530
the two lines we looked at: the scale, and
a threshold of zero point seven five.

2019
02:12:45,030 --> 02:12:48,180
Lint checks the copy before anything is
spent.

2020
02:12:52,127 --> 02:12:55,227
It prints the familiar warning, and no
errors.

2021
02:12:59,433 --> 02:13:03,608
The twelve saved trials are in their run
folder, so we change into it.

2022
02:13:06,033 --> 02:13:10,504
Then we re-grade them with the copy, and
the verbose flag asks for each grader's

2023
02:13:10,504 --> 02:13:10,783
line.

2024
02:13:11,283 --> 02:13:13,858
Each block will end in a score line.

2025
02:13:19,740 --> 02:13:23,954
A little later there are twelve blocks,
one for each trial, and these are the last

2026
02:13:23,954 --> 02:13:24,365
of them.

2027
02:13:34,567 --> 02:13:37,017
Here's one of those blocks in full.

2028
02:13:37,517 --> 02:13:41,392
The judge's line has a cross, and its
reason quotes the entry.

2029
02:13:41,892 --> 02:13:44,392
The pull request check has a tick.

2030
02:13:44,892 --> 02:13:49,252
And the score line says sixty-two point
five percent, which, in the table, is a

2031
02:13:49,252 --> 02:13:50,467
judge's answer of two.

2032
02:13:50,967 --> 02:13:55,814
A two is below the judge's threshold, so
the judge fails, and the trial fails with

2033
02:13:55,814 --> 02:13:55,992
it.

2034
02:13:59,200 --> 02:14:03,607
The blocks don't name the model or the
trial, so we match each one to its row by

2035
02:14:03,607 --> 02:14:05,150
the entry its reason quotes.

2036
02:14:05,650 --> 02:14:09,600
Then each score goes beside the yes-or-no
verdict and our label.

2037
02:14:10,100 --> 02:14:14,050
The three entries that say how the change
was coded all got a two.

2038
02:14:14,550 --> 02:14:17,900
The entries we labelled yes got fours and
fives.

2039
02:14:18,400 --> 02:14:21,800
The two entries that only hint at how both
got a four.

2040
02:14:22,300 --> 02:14:26,421
At our threshold a four passes, so both
now pass, and since the yes-or-no judge

2041
02:14:26,421 --> 02:14:28,925
had failed one of them, one verdict has
changed.

2042
02:14:29,425 --> 02:14:31,500
We had labelled both no.

2043
02:14:35,700 --> 02:14:39,629
Now for the average: we put the scoring
block from before at the end of the copy,

2044
02:14:39,629 --> 02:14:43,656
with half the weight on the judge, half on
the check, and a threshold of zero point

2045
02:14:43,656 --> 02:14:43,850
six.

2046
02:14:47,300 --> 02:14:51,842
Lint now says the file is valid, and the
warning has gone, because the file says

2047
02:14:51,842 --> 02:14:53,375
how to combine its graders.

2048
02:14:55,875 --> 02:15:00,500
Then it's the same re-grade, in the same
run folder, for twelve more judge calls.

2049
02:15:02,907 --> 02:15:06,257
This time, each score line ends with the
threshold.

2050
02:15:12,587 --> 02:15:16,537
These last blocks have passed, and so has
every block above them.

2051
02:15:26,767 --> 02:15:28,617
Here's one of the twelve.

2052
02:15:29,117 --> 02:15:33,392
The judge's line still has a cross, and a
reason that faults the entry.

2053
02:15:33,892 --> 02:15:35,942
But the trial has a tick.

2054
02:15:36,442 --> 02:15:40,798
The judge's two rescales to a quarter, the
check adds a full one, and half of each

2055
02:15:40,798 --> 02:15:44,092
comes to zero point six two five, which
reaches the threshold.

2056
02:15:44,592 --> 02:15:48,617
Four of the twelve passed this way, with a
cross from the judge inside.

2057
02:15:51,833 --> 02:15:55,158
Last, we take the scoring block out of the
copy again.

2058
02:15:55,658 --> 02:15:58,908
The scores go into our notes file as a new
column.

2059
02:15:59,408 --> 02:16:01,983
Under the table goes one sentence.

2060
02:16:02,483 --> 02:16:05,458
One verdict changed between the two
judges.

2061
02:16:05,958 --> 02:16:10,608
Against our twelve labels, the one-to-five
judge has TPR seven of seven.

2062
02:16:11,108 --> 02:16:13,583
Its TNR is three of five.

2063
02:16:16,800 --> 02:16:18,875
So what did the scores show?

2064
02:16:19,375 --> 02:16:23,730
For one thing, the scale did show which
failures were close, and which were far

2065
02:16:23,730 --> 02:16:23,950
off.

2066
02:16:24,450 --> 02:16:27,350
These entries say how the change was
coded.

2067
02:16:27,850 --> 02:16:30,425
These start with what users notice.

2068
02:16:30,925 --> 02:16:35,119
If we were choosing what to fix in the
skill, the entries that are far off say

2069
02:16:35,119 --> 02:16:35,925
where to start.

2070
02:16:37,133 --> 02:16:40,608
The scale also changed a verdict, and not
for the better.

2071
02:16:41,108 --> 02:16:45,679
With the judge's threshold at zero point
seven five, two entries passed that we had

2072
02:16:45,679 --> 02:16:48,983
labelled no, so TNR fell from four of five
to three of five.

2073
02:16:49,483 --> 02:16:53,725
At the default threshold of a half, a
three would pass as well; in this grading

2074
02:16:53,725 --> 02:16:56,033
no entry got one, so the count is the
same.

2075
02:16:56,533 --> 02:17:00,108
A threshold is a decision about where good
enough starts.

2076
02:17:00,608 --> 02:17:04,676
The way to know whether it sits where
you'd put it is the same check as before:

2077
02:17:04,676 --> 02:17:06,633
compare its verdicts with your labels.

2078
02:17:07,833 --> 02:17:12,429
We graded the same twelve entries twice:
once without the scoring block, and once

2079
02:17:12,429 --> 02:17:12,883
with it.

2080
02:17:13,383 --> 02:17:17,700
The judge's answer changed for one of
them: a four the first time, and a three

2081
02:17:17,700 --> 02:17:18,308
the second.

2082
02:17:18,808 --> 02:17:23,333
At the judge's own threshold, that's a
pass and then a fail, for the same entry.

2083
02:17:23,833 --> 02:17:28,385
A judge is a model, so its answer can vary
from one grading to the next, and near the

2084
02:17:28,385 --> 02:17:31,383
threshold that variation turns into a
different verdict.

2085
02:17:31,883 --> 02:17:35,108
The entries that got a two got a two both
times.

2086
02:17:36,333 --> 02:17:40,883
The scale also invited the judge to mark
things the rubric didn't ask about.

2087
02:17:41,383 --> 02:17:46,083
This entry got a four, not a five, though
we and the yes-or-no judge both said yes.

2088
02:17:46,583 --> 02:17:51,162
Its reason says the entry could be more
concrete, with an example date, which our

2089
02:17:51,162 --> 02:17:52,858
one criterion doesn't ask for.

2090
02:17:56,358 --> 02:18:00,783
A rubric with one criterion keeps a
yes-or-no judge on its question.

2091
02:18:01,283 --> 02:18:04,858
Given a scale, the same judge finds other
things to mark.

2092
02:18:06,067 --> 02:18:08,317
Then there's the scoring block.

2093
02:18:08,817 --> 02:18:12,217
These are the trials that passed with a
cross from the judge.

2094
02:18:12,717 --> 02:18:16,728
The judge failed each of them, in plain
words, and each passed as a trial, because

2095
02:18:16,728 --> 02:18:19,467
the check scored a full one, and the
average carried it.

2096
02:18:19,967 --> 02:18:23,567
The check nearly always passes, and it
carries half the weight.

2097
02:18:24,067 --> 02:18:28,355
So while the check passes, a trial fails
at this threshold only if the judge

2098
02:18:28,355 --> 02:18:29,992
answers one, the lowest mark.

2099
02:18:30,492 --> 02:18:34,611
All twelve trials passed, so nothing in a
summary would say that the judge had

2100
02:18:34,611 --> 02:18:35,667
failed four of them.

2101
02:18:36,167 --> 02:18:38,942
That's how a failure hides in an average.

2102
02:18:40,167 --> 02:18:44,503
So we follow Vally's advice: thresholds
are for judged quality, and pass-or-fail

2103
02:18:44,503 --> 02:18:46,292
checks stay out of the weighting.

2104
02:18:46,792 --> 02:18:50,192
From here on, the yes-or-no judge decides
the verdict.

2105
02:18:50,692 --> 02:18:55,131
The one-to-five judge is a tool to reach
for when you want to know how far off a

2106
02:18:55,131 --> 02:18:55,742
failure is.

2107
02:18:56,967 --> 02:18:59,967
Scores and counts both move after an edit.

2108
02:19:00,467 --> 02:19:03,942
If seven of ten becomes nine of ten, is
that real?

2109
02:19:05,167 --> 02:19:09,817
After an edit to the skill, seven of ten
trials passing becomes nine of ten.

2110
02:19:10,317 --> 02:19:14,478
That looks like an improvement, but the
same model with the same request gives

2111
02:19:14,478 --> 02:19:16,292
different results from run to run.

2112
02:19:16,792 --> 02:19:21,151
So some of that gain may be chance, and
we've already edited this skill four times

2113
02:19:21,151 --> 02:19:22,692
on counts smaller than these.

2114
02:19:23,192 --> 02:19:27,497
Here, you'll make one more edit, measure
the skill before and after it, and say in

2115
02:19:27,497 --> 02:19:29,492
one sentence what the result supports.

2116
02:19:30,700 --> 02:19:34,175
Let's start with what a count from a few
trials can tell us.

2117
02:19:34,675 --> 02:19:38,775
When seven of ten trials pass, seventy
percent is an estimate.

2118
02:19:39,275 --> 02:19:42,675
Ten more trials might give five passes, or
nine.

2119
02:19:43,175 --> 02:19:47,675
The true pass rate is the one we'd see
over a very large number of trials.

2120
02:19:48,175 --> 02:19:52,800
All we know is a range for it: probably
between forty and eighty-nine percent.

2121
02:19:53,300 --> 02:19:57,494
Probably means that a range worked out
this way holds the true rate about

2122
02:19:57,494 --> 02:19:59,275
ninety-five times in a hundred.

2123
02:20:00,500 --> 02:20:04,275
Now back to the edit that took seven of
ten to nine of ten.

2124
02:20:04,775 --> 02:20:08,730
Here are the two ranges, forty to
eighty-nine, and sixty to ninety-eight,

2125
02:20:08,730 --> 02:20:10,625
and they overlap almost completely.

2126
02:20:11,125 --> 02:20:15,444
If the skill's true pass rate were eighty
percent both before and after, it could

2127
02:20:15,444 --> 02:20:17,150
easily produce those two counts.

2128
02:20:17,650 --> 02:20:20,275
So this result doesn't show a change.

2129
02:20:20,775 --> 02:20:24,938
With the same two rates over a hundred
trials each, the ranges don't touch, and

2130
02:20:24,938 --> 02:20:26,150
that would be evidence.

2131
02:20:27,367 --> 02:20:29,592
Three habits follow from that.

2132
02:20:30,092 --> 02:20:34,496
First, we compare like with like: the same
stimuli, the same graders and the same

2133
02:20:34,496 --> 02:20:36,942
model, with both versions run in one
sitting.

2134
02:20:37,442 --> 02:20:41,549
Second, we report counts, and not only
percentages, because one hundred percent

2135
02:20:41,549 --> 02:20:44,617
could be three of three, or three hundred
of three hundred.

2136
02:20:45,117 --> 02:20:49,342
Third, when the ranges overlap, we say
that these trials cannot tell.

2137
02:20:49,842 --> 02:20:54,173
That doesn't mean the edit did nothing; it
means we don't know yet, and more trials

2138
02:20:54,173 --> 02:20:55,217
are how to find out.

2139
02:20:56,434 --> 02:21:01,377
More trials narrow a range, but slowly: it
takes about four times the trials to halve

2140
02:21:01,377 --> 02:21:01,959
its width.

2141
02:21:02,459 --> 02:21:06,346
That's the real price of certainty, and
the reason to spend trials where a

2142
02:21:06,346 --> 02:21:07,659
decision depends on them.

2143
02:21:08,867 --> 02:21:13,292
The arithmetic is tedious, so the course
has a small calculator for it.

2144
02:21:13,792 --> 02:21:18,142
It takes a count before and a count after,
and shows the range around each.

2145
02:21:18,642 --> 02:21:22,442
Then it says whether the two ranges
overlap, which here they do.

2146
02:21:23,667 --> 02:21:27,517
That's how to read a count; here's the
experiment that will give us some.

2147
02:21:28,017 --> 02:21:32,286
It's an experiment file, which names
variants of one eval, and this one comes

2148
02:21:32,286 --> 02:21:33,617
with the starter folder.

2149
02:21:34,117 --> 02:21:39,092
The eval is the one graded by our judge:
three requests worded in coding terms.

2150
02:21:39,592 --> 02:21:44,174
Runs, set to three under overrides, gives
every variant three trials of each

2151
02:21:44,174 --> 02:21:44,717
stimulus.

2152
02:21:45,217 --> 02:21:48,842
Both of our models, haiku and terra, get
two variants each.

2153
02:21:49,342 --> 02:21:53,795
One uses a copy of the skill as it is
before our edit, in a folder named

2154
02:21:53,795 --> 02:21:55,217
changelog entry before.

2155
02:21:55,717 --> 02:21:59,592
Its partner uses the skill itself, which
we're about to edit.

2156
02:22:00,800 --> 02:22:05,214
When the experiment runs, each stimulus
that wasn't a clean pass gets a block,

2157
02:22:05,214 --> 02:22:07,025
headed by its variant and model.

2158
02:22:07,525 --> 02:22:11,325
With a judge, the block lists the judge's
verdict for each trial.

2159
02:22:11,825 --> 02:22:15,910
Here are some from a run of this
experiment, cut down to their first two

2160
02:22:15,910 --> 02:22:16,250
lines.

2161
02:22:16,750 --> 02:22:21,000
After the stimulus name comes the count of
trials that passed and failed.

2162
02:22:21,500 --> 02:22:26,050
A stimulus with no block passed all of its
trials, so the blocks are enough to count

2163
02:22:26,050 --> 02:22:27,350
trials for each variant.

2164
02:22:28,567 --> 02:22:33,517
The saved report, in the experiment's run
folder, gives the same counts another way.

2165
02:22:34,017 --> 02:22:38,286
When a stimulus runs more than once, its
row gains three columns, and in their

2166
02:22:38,286 --> 02:22:40,092
names, k is the number of trials.

2167
02:22:40,592 --> 02:22:44,711
This row is from the same run: one
stimulus, three trials, two of which

2168
02:22:44,711 --> 02:22:45,117
passed.

2169
02:22:45,617 --> 02:22:49,767
Pass rate is the count: trials that
passed, out of trials that finished.

2170
02:22:50,267 --> 02:22:54,569
The first, pass at k, asks whether at
least one of the k trials passed, so it

2171
02:22:54,569 --> 02:22:56,692
answers: can the agent do this at all?

2172
02:22:57,192 --> 02:23:00,242
Here one did, so it shows one hundred
percent.

2173
02:23:00,742 --> 02:23:05,407
The second, pass hat k, asks a harder
question: how likely is it that k trials

2174
02:23:05,407 --> 02:23:06,842
in a row would all pass?

2175
02:23:07,342 --> 02:23:11,770
Passing two times in three, three times
running, comes to just under thirty

2176
02:23:11,770 --> 02:23:12,242
percent.

2177
02:23:13,467 --> 02:23:17,367
So which column describes a skill that has
to work every time?

2178
02:23:17,867 --> 02:23:22,886
It's pass hat k, because a single passing
trial is enough to make pass at k show one

2179
02:23:22,886 --> 02:23:23,842
hundred percent.

2180
02:23:24,342 --> 02:23:28,614
If any trial ends in error, the stimulus
shows dashes in these columns, and its

2181
02:23:28,614 --> 02:23:31,642
finished trials are counted in the
terminal output only.

2182
02:23:32,142 --> 02:23:36,367
None of these columns gives a range, which
is what the calculator is for.

2183
02:23:37,567 --> 02:23:41,567
That's what the experiment prints; now
let's use it on a real edit.

2184
02:23:42,067 --> 02:23:45,842
Our judge found entries that still say how
a fix was coded.

2185
02:23:46,342 --> 02:23:51,042
Until now, the skill's second rule only
said to write for people who use the tool.

2186
02:23:51,542 --> 02:23:56,007
Our edit adds two lines to it: say what
they'll notice, and don't say how it was

2187
02:23:56,007 --> 02:23:56,342
coded.

2188
02:23:56,842 --> 02:24:01,359
It also names what to leave out: any
library, algorithm, data structure or

2189
02:24:01,359 --> 02:24:02,092
source file.

2190
02:24:02,592 --> 02:24:07,342
To compare fairly, we keep a copy of the
skill as it was, beside the edited one.

2191
02:24:08,567 --> 02:24:13,252
Each variant runs three stimuli three
times, so every count will be out of nine

2192
02:24:13,252 --> 02:24:13,667
trials.

2193
02:24:14,167 --> 02:24:18,605
What would you expect: will the count go
up on both models, and will nine trials be

2194
02:24:18,605 --> 02:24:19,567
enough to show it?

2195
02:24:22,067 --> 02:24:24,942
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

2196
02:24:26,167 --> 02:24:30,492
First, we copy the skill folder, so the
version before the edit is kept.

2197
02:24:34,974 --> 02:24:39,882
With the copy made, the new rule goes into
the skill itself, which is an edit to one

2198
02:24:39,882 --> 02:24:40,174
file.

2199
02:24:40,674 --> 02:24:44,249
Before spending anything, a dry run checks
the plan.

2200
02:24:44,749 --> 02:24:48,999
It starts no agent, and it should list
four plans, one for each variant.

2201
02:24:49,760 --> 02:24:53,882
It does: the list is longer than the
screen, and these are the last of the

2202
02:24:53,882 --> 02:24:54,160
four.

2203
02:25:04,367 --> 02:25:08,966
Now the experiment itself: both versions
of the skill, on both models, two trials

2204
02:25:08,966 --> 02:25:11,692
at a time, saved in a results folder of
its own.

2205
02:25:12,192 --> 02:25:16,367
It spends thirty-six agent runs, and
thirty-six calls to the judge.

2206
02:25:16,867 --> 02:25:21,149
We're after the table at the end: its
stimuli row counts, for each variant, the

2207
02:25:21,149 --> 02:25:23,317
stimuli that passed in all three trials.

2208
02:25:25,600 --> 02:25:29,922
Several minutes later, that row reads one
of three, then three of three, for haiku,

2209
02:25:29,922 --> 02:25:32,525
and zero of three, then three of three,
for terra.

2210
02:25:38,025 --> 02:25:42,861
Terra's zero isn't three failures: each of
its stimuli lost a trial to an error, and

2211
02:25:42,861 --> 02:25:44,875
an unfinished trial has no verdict.

2212
02:25:47,375 --> 02:25:50,950
Those are counts of stimuli, and a range
needs trials.

2213
02:25:54,167 --> 02:25:58,674
Above the table, the output has a block
for each stimulus that wasn't a clean

2214
02:25:58,674 --> 02:25:58,967
pass.

2215
02:25:59,467 --> 02:26:03,486
For each variant, we add up the trials
that passed over its three stimuli,

2216
02:26:03,486 --> 02:26:05,767
leaving out any trial that ended in error.

2217
02:26:06,267 --> 02:26:10,492
On haiku, five of nine passed before the
edit, and nine of nine after.

2218
02:26:10,992 --> 02:26:15,746
Terra passed five of six before, with
three trials lost to errors, and nine of

2219
02:26:15,746 --> 02:26:16,417
nine after.

2220
02:26:17,634 --> 02:26:21,084
Now the range around each count, from the
calculator.

2221
02:26:21,584 --> 02:26:26,441
For haiku, it's twenty-seven to eighty-one
percent before, and seventy to one hundred

2222
02:26:26,441 --> 02:26:26,784
after.

2223
02:26:27,284 --> 02:26:30,559
Those two ranges overlap, by eleven
points.

2224
02:26:31,059 --> 02:26:35,247
For terra, it's forty-four to
ninety-seven, and seventy to one hundred,

2225
02:26:35,247 --> 02:26:36,309
which overlap too.

2226
02:26:37,534 --> 02:26:42,449
The last step is to say what the result
supports, and no more, in one sentence per

2227
02:26:42,449 --> 02:26:42,809
model.

2228
02:26:43,309 --> 02:26:47,472
For haiku, ours says the judge's passes
went from five of nine to nine of nine,

2229
02:26:47,472 --> 02:26:51,109
and that these trials can't yet tell
whether the improvement is real.

2230
02:26:51,609 --> 02:26:55,777
For terra, it says they went from five of
six to nine of nine, and that these trials

2231
02:26:55,777 --> 02:26:58,159
can't tell whether the rule made any
difference.

2232
02:26:59,367 --> 02:27:02,292
So what should we make of those two
sentences?

2233
02:27:02,792 --> 02:27:07,242
On haiku, four trials failed before the
edit, three of them on one request.

2234
02:27:07,742 --> 02:27:10,042
After the edit, none failed.

2235
02:27:10,542 --> 02:27:13,467
Even so, the two ranges still overlap.

2236
02:27:13,967 --> 02:27:18,489
Nine trials a side are too few to pull
them apart, and more trials are the way to

2237
02:27:18,489 --> 02:27:18,992
find out.

2238
02:27:19,492 --> 02:27:23,701
On terra, only one finished trial failed
before the edit, so there was almost

2239
02:27:23,701 --> 02:27:26,817
nothing left to fix, and nothing these
trials could show.

2240
02:27:27,317 --> 02:27:31,529
Neither result is a failure of the edit:
both are honest statements of what a

2241
02:27:31,529 --> 02:27:33,717
handful of trials can and can't tell us.

2242
02:27:34,934 --> 02:27:38,459
Now let's apply the same honesty to the
earlier fixes.

2243
02:27:38,959 --> 02:27:43,064
When we added the rule for follow-up
requests, haiku's conversation test went

2244
02:27:43,064 --> 02:27:44,984
from two of three to three of three.

2245
02:27:45,484 --> 02:27:49,837
When we rewrote the skill's description,
the loading test had no failure left to

2246
02:27:49,837 --> 02:27:54,082
fix: terra passed all eleven trials that
finished before, and eleven of twelve

2247
02:27:54,082 --> 02:27:54,409
after.

2248
02:27:54,909 --> 02:27:58,509
In the calculator, the ranges overlap for
both fixes.

2249
02:27:59,009 --> 02:28:02,084
So those runs didn't prove that the fixes
worked.

2250
02:28:02,584 --> 02:28:06,534
What made the follow-up rule reasonable
was something else: a patch showed what

2251
02:28:06,534 --> 02:28:10,484
caused the failure, the rule addressed
exactly that cause, and the result moved

2252
02:28:10,484 --> 02:28:11,184
the right way.

2253
02:28:11,684 --> 02:28:15,815
That's a sound reason to keep a rule, but
it isn't the same as having measured it,

2254
02:28:15,815 --> 02:28:18,234
and it's worth knowing which of the two we
have.

2255
02:28:19,434 --> 02:28:23,599
Even our clearest early fix needs this
care: on terra, the grader for made-up

2256
02:28:23,599 --> 02:28:27,980
numbers went from one of three to three of
three, and alone, those ranges overlap

2257
02:28:27,980 --> 02:28:28,359
widely.

2258
02:28:28,859 --> 02:28:32,790
What makes that result strong is
everything around it: five of five trials

2259
02:28:32,790 --> 02:28:37,040
with the same failure before the fix, and
the same stimulus passing in every run

2260
02:28:37,040 --> 02:28:37,359
since.

2261
02:28:37,859 --> 02:28:41,659
The run with no skill at all showed that
the first draft caused it.

2262
02:28:42,159 --> 02:28:46,537
Several small results that agree, a cause
we can see in a patch, and a comparison

2263
02:28:46,537 --> 02:28:49,834
with no skill: that's what a result we can
defend looks like.

2264
02:28:51,034 --> 02:28:55,709
One more caution: every count here is only
as good as the grader that produced it.

2265
02:28:56,209 --> 02:29:00,295
If a grader passes work that's wrong, a
range around its count is a careful

2266
02:29:00,295 --> 02:29:01,984
measurement of the wrong thing.

2267
02:29:02,484 --> 02:29:06,703
So we test the graders first, with the
oracle and with saved trials, and only

2268
02:29:06,703 --> 02:29:07,909
then work out a range.

2269
02:29:09,134 --> 02:29:12,634
One row of the experiment's table hasn't
come up yet.

2270
02:29:13,134 --> 02:29:17,090
Beneath the counts are each variant's
tokens, added up over all of its trials;

2271
02:29:17,090 --> 02:29:20,134
they're the agent's tokens, and the
judge's aren't included.

2272
02:29:20,634 --> 02:29:24,773
In our run, the two models differ from
each other by more than the edit changed

2273
02:29:24,773 --> 02:29:25,559
either of them.

2274
02:29:26,059 --> 02:29:30,584
What that difference costs, for each trial
that passes, is a question for later.

2275
02:29:31,800 --> 02:29:35,425
We can now say what a before-and-after
result supports.

2276
02:29:35,925 --> 02:29:40,678
That same table also printed tokens for
each variant, and they differ by model: so

2277
02:29:40,678 --> 02:29:42,475
what does a passing trial cost?

2278
02:29:43,700 --> 02:29:48,403
Every comparison table so far has ended
with a row of tokens, and it's tempting to

2279
02:29:48,403 --> 02:29:51,500
read the variant with fewer tokens as the
cheaper one.

2280
02:29:52,000 --> 02:29:57,256
When we ran it, a typical trial on terra
used far fewer tokens than one on a newer

2281
02:29:57,256 --> 02:29:58,025
haiku model.

2282
02:29:58,525 --> 02:30:02,685
But each model has its own price per
token, and a cheap trial that fails has

2283
02:30:02,685 --> 02:30:03,725
bought you nothing.

2284
02:30:04,225 --> 02:30:08,492
Here, you'll run two models at two
settings, work out what each passing trial

2285
02:30:08,492 --> 02:30:09,600
costs, and pick one.

2286
02:30:10,800 --> 02:30:13,300
Let's start with the figure itself.

2287
02:30:13,800 --> 02:30:17,949
Cost per passing trial is a variant's
total cost, over all of its trials,

2288
02:30:17,949 --> 02:30:20,450
divided by the number of trials that
passed.

2289
02:30:20,950 --> 02:30:23,675
It's what one useful result costs you.

2290
02:30:24,900 --> 02:30:29,706
Here's a made-up pair of variants, priced
in AIU, which are cents: variant A costs

2291
02:30:29,706 --> 02:30:32,050
two AIU a trial, and passes five of ten.

2292
02:30:32,550 --> 02:30:36,575
Variant B costs three AIU a trial, and
passes all ten.

2293
02:30:37,075 --> 02:30:41,425
For A, that's twenty AIU for five passes:
four AIU each.

2294
02:30:41,925 --> 02:30:46,075
For B, thirty AIU buys ten passes: three
AIU each.

2295
02:30:46,575 --> 02:30:50,800
So the variant with the cheaper trial
gives the more expensive result.

2296
02:30:51,300 --> 02:30:54,500
Every failure is paid for, and then thrown
away.

2297
02:30:55,700 --> 02:30:59,825
With several variants, we put each one's
pass rate beside its cost.

2298
02:31:00,325 --> 02:31:04,584
Then we look for a variant that's beaten
on both counts: some other variant costs

2299
02:31:04,584 --> 02:31:06,425
less, and passes at least as often.

2300
02:31:06,925 --> 02:31:09,875
Any variant beaten that way can be
dropped.

2301
02:31:10,375 --> 02:31:14,850
Those left are real choices, a trade
between paying more and passing more.

2302
02:31:15,350 --> 02:31:18,375
Which to pick depends on what a failure
costs you.

2303
02:31:19,600 --> 02:31:24,145
This way of comparing comes from research
on agent benchmarks, which argued that

2304
02:31:24,145 --> 02:31:27,100
every evaluation should report cost beside
accuracy.

2305
02:31:27,600 --> 02:31:31,635
Its authors had found simple agents
matching the leaders of accuracy-only

2306
02:31:31,635 --> 02:31:33,625
rankings, at a fraction of the cost.

2307
02:31:34,833 --> 02:31:38,558
That's the figure; now let's see where its
two numbers are recorded.

2308
02:31:39,058 --> 02:31:44,108
Every experiment saves a folder for each
variant, and each folder holds its own

2309
02:31:44,108 --> 02:31:45,258
results dot jsonl.

2310
02:31:45,758 --> 02:31:50,822
Inside is one record per trial: the record
we've met before, with the variant's name

2311
02:31:50,822 --> 02:31:51,183
added.

2312
02:31:52,400 --> 02:31:55,250
Here's one of them, spread over several
lines.

2313
02:31:55,750 --> 02:31:59,050
Variant names the variant this trial
belongs to.

2314
02:31:59,550 --> 02:32:03,225
Status is success for a trial that
finished, as before.

2315
02:32:03,725 --> 02:32:08,250
And passed, under grade result, is the
verdict: passing trials say true here.

2316
02:32:09,467 --> 02:32:12,842
Further down, the token usage ends with
two blocks.

2317
02:32:13,342 --> 02:32:17,750
Next, by model splits the tokens between
models, and an agent that called two

2318
02:32:17,750 --> 02:32:19,067
models would list both.

2319
02:32:19,567 --> 02:32:22,592
Then the cost, in nano-AIU as before.

2320
02:32:23,092 --> 02:32:27,648
This trial's amount is about point two
three AIU, under a quarter of a cent, for

2321
02:32:27,648 --> 02:32:29,642
a hundred and five thousand tokens.

2322
02:32:30,142 --> 02:32:35,272
That cost appears only when every model
call in the trial reported Copilot as its

2323
02:32:35,272 --> 02:32:35,842
provider.

2324
02:32:37,067 --> 02:32:39,642
Two things are missing from that figure.

2325
02:32:40,142 --> 02:32:44,862
Calls that an LLM judge makes to grade the
trial aren't counted, so for an eval with

2326
02:32:44,862 --> 02:32:46,717
a judge, the true cost is higher.

2327
02:32:47,217 --> 02:32:51,812
Also, a trial that ended in ERROR before
the agent finished may have no usage

2328
02:32:51,812 --> 02:32:52,767
recorded at all.

2329
02:32:53,967 --> 02:32:57,317
Vally itself prints no total cost for a
variant.

2330
02:32:57,817 --> 02:33:02,487
Each experiment's table gives total
tokens, its report gives median tokens,

2331
02:33:02,487 --> 02:33:04,667
and neither turns tokens into cost.

2332
02:33:05,167 --> 02:33:09,619
So the course has a small calculator: a
page that reads the results files you

2333
02:33:09,619 --> 02:33:10,892
pick, in your browser.

2334
02:33:11,392 --> 02:33:15,647
For each variant, it adds up the cost of
every trial, counts the trials that

2335
02:33:15,647 --> 02:33:16,767
passed, and divides.

2336
02:33:17,967 --> 02:33:21,842
Besides the model, we'll vary one more
setting: reasoning effort.

2337
02:33:22,342 --> 02:33:26,842
Some models can work through a problem in
hidden reasoning, before they reply or

2338
02:33:26,842 --> 02:33:27,517
call a tool.

2339
02:33:28,017 --> 02:33:32,017
Reasoning effort sets how much of that the
agent's model does.

2340
02:33:32,517 --> 02:33:37,139
More effort usually means more tokens and
more time on each call, and sometimes

2341
02:33:37,139 --> 02:33:38,017
better results.

2342
02:33:39,233 --> 02:33:43,709
In an eval file, reasoning effort goes
under defaults, beside the model, so an

2343
02:33:43,709 --> 02:33:46,808
experiment can vary it exactly as it
varies the model.

2344
02:33:47,308 --> 02:33:50,408
Which values are accepted depends on the
model.

2345
02:33:50,908 --> 02:33:55,476
Vally refuses a value the model doesn't
accept before any session starts, and its

2346
02:33:55,476 --> 02:33:58,183
message lists the values that model does
accept.

2347
02:33:58,683 --> 02:34:03,479
So asking for a value that no model has is
a free way to see each model's list, and

2348
02:34:03,479 --> 02:34:04,808
that's how we'll begin.

2349
02:34:06,033 --> 02:34:08,608
Here's the experiment file we'll run.

2350
02:34:09,108 --> 02:34:13,765
It runs the changelog eval file: five
stimuli from earlier, each graded by code,

2351
02:34:13,765 --> 02:34:16,908
with no judge, so the recorded cost is the
whole cost.

2352
02:34:17,408 --> 02:34:21,858
Its variants may differ in the model and
in reasoning effort, and in nothing else.

2353
02:34:22,358 --> 02:34:26,812
There are three trials of each stimulus,
which makes fifteen trials per variant,

2354
02:34:26,812 --> 02:34:27,758
and sixty in all.

2355
02:34:28,258 --> 02:34:32,758
Then come the four variants: two models,
each at low effort and at high effort.

2356
02:34:33,258 --> 02:34:37,731
For haiku, the file names haiku five point
five, a newer model in the same family,

2357
02:34:37,731 --> 02:34:42,313
because the haiku model we've used so far
takes no effort setting, as the first step

2358
02:34:42,313 --> 02:34:42,858
will show.

2359
02:34:44,067 --> 02:34:47,542
That's the file; now let's run it and
price each variant.

2360
02:34:48,042 --> 02:34:52,524
We'll end with one sentence that names the
variant we'd choose, and the numbers

2361
02:34:52,524 --> 02:34:53,092
behind it.

2362
02:34:53,592 --> 02:34:57,592
Which of the four would you expect to cost
least for each passing trial?

2363
02:35:00,092 --> 02:35:02,967
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

2364
02:35:04,167 --> 02:35:08,462
First, the free check: we run the eval
file with the one typo-fix request, from

2365
02:35:08,462 --> 02:35:10,692
the start of the course, on three models.

2366
02:35:11,192 --> 02:35:16,115
Dash dash reasoning effort sets the effort
for every trial of a run, as dash dash

2367
02:35:16,115 --> 02:35:17,392
model sets the model.

2368
02:35:17,892 --> 02:35:21,867
We give it a value that no model accepts:
here, the word list.

2369
02:35:22,367 --> 02:35:26,742
Every trial should be rejected before it
starts, so nothing is spent.

2370
02:35:34,300 --> 02:35:38,887
Each trial ends in ERROR, as planned, and
each error names the efforts that its

2371
02:35:38,887 --> 02:35:39,700
model accepts.

2372
02:35:48,900 --> 02:35:53,500
That output runs past one screen, so
here's what it said, model by model.

2373
02:35:54,000 --> 02:35:58,379
Haiku four point five, the model we've
used so far, doesn't support reasoning

2374
02:35:58,379 --> 02:35:59,175
effort at all.

2375
02:35:59,675 --> 02:36:03,975
Haiku five point five accepts five values,
from low to max.

2376
02:36:04,475 --> 02:36:08,275
Terra accepts the same five, and also a
value named none.

2377
02:36:08,775 --> 02:36:12,675
Low and high are accepted by both, so
those are our two efforts.

2378
02:36:13,900 --> 02:36:17,325
Our experiment file is now in the evals
folder.

2379
02:36:17,825 --> 02:36:22,316
Before spending anything, we check the
plan with a dry run, which starts no

2380
02:36:22,316 --> 02:36:22,675
agent.

2381
02:36:27,340 --> 02:36:31,583
It prints a block for each of the four
variants, naming its model, and the last

2382
02:36:31,583 --> 02:36:32,765
of them are on screen.

2383
02:36:41,967 --> 02:36:46,646
Now the real run: the same command without
the dry run, with two workers, saved under

2384
02:36:46,646 --> 02:36:47,967
results, lesson sixteen.

2385
02:36:48,467 --> 02:36:51,217
It spends sixty short agent runs.

2386
02:36:51,717 --> 02:36:56,222
At the end, we're after the table, with
its row of stimuli passed and its row of

2387
02:36:56,222 --> 02:36:56,617
tokens.

2388
02:36:58,880 --> 02:37:03,809
Several minutes later, every variant shows
five of five stimuli passed, so Vally says

2389
02:37:03,809 --> 02:37:05,780
that no variant beat the baseline.

2390
02:37:10,280 --> 02:37:14,697
Under that is the row of total tokens,
where the two terra variants are lowest,

2391
02:37:14,697 --> 02:37:16,430
and nothing here mentions cost.

2392
02:37:19,930 --> 02:37:22,405
So let's look in the results folder.

2393
02:37:24,247 --> 02:37:28,714
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run, and inside it, as we saw, is a

2394
02:37:28,714 --> 02:37:30,022
folder for each variant.

2395
02:37:32,933 --> 02:37:37,333
We give those four results files to the
cost calculator, which reads them in the

2396
02:37:37,333 --> 02:37:39,258
browser and sends nothing anywhere.

2397
02:37:39,758 --> 02:37:43,957
The page shows a card for each variant,
with the trials, how many passed, how many

2398
02:37:43,957 --> 02:37:47,183
ended in ERROR, the total cost, and the
cost per passing trial.

2399
02:37:47,683 --> 02:37:52,477
Underneath is the bill for the whole
experiment: about ninety-one AIU, which is

2400
02:37:52,477 --> 02:37:53,508
ninety-one cents.

2401
02:37:54,733 --> 02:37:57,733
Here are the columns we need, side by
side.

2402
02:37:58,233 --> 02:38:01,608
All four variants passed fifteen of
fifteen trials.

2403
02:38:02,108 --> 02:38:06,708
At low effort, haiku cost about point two
one AIU for each passing trial.

2404
02:38:07,208 --> 02:38:11,158
At high effort, haiku passed no more
often, and cost a little more.

2405
02:38:11,658 --> 02:38:16,552
Both terra variants passed no more often,
and cost thirteen to fourteen times as

2406
02:38:16,552 --> 02:38:16,858
much.

2407
02:38:17,358 --> 02:38:20,533
So haiku low beats the other three on both
counts.

2408
02:38:21,033 --> 02:38:25,355
That gives us our sentence: on these five
stimuli, haiku at low effort passed all

2409
02:38:25,355 --> 02:38:29,784
fifteen trials at point two one AIU each,
against two point seven six and two point

2410
02:38:29,784 --> 02:38:32,558
eight three on terra, so we'd run them on
haiku low.

2411
02:38:35,767 --> 02:38:38,292
So what should we make of that table?

2412
02:38:38,792 --> 02:38:43,567
Every trial passed, so here, cost per
passing trial is simply cost per trial.

2413
02:38:44,067 --> 02:38:48,687
But the variants that cost the most by far
are the ones that used the fewest tokens:

2414
02:38:48,687 --> 02:38:50,392
tokens measure work, not price.

2415
02:38:51,600 --> 02:38:56,500
The cost of a token depends on the model,
even between two versions of one family.

2416
02:38:57,000 --> 02:39:01,816
Our very first haiku trial, on version
four point five, cost about two point four

2417
02:39:01,816 --> 02:39:04,075
AIU for every hundred thousand tokens.

2418
02:39:04,575 --> 02:39:09,596
This run's haiku five point five record
cost about point two two AIU for the same

2419
02:39:09,596 --> 02:39:10,650
number of tokens.

2420
02:39:11,150 --> 02:39:15,850
On our account, that makes the newer model
about eleven times cheaper per token.

2421
02:39:16,350 --> 02:39:20,699
So the cost we measure on one model can't
be carried over to another: change the

2422
02:39:20,699 --> 02:39:22,275
model, and you measure again.

2423
02:39:23,500 --> 02:39:27,425
Now the habit from before: how far can
these counts be trusted?

2424
02:39:27,925 --> 02:39:32,697
Fifteen passes in fifteen trials gives a
range of eighty to a hundred percent, and

2425
02:39:32,697 --> 02:39:34,675
every variant has that same range.

2426
02:39:35,175 --> 02:39:39,346
So these trials can't tell the variants
apart on pass rate, and here they don't

2427
02:39:39,346 --> 02:39:41,775
need to: the case for haiku low rests on
cost.

2428
02:39:42,275 --> 02:39:46,673
Each model's most expensive trial cost
between two and three times its cheapest,

2429
02:39:46,673 --> 02:39:51,015
but the two bands are well apart: every
haiku trial cost less than the cheapest

2430
02:39:51,015 --> 02:39:51,675
terra trial.

2431
02:39:52,175 --> 02:39:56,622
So the ranges make our sentence more
careful: haiku low is as good, as far as

2432
02:39:56,622 --> 02:39:58,875
these trials can tell, and far cheaper.

2433
02:40:00,100 --> 02:40:04,433
That statement is about these five
requests only, the ones the skill now

2434
02:40:04,433 --> 02:40:05,275
passes easily.

2435
02:40:05,775 --> 02:40:09,923
But the wording requests, the ones graded
by a judge, are where the models differed,

2436
02:40:09,923 --> 02:40:13,775
and they'd need pricing the same way
before changing the model for everything.

2437
02:40:14,275 --> 02:40:18,719
Prices change, too, and your account's may
differ from ours, so treat these numbers

2438
02:40:18,719 --> 02:40:21,825
as a method you can repeat, not as facts
about the models.

2439
02:40:23,033 --> 02:40:26,433
Let's put that in dollars, at one cent per
AIU.

2440
02:40:26,933 --> 02:40:30,758
This experiment's sixty trials came to
ninety-one cents.

2441
02:40:31,258 --> 02:40:36,433
One check after every edit, with these
five stimuli on two models, is ten trials.

2442
02:40:36,933 --> 02:40:40,785
On the two models from earlier in the
course, where our trials have averaged

2443
02:40:40,785 --> 02:40:44,586
about two point four AIU, that's
twenty-four cents an edit, or four dollars

2444
02:40:44,586 --> 02:40:46,208
eighty for twenty edits a month.

2445
02:40:46,708 --> 02:40:52,158
On haiku low alone, the same five stimuli
would cost about one AIU: a cent an edit.

2446
02:40:52,658 --> 02:40:56,709
Running every stimulus in the course each
night, about thirty trials, comes to

2447
02:40:56,709 --> 02:41:00,812
roughly twenty-one dollars over thirty
nights at that earlier average, plus the

2448
02:41:00,812 --> 02:41:02,733
judge calls that Vally doesn't count.

2449
02:41:03,933 --> 02:41:08,354
For scale, verifying this whole course
took about eleven dollars of agent runs,

2450
02:41:08,354 --> 02:41:11,208
plus judge calls, which isn't large for
one person.

2451
02:41:11,708 --> 02:41:15,915
But it grows with the stimuli, the trials,
the models, and how often you run, which

2452
02:41:15,915 --> 02:41:19,158
is why the cheapest check that answers the
question comes first.

2453
02:41:19,658 --> 02:41:23,947
Your plan's monthly allowance is spent
first, so much of this may cost you

2454
02:41:23,947 --> 02:41:24,758
nothing extra.

2455
02:41:25,967 --> 02:41:29,167
We can price the models a Copilot account
offers.

2456
02:41:29,667 --> 02:41:33,928
An app may call a model through a service
of its own, or one the team runs: can

2457
02:41:33,928 --> 02:41:37,542
Vally run that model too, and what does a
passing trial cost there?

2458
02:41:38,767 --> 02:41:43,237
Every trial so far has gone through our
Copilot account: Vally asked for a model

2459
02:41:43,237 --> 02:41:45,192
by name, and the account served it.

2460
02:41:45,692 --> 02:41:48,067
An app may not work that way.

2461
02:41:48,567 --> 02:41:52,615
Teams often call a model through a
provider they pay directly, or one they

2462
02:41:52,615 --> 02:41:55,842
run themselves, for reasons of price, data
rules, or speed.

2463
02:41:56,342 --> 02:42:00,359
Here, you'll point one variant of an
experiment at a model running on our own

2464
02:42:00,359 --> 02:42:02,967
computer, and work out what its trials
would cost.

2465
02:42:04,167 --> 02:42:06,542
Let's start with why that matters.

2466
02:42:07,042 --> 02:42:10,492
We've seen two models take the same skill
two ways.

2467
02:42:10,992 --> 02:42:15,492
Less obviously, one model served two ways
can behave differently too.

2468
02:42:15,992 --> 02:42:20,548
Whoever serves a model decides how it's
stored, and that's often compressed to fit

2469
02:42:20,548 --> 02:42:21,492
smaller machines.

2470
02:42:21,992 --> 02:42:26,623
They decide how the conversation, and the
agent's tool calls, are turned into the

2471
02:42:26,623 --> 02:42:27,767
text the model sees.

2472
02:42:28,267 --> 02:42:30,942
They also choose its default settings.

2473
02:42:31,442 --> 02:42:35,684
Each of those can change what the agent
does, so a result for the same model from

2474
02:42:35,684 --> 02:42:39,192
somewhere else says little about yours,
even when the name matches.

2475
02:42:40,400 --> 02:42:42,900
Here's a case from our own computer.

2476
02:42:43,400 --> 02:42:47,867
Another local model failed every trial
before it changed a file, because the

2477
02:42:47,867 --> 02:42:50,100
server refused the agent's tool calls.

2478
02:42:50,600 --> 02:42:54,150
That model may well be able to write a
changelog entry.

2479
02:42:54,650 --> 02:42:58,025
Only a run through that endpoint could
show the problem.

2480
02:42:58,525 --> 02:43:02,506
So we evaluate through the endpoint the
app calls, with the model name the app

2481
02:43:02,506 --> 02:43:05,925
sends, and we keep the stimuli, the
graders and the skill the same.

2482
02:43:07,134 --> 02:43:10,809
That's the reasoning; now for how Vally
reaches a model at all.

2483
02:43:11,309 --> 02:43:15,952
When Vally says executor, it means the
part of it that runs the agent for each

2484
02:43:15,952 --> 02:43:16,309
trial.

2485
02:43:16,809 --> 02:43:20,734
It starts a session, sends the stimulus,
and records what happened.

2486
02:43:21,234 --> 02:43:26,203
Unless a file says otherwise, Vally uses
its built-in executor, named copilot SDK,

2487
02:43:26,203 --> 02:43:29,234
which reaches models through your Copilot
account.

2488
02:43:29,734 --> 02:43:34,159
Having that executor call a provider you
choose, with your own credentials, is

2489
02:43:34,159 --> 02:43:35,634
called bring your own key.

2490
02:43:36,834 --> 02:43:40,709
The executor is chosen in an eval file,
under defaults.

2491
02:43:41,209 --> 02:43:45,359
And in an experiment, a variant can choose
it too, under its overrides.

2492
02:43:45,859 --> 02:43:49,801
Providers come in two kinds: hosted,
meaning a service you pay, or local,

2493
02:43:49,801 --> 02:43:53,959
meaning a program on your own computer;
this file is set up for a hosted one.

2494
02:43:54,459 --> 02:43:58,549
Here, the model line gives the name the
provider knows the model by, and Vally

2495
02:43:58,549 --> 02:44:00,909
doesn't check it against your account's
list.

2496
02:44:01,409 --> 02:44:06,045
The executor is still the built-in one;
only where it sends the model's requests

2497
02:44:06,045 --> 02:44:06,509
changes.

2498
02:44:07,009 --> 02:44:11,539
The executor's settings go in a block
named config, and here that's a single

2499
02:44:11,539 --> 02:44:12,434
provider block.

2500
02:44:13,634 --> 02:44:16,484
The provider block has three fields here.

2501
02:44:16,984 --> 02:44:21,784
Base URL is the provider's address, and
it's the only field that's required.

2502
02:44:22,284 --> 02:44:25,559
Type names the kind of interface the
provider speaks.

2503
02:44:26,059 --> 02:44:30,737
Its default is the interface that Open
AI's own servers use, and many other

2504
02:44:30,737 --> 02:44:32,484
servers copy that interface.

2505
02:44:32,984 --> 02:44:35,834
The last field is API key env.

2506
02:44:36,334 --> 02:44:40,884
It holds the name of an environment
variable, and that variable holds the key.

2507
02:44:42,100 --> 02:44:46,624
The key never goes in the file, because
eval files are shared, and kept with

2508
02:44:46,624 --> 02:44:47,100
results.

2509
02:44:47,600 --> 02:44:52,481
Vally enforces that: the provider block
has no field for a key, and lint rejects

2510
02:44:52,481 --> 02:44:52,725
one.

2511
02:44:53,225 --> 02:44:57,607
Vally takes the key from the environment
of the vally command, when each trial

2512
02:44:57,607 --> 02:44:58,000
starts.

2513
02:44:58,500 --> 02:45:03,160
If that variable isn't set, lint still
passes, but every trial stops with ERROR

2514
02:45:03,160 --> 02:45:04,575
before the agent starts.

2515
02:45:05,800 --> 02:45:08,900
A model on your own computer needs no key.

2516
02:45:09,400 --> 02:45:13,924
Ollama is a free program that downloads
open models, and serves them on your

2517
02:45:13,924 --> 02:45:14,400
machine.

2518
02:45:14,900 --> 02:45:19,634
It answers at a local address, with that
same Open AI interface, so a provider

2519
02:45:19,634 --> 02:45:22,000
block with only the base URL is enough.

2520
02:45:23,200 --> 02:45:26,025
One thing changes in what a trial saves.

2521
02:45:26,525 --> 02:45:30,175
Its tokens are counted as before, under
token usage.

2522
02:45:30,675 --> 02:45:35,289
Its cost is absent, because a cost is
recorded only when every call in the trial

2523
02:45:35,289 --> 02:45:36,500
went through Copilot.

2524
02:45:37,000 --> 02:45:41,617
So we price the tokens ourselves, at our
provider's rates, from the counts for each

2525
02:45:41,617 --> 02:45:41,950
model.

2526
02:45:42,450 --> 02:45:45,800
In this trial from our run, nothing came
from cache.

2527
02:45:46,300 --> 02:45:50,950
So on every turn, the whole conversation
was counted again as new input.

2528
02:45:51,450 --> 02:45:55,887
One more consequence: a judge still goes
through your Copilot account, because the

2529
02:45:55,887 --> 02:45:58,375
provider's credentials are for the agent
only.

2530
02:45:59,600 --> 02:46:04,178
Here's the experiment file from the cost
comparison, as it stands once a fifth

2531
02:46:04,178 --> 02:46:05,175
variant is added.

2532
02:46:05,675 --> 02:46:10,208
We've named the new variant qwen local,
and it runs a small open model, qwen three

2533
02:46:10,208 --> 02:46:11,700
point five, through Ollama.

2534
02:46:12,200 --> 02:46:16,050
It's one that can call the agent's tools,
and the one that worked for us.

2535
02:46:16,550 --> 02:46:20,800
The vary list has to name every setting
that differs between variants.

2536
02:46:21,300 --> 02:46:25,199
Three of this variant's settings differ,
so the list gains three paths: the

2537
02:46:25,199 --> 02:46:27,850
executor, the time limit, and the number
of trials.

2538
02:46:28,350 --> 02:46:32,773
If one were missing, say the executor
path, a dry run would stop and report

2539
02:46:32,773 --> 02:46:33,775
experiment drift.

2540
02:46:35,000 --> 02:46:38,275
Down at the end of the file is the variant
itself.

2541
02:46:38,775 --> 02:46:43,717
Because a local model is slow, its time
limit is nine minutes, instead of the eval

2542
02:46:43,717 --> 02:46:44,500
file's three.

2543
02:46:45,000 --> 02:46:49,396
It runs each stimulus once, where the
other variants run each one three times,

2544
02:46:49,396 --> 02:46:51,650
so its pass count rests on fewer trials.

2545
02:46:52,150 --> 02:46:55,400
Its provider block holds only Ollama's
address.

2546
02:46:56,600 --> 02:46:59,775
We'll run it with one worker, not our
usual two.

2547
02:47:00,275 --> 02:47:04,705
This local server answers one request at a
time, so with two workers each trial would

2548
02:47:04,705 --> 02:47:07,675
wait on the other, and could run out of
its nine minutes.

2549
02:47:08,900 --> 02:47:13,575
To price the tokens we need a price, and a
model on our own computer has none.

2550
02:47:14,075 --> 02:47:18,652
So we've made one up for the arithmetic:
ten cents per million input tokens, and

2551
02:47:18,652 --> 02:47:20,825
forty cents per million output tokens.

2552
02:47:21,325 --> 02:47:25,985
This rate isn't any provider's price;
yours are on your provider's pricing page,

2553
02:47:25,985 --> 02:47:27,150
or in your contract.

2554
02:47:28,367 --> 02:47:30,767
That's the file; now let's run it.

2555
02:47:31,267 --> 02:47:35,888
We'll check Ollama, check the plan, run
only the new variant, and then price its

2556
02:47:35,888 --> 02:47:36,292
tokens.

2557
02:47:36,792 --> 02:47:41,267
On these five stimuli, the account's
variants passed every trial that finished.

2558
02:47:41,767 --> 02:47:46,572
How many would you expect a small local
model to pass, and what would each passing

2559
02:47:46,572 --> 02:47:47,217
trial cost?

2560
02:47:49,717 --> 02:47:52,592
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

2561
02:47:53,800 --> 02:47:58,777
First, Ollama itself: older versions can't
run this model, so we check for zero point

2562
02:47:58,777 --> 02:48:00,475
seventeen point one or later.

2563
02:48:02,380 --> 02:48:04,180
Ours is newer.

2564
02:48:04,680 --> 02:48:09,445
Our model is a download of about six point
six gigabytes, which we already have, so

2565
02:48:09,445 --> 02:48:10,880
we list what's installed.

2566
02:48:12,287 --> 02:48:16,613
There's the line for qwen three point
five; Ollama serves it while its app is

2567
02:48:16,613 --> 02:48:17,062
running.

2568
02:48:20,267 --> 02:48:24,542
The variant is now in the experiment file,
exactly as we saw it.

2569
02:48:25,042 --> 02:48:28,642
Next, a dry run, which checks the plan and
starts nothing.

2570
02:48:29,142 --> 02:48:32,242
We're looking for a last block headed qwen
local.

2571
02:48:33,507 --> 02:48:36,682
There it is, the fifth plan, with our
model's name.

2572
02:48:45,900 --> 02:48:50,799
Now the run: dash dash variant runs one
named variant, so the earlier results stay

2573
02:48:50,799 --> 02:48:51,575
as they were.

2574
02:48:52,075 --> 02:48:55,625
We give it one worker, and a results
folder of its own.

2575
02:48:56,125 --> 02:49:00,506
That's one trial of each of the five
stimuli, all on our computer, so it spends

2576
02:49:00,506 --> 02:49:02,225
nothing on the Copilot account.

2577
02:49:04,514 --> 02:49:09,164
About a quarter of an hour later, the
table has one column: qwen local passed

2578
02:49:09,164 --> 02:49:09,889
two of five.

2579
02:49:14,389 --> 02:49:18,739
The stimuli that failed are listed further
up, and we'll go through them shortly.

2580
02:49:21,239 --> 02:49:24,539
The run is saved in a folder named with
the time.

2581
02:49:26,347 --> 02:49:27,722
There it is.

2582
02:49:30,667 --> 02:49:34,167
One line in that output looks like a
problem, and isn't.

2583
02:49:34,667 --> 02:49:39,042
It says the comparison is unavailable,
because the baseline wasn't run.

2584
02:49:39,542 --> 02:49:44,067
With dash dash variant, only qwen local
ran, so this run has nothing from the

2585
02:49:44,067 --> 02:49:46,417
baseline, haiku low, to compare it with.

2586
02:49:46,917 --> 02:49:51,892
The earlier results sit in a different run
folder, so we'll make that comparison by

2587
02:49:51,892 --> 02:49:52,192
hand.

2588
02:49:53,400 --> 02:49:58,112
The course's cost calculator takes this
variant's results file, just as it took

2589
02:49:58,112 --> 02:49:59,125
the earlier ones.

2590
02:49:59,625 --> 02:50:04,325
The calculator counts five trials, two of
them passing, and none ending in ERROR.

2591
02:50:04,825 --> 02:50:09,250
Every cost is a dash, because no cost was
recorded for any trial.

2592
02:50:12,467 --> 02:50:14,967
So we add up the tokens ourselves.

2593
02:50:15,467 --> 02:50:20,447
Each record in the results file holds that
trial's input and output tokens for each

2594
02:50:20,447 --> 02:50:21,467
model, as we saw.

2595
02:50:21,967 --> 02:50:26,491
Across the five trials, that's about three
hundred and sixty-five thousand input

2596
02:50:26,491 --> 02:50:29,092
tokens, and under four thousand output
tokens.

2597
02:50:33,300 --> 02:50:36,750
Each total is priced at its rate per
million tokens.

2598
02:50:37,250 --> 02:50:41,757
Nearly all of the cost is input: about
three and a half cents, against a fraction

2599
02:50:41,757 --> 02:50:42,925
of a cent for output.

2600
02:50:43,425 --> 02:50:47,275
Together, the five trials would cost about
three point eight cents.

2601
02:50:47,775 --> 02:50:52,550
Only two of them passed, so each passing
trial cost about one point nine cents.

2602
02:50:53,050 --> 02:50:57,447
For comparison, haiku low came to about a
fifth of a cent per passing trial, and

2603
02:50:57,447 --> 02:50:59,700
terra low to about two point eight cents.

2604
02:51:02,900 --> 02:51:05,225
So what changed, and what didn't?

2605
02:51:05,725 --> 02:51:07,900
A good deal carried over.

2606
02:51:08,400 --> 02:51:13,429
The same eval file, stimuli, graders and
skill ran, and the results had the shape

2607
02:51:13,429 --> 02:51:13,925
we know.

2608
02:51:14,425 --> 02:51:18,591
Changing the provider was a few lines in
one variant, and that's the payoff of

2609
02:51:18,591 --> 02:51:20,300
evaluating through the endpoint.

2610
02:51:21,500 --> 02:51:23,850
The results did not carry over.

2611
02:51:24,350 --> 02:51:28,816
This model passed two of five, where the
account's variants passed every trial that

2612
02:51:28,816 --> 02:51:29,300
finished.

2613
02:51:29,800 --> 02:51:33,825
Its failures are ones we've met before:
the security fix filed under Fixed, an

2614
02:51:33,825 --> 02:51:37,902
entry for the internal refactor, and,
after a release, an entry under a version

2615
02:51:37,902 --> 02:51:40,275
number it made up, with no Unreleased
heading.

2616
02:51:40,775 --> 02:51:45,075
The saved trials show why: the skill was
in the workspace, but only one of the five

2617
02:51:45,075 --> 02:51:47,975
trials loaded it, and none of the three
that failed did.

2618
02:51:48,475 --> 02:51:52,650
So the question of whether the skill loads
comes back with each new model.

2619
02:51:53,867 --> 02:51:57,267
Is two of five too few trials to say
anything?

2620
02:51:57,767 --> 02:52:02,142
Two passes in five gives a range of twelve
to seventy-seven percent.

2621
02:52:02,642 --> 02:52:06,367
Haiku low's fifteen in fifteen gives
eighty to a hundred.

2622
02:52:06,867 --> 02:52:11,636
These ranges don't overlap, so even five
trials show this model doing worse on

2623
02:52:11,636 --> 02:52:12,492
these stimuli.

2624
02:52:12,992 --> 02:52:17,705
A count that low is enough to say worse;
saying by how much would take many more

2625
02:52:17,705 --> 02:52:18,117
trials.

2626
02:52:19,334 --> 02:52:21,584
Cost didn't carry over either.

2627
02:52:22,084 --> 02:52:26,306
Run locally, the tokens cost nothing,
though each trial took minutes, and used

2628
02:52:26,306 --> 02:52:28,309
the computer's memory the whole time.

2629
02:52:28,809 --> 02:52:33,580
Priced at our made-up rate, each passing
trial cost about nine times what haiku

2630
02:52:33,580 --> 02:52:34,184
low's did.

2631
02:52:34,684 --> 02:52:37,184
It was still cheaper than terra low's.

2632
02:52:37,684 --> 02:52:41,838
Two things drove that, and neither is the
price per token: three of five trials

2633
02:52:41,838 --> 02:52:45,993
failed, and with nothing from cache, every
turn paid for the whole conversation

2634
02:52:45,993 --> 02:52:46,309
again.

2635
02:52:46,809 --> 02:52:50,909
So a low price per token is not a low cost
per passing trial.

2636
02:52:52,134 --> 02:52:56,785
Scope matters here: this is one model,
served one way, on one computer, with a

2637
02:52:56,785 --> 02:52:57,859
price we invented.

2638
02:52:58,359 --> 02:53:02,770
From a hosted provider, the same model
might load the skill, cache its input, or

2639
02:53:02,770 --> 02:53:03,984
be priced differently.

2640
02:53:04,484 --> 02:53:08,705
What carries over is the method: run the
endpoint your app calls, count the tokens,

2641
02:53:08,705 --> 02:53:11,909
price them at your own rates, and divide
by the passing trials.

2642
02:53:13,134 --> 02:53:17,714
We can now price any model, including one
we run ourselves, where a single trial

2643
02:53:17,714 --> 02:53:18,459
took minutes.

2644
02:53:18,959 --> 02:53:23,783
One trial that loops can cost or take many
times the typical one, so how do we fail,

2645
02:53:23,783 --> 02:53:25,909
or stop, a trial that takes too much?

2646
02:53:27,133 --> 02:53:31,108
Every grader we've written so far asks
whether the work is right.

2647
02:53:31,608 --> 02:53:33,758
None of them asks what it took.

2648
02:53:34,258 --> 02:53:38,605
If a trial writes the right entry after
reading the same file forty times, it

2649
02:53:38,605 --> 02:53:41,033
passes them all, and we pay for every
call.

2650
02:53:41,533 --> 02:53:45,693
One trial that loops can cost ten times
the usual, and a run every night

2651
02:53:45,693 --> 02:53:47,658
multiplies whatever slips through.

2652
02:53:48,158 --> 02:53:52,472
Here, you'll put limits on what a trial
may use, and test each limit on trials

2653
02:53:52,472 --> 02:53:53,633
you've already saved.

2654
02:53:54,833 --> 02:53:57,183
Let's start with what a budget is.

2655
02:53:57,683 --> 02:54:02,188
When we say budget, we mean a limit on
what one trial may use: tokens, time,

2656
02:54:02,188 --> 02:54:03,433
turns, or tool calls.

2657
02:54:03,933 --> 02:54:07,583
Written as a grader, it fails any trial
that goes over.

2658
02:54:08,800 --> 02:54:11,175
It catches two kinds of trouble.

2659
02:54:11,675 --> 02:54:15,325
One is a trial that loops: a single run
gone wrong.

2660
02:54:15,825 --> 02:54:20,717
The second is a cost regression: an edit
to the skill after which every trial takes

2661
02:54:20,717 --> 02:54:22,250
more tokens, or more time.

2662
02:54:22,750 --> 02:54:25,675
No grader of the result would notice that.

2663
02:54:26,900 --> 02:54:31,425
Guessed budgets tend to be wrong in one of
the two ways a grader can be wrong.

2664
02:54:31,925 --> 02:54:34,275
Too low, and it fails good work.

2665
02:54:34,775 --> 02:54:37,200
Too high, and it can never fail.

2666
02:54:37,700 --> 02:54:42,452
So we set it from trials we've measured,
and a saved run is a free test of whatever

2667
02:54:42,452 --> 02:54:43,025
we choose.

2668
02:54:44,233 --> 02:54:48,333
One more thing: a budget stays beside a
check that the work was done.

2669
02:54:48,833 --> 02:54:53,233
Giving up after one turn keeps a trial
well inside every budget.

2670
02:54:53,733 --> 02:54:58,083
So the budget says not too much, and only
the other graders say it was right.

2671
02:54:59,300 --> 02:55:02,625
That's the idea; here are the graders that
express it.

2672
02:55:03,125 --> 02:55:07,541
Every trial record ends with a metrics
block, and four built-in graders each

2673
02:55:07,541 --> 02:55:09,750
compare one of its numbers with a max.

2674
02:55:10,250 --> 02:55:13,950
Token budget counts input and output
tokens together.

2675
02:55:14,450 --> 02:55:17,225
Turn count counts the model's replies.

2676
02:55:17,725 --> 02:55:21,100
Wall time is the agent's working time, by
the clock.

2677
02:55:21,600 --> 02:55:25,275
Fourth, tool call count counts the tool
calls a trial makes.

2678
02:55:26,500 --> 02:55:30,450
That word, turn, needs some care, because
it has had another meaning.

2679
02:55:30,950 --> 02:55:36,198
In a conversation, a turn was one request;
here, it's one reply from the model inside

2680
02:55:36,198 --> 02:55:37,125
a single trial.

2681
02:55:37,625 --> 02:55:42,415
Even with one request, a trial takes
several replies, and turn count counts the

2682
02:55:42,415 --> 02:55:42,900
replies.

2683
02:55:44,100 --> 02:55:48,625
Here's how they look in the eval file, at
the end of one stimulus's graders.

2684
02:55:49,125 --> 02:55:54,175
Each takes one setting, max, and passes
when the trial's number is at or under it.

2685
02:55:54,675 --> 02:55:57,100
Times are written with their unit.

2686
02:55:57,600 --> 02:56:00,775
None of the three has a name, and that's
on purpose.

2687
02:56:01,275 --> 02:56:05,912
Grader names must be unique across the
whole file, and these three go under all

2688
02:56:05,912 --> 02:56:06,675
five stimuli.

2689
02:56:07,175 --> 02:56:11,250
Left unnamed, each one gets a name from
its type and its limit.

2690
02:56:12,466 --> 02:56:15,991
These graders work only from what the
trial record keeps.

2691
02:56:16,491 --> 02:56:21,053
So a re-grade with vally grade judges them
exactly as a real run would, unlike a

2692
02:56:21,053 --> 02:56:23,391
script grader, which needs the workspace.

2693
02:56:23,891 --> 02:56:27,491
That makes a budget cheap to test, with no
agent run.

2694
02:56:28,700 --> 02:56:31,125
What happens just over the line?

2695
02:56:31,625 --> 02:56:36,172
Once a trial is over the max, the grader's
score drops only as far as the trial went

2696
02:56:36,172 --> 02:56:39,475
over, so a trial that is barely over still
scores almost one.

2697
02:56:39,975 --> 02:56:44,590
Its verdict doesn't soften: the grader
fails, and since every grader must pass,

2698
02:56:44,590 --> 02:56:45,525
the trial fails.

2699
02:56:46,733 --> 02:56:51,217
Budget graders judge a trial after it has
finished, when the tokens are already

2700
02:56:51,217 --> 02:56:51,558
spent.

2701
02:56:52,058 --> 02:56:56,483
To stop an agent while it's working, a
stimulus takes a constraints block.

2702
02:56:56,983 --> 02:57:01,203
It sits under one stimulus; there's no
version for the whole file, so each

2703
02:57:01,203 --> 02:57:02,458
stimulus gets its own.

2704
02:57:02,958 --> 02:57:06,508
Max agent duration is how long the agent
may work.

2705
02:57:07,008 --> 02:57:11,463
At that limit, Vally stops it, and then
grades whatever it had done by then, like

2706
02:57:11,463 --> 02:57:12,508
any finished trial.

2707
02:57:13,008 --> 02:57:16,883
In the trial record, the end reason then
says agent timeout.

2708
02:57:18,100 --> 02:57:20,575
Three limits can go in that block.

2709
02:57:21,075 --> 02:57:25,411
Max agent duration stops the agent and
grades its partial work, which suits an

2710
02:57:25,411 --> 02:57:28,025
unfinished attempt that is still worth
grading.

2711
02:57:28,525 --> 02:57:33,376
Max duration is a hard limit: a trial that
reaches it is stopped, and counted as a

2712
02:57:33,376 --> 02:57:33,850
failure.

2713
02:57:34,350 --> 02:57:38,844
It does for one stimulus what the timeout
in the file's defaults does for all of

2714
02:57:38,844 --> 02:57:39,125
them.

2715
02:57:39,625 --> 02:57:44,460
If a stimulus sets both, the agent's limit
has to be the shorter one; otherwise the

2716
02:57:44,460 --> 02:57:46,325
hard limit ends the trial first.

2717
02:57:46,825 --> 02:57:51,050
Max turns is documented as the most turns
a trial may take.

2718
02:57:51,550 --> 02:57:55,773
Vally's docs don't say what happens at
that limit, so we count turns with the

2719
02:57:55,773 --> 02:57:57,200
turn count grader instead.

2720
02:57:58,400 --> 02:58:00,950
That's how they work; now let's set some.

2721
02:58:01,450 --> 02:58:06,277
First, we find out what a typical trial
used in the cost experiment we already

2722
02:58:06,277 --> 02:58:06,525
ran.

2723
02:58:07,025 --> 02:58:10,937
Then we set budgets just above that, lint
the file, and test them on that

2724
02:58:10,937 --> 02:58:14,475
experiment's saved trials with vally
grade, which starts no agent.

2725
02:58:14,975 --> 02:58:19,722
After that we adjust them, add a time
limit, and run the five stimuli once, with

2726
02:58:19,722 --> 02:58:20,375
vally eval.

2727
02:58:20,875 --> 02:58:25,810
With budgets just above the typical trial,
how many good trials would you expect them

2728
02:58:25,810 --> 02:58:26,275
to fail?

2729
02:58:28,775 --> 02:58:31,650
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

2730
02:58:32,866 --> 02:58:37,426
The experiment's report has a table for
each variant, with the median tokens,

2731
02:58:37,426 --> 02:58:39,616
turns and duration of every stimulus.

2732
02:58:40,116 --> 02:58:44,425
Across all four variants, we take the
largest value in each of those three

2733
02:58:44,425 --> 02:58:44,891
columns.

2734
02:58:45,391 --> 02:58:49,778
In our run, that's a little over one
hundred six thousand tokens, five turns,

2735
02:58:49,778 --> 02:58:51,316
and about thirteen seconds.

2736
02:58:54,533 --> 02:58:59,099
We add the three budget graders to the end
of every stimulus's graders, with those

2737
02:58:59,099 --> 02:59:00,658
numbers rounded up a little.

2738
02:59:01,158 --> 02:59:06,033
Ours are one hundred seven thousand
tokens, five turns, and thirteen seconds.

2739
02:59:09,233 --> 02:59:12,133
First, we lint the file, which costs
nothing.

2740
02:59:15,366 --> 02:59:19,687
No errors, and one warning we've met: with
several graders and no scoring block,

2741
02:59:19,687 --> 02:59:22,441
every grader must pass, which is what we
want here.

2742
02:59:28,100 --> 02:59:32,957
Now the free test: we change into the cost
experiment's run folder, and then into one

2743
02:59:32,957 --> 02:59:34,100
variant, haiku high.

2744
02:59:35,560 --> 02:59:40,053
This is the re-grade we've used before:
the eval file is four folders up, the run

2745
02:59:40,053 --> 02:59:43,160
dir is this folder, and the saved results
are the input.

2746
02:59:43,660 --> 02:59:48,288
Script graders can't be re-graded, so two
of the stimuli will show a failing line

2747
02:59:48,288 --> 02:59:49,260
whatever happens.

2748
02:59:49,760 --> 02:59:53,335
What we're looking for is a failing line
that names a budget.

2749
02:59:55,806 --> 03:00:00,219
Fifteen blocks come back, more than fits
on a screen, and one of them, further up,

2750
03:00:00,219 --> 03:00:01,456
names the token budget.

2751
03:00:06,666 --> 03:00:09,641
Here's that block, as the re-grade printed
it.

2752
03:00:10,141 --> 03:00:14,566
One two changes trial went over the token
budget, by about one percent.

2753
03:00:15,066 --> 03:00:19,516
Its other failing line is the script
grader, which a re-grade can't run.

2754
03:00:20,016 --> 03:00:23,896
In an earlier run, a trial went
twenty-eight tokens over, still printed a

2755
03:00:23,896 --> 03:00:26,766
score of one hundred percent, and failed
all the same.

2756
03:00:29,966 --> 03:00:33,616
Now the same test on the other model's
variant, terra high.

2757
03:00:35,266 --> 03:00:38,716
It's the same command, on that variant's
saved trials.

2758
03:00:42,506 --> 03:00:47,144
This time it's the turn count budget: two
trials each took one turn more than the

2759
03:00:47,144 --> 03:00:47,831
max of five.

2760
03:00:52,331 --> 03:00:55,206
No trial went over the wall time budget.

2761
03:00:58,433 --> 03:01:01,333
So which is wrong: the budgets, or the
trials?

2762
03:01:01,833 --> 03:01:06,123
For each trial a budget failed, we ask
whether it did the work, and whether it

2763
03:01:06,123 --> 03:01:06,508
looped.

2764
03:01:07,008 --> 03:01:11,458
All three of these trials passed when the
experiment ran, and none of them looped.

2765
03:01:11,958 --> 03:01:16,552
The token failure was about one percent
over, and the two turn failures took one

2766
03:01:16,552 --> 03:01:17,758
turn more than usual.

2767
03:01:18,258 --> 03:01:22,782
So the budgets were wrong, not the trials:
set just above typical, they failed three

2768
03:01:22,782 --> 03:01:24,883
good trials of the thirty we re-graded.

2769
03:01:28,100 --> 03:01:32,696
Good trials vary from one to the next, and
a budget has to leave room for that, so we

2770
03:01:32,696 --> 03:01:35,725
raise each one to about twice the largest
typical value.

2771
03:01:36,225 --> 03:01:41,275
Ours become two hundred fifteen thousand
tokens, ten turns, and thirty seconds.

2772
03:01:44,500 --> 03:01:47,600
Then the same re-grade again, here in
terra high.

2773
03:01:54,193 --> 03:01:59,068
Only the script graders fail now, as they
did before; no block names a budget.

2774
03:02:06,873 --> 03:02:09,123
Once more in haiku high.

2775
03:02:15,720 --> 03:02:17,495
It's the same there.

2776
03:02:28,186 --> 03:02:31,986
That's the free testing done, so we go
back to the practice folder.

2777
03:02:34,900 --> 03:02:38,999
Under the first budgets, three trials
failed a budget; under the wider ones,

2778
03:02:38,999 --> 03:02:40,725
every budget passes every trial.

2779
03:02:41,225 --> 03:02:43,700
No agent ran to find that out.

2780
03:02:44,900 --> 03:02:49,192
Last, the time limit: the constraints
block from earlier is now under each of

2781
03:02:49,192 --> 03:02:52,425
the five stimuli, with a max agent
duration of one minute.

2782
03:02:55,466 --> 03:02:57,566
Lint has no errors.

2783
03:03:03,066 --> 03:03:07,861
Now one run of the five stimuli on both
models, with the budgets and the limit in

2784
03:03:07,861 --> 03:03:08,216
place.

2785
03:03:08,716 --> 03:03:11,366
It spends ten short agent runs.

2786
03:03:11,866 --> 03:03:15,241
What we're hoping for is five of five
under each model.

2787
03:03:18,900 --> 03:03:23,456
About a minute later, that's what both
models show: every trial passed, budgets

2788
03:03:23,456 --> 03:03:23,975
included.

2789
03:03:33,200 --> 03:03:37,661
During that run, the largest trial used
about seventy-six thousand tokens and five

2790
03:03:37,661 --> 03:03:40,600
turns, and the slowest took twelve and a
half seconds.

2791
03:03:41,100 --> 03:03:45,570
So every trial stayed well inside the
budgets, and the one-minute limit never

2792
03:03:45,570 --> 03:03:46,325
came into it.

2793
03:03:49,533 --> 03:03:51,583
So what did the limits catch?

2794
03:03:52,083 --> 03:03:56,750
Our first budgets, set just above the
typical trial, failed three good trials of

2795
03:03:56,750 --> 03:03:57,158
thirty.

2796
03:03:57,658 --> 03:04:02,185
That's a grader failing correct work, and
the saved trials caught it before any

2797
03:04:02,185 --> 03:04:02,758
agent ran.

2798
03:04:03,258 --> 03:04:08,277
It happened because a typical trial is a
middle value: half of all trials are above

2799
03:04:08,277 --> 03:04:08,458
it.

2800
03:04:08,958 --> 03:04:12,483
Terra took four, five or six turns for the
same request.

2801
03:04:12,983 --> 03:04:16,383
So a budget at the median fails normal
variation.

2802
03:04:17,600 --> 03:04:22,324
Budgets are for the trial that runs away,
at several times the usual cost, so they

2803
03:04:22,324 --> 03:04:23,650
need room above normal.

2804
03:04:24,150 --> 03:04:29,025
Twice the largest typical trial still
fails a loop that costs ten times as much.

2805
03:04:29,525 --> 03:04:33,025
It passes a trial that took one turn more
than usual.

2806
03:04:33,525 --> 03:04:37,668
Whenever the model, the effort or the
skill changes, the budgets get tested

2807
03:04:37,668 --> 03:04:39,050
again, the same free way.

2808
03:04:40,266 --> 03:04:43,391
It's worth seeing what a token budget
doesn't do.

2809
03:04:43,891 --> 03:04:48,591
One budget holds for every model the eval
runs on, and the models differ.

2810
03:04:49,091 --> 03:04:53,450
Across our runs, a typical trial used
about a hundred and five thousand tokens

2811
03:04:53,450 --> 03:04:57,752
on haiku five point five, seventy-five
thousand on haiku four point five, and

2812
03:04:57,752 --> 03:04:59,316
sixty-one thousand on terra.

2813
03:04:59,816 --> 03:05:03,716
So a budget set for the hungriest model is
loose for the others.

2814
03:05:04,216 --> 03:05:08,290
Tokens aren't price, either, because a
token costs a different amount on each

2815
03:05:08,290 --> 03:05:12,416
model: in the cost experiment, terra used
the fewest tokens and cost the most.

2816
03:05:12,916 --> 03:05:17,701
Token budgets catch a trial that loops;
choosing a cheaper model is what cost per

2817
03:05:17,701 --> 03:05:18,941
passing trial is for.

2818
03:05:20,166 --> 03:05:24,788
Our time limit did nothing in that run,
which is what a limit should do on a good

2819
03:05:24,788 --> 03:05:25,016
day.

2820
03:05:25,516 --> 03:05:28,241
It's there for the day an agent stalls.

2821
03:05:28,741 --> 03:05:32,838
Then a nightly run loses one minute on
that trial, where the file's timeout would

2822
03:05:32,838 --> 03:05:35,316
lose three, and it still grades what was
written.

2823
03:05:36,534 --> 03:05:39,084
Budgets now guard every trial.

2824
03:05:39,584 --> 03:05:44,352
Before relying on an edit, we want a check
that says no worse than before: can Vally

2825
03:05:44,352 --> 03:05:45,034
decide that?

2826
03:05:46,233 --> 03:05:50,719
After one edit to the skill, the passing
trials on one model went from five of nine

2827
03:05:50,719 --> 03:05:53,583
to nine of nine, and the two ranges still
overlapped.

2828
03:05:54,083 --> 03:05:57,683
So those trials couldn't yet say the edit
made things better.

2829
03:05:58,183 --> 03:06:02,565
Before every change we keep, there's a
narrower question: is it any worse than

2830
03:06:02,565 --> 03:06:02,958
before?

2831
03:06:03,458 --> 03:06:07,911
Here, you'll judge two saved runs side by
side, and run a check that's built to fail

2832
03:06:07,911 --> 03:06:09,608
when a change made things worse.

2833
03:06:10,833 --> 03:06:13,708
Let's start with what our graders can't
see.

2834
03:06:14,208 --> 03:06:18,608
Each grader takes one trial at a time, and
holds it against a fixed check.

2835
03:06:19,108 --> 03:06:23,453
That can't answer a question like: is the
new skill's entry better than the old

2836
03:06:23,453 --> 03:06:23,783
one's?

2837
03:06:24,283 --> 03:06:28,390
Here are two entries for the same request,
and the graders passed both, so the

2838
03:06:28,390 --> 03:06:30,233
difference between them disappears.

2839
03:06:30,733 --> 03:06:35,483
A one-to-five scale has the same limit,
since it places each entry by itself.

2840
03:06:36,700 --> 03:06:39,300
A pairwise judge works differently.

2841
03:06:39,800 --> 03:06:43,981
It's an LLM judge that's shown two answers
to the same request, one from each

2842
03:06:43,981 --> 03:06:46,750
version, and says which is better, and by
how much.

2843
03:06:47,250 --> 03:06:52,041
Comparing two answers side by side is
easier than scoring either one alone, for

2844
03:06:52,041 --> 03:06:53,375
people and for models.

2845
03:06:53,875 --> 03:06:57,475
That's why a pairwise judge notices
smaller changes.

2846
03:06:58,700 --> 03:07:01,125
It has one well-known weakness.

2847
03:07:01,625 --> 03:07:06,785
Shown two answers, a judge tends to prefer
one position, often the first, whatever is

2848
03:07:06,785 --> 03:07:07,150
in it.

2849
03:07:07,650 --> 03:07:11,600
So Vally asks twice, with the two answers
in the opposite order.

2850
03:07:12,100 --> 03:07:15,450
When both orders agree, the pair gets that
verdict.

2851
03:07:15,950 --> 03:07:19,000
When they disagree, the pair is scored as
a tie.

2852
03:07:20,200 --> 03:07:23,225
A pairwise judge doesn't replace graders.

2853
03:07:23,725 --> 03:07:27,800
It can't say that both versions are wrong,
only which one is less wrong.

2854
03:07:28,300 --> 03:07:31,350
So graders stay for the question: is it
right?

2855
03:07:31,850 --> 03:07:35,775
The side-by-side judgement is for the
other one: is it worse?

2856
03:07:37,000 --> 03:07:39,900
That's the idea; here's the command that
does it.

2857
03:07:40,400 --> 03:07:45,083
Vally compare runs a pairwise judge over
two sets of saved trials, and adds up the

2858
03:07:45,083 --> 03:07:47,025
result, without starting an agent.

2859
03:07:47,525 --> 03:07:52,006
Its baseline is the version we compare
against, and Vally's word for the version

2860
03:07:52,006 --> 03:07:53,575
we changed is the treatment.

2861
03:07:54,075 --> 03:07:57,943
Here, both are variants of our
before-and-after experiment: one model,

2862
03:07:57,943 --> 03:08:00,375
with the skill before the edit and after
it.

2863
03:08:00,875 --> 03:08:05,166
Next, the judge has to be named with dash
dash judge model, because this command

2864
03:08:05,166 --> 03:08:09,350
doesn't take it from the eval file, and
its default may be a model under test.

2865
03:08:09,850 --> 03:08:14,025
Finally, dash dash output names a file for
a record of every pair.

2866
03:08:15,233 --> 03:08:19,108
Vally compare can also be given a whole
experiment folder.

2867
03:08:19,608 --> 03:08:23,743
Then the baseline is the one the
experiment file names, here haiku before,

2868
03:08:23,743 --> 03:08:26,258
and every other variant is judged against
it.

2869
03:08:26,758 --> 03:08:31,232
That would set the terra variants against
haiku, and mix a change of model into a

2870
03:08:31,232 --> 03:08:32,558
question about the edit.

2871
03:08:33,058 --> 03:08:37,008
So we'll name two variant folders instead,
one model at a time.

2872
03:08:37,508 --> 03:08:42,008
Each holds its own results dot jsonl,
which is all compare needs.

2873
03:08:43,233 --> 03:08:48,326
Vally pairs the trials by stimulus name
and trial number: trial zero with trial

2874
03:08:48,326 --> 03:08:49,358
zero, and so on.

2875
03:08:49,858 --> 03:08:53,873
For each pair, the judge works from the
rubric saved with the trials, and gives a

2876
03:08:53,873 --> 03:08:55,558
verdict from the treatment's side.

2877
03:08:56,058 --> 03:09:00,458
Much better scores plus one, and slightly
better, plus zero point four.

2878
03:09:00,958 --> 03:09:02,683
Ties score zero.

2879
03:09:03,183 --> 03:09:06,233
The worse verdicts mirror those, below
zero.

2880
03:09:06,733 --> 03:09:10,158
Each pair costs two judge calls, one for
each order.

2881
03:09:11,366 --> 03:09:15,491
Here's what compare printed in an earlier
run, on other saved trials.

2882
03:09:15,991 --> 03:09:20,591
First, the win rate is the share of pairs
that the treatment won: six of nine here.

2883
03:09:21,091 --> 03:09:25,066
Ties count against it, so a win rate isn't
the same as not worse.

2884
03:09:25,566 --> 03:09:29,316
Next is the mean of the nine scores, with
a range around it.

2885
03:09:29,816 --> 03:09:34,157
That range is a confidence interval: the
kind we put around a count, here around a

2886
03:09:34,157 --> 03:09:36,591
mean, and the true mean is probably inside
it.

2887
03:09:37,091 --> 03:09:39,716
This one lies wholly above zero.

2888
03:09:40,933 --> 03:09:44,583
Below that is a second test, and it
ignores the judge.

2889
03:09:45,083 --> 03:09:49,160
This test looks only at the graders' pass
or fail, and counts the pairs that

2890
03:09:49,160 --> 03:09:52,433
flipped: five went from fail to pass, and
none the other way.

2891
03:09:52,933 --> 03:09:57,251
You can see the test's name on that line,
and it asks whether a split like five to

2892
03:09:57,251 --> 03:09:58,883
nothing could easily be chance.

2893
03:09:59,383 --> 03:10:04,333
From chance alone, a split that lopsided
turns up about six times in a hundred.

2894
03:10:04,833 --> 03:10:09,508
The usual bar for calling a result
significant is under five in a hundred.

2895
03:10:10,008 --> 03:10:12,558
So this one is not significant.

2896
03:10:13,058 --> 03:10:16,897
Last come the metric changes: the
treatment minus the baseline for each

2897
03:10:16,897 --> 03:10:19,708
measure, averaged over the pairs, each
with a range.

2898
03:10:20,208 --> 03:10:24,233
The words high variance mean the pairs
differed a lot from one another.

2899
03:10:25,433 --> 03:10:30,358
One option turns compare into a regression
check: dash dash fail on regression.

2900
03:10:30,858 --> 03:10:35,308
With it, the command exits one when the
treatment is significantly worse.

2901
03:10:35,808 --> 03:10:40,633
That means its mean score is below zero,
and its whole range is below zero too.

2902
03:10:41,133 --> 03:10:45,233
So a negative mean isn't enough if the
range reaches above zero.

2903
03:10:45,733 --> 03:10:50,276
Nothing else counts: a rise in tokens
doesn't change the exit code, and neither

2904
03:10:50,276 --> 03:10:51,483
does the second test.

2905
03:10:51,983 --> 03:10:56,231
Apart from that, with or without the
option, compare exits one when some pair

2906
03:10:56,231 --> 03:10:58,383
couldn't be judged, even after retries.

2907
03:10:58,883 --> 03:11:03,310
To print the exit code, there's echo
dollar question mark, typed straight after

2908
03:11:03,310 --> 03:11:03,983
the command.

2909
03:11:05,200 --> 03:11:09,059
That's how compare works; now let's run it
on the saved trials of our

2910
03:11:09,059 --> 03:11:10,625
before-and-after experiment.

2911
03:11:11,125 --> 03:11:14,850
First, the before skill against the after
skill, on haiku.

2912
03:11:15,350 --> 03:11:18,700
Then the same on terra, with the
regression check on.

2913
03:11:19,200 --> 03:11:23,577
Last, haiku again with the two roles
swapped, so the before skill becomes the

2914
03:11:23,577 --> 03:11:24,600
change under test.

2915
03:11:25,100 --> 03:11:28,250
Which of the three would you expect the
check to fail?

2916
03:11:30,750 --> 03:11:33,625
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

2917
03:11:34,833 --> 03:11:39,083
All three run from inside the experiment's
folder, so we change into it.

2918
03:11:41,533 --> 03:11:43,333
Let's see what it holds.

2919
03:11:44,160 --> 03:11:48,310
There's one folder for each variant,
beside the experiment's report.

2920
03:11:52,533 --> 03:11:56,872
First, haiku: the before skill as the
baseline, and the after skill as the

2921
03:11:56,872 --> 03:11:57,458
treatment.

2922
03:11:57,958 --> 03:12:01,783
It spends eighteen judge calls, two for
each of nine pairs.

2923
03:12:02,283 --> 03:12:05,958
We're after the mean score, and whether
its range clears zero.

2924
03:12:09,420 --> 03:12:13,967
About two minutes later, the treatment has
won four of nine pairs, and the mean is

2925
03:12:13,967 --> 03:12:15,520
plus zero point three three.

2926
03:12:20,020 --> 03:12:25,163
Its range runs from minus zero point zero
eight to plus zero point seven five, so it

2927
03:12:25,163 --> 03:12:26,020
includes zero.

2928
03:12:29,520 --> 03:12:34,139
On tokens, the range is wholly below zero:
the after skill used about fourteen

2929
03:12:34,139 --> 03:12:35,620
thousand fewer per trial.

2930
03:12:39,833 --> 03:12:44,414
The same command also wrote its record:
one line of JSON, spread out here so we

2931
03:12:44,414 --> 03:12:45,458
can go through it.

2932
03:12:45,958 --> 03:12:49,833
This pair is the dates request, trial zero
from each version.

2933
03:12:50,333 --> 03:12:55,109
The pairwise judge found the after skill's
entry slightly worse, for a score of minus

2934
03:12:55,109 --> 03:12:56,008
zero point four.

2935
03:12:56,508 --> 03:13:00,999
Its reason is kept too: the before entry
adds an example date, which makes the

2936
03:13:00,999 --> 03:13:02,208
change more concrete.

2937
03:13:05,708 --> 03:13:09,133
On this same pair, both entries had passed
their graders.

2938
03:13:09,633 --> 03:13:13,883
In another pair, the two orders agreed on
the winner but not on how much, so the

2939
03:13:13,883 --> 03:13:15,583
record keeps the weaker verdict.

2940
03:13:18,800 --> 03:13:23,400
Now the second model, with the regression
check on, and a record file of its own.

2941
03:13:23,900 --> 03:13:28,154
On the result, there are two things to
look at: the mean score's range, and the

2942
03:13:28,154 --> 03:13:28,800
tokens line.

2943
03:13:37,120 --> 03:13:41,420
This time only six pairs were judged:
three of the before trials did not finish,

2944
03:13:41,420 --> 03:13:44,270
so their partners were left out, as the
warning says.

2945
03:13:48,770 --> 03:13:53,381
The judged score barely moved: three of
the six are ties, and the range includes

2946
03:13:53,381 --> 03:13:53,670
zero.

2947
03:13:57,170 --> 03:14:02,020
The tokens fell, by about eight thousand
per trial, with a range wholly below zero.

2948
03:14:05,520 --> 03:14:07,395
So did the check fail?

2949
03:14:08,473 --> 03:14:11,973
Exit code zero: no regression in the
judged score.

2950
03:14:15,200 --> 03:14:19,303
To see whether the check fails, we swap
the two roles: the after skill is the

2951
03:14:19,303 --> 03:14:22,500
baseline now, and the before skill is the
change under test.

2952
03:14:23,000 --> 03:14:27,473
A consistent judge would print the first
comparison's mean with its sign flipped:

2953
03:14:27,473 --> 03:14:29,075
minus zero point three three.

2954
03:14:33,973 --> 03:14:38,198
This one printed exactly that, and its
range reaches above zero.

2955
03:14:42,698 --> 03:14:45,148
So what does the check make of that?

2956
03:14:46,273 --> 03:14:50,973
Exit code zero: the check passed, because
the range isn't wholly below zero.

2957
03:14:54,200 --> 03:14:56,725
Here are all three, side by side.

2958
03:14:57,225 --> 03:14:59,950
So which of the three did the check fail?

2959
03:15:00,450 --> 03:15:02,425
None of them, in our run.

2960
03:15:02,925 --> 03:15:07,965
The swapped comparison came closest: its
mean is below zero, but its range crosses

2961
03:15:07,965 --> 03:15:08,150
it.

2962
03:15:11,366 --> 03:15:13,341
So what does this support?

2963
03:15:13,841 --> 03:15:18,383
Counting passes on haiku gave two ranges
that overlapped, so the counts couldn't

2964
03:15:18,383 --> 03:15:18,666
tell.

2965
03:15:19,166 --> 03:15:23,631
The second test says the same: four pairs
flipped from fail to pass, none flipped

2966
03:15:23,631 --> 03:15:26,166
back, and it still calls that not
significant.

2967
03:15:26,666 --> 03:15:31,577
Its number is coin-toss odds: four tosses
all landing the same way happen one time

2968
03:15:31,577 --> 03:15:32,116
in eight.

2969
03:15:32,616 --> 03:15:37,063
The pairwise judge leans the same way,
with a mean above zero, but its range

2970
03:15:37,063 --> 03:15:38,116
includes zero too.

2971
03:15:38,616 --> 03:15:43,326
So in our run, all three agree: the edit
looks better, and these trials can't yet

2972
03:15:43,326 --> 03:15:43,791
show it.

2973
03:15:44,291 --> 03:15:48,568
An earlier run, on other saved trials,
went further: there the pairwise range was

2974
03:15:48,568 --> 03:15:51,366
wholly above zero, while the counts still
overlapped.

2975
03:15:51,866 --> 03:15:56,104
A pairwise judge can do that, because it
compares the two entries of each pair

2976
03:15:56,104 --> 03:15:59,091
directly, and sees differences that pass
or fail can't.

2977
03:16:00,300 --> 03:16:02,975
Hold that loosely, for three reasons.

2978
03:16:03,475 --> 03:16:07,283
First, the nine pairs aren't nine
independent requests: they're three

2979
03:16:07,283 --> 03:16:09,050
stimuli, tried three times each.

2980
03:16:09,550 --> 03:16:13,200
So the range looks narrower than the
evidence really allows.

2981
03:16:13,700 --> 03:16:17,961
Next, the judge is a model, and this
pairwise judge hasn't been checked against

2982
03:16:17,961 --> 03:16:20,550
our own labels, the way the yes-or-no
judge was.

2983
03:16:21,050 --> 03:16:23,975
Third, it varies from one run to the next.

2984
03:16:24,475 --> 03:16:28,437
Two earlier runs of the swapped
comparison, both on the same saved trials,

2985
03:16:28,437 --> 03:16:32,400
landed on both sides of the line: one
failed the check, and one passed it.

2986
03:16:32,900 --> 03:16:37,050
Our own run passed it too, with a range
that reaches above zero.

2987
03:16:37,550 --> 03:16:42,100
So trust the sign, and whether the range
crosses zero, not the second decimal.

2988
03:16:43,300 --> 03:16:46,550
Now the other half of the question: worse
in cost.

2989
03:16:47,050 --> 03:16:51,500
In our run, the edit made nothing cost
more: tokens fell on both models.

2990
03:16:52,000 --> 03:16:56,657
In an earlier run, on other saved trials,
the after skill took about eleven thousand

2991
03:16:56,657 --> 03:17:00,927
more tokens per trial on terra, with a
range wholly above zero, and the check

2992
03:17:00,927 --> 03:17:01,925
still exited zero.

2993
03:17:02,425 --> 03:17:06,025
That's by design: the check looks at the
judged score alone.

2994
03:17:06,525 --> 03:17:10,953
So cost needs a guard of its own: the
budget graders, with their limits set from

2995
03:17:10,953 --> 03:17:11,950
the before trials.

2996
03:17:12,450 --> 03:17:17,000
Then an edit that costs more fails a
trial, like any other grader's failure.

2997
03:17:18,200 --> 03:17:22,357
One last piece of how this works: an
experiment can run the same judging as

2998
03:17:22,357 --> 03:17:24,575
soon as it ends, with dash dash compare.

2999
03:17:25,075 --> 03:17:29,575
That option only reports: a regression it
finds doesn't fail the command.

3000
03:17:30,075 --> 03:17:34,840
Vally's own guide gives the order: run the
experiment, then run compare on its folder

3001
03:17:34,840 --> 03:17:37,700
with fail on regression, as the step that
can fail.

3002
03:17:38,900 --> 03:17:42,966
There are many moving parts now, and runs
sometimes end in error, or fail for

3003
03:17:42,966 --> 03:17:44,550
reasons that aren't the skill.

3004
03:17:45,050 --> 03:17:47,875
When a run goes wrong, where do we look
first?

3005
03:17:49,100 --> 03:17:53,229
Now that our eval files have setup,
budgets, judges and several models, more

3006
03:17:53,229 --> 03:17:56,000
can go wrong that has nothing to do with
the skill.

3007
03:17:56,500 --> 03:18:01,085
When a run ends in ERROR, or comes out
flaky, or fails when nobody expected it

3008
03:18:01,085 --> 03:18:03,025
to, running it again is tempting.

3009
03:18:03,525 --> 03:18:08,113
But a run costs agent runs and minutes,
and it usually prints the same error

3010
03:18:08,113 --> 03:18:08,475
again.

3011
03:18:08,975 --> 03:18:13,268
Here, you'll take four broken eval files,
find each fault from what the failed run

3012
03:18:13,268 --> 03:18:16,200
left behind, and prove each fix with the
cheapest check.

3013
03:18:17,400 --> 03:18:19,875
Let's start with the order to look in.

3014
03:18:20,375 --> 03:18:24,582
Vally's debugging guide splits the work
into four questions, and each has to be

3015
03:18:24,582 --> 03:18:26,925
answered before the next one means
anything.

3016
03:18:27,425 --> 03:18:31,425
First: is the eval file valid, so that
Vally accepts it at all?

3017
03:18:31,925 --> 03:18:36,000
Second: did it complete, so that every
trial finished and was graded?

3018
03:18:36,500 --> 03:18:40,525
Third: why was the result unexpected, when
the trial did finish?

3019
03:18:41,025 --> 03:18:43,000
Fourth: does the fix work?

3020
03:18:43,500 --> 03:18:48,244
Graders can't be wrong about a trial that
didn't finish, and a trial can't finish if

3021
03:18:48,244 --> 03:18:49,600
the eval file is broken.

3022
03:18:50,800 --> 03:18:55,026
That order is also an order of cost: the
first three are answered from what the

3023
03:18:55,026 --> 03:18:57,700
failed run already printed and saved, for
nothing.

3024
03:18:58,200 --> 03:19:02,425
Only the fourth may need a new run, and
that depends on what the fix changed.

3025
03:19:03,633 --> 03:19:08,494
Fixing a grader changes nothing the agent
did, so the saved trials can be graded

3026
03:19:08,494 --> 03:19:08,858
again.

3027
03:19:09,358 --> 03:19:14,058
Fixing the setup, or a time limit, changes
the run itself, so it needs a new run, and

3028
03:19:14,058 --> 03:19:16,158
often one model is enough to prove it.

3029
03:19:17,367 --> 03:19:21,742
Those are the questions; here's where each
answer is kept, starting with the first.

3030
03:19:22,242 --> 03:19:26,556
Vally lint reads an eval file without
starting anything, and reports unknown

3031
03:19:26,556 --> 03:19:29,792
field names, missing files, and values of
the wrong kind.

3032
03:19:30,292 --> 03:19:34,217
Vally eval runs the same checks before it
starts any agent.

3033
03:19:34,717 --> 03:19:39,442
What neither can see is what a value means
to the program that will use it later.

3034
03:19:40,667 --> 03:19:44,592
One of our four files uses a field we
haven't met: commands.

3035
03:19:45,092 --> 03:19:49,361
It's a list, under the agent environment,
of shell commands that Vally runs in the

3036
03:19:49,361 --> 03:19:52,642
workspace, after copying the files and
before the agent starts.

3037
03:19:53,142 --> 03:19:56,442
Each entry is one command, written as a
string.

3038
03:19:56,942 --> 03:20:01,142
Whatever the commands create is part of
the starting state, so it doesn't appear

3039
03:20:01,142 --> 03:20:02,192
in the trial's diff.

3040
03:20:03,400 --> 03:20:06,375
There's a trap here, and it's in YAML
itself.

3041
03:20:06,875 --> 03:20:11,685
Inside a list item, a colon followed by a
space turns the line into a key and a

3042
03:20:11,685 --> 03:20:12,050
value.

3043
03:20:12,550 --> 03:20:17,150
So this entry isn't a string: it's a small
mapping, with echo note as its key.

3044
03:20:17,650 --> 03:20:21,675
Lint accepts it, because a list item is
allowed to hold a mapping.

3045
03:20:22,175 --> 03:20:26,464
Whatever runs the command then gets a
mapping where it expects a string, and

3046
03:20:26,464 --> 03:20:27,875
says so in its own words.

3047
03:20:28,375 --> 03:20:31,650
Putting the whole line in quotes makes it
a string again.

3048
03:20:32,867 --> 03:20:36,017
Question two is whether each trial
completed.

3049
03:20:36,517 --> 03:20:41,161
Each trial that didn't is reported as
ERROR, with one line of explanation, and

3050
03:20:41,161 --> 03:20:45,092
the same text is saved in the trial's
record in results dot jsonl.

3051
03:20:45,592 --> 03:20:49,792
The trial's own folder shows how far it
got, by which files exist.

3052
03:20:50,292 --> 03:20:55,161
With only metadata dot json, no agent
session started, so the failure came

3053
03:20:55,161 --> 03:20:56,017
during setup.

3054
03:20:56,517 --> 03:21:00,959
With a session log, events dot jsonl, but
no patch, the agent started and was

3055
03:21:00,959 --> 03:21:02,517
stopped before it finished.

3056
03:21:03,017 --> 03:21:07,219
With both, the agent finished, and
anything wrong is in the grading or in the

3057
03:21:07,219 --> 03:21:07,492
work.

3058
03:21:08,700 --> 03:21:13,971
That session log, events dot jsonl, is the
agent's own raw record, one event per

3059
03:21:13,971 --> 03:21:14,300
line.

3060
03:21:14,800 --> 03:21:19,224
It matters here because, when a trial is
stopped, it's the one place that shows how

3061
03:21:19,224 --> 03:21:21,250
far the agent got, and what that cost.

3062
03:21:21,750 --> 03:21:25,350
Here's how the log ends when a trial runs
out of time.

3063
03:21:25,850 --> 03:21:28,225
This line is the skill loading.

3064
03:21:28,725 --> 03:21:33,432
This one's type is abort, and its reason
says the stop came from outside the agent:

3065
03:21:33,432 --> 03:21:34,850
here, Vally's time limit.

3066
03:21:35,350 --> 03:21:40,308
This last one is a usage checkpoint: what
the session had cost so far, about three

3067
03:21:40,308 --> 03:21:40,550
AIU.

3068
03:21:41,050 --> 03:21:44,100
So a trial that ends in ERROR isn't free.

3069
03:21:45,300 --> 03:21:49,725
Question three is for a trial that
finished with a verdict we didn't expect.

3070
03:21:50,225 --> 03:21:54,694
We start with the grader's evidence, the
line Vally prints after each failing

3071
03:21:54,694 --> 03:21:55,100
grader.

3072
03:21:55,600 --> 03:21:59,736
Then we go through the trial's patch
ourselves, and decide who is wrong: the

3073
03:21:59,736 --> 03:22:00,825
agent or the grader.

3074
03:22:01,325 --> 03:22:05,679
One flag helps here, dash dash verbose:
under each failing trial, it adds the

3075
03:22:05,679 --> 03:22:09,525
number of turns, tool calls and tokens,
and the agent's final reply.

3076
03:22:10,025 --> 03:22:14,798
Trials stopped mid-session have no final
reply, so verbose adds nothing to a

3077
03:22:14,798 --> 03:22:15,300
timeout.

3078
03:22:16,500 --> 03:22:19,725
Graders can also fail to reach a verdict
at all.

3079
03:22:20,225 --> 03:22:25,361
An LLM judge is a call to another model,
and that call can fail; Vally doesn't try

3080
03:22:25,361 --> 03:22:25,925
it again.

3081
03:22:26,425 --> 03:22:31,088
In the trial's record, the grader then has
a status of error, which a grader that

3082
03:22:31,088 --> 03:22:32,700
simply said no doesn't have.

3083
03:22:33,200 --> 03:22:36,950
Its score is recorded as zero, although
nothing was judged.

3084
03:22:37,450 --> 03:22:40,550
The reason is in its evidence, word for
word.

3085
03:22:41,050 --> 03:22:45,627
In the run's output, the stimulus is
reported as ERROR, with a line saying its

3086
03:22:45,627 --> 03:22:46,800
verdict isn't valid.

3087
03:22:48,000 --> 03:22:52,162
Question four is whether the fix works,
and vally grade, which re-grades the

3088
03:22:52,162 --> 03:22:54,900
trials a run saved, is usually the
cheapest proof.

3089
03:22:55,400 --> 03:22:59,653
Diff graders work from the saved diffs,
and trajectory graders from the saved

3090
03:22:59,653 --> 03:23:02,525
trajectories, so re-grading with them
costs nothing.

3091
03:23:03,025 --> 03:23:07,775
Re-grading with a judge costs one judge
call per trial, and no agent runs.

3092
03:23:08,275 --> 03:23:12,556
File and script graders need the
workspace, which a saved run doesn't keep,

3093
03:23:12,556 --> 03:23:14,725
so with them a re-grade ends in ERROR.

3094
03:23:15,225 --> 03:23:19,647
Changes to anything before or during the
agent's session, such as the setup, the

3095
03:23:19,647 --> 03:23:22,300
time limit or the skill, only show in a
new run.

3096
03:23:23,500 --> 03:23:26,950
That's where the answers are kept; now
let's put them to work.

3097
03:23:27,450 --> 03:23:31,575
Our starter folder has four eval files,
each with one fault.

3098
03:23:32,075 --> 03:23:36,225
For each one, we run it once, answer the
four questions from what the run left, fix

3099
03:23:36,225 --> 03:23:38,525
it, and prove the fix with the cheapest
check.

3100
03:23:39,025 --> 03:23:42,100
Each file's first run goes wrong on
purpose.

3101
03:23:42,600 --> 03:23:46,150
Which of the four fixes would you expect
to need a new run?

3102
03:23:48,650 --> 03:23:51,525
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

3103
03:23:52,733 --> 03:23:56,708
First, we copy the four broken files into
our practice folder.

3104
03:24:00,253 --> 03:24:03,078
Question one: is the first file valid?

3105
03:24:06,793 --> 03:24:08,643
And lint says it is.

3106
03:24:11,143 --> 03:24:13,368
Question two: does it complete?

3107
03:24:13,868 --> 03:24:18,518
This starts one trial on each model, and
here we expect both to end in ERROR.

3108
03:24:22,780 --> 03:24:27,523
Both did, for the same reason: a command
had to be a string, and Vally was handed a

3109
03:24:27,523 --> 03:24:27,980
mapping.

3110
03:24:38,200 --> 03:24:42,175
Let's see how far those trials got,
starting with what the run saved.

3111
03:24:44,000 --> 03:24:48,175
There's one folder, named with the time of
the run, and we change into it.

3112
03:24:50,660 --> 03:24:54,760
Inside, each trial has a folder of its
own; this one is haiku's.

3113
03:24:58,107 --> 03:25:03,407
It holds metadata dot json and nothing
else, so no agent session ever started.

3114
03:25:06,907 --> 03:25:09,857
So the fault is in the setup, not in the
skill.

3115
03:25:12,767 --> 03:25:16,217
That brings us to question three: what did
the setup do?

3116
03:25:16,717 --> 03:25:19,442
Here's the file, with its commands entry.

3117
03:25:19,942 --> 03:25:24,782
It's the trap we saw, a colon and a space
after the word fix, and the same bad value

3118
03:25:24,782 --> 03:25:26,742
tripped Vally's report writer too.

3119
03:25:27,967 --> 03:25:31,942
Our fix is to put the whole command in
quotes, so it's one string.

3120
03:25:32,442 --> 03:25:36,868
That changes the setup, which happens
before the agent starts, so proving it

3121
03:25:36,868 --> 03:25:37,567
takes a run.

3122
03:25:38,767 --> 03:25:43,617
One model is enough to show that the setup
works, so this spends one agent run.

3123
03:25:54,067 --> 03:25:57,267
About ten seconds later, the stimulus
passes.

3124
03:26:02,467 --> 03:26:06,709
Our second file is called quick; it passes
lint as well, so we go straight to

3125
03:26:06,709 --> 03:26:08,417
question two: does it complete?

3126
03:26:08,917 --> 03:26:12,442
With verbose added, we'll see whatever
detail there is.

3127
03:26:12,942 --> 03:26:17,592
This file is set up to run out of time, so
we expect ERROR on both models.

3128
03:26:20,467 --> 03:26:24,538
Both trials were stopped: the stimulus
timed out after five thousand

3129
03:26:24,538 --> 03:26:25,317
milliseconds.

3130
03:26:29,817 --> 03:26:34,817
The verbose flag added nothing, because
neither agent had a final reply to show.

3131
03:26:38,033 --> 03:26:42,133
Again we go into the folder this run
saved, and look at one trial.

3132
03:26:44,800 --> 03:26:49,013
This time there's a session log, events
dot jsonl, and no patch: the agent

3133
03:26:49,013 --> 03:26:50,550
started, and didn't finish.

3134
03:26:54,767 --> 03:26:59,184
Terra's session log ends the same way: the
skill loads, then the abort, then the

3135
03:26:59,184 --> 03:26:59,792
checkpoint.

3136
03:27:00,292 --> 03:27:04,695
What's new is the bill: about three AIU
for this trial, and about three quarters

3137
03:27:04,695 --> 03:27:06,292
of one for the other model's.

3138
03:27:06,792 --> 03:27:09,967
That was spent on two trials that produced
nothing.

3139
03:27:11,167 --> 03:27:14,067
Question three: why did they run out of
time?

3140
03:27:14,567 --> 03:27:19,348
Under defaults, the file sets a timeout of
five seconds, and that's the hard limit on

3141
03:27:19,348 --> 03:27:19,967
each trial.

3142
03:27:20,467 --> 03:27:24,772
When we ran this same request earlier,
with budgets, its two trials took about

3143
03:27:24,772 --> 03:27:26,042
eight and nine seconds.

3144
03:27:26,542 --> 03:27:31,318
So nothing is wrong with the skill or the
agent: five seconds just isn't enough for

3145
03:27:31,318 --> 03:27:32,067
this request.

3146
03:27:33,267 --> 03:27:37,242
We change it to three minutes, the value
in our other eval files.

3147
03:27:37,742 --> 03:27:42,017
This timeout is different from the max
agent duration we used before.

3148
03:27:42,517 --> 03:27:46,467
That one stops only the agent, and still
grades what it had done.

3149
03:27:46,967 --> 03:27:51,417
Nothing saved can show a trial finishing,
so this fix needs a run too.

3150
03:27:52,633 --> 03:27:55,658
That's two agent runs, one for each model.

3151
03:28:06,500 --> 03:28:09,725
This time both trials finish, and both
pass.

3152
03:28:17,933 --> 03:28:22,669
Our third file is called entry; it passes
lint too, and we run it with verbose

3153
03:28:22,669 --> 03:28:23,033
again.

3154
03:28:23,533 --> 03:28:26,308
Here we expect both models to fail.

3155
03:28:35,027 --> 03:28:39,381
Both failed the same grader, adds an
entry: its pattern wasn't found in the

3156
03:28:39,381 --> 03:28:40,252
workspace diff.

3157
03:28:49,467 --> 03:28:53,067
Further up, verbose printed each agent's
final reply.

3158
03:28:53,567 --> 03:28:57,617
Haiku says it added the typo fix to the
changelog, under Fixed.

3159
03:28:58,117 --> 03:29:00,267
Terra says much the same.

3160
03:29:00,767 --> 03:29:04,967
So either both agents are wrong about
their own work, or the grader is.

3161
03:29:06,167 --> 03:29:10,842
The patch settles question three, so we go
into this run's folder and print haiku's.

3162
03:29:14,480 --> 03:29:19,380
There's an added Fixed heading, and under
it an added entry that mentions notes add.

3163
03:29:23,880 --> 03:29:27,680
That's a correct entry, so the grader is
the one that's wrong.

3164
03:29:30,600 --> 03:29:32,925
Here's the grader, with its pattern.

3165
03:29:33,425 --> 03:29:37,737
It's missing the switch that makes a caret
match at the start of every line, so this

3166
03:29:37,737 --> 03:29:40,150
caret matches only the very start of the
patch.

3167
03:29:40,650 --> 03:29:45,447
For a diff contains grader, that means the
pattern can never match, and the grader

3168
03:29:45,447 --> 03:29:46,325
can never pass.

3169
03:29:47,533 --> 03:29:49,408
We put the switch back.

3170
03:29:49,908 --> 03:29:54,083
This time the fix is in a grader, so the
saved trials can prove it.

3171
03:29:55,300 --> 03:29:59,805
From the run's folder, vally grade applies
the corrected grader to the two saved

3172
03:29:59,805 --> 03:30:01,325
trials, with no agent runs.

3173
03:30:01,825 --> 03:30:06,482
It takes the trials from results dot
jsonl, and dash dash run dir points at the

3174
03:30:06,482 --> 03:30:08,250
folder that holds their diffs.

3175
03:30:08,750 --> 03:30:11,900
Both blocks now say one of one graders
passed.

3176
03:30:19,100 --> 03:30:23,563
Our fourth file passes lint with one
warning we know, and it has two graders: a

3177
03:30:23,563 --> 03:30:24,750
pattern, and a judge.

3178
03:30:25,250 --> 03:30:29,350
Here the agents are expected to finish,
and the judge call to fail.

3179
03:30:35,680 --> 03:30:40,630
Both stimuli are reported as ERROR, and
the reason is the judge: its call failed.

3180
03:30:45,130 --> 03:30:49,360
Much of what's on screen is the list of
models our account does have, and under

3181
03:30:49,360 --> 03:30:52,305
the summary, each trial's grade is marked
as not valid.

3182
03:30:56,533 --> 03:31:00,033
Let's go back to one block, for questions
two and three.

3183
03:31:00,533 --> 03:31:04,683
It says one of two graders, so the pattern
grader ran, and it passed.

3184
03:31:05,183 --> 03:31:08,808
That means the agents finished, and only
the judge failed.

3185
03:31:09,308 --> 03:31:14,158
Its evidence gives the reason: the judge
model's name isn't on the account's list.

3186
03:31:14,658 --> 03:31:19,318
One character differs, just before the
last five: the eval file has a hyphen, and

3187
03:31:19,318 --> 03:31:20,583
the account has a dot.

3188
03:31:21,800 --> 03:31:25,425
Fixing it takes one character, in the
judge model line.

3189
03:31:25,925 --> 03:31:30,377
Because this file also has a diff grader,
the run saved the diffs, so both trials

3190
03:31:30,377 --> 03:31:32,850
can be judged again without running an
agent.

3191
03:31:34,067 --> 03:31:38,642
So we re-grade from the run's folder, with
verbose, to see the judge's reasons.

3192
03:31:39,142 --> 03:31:41,442
That costs two judge calls.

3193
03:31:47,667 --> 03:31:51,938
This time the judge reaches a verdict on
both trials, and each of its reasons

3194
03:31:51,938 --> 03:31:53,492
begins by quoting the entry.

3195
03:31:59,992 --> 03:32:04,283
The judge passes one, and fails the other,
whose entry mentions caching: a real

3196
03:32:04,283 --> 03:32:07,217
verdict about the work, which is what the
fix was for.

3197
03:32:12,433 --> 03:32:15,633
So here are the four, with what proved
each fix.

3198
03:32:16,133 --> 03:32:20,222
For the command taken as a mapping, and
for the five-second timeout, the fix

3199
03:32:20,222 --> 03:32:22,858
changed the run itself, so each needed a
new run.

3200
03:32:23,358 --> 03:32:27,661
For the pattern, and for the judge model's
name, the fault was in the grading, so

3201
03:32:27,661 --> 03:32:29,733
re-grading the saved trials was enough.

3202
03:32:30,233 --> 03:32:33,683
All four took nine agent runs and two
judge calls.

3203
03:32:34,900 --> 03:32:37,375
So what did those four have in common?

3204
03:32:37,875 --> 03:32:40,450
None of them was a fault in the skill.

3205
03:32:40,950 --> 03:32:43,175
Two of them looked like results.

3206
03:32:43,675 --> 03:32:47,900
Graders that can't pass report a failure
that looks just like a real one.

3207
03:32:48,400 --> 03:32:51,250
Failed judge calls leave a score of zero.

3208
03:32:51,750 --> 03:32:56,063
That's why the order matters: we settle
whether the eval is valid, and whether it

3209
03:32:56,063 --> 03:32:58,725
completed, before believing what any
verdict says.

3210
03:32:59,933 --> 03:33:04,279
It's worth keeping these as a table: the
symptom, where to look, and the cheapest

3211
03:33:04,279 --> 03:33:05,083
proof of a fix.

3212
03:33:05,583 --> 03:33:08,208
Two rows deserve a second look.

3213
03:33:08,708 --> 03:33:12,818
Timed-out trials still cost money, so
running a timeout again without raising

3214
03:33:12,818 --> 03:33:15,433
the limit pays the same again for the same
ERROR.

3215
03:33:15,933 --> 03:33:18,683
Judge calls that fail aren't tried again.

3216
03:33:19,183 --> 03:33:23,389
So if a judge fails because its service
was down for a moment, the trials are

3217
03:33:23,389 --> 03:33:26,283
still on disk, and re-grading them is the
second try.

3218
03:33:27,500 --> 03:33:30,000
We can measure, gate and debug.

3219
03:33:30,500 --> 03:33:34,225
What's left is to make it a routine that a
teammate can repeat.

3220
03:33:35,433 --> 03:33:39,546
Everything we've built works when we
remember to run it, in the right order,

3221
03:33:39,546 --> 03:33:40,683
with the right flags.

3222
03:33:41,183 --> 03:33:45,458
That isn't a routine, and it won't survive
a busy week or a new teammate.

3223
03:33:45,958 --> 03:33:50,249
Meanwhile the skill will be edited again,
and the models under it will change

3224
03:33:50,249 --> 03:33:51,308
without telling us.

3225
03:33:51,808 --> 03:33:56,153
Here, you'll make the right check the easy
one: one short command after every edit, a

3226
03:33:56,153 --> 03:33:59,783
longer one before sharing, and one page
that says what the team agreed.

3227
03:34:01,000 --> 03:34:03,675
Let's start with what each check costs.

3228
03:34:04,175 --> 03:34:08,750
We now have five ways to check the skill,
and they differ enormously in cost.

3229
03:34:09,250 --> 03:34:14,175
Lint, the oracle and a re-grade start no
agent, so for code graders they're free.

3230
03:34:14,675 --> 03:34:19,095
The other two are new here: a quick run of
the stimuli that code can check, and a

3231
03:34:19,095 --> 03:34:20,350
full run of everything.

3232
03:34:20,850 --> 03:34:25,286
On two models, the quick one spends ten
agent runs, and the full one spends

3233
03:34:25,286 --> 03:34:25,700
thirty.

3234
03:34:26,900 --> 03:34:31,600
The rule is to use the cheapest check that
can answer the question we actually have.

3235
03:34:32,100 --> 03:34:35,050
After editing an eval file, that's lint.

3236
03:34:35,550 --> 03:34:39,881
After changing a grader, it's the oracle,
which tries a correct answer and an

3237
03:34:39,881 --> 03:34:40,725
untouched file.

3238
03:34:41,225 --> 03:34:45,575
After changing a pattern, a budget or a
rubric, a re-grade says what the new

3239
03:34:45,575 --> 03:34:47,750
version makes of trials already saved.

3240
03:34:48,250 --> 03:34:52,775
A script grader can't be re-graded,
because the workspace it reads is gone.

3241
03:34:53,275 --> 03:34:56,500
So a change to a script goes back to the
oracle.

3242
03:34:57,000 --> 03:35:01,498
After every edit to the skill, the quick
run asks whether anything code can check

3243
03:35:01,498 --> 03:35:03,275
has broken, or gone over budget.

3244
03:35:03,775 --> 03:35:08,458
Finally, before sharing the skill, or when
a model changes, the full run asks whether

3245
03:35:08,458 --> 03:35:09,725
everything still holds.

3246
03:35:10,933 --> 03:35:15,633
Three more checks sit outside that order,
because each answers a one-off question.

3247
03:35:16,133 --> 03:35:19,688
After a large rewrite, the
with-and-without experiment says whether

3248
03:35:19,688 --> 03:35:21,333
the skill still beats no skill.

3249
03:35:21,833 --> 03:35:26,317
To learn whether one edit really helped,
we run a before-and-after experiment, then

3250
03:35:26,317 --> 03:35:29,558
vally compare, which spends judge calls
and starts no agent.

3251
03:35:30,058 --> 03:35:34,895
Finally, cost per passing trial is what to
compare before moving to a different model

3252
03:35:34,895 --> 03:35:36,033
or reasoning effort.

3253
03:35:37,233 --> 03:35:40,408
For a team, though, the commands are the
easy part.

3254
03:35:40,908 --> 03:35:45,460
What drifts is agreement: what deserves a
grader, where grading files are kept, how

3255
03:35:45,460 --> 03:35:47,983
a result is reported, and what a run may
cost.

3256
03:35:48,483 --> 03:35:51,908
So we'll write those down on one page,
while they're fresh.

3257
03:35:52,408 --> 03:35:56,761
People can disagree with something written
down, and that's how the agreement gets

3258
03:35:56,761 --> 03:35:57,133
better.

3259
03:35:58,333 --> 03:36:02,533
That's the plan; here's what Vally gives
us to build it, starting with tags.

3260
03:36:03,033 --> 03:36:07,736
When we say tag, we mean a label on a
stimulus: a name and a value, on a line

3261
03:36:07,736 --> 03:36:08,408
named tags.

3262
03:36:08,908 --> 03:36:13,613
Here the name is tier and the value is
quick; both words are ours, and any words

3263
03:36:13,613 --> 03:36:14,083
will do.

3264
03:36:14,583 --> 03:36:19,683
All five stimuli in this file get the same
line, because all five are graded by code.

3265
03:36:20,900 --> 03:36:23,950
Next, the suite: a named group of stimuli.

3266
03:36:24,450 --> 03:36:29,100
It's a list of eval files, optionally
narrowed to the stimuli with a given tag.

3267
03:36:29,600 --> 03:36:34,639
Suites are defined in a file named dot
vally dot yaml, in the folder we run Vally

3268
03:36:34,639 --> 03:36:34,950
from.

3269
03:36:35,450 --> 03:36:39,829
This one defines two; the first is named
quick, which is what we'll type on the

3270
03:36:39,829 --> 03:36:40,550
command line.

3271
03:36:41,050 --> 03:36:45,396
The quick suite draws on one eval file,
and its filter is matched against each

3272
03:36:45,396 --> 03:36:48,350
stimulus's tags, so it keeps the five we
just tagged.

3273
03:36:48,850 --> 03:36:53,912
The second, full, lists four eval files
with no filter, so every stimulus in them

3274
03:36:53,912 --> 03:36:54,225
runs.

3275
03:36:54,725 --> 03:36:59,062
Those four hold the single requests, the
conversation, the loading test, and the

3276
03:36:59,062 --> 03:36:59,875
judged wording.

3277
03:37:01,100 --> 03:37:05,979
To run a suite, vally eval takes dash dash
suite and the suite's name, in place of

3278
03:37:05,979 --> 03:37:07,050
dash e and a file.

3279
03:37:07,550 --> 03:37:10,850
Everything else about the command works as
before.

3280
03:37:11,350 --> 03:37:16,225
One thing to know: a suite only names
stimuli, and adds no graders of its own.

3281
03:37:17,433 --> 03:37:21,785
The five quick stimuli are the ones that
already have budgets, so the quick suite

3282
03:37:21,785 --> 03:37:22,483
carries them.

3283
03:37:22,983 --> 03:37:27,712
Every trial is checked against a ceiling
on its tokens, on its turns, and on its

3284
03:37:27,712 --> 03:37:28,008
time.

3285
03:37:30,508 --> 03:37:34,908
The agent is also stopped after a minute,
and whatever it had done by then is

3286
03:37:34,908 --> 03:37:35,308
graded.

3287
03:37:35,808 --> 03:37:40,315
When a trial goes over a budget, its
stimulus fails, exactly as it would for a

3288
03:37:40,315 --> 03:37:41,008
wrong entry.

3289
03:37:41,508 --> 03:37:45,533
That's what makes the quick suite a cost
check as well as a quality check.

3290
03:37:46,733 --> 03:37:51,658
The last piece is the exit code, which is
what scripts and automated checks act on.

3291
03:37:52,158 --> 03:37:57,163
Unless told otherwise, vally eval exits
with zero even when stimuli fail, because

3292
03:37:57,163 --> 03:37:58,708
the run itself completed.

3293
03:37:59,208 --> 03:38:03,703
Adding dash dash require pass changes
that: any failing stimulus makes the exit

3294
03:38:03,703 --> 03:38:06,833
code one, whichever grader failed it,
budgets included.

3295
03:38:07,333 --> 03:38:11,358
When a trial ends in error, the exit code
is one either way.

3296
03:38:11,858 --> 03:38:14,558
Vally grade accepts the same option.

3297
03:38:15,766 --> 03:38:20,057
One kind of failure gets past every
grader: an edit that makes the entries a

3298
03:38:20,057 --> 03:38:22,766
little worse, while every pattern still
matches.

3299
03:38:23,266 --> 03:38:25,816
That's what the pairwise judge is for.

3300
03:38:26,316 --> 03:38:30,891
Vally compare gives it two runs of the
same stimuli, a baseline and a change, and

3301
03:38:30,891 --> 03:38:32,641
it scores each pair of answers.

3302
03:38:33,141 --> 03:38:37,482
With dash dash fail on regression, the
command exits with one when the change

3303
03:38:37,482 --> 03:38:38,666
scored clearly worse.

3304
03:38:39,166 --> 03:38:43,416
Clearly worse means that the whole range
around its score is below zero.

3305
03:38:43,916 --> 03:38:47,866
Earlier, we tested it by treating the
older skill as the change.

3306
03:38:48,366 --> 03:38:52,944
When we ran it, the range reached just
above zero, so the command exited with

3307
03:38:52,944 --> 03:38:53,241
zero.

3308
03:38:53,741 --> 03:38:58,575
In an earlier run of the same comparison,
the whole range was below zero, and it

3309
03:38:58,575 --> 03:38:59,541
exited with one.

3310
03:39:00,041 --> 03:39:05,021
It needs a before-and-after experiment and
about eighteen judge calls, so it belongs

3311
03:39:05,021 --> 03:39:07,866
before we rely on an edit, not after every
edit.

3312
03:39:08,366 --> 03:39:13,366
It also gates on the judged score alone; a
rise in tokens is the budgets' job.

3313
03:39:14,566 --> 03:39:17,866
That's how the pieces work; now let's put
them to work.

3314
03:39:18,366 --> 03:39:22,875
The tags and the suites file are in place
in our practice folder, exactly as we just

3315
03:39:22,875 --> 03:39:23,841
went through them.

3316
03:39:24,341 --> 03:39:28,838
Here's a question first: suppose we ran
the quick suite without require pass, and

3317
03:39:28,838 --> 03:39:30,891
one trial went over its token budget.

3318
03:39:31,391 --> 03:39:35,366
Nothing else fails, and nothing errors:
what would the exit code be?

3319
03:39:38,366 --> 03:39:42,444
It would be zero: the budget fails that
stimulus, but without the option, a

3320
03:39:42,444 --> 03:39:44,891
failing verdict doesn't change the exit
code.

3321
03:39:45,391 --> 03:39:49,316
So anything automated has to add the
option, and we will too.

3322
03:39:50,533 --> 03:39:54,083
First, we lint the eval file, since we've
just edited it.

3323
03:39:57,333 --> 03:40:01,508
There's one warning, the usual one about
scoring defaults, and no errors.

3324
03:40:04,008 --> 03:40:08,363
Now the quick suite, on both models, two
workers at a time, with require pass so

3325
03:40:08,363 --> 03:40:09,833
that a failure is an error.

3326
03:40:10,333 --> 03:40:14,758
Its results go to a folder of their own,
and it spends ten agent runs.

3327
03:40:15,258 --> 03:40:19,858
The table at the end counts the stimuli
that passed, out of five, for each model.

3328
03:40:22,166 --> 03:40:27,016
About a minute later, every stimulus has
passed on both models, budgets included.

3329
03:40:30,516 --> 03:40:32,716
So what was its exit code?

3330
03:40:33,833 --> 03:40:35,833
Zero: nothing failed.

3331
03:40:39,033 --> 03:40:43,248
A passing run can't show the option at
work, so let's see a wrong entry fail

3332
03:40:43,248 --> 03:40:45,133
loudly, without spending anything.

3333
03:40:45,633 --> 03:40:50,086
The run we'll use is the first one we
graded, before the skill had its rules, so

3334
03:40:50,086 --> 03:40:51,533
it has plenty of failures.

3335
03:40:52,033 --> 03:40:56,434
None of its graders is a script, and a
script grader is the one kind that can't

3336
03:40:56,434 --> 03:40:57,158
be re-graded.

3337
03:40:57,658 --> 03:41:00,358
We change into that run's folder.

3338
03:41:03,200 --> 03:41:07,525
Then we re-grade it with today's eval
file, and with require pass.

3339
03:41:14,233 --> 03:41:18,478
These are the failing blocks from that
run, each now with the budgets among its

3340
03:41:18,478 --> 03:41:18,908
graders.

3341
03:41:23,408 --> 03:41:27,753
Every budget passed in all eighteen
trials, three stimuli three times on two

3342
03:41:27,753 --> 03:41:30,383
models; only the graders of the result
failed.

3343
03:41:32,883 --> 03:41:35,433
So what's the exit code this time?

3344
03:41:36,513 --> 03:41:39,738
It's one: a failing verdict is now an
error.

3345
03:41:42,966 --> 03:41:47,504
Next, a trial that's over budget, which
none of ours is, so we make one for a

3346
03:41:47,504 --> 03:41:47,916
moment.

3347
03:41:48,416 --> 03:41:52,639
In the typo fix stimulus, we lower the
token budget from two hundred fifteen

3348
03:41:52,639 --> 03:41:56,416
thousand to seventy thousand, which is
below what those trials used.

3349
03:41:57,633 --> 03:42:01,433
We run the same re-grade again, and then
print the exit code.

3350
03:42:10,626 --> 03:42:12,201
It's one again.

3351
03:42:19,433 --> 03:42:23,683
That output is longer than one screen, so
here's the part that changed.

3352
03:42:24,183 --> 03:42:29,316
These typo fix trials passed every grader
a moment ago; now they fail, on the budget

3353
03:42:29,316 --> 03:42:29,683
alone.

3354
03:42:30,183 --> 03:42:34,633
Seventy-four thousand tokens is over a
maximum of seventy thousand.

3355
03:42:35,133 --> 03:42:38,283
The score still says ninety-eight point
eight percent.

3356
03:42:38,783 --> 03:42:43,133
That's because going slightly over a
budget costs only a little of the score.

3357
03:42:43,633 --> 03:42:48,312
With no scoring block, though, the score
decides nothing: every grader has to pass,

3358
03:42:48,312 --> 03:42:49,158
and one didn't.

3359
03:42:49,658 --> 03:42:54,308
So a trial that did the work and cost too
much is a failing verdict like any other.

3360
03:42:54,808 --> 03:42:58,758
With that seen, the budget goes back to
two hundred fifteen thousand.

3361
03:42:59,966 --> 03:43:04,730
Now everything, once: the full suite, with
the same options, into its own results

3362
03:43:04,730 --> 03:43:05,141
folder.

3363
03:43:05,641 --> 03:43:09,016
It spends thirty agent runs and six judge
calls.

3364
03:43:09,516 --> 03:43:13,516
The table at the end has a row for each of
the four eval files.

3365
03:43:16,646 --> 03:43:21,696
Several minutes later, every stimulus has
passed on both models, in all four files.

3366
03:43:27,196 --> 03:43:29,246
Let's see what it reported.

3367
03:43:30,373 --> 03:43:34,023
Zero again: nothing failed, budgets and
judge included.

3368
03:43:37,233 --> 03:43:41,849
That was our second run of the full suite;
the first, a few minutes before it, ended

3369
03:43:41,849 --> 03:43:42,508
differently.

3370
03:43:43,008 --> 03:43:47,533
Here's the first run's table, with the
three stimuli Vally listed above it.

3371
03:43:48,033 --> 03:43:50,433
They're two different kinds of trouble.

3372
03:43:50,933 --> 03:43:55,008
On haiku, two trials ended in error,
because their sessions dropped.

3373
03:43:55,508 --> 03:44:00,047
As before, an error isn't a verdict: the
trial didn't complete, so it says nothing

3374
03:44:00,047 --> 03:44:00,933
about the skill.

3375
03:44:01,433 --> 03:44:04,808
So we ran the suite again, and that's the
run we kept.

3376
03:44:05,308 --> 03:44:09,763
The other is a real fail: on terra, a
question that shouldn't load the skill did

3377
03:44:09,763 --> 03:44:10,208
load it.

3378
03:44:10,708 --> 03:44:14,083
The loading grader that forbids that is
what caught it.

3379
03:44:14,583 --> 03:44:19,308
Either problem alone makes the command
exit with one, and that first run did.

3380
03:44:20,533 --> 03:44:24,008
One thing is left: the page that says what
we've agreed.

3381
03:44:24,508 --> 03:44:29,683
Ours is a file named conventions, with ten
headings and a sentence or two under each.

3382
03:44:30,183 --> 03:44:34,347
It opens with what earns a grader, code
before judge, where files are kept, and

3383
03:44:34,347 --> 03:44:36,983
what we check before trusting a grader or
a judge.

3384
03:44:37,483 --> 03:44:42,233
Then come what we run and when, how we
report a result, and what a run may cost.

3385
03:44:42,733 --> 03:44:47,254
The last two are what to do when a run
goes wrong, and which models we used, on

3386
03:44:47,254 --> 03:44:47,883
which date.

3387
03:44:48,383 --> 03:44:52,845
That last one matters because models and
their prices change: a result means little

3388
03:44:52,845 --> 03:44:55,533
without knowing which model produced it,
and when.

3389
03:44:56,733 --> 03:44:58,933
So where does the skill stand?

3390
03:44:59,433 --> 03:45:03,723
At the start, it was a first draft that
one model answered by making up a pull

3391
03:45:03,723 --> 03:45:06,033
request number, behind a tick that hid it.

3392
03:45:06,533 --> 03:45:11,033
With our first graders, one pair of
stimulus and model passed, out of six.

3393
03:45:11,533 --> 03:45:15,838
Now the suites cover a conversation, a
loading test with near-miss requests, a

3394
03:45:15,838 --> 03:45:20,033
judged quality that no pattern can see,
and budgets on every quick stimulus.

3395
03:45:20,533 --> 03:45:24,383
In our last run, every stimulus in them
passed on both models.

3396
03:45:24,883 --> 03:45:29,062
Every rule in the skill is there because a
trial showed it was needed, and every

3397
03:45:29,062 --> 03:45:31,308
grader is there because a failure was
seen.

3398
03:45:32,533 --> 03:45:34,808
Now, what does the routine cost?

3399
03:45:35,308 --> 03:45:40,010
In our run, the quick suite's ten trials
cost about twenty-four AIU, which is about

3400
03:45:40,010 --> 03:45:41,483
twenty-four cents an edit.

3401
03:45:41,983 --> 03:45:47,170
The full suite's thirty trials cost about
sixty-seven AIU, with its six judge calls

3402
03:45:47,170 --> 03:45:47,608
on top.

3403
03:45:48,108 --> 03:45:52,466
None of the quick trials came near the
token budget: the largest used ninety-one

3404
03:45:52,466 --> 03:45:55,408
thousand tokens, against two hundred
fifteen thousand.

3405
03:45:56,633 --> 03:45:59,908
Those figures are for the two models we've
used throughout.

3406
03:46:00,408 --> 03:46:04,839
Earlier, in the cost experiment, the newer
haiku model at low effort passed all

3407
03:46:04,839 --> 03:46:09,158
fifteen of its trials on these same
stimuli, at about a fifth of an AIU each.

3408
03:46:09,658 --> 03:46:14,458
On that one variant, a quick suite would
cost about one AIU an edit: a cent.

3409
03:46:14,958 --> 03:46:19,277
Our conventions say how to make that move:
compare cost per passing trial, re-test

3410
03:46:19,277 --> 03:46:22,858
the budgets on its saved trials, and run
the full suite on it first.

3411
03:46:24,066 --> 03:46:28,516
One caution goes with that last table:
it's one trial per stimulus.

3412
03:46:29,016 --> 03:46:33,262
In our two runs of the full suite, the
same loading question failed on terra the

3413
03:46:33,262 --> 03:46:35,066
first time, and passed the second.

3414
03:46:35,566 --> 03:46:40,018
A single passing trial shows that a
stimulus can pass; it doesn't show that it

3415
03:46:40,018 --> 03:46:41,216
will pass every time.

3416
03:46:41,716 --> 03:46:44,866
The suites are how we find out when that
changes.

3417
03:46:46,067 --> 03:46:49,167
That completes the checks on the skill by
itself.

3418
03:46:49,667 --> 03:46:53,987
Now a team has put the skill inside their
release tooling: does it still hold when

3419
03:46:53,987 --> 03:46:56,042
an app, not a person, starts the agent?

3420
03:46:57,267 --> 03:47:02,367
So far, every request came from a person,
describing one change in their own words.

3421
03:47:02,867 --> 03:47:07,074
Now the team that owns this changelog has
built our skill into their release

3422
03:47:07,074 --> 03:47:07,517
tooling.

3423
03:47:08,017 --> 03:47:12,058
Before each release, a small tool lists
the pull requests merged since the last

3424
03:47:12,058 --> 03:47:16,303
one, saves the list as a file, and asks an
agent to write the changelog entries for

3425
03:47:16,303 --> 03:47:16,917
all of them.

3426
03:47:17,417 --> 03:47:21,592
Nobody types a request, and nobody checks
the entries before they ship.

3427
03:47:22,092 --> 03:47:26,426
Here, you'll set up a stimulus that starts
the agent exactly where that tool does,

3428
03:47:26,426 --> 03:47:29,492
and grade what it writes with a test the
agent never sees.

3429
03:47:30,700 --> 03:47:35,227
Let's start with what a stimulus is, when
the thing under test is a feature of an

3430
03:47:35,227 --> 03:47:35,450
app.

3431
03:47:35,950 --> 03:47:38,800
It has two halves, and both come from the
app.

3432
03:47:39,300 --> 03:47:43,793
One half is the prompt: whatever the app
sends, which is usually short, always the

3433
03:47:43,793 --> 03:47:45,600
same, and written by a developer.

3434
03:47:46,100 --> 03:47:50,713
The other is the workspace: whatever the
app has prepared by the time it calls the

3435
03:47:50,713 --> 03:47:51,050
agent.

3436
03:47:51,550 --> 03:47:55,735
If a stimulus rewords the prompt more
kindly, or tidies the workspace, it

3437
03:47:55,735 --> 03:47:58,200
measures a situation the app never
creates.

3438
03:47:59,400 --> 03:48:02,775
So we copy the prompt from the app's code,
word for word.

3439
03:48:03,275 --> 03:48:07,565
For the workspace, we run the app's own
setup steps, instead of writing their

3440
03:48:07,565 --> 03:48:08,400
output by hand.

3441
03:48:08,900 --> 03:48:12,350
Next month, the team might change the
format of that list.

3442
03:48:12,850 --> 03:48:16,925
But an eval that runs their tool sees the
new format straight away.

3443
03:48:17,425 --> 03:48:21,819
An eval with a hand-written copy of the
old list keeps passing, and tells you

3444
03:48:21,819 --> 03:48:22,275
nothing.

3445
03:48:23,500 --> 03:48:27,960
Our release tool's prompt is one sentence:
it asks for the Unreleased entries, for

3446
03:48:27,960 --> 03:48:31,550
every change listed in a file named
merged, in the release folder.

3447
03:48:32,050 --> 03:48:35,450
Its setup is one command, which writes
that file.

3448
03:48:35,950 --> 03:48:38,800
Those two things are what we're going to
reproduce.

3449
03:48:40,000 --> 03:48:44,700
That's the idea; here's how an eval file
reproduces it, starting with the tool.

3450
03:48:45,200 --> 03:48:50,023
First, the release tool's opening job is
done by a short Node script named list

3451
03:48:50,023 --> 03:48:50,450
merged.

3452
03:48:50,950 --> 03:48:55,317
In the real one, the list comes from the
team's issue tracker; ours prints a fixed

3453
03:48:55,317 --> 03:48:57,500
list, so every trial starts the same way.

3454
03:48:58,000 --> 03:49:03,050
Each line holds a pull request number and
a type, as in the team's commit messages.

3455
03:49:03,550 --> 03:49:08,574
Feat means a new feature, and fix means a
bug fix: users notice both, so both need

3456
03:49:08,574 --> 03:49:09,125
an entry.

3457
03:49:09,625 --> 03:49:14,425
Refactor means only the code changed, and
for that, our skill says to add nothing.

3458
03:49:14,925 --> 03:49:19,598
Earlier, a refactor request said outright
that nothing changes for users; this list

3459
03:49:19,598 --> 03:49:22,300
says only refactor, and the agent has to
decide.

3460
03:49:23,500 --> 03:49:28,326
An eval file can run a command like that
one, with a key we've met once before:

3461
03:49:28,326 --> 03:49:28,875
commands.

3462
03:49:29,375 --> 03:49:33,575
Inside an agent environment, commands is a
list of shell commands.

3463
03:49:34,075 --> 03:49:38,099
Vally runs them in the workspace, in
order, after it has copied in the files

3464
03:49:38,099 --> 03:49:40,375
and the skill, and before the agent
starts.

3465
03:49:40,875 --> 03:49:45,275
Each command gets sixty seconds, unless
the file sets a longer limit.

3466
03:49:45,775 --> 03:49:48,125
That order matters for grading.

3467
03:49:48,625 --> 03:49:52,783
Whatever a command creates is part of the
starting state, exactly like a staged

3468
03:49:52,783 --> 03:49:56,150
file: the agent can open it, and it
doesn't appear in the patch.

3469
03:49:56,650 --> 03:49:59,250
Only what the agent changes does.

3470
03:50:00,467 --> 03:50:03,192
Here's the eval file for the release tool.

3471
03:50:03,692 --> 03:50:08,168
Our skill is installed as before, and the
tool is staged into the workspace, like

3472
03:50:08,168 --> 03:50:08,942
the changelog.

3473
03:50:09,442 --> 03:50:13,742
There's one stimulus, and its prompt is
the app's sentence, word for word.

3474
03:50:14,242 --> 03:50:18,767
Under it, the stimulus has an agent
environment of its own, with two commands.

3475
03:50:19,267 --> 03:50:23,785
One makes the release folder; the other
runs the tool, and saves what it prints as

3476
03:50:23,785 --> 03:50:24,667
the merged file.

3477
03:50:25,167 --> 03:50:29,345
Each command is in quotes, because an
unquoted command with a colon and a space

3478
03:50:29,345 --> 03:50:32,942
in it would be taken for YAML structure,
and lint wouldn't catch it.

3479
03:50:34,167 --> 03:50:38,167
Two more fields can prepare the start, and
this eval needs neither.

3480
03:50:38,667 --> 03:50:42,755
One sets environment variables on the
agent's process, for an app that passes

3481
03:50:42,755 --> 03:50:47,055
its settings that way; their values can
show up in saved trials, so it's no place

3482
03:50:47,055 --> 03:50:47,692
for secrets.

3483
03:50:48,192 --> 03:50:52,401
The other, git, builds the workspace from
a repository instead of copied files,

3484
03:50:52,401 --> 03:50:55,492
which is how a real release tool would get
at the history.

3485
03:50:55,992 --> 03:51:00,692
Ours has no repository, so we leave git as
the documentation shows it.

3486
03:51:01,900 --> 03:51:05,900
So the agent starts where the app would;
now, what should a grader check?

3487
03:51:06,400 --> 03:51:11,016
For each feature and each fix on the list,
the changelog needs exactly one line,

3488
03:51:11,016 --> 03:51:12,400
citing its pull request.

3489
03:51:12,900 --> 03:51:15,275
For the refactor, it needs none.

3490
03:51:15,775 --> 03:51:20,329
What's correct depends on the list, so the
check has to open the merged file as well

3491
03:51:20,329 --> 03:51:21,250
as the changelog.

3492
03:51:21,750 --> 03:51:25,150
No pattern on the diff can do that; it
takes a script.

3493
03:51:26,367 --> 03:51:30,573
We've run a grading script before, with
the program grader, which expects the

3494
03:51:30,573 --> 03:51:32,267
script to print a line of JSON.

3495
03:51:32,767 --> 03:51:37,506
The run command grader is simpler; despite
its name, it has nothing to do with the

3496
03:51:37,506 --> 03:51:39,067
setup commands we just saw.

3497
03:51:39,567 --> 03:51:44,123
It runs a shell command in the graded copy
of the workspace, and passes when the

3498
03:51:44,123 --> 03:51:45,717
command's exit code is zero.

3499
03:51:46,217 --> 03:51:50,642
It can also check what the command prints,
but the exit code is the heart of it.

3500
03:51:51,142 --> 03:51:55,635
So any test you already have works
unchanged: a test suite, a linter, or a

3501
03:51:55,635 --> 03:51:56,667
script like ours.

3502
03:51:57,867 --> 03:52:00,167
Here's the rest of the stimulus.

3503
03:52:00,667 --> 03:52:04,909
Its grading environment stages our script,
so the agent never has it: Vally's

3504
03:52:04,909 --> 03:52:09,317
documentation calls these grader-only
files, and a test run this way is a hidden

3505
03:52:09,317 --> 03:52:09,592
test.

3506
03:52:10,092 --> 03:52:14,942
Then comes the grader, named every change
handled, which runs the script with node.

3507
03:52:16,167 --> 03:52:18,767
Now the script itself, which is short.

3508
03:52:19,267 --> 03:52:23,663
It opens two files: the changelog, and the
merged list, which is still there because

3509
03:52:23,663 --> 03:52:25,442
it was part of the starting state.

3510
03:52:25,942 --> 03:52:28,892
It keeps the lines of the Unreleased
section.

3511
03:52:29,392 --> 03:52:33,410
Then, for each change on the list, it
takes the number and the type, and finds

3512
03:52:33,410 --> 03:52:35,367
the lines that cite that pull request.

3513
03:52:35,867 --> 03:52:38,267
For a refactor, there must be none.

3514
03:52:38,767 --> 03:52:43,434
A feature or a fix must be cited on
exactly one line, and that line must not

3515
03:52:43,434 --> 03:52:45,092
cite a second pull request.

3516
03:52:45,592 --> 03:52:50,138
Between them, those checks catch a skipped
change, an entry for the refactor, and two

3517
03:52:50,138 --> 03:52:51,742
changes merged into one entry.

3518
03:52:52,242 --> 03:52:57,142
On any failure, the script exits with code
one, and that exit code is the verdict.

3519
03:52:58,367 --> 03:53:02,928
One limit is worth knowing: a hidden test
needs the finished workspace, which Vally

3520
03:53:02,928 --> 03:53:03,642
doesn't keep.

3521
03:53:04,142 --> 03:53:08,749
So, like a program grader, it can't be
re-graded from saved trials; graders that

3522
03:53:08,749 --> 03:53:11,167
only need the patch or the trajectory can.

3523
03:53:12,367 --> 03:53:15,617
That's the setup and the test; now let's
run them.

3524
03:53:16,117 --> 03:53:19,617
First, we run the release tool by hand, to
see the list.

3525
03:53:20,117 --> 03:53:24,167
Then we lint the eval file, and run it
five times on each model.

3526
03:53:24,667 --> 03:53:27,592
After that, we open a patch from each
model.

3527
03:53:28,092 --> 03:53:32,564
Three changes are on the list, and one of
them is a refactor: what would you expect

3528
03:53:32,564 --> 03:53:33,642
each model to write?

3529
03:53:36,142 --> 03:53:39,017
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

3530
03:53:40,234 --> 03:53:44,384
We're in the practice folder, and this is
the release tool, run by hand.

3531
03:53:46,594 --> 03:53:50,394
Three changes come back: a feature, a fix,
and a refactor.

3532
03:53:53,894 --> 03:53:58,373
Both new files are in place, the hidden
test and the eval file, so we lint before

3533
03:53:58,373 --> 03:53:59,369
spending anything.

3534
03:54:02,420 --> 03:54:04,620
Lint finds nothing wrong.

3535
03:54:07,834 --> 03:54:12,509
Now the app's situation, on both models:
five trials each, two at a time.

3536
03:54:13,009 --> 03:54:15,134
That's ten agent runs.

3537
03:54:15,634 --> 03:54:20,617
The table at the end counts stimuli, not
trials: this file has one stimulus, and it

3538
03:54:20,617 --> 03:54:22,659
passes only if all five trials do.

3539
03:54:24,980 --> 03:54:29,630
About a minute later, that table shows a
cross for haiku, and a tick for terra.

3540
03:54:33,130 --> 03:54:37,666
Just above it is the grader that failed,
every change handled, with its evidence:

3541
03:54:37,666 --> 03:54:41,305
the command exited with code one in three
of haiku's five trials.

3542
03:54:47,534 --> 03:54:51,359
The run is saved in a folder named for the
time it started.

3543
03:54:53,200 --> 03:54:57,125
We change into it, and then into the
folder for our one stimulus.

3544
03:54:57,625 --> 03:55:02,056
Let's open one of haiku's failing trials
first: trial two, which is folder one,

3545
03:55:02,056 --> 03:55:04,075
because the folders count from zero.

3546
03:55:06,900 --> 03:55:11,074
Only the changelog is in the patch: the
merged file isn't, because the setup

3547
03:55:11,074 --> 03:55:13,325
command made it before the agent started.

3548
03:55:17,825 --> 03:55:22,263
There are entries for the feature and the
fix, and a third, under Changed, for the

3549
03:55:22,263 --> 03:55:22,750
refactor.

3550
03:55:27,967 --> 03:55:31,517
For comparison, here's a trial from terra,
which passed.

3551
03:55:34,207 --> 03:55:38,607
It has two entries, for the feature and
the fix, and nothing for the refactor.

3552
03:55:47,834 --> 03:55:50,184
So what did the hidden test show?

3553
03:55:50,684 --> 03:55:55,122
When we ran it, neither model skipped a
change, and neither merged two into one

3554
03:55:55,122 --> 03:55:55,459
entry.

3555
03:55:55,959 --> 03:56:00,594
Every failure was the same kind: haiku
wrote an entry for the refactor in three

3556
03:56:00,594 --> 03:56:01,709
of its five trials.

3557
03:56:02,209 --> 03:56:06,836
In the patch we opened, that entry sits
under Changed and names a source file; our

3558
03:56:06,836 --> 03:56:07,909
skill forbids both.

3559
03:56:08,409 --> 03:56:12,759
Terra left the refactor out every time,
and said why in its reply.

3560
03:56:13,967 --> 03:56:18,817
The run's records add one more piece of
evidence: which trials loaded our skill.

3561
03:56:19,317 --> 03:56:23,117
Terra loaded it in all five trials, and
haiku in three.

3562
03:56:23,617 --> 03:56:28,068
Haiku's two trials without the skill both
recorded the refactor; of the three with

3563
03:56:28,068 --> 03:56:31,217
it, two left the refactor out, and one
recorded it anyway.

3564
03:56:31,717 --> 03:56:35,703
Our hidden test couldn't have told us
that: it sees only the finished changelog,

3565
03:56:35,703 --> 03:56:39,092
not whether the agent opened the list, or
used the skill on the way.

3566
03:56:40,300 --> 03:56:42,625
So what does this run support?

3567
03:56:43,125 --> 03:56:46,792
The app's input is shorter than a
person's: its prompt is one fixed

3568
03:56:46,792 --> 03:56:51,115
sentence, and its list says only refactor,
where a person had said that nothing

3569
03:56:51,115 --> 03:56:52,100
changes for users.

3570
03:56:52,600 --> 03:56:57,016
With that input, haiku skipped the skill
in two of its five trials, and recorded

3571
03:56:57,016 --> 03:56:58,175
the refactor in both.

3572
03:56:58,675 --> 03:57:03,175
Loading isn't the whole story, since one
trial loaded the skill and recorded the

3573
03:57:03,175 --> 03:57:04,075
refactor anyway.

3574
03:57:04,575 --> 03:57:08,890
Five trials on each model, in one run,
can't prove a cause, but they suggest that

3575
03:57:08,890 --> 03:57:11,500
the app's wording matters as much as the
skill's.

3576
03:57:12,000 --> 03:57:16,225
That's the kind of difference an app
brings in without anyone noticing.

3577
03:57:17,434 --> 03:57:20,809
Passing the test isn't the same as being
right, either.

3578
03:57:21,309 --> 03:57:25,515
Terra's entries for the feature each say
what the new flag filters by, and they

3579
03:57:25,515 --> 03:57:27,059
don't all say the same thing.

3580
03:57:27,559 --> 03:57:32,031
The list doesn't say; the flag might just
as well filter by the date a note was

3581
03:57:32,031 --> 03:57:32,484
changed.

3582
03:57:32,984 --> 03:57:37,221
None of our graders checks for details the
list doesn't support, and in an app,

3583
03:57:37,221 --> 03:57:39,259
nobody sees the entry before it ships.

3584
03:57:40,467 --> 03:57:43,592
The hidden test sees only the finished
changelog.

3585
03:57:44,092 --> 03:57:48,457
In an app, how the agent got there matters
too: did it read the list, and did it try

3586
03:57:48,457 --> 03:57:49,392
to commit or push?

3587
03:57:50,600 --> 03:57:54,075
Inside the release tool, nobody watches
the agent work.

3588
03:57:54,575 --> 03:57:59,119
The team cares about the changelog, and
also about two things the changelog can't

3589
03:57:59,119 --> 03:57:59,400
show.

3590
03:57:59,900 --> 03:58:04,062
The entries have to come from the list the
tool wrote, and not from guesses that

3591
03:58:04,062 --> 03:58:05,050
happen to be right.

3592
03:58:05,550 --> 03:58:10,104
And the agent must never commit or push:
the release tool does that itself, after a

3593
03:58:10,104 --> 03:58:11,750
person has checked the result.

3594
03:58:12,250 --> 03:58:15,225
Our hidden test only sees the finished
file.

3595
03:58:15,725 --> 03:58:20,074
Here, you'll add graders that check the
steps the agent took, and test each one

3596
03:58:20,074 --> 03:58:20,900
before it runs.

3597
03:58:22,100 --> 03:58:25,250
Let's start with when a step is worth
grading at all.

3598
03:58:25,750 --> 03:58:28,350
The default is to grade the outcome.

3599
03:58:28,850 --> 03:58:33,290
Agents find valid routes nobody planned
for, and a grader that insists on one

3600
03:58:33,290 --> 03:58:35,250
sequence of steps fails good work.

3601
03:58:35,750 --> 03:58:40,550
An agent that opens a file with one tool
instead of another has still opened it.

3602
03:58:41,050 --> 03:58:45,380
So we check the route only when the app
depends on it, and then only the step it

3603
03:58:45,380 --> 03:58:45,975
depends on.

3604
03:58:47,200 --> 03:58:51,396
Sometimes a step is required: the app
relies on the agent working from the list,

3605
03:58:51,396 --> 03:58:55,225
because an entry written from the file
names would be right only by luck.

3606
03:58:55,725 --> 03:59:00,509
A step can be forbidden: a commit, a push,
a deleted file, anything with effects

3607
03:59:00,509 --> 03:59:01,825
outside the workspace.

3608
03:59:02,325 --> 03:59:06,750
A commit made by the agent can't be undone
by throwing the workspace away.

3609
03:59:07,250 --> 03:59:11,741
And a route can be too long: an agent that
repeats the same call costs money and

3610
03:59:11,741 --> 03:59:13,425
time, even when it gets there.

3611
03:59:14,633 --> 03:59:18,108
Even a route grader should be as loose as
the requirement.

3612
03:59:18,608 --> 03:59:21,458
Opening the merged list is the
requirement.

3613
03:59:21,958 --> 03:59:27,163
Using one particular tool on it is one way
to meet that requirement, and not the only

3614
03:59:27,163 --> 03:59:27,408
one.

3615
03:59:27,908 --> 03:59:30,933
That difference shapes the first grader
we'll write.

3616
03:59:32,133 --> 03:59:35,808
That's when to check a step; now for where
the steps are recorded.

3617
03:59:36,308 --> 03:59:41,021
Every time the agent uses a tool, the
trial's trajectory, its record of what the

3618
03:59:41,021 --> 03:59:43,083
agent did, gains a tool call event.

3619
03:59:43,583 --> 03:59:48,338
Here's one from the saved run, spread out
over several lines: an agent opening the

3620
03:59:48,338 --> 03:59:49,033
merged list.

3621
03:59:49,533 --> 03:59:53,883
Tool name is the tool's name; the agent's
runtime decides the names.

3622
03:59:54,383 --> 03:59:59,202
Arguments hold what the tool was given: a
file tool gets a path, and bash gets a

3623
03:59:59,202 --> 03:59:59,683
command.

3624
04:00:00,900 --> 04:00:04,100
You don't need to open that file to learn
the names.

3625
04:00:04,600 --> 04:00:08,925
The report has a Tool Calls column, which
totals them for each stimulus.

3626
04:00:09,425 --> 04:00:14,081
For haiku's five saved trials, it counted
eighteen views, five edits, three skill

3627
04:00:14,081 --> 04:00:15,575
calls, and two bash calls.

3628
04:00:16,075 --> 04:00:20,758
View opens a file, edit and apply patch
change one, bash runs a shell command, and

3629
04:00:20,758 --> 04:00:21,900
skill loads a skill.

3630
04:00:23,100 --> 04:00:26,125
The tool calls grader works from those
events.

3631
04:00:26,625 --> 04:00:30,800
Here are two, added to the release eval
file after the hidden test.

3632
04:00:31,300 --> 04:00:33,950
The first is named reads the list.

3633
04:00:34,450 --> 04:00:39,129
Under required, it has one entry, and
every entry there must match at least one

3634
04:00:39,129 --> 04:00:39,425
call.

3635
04:00:39,925 --> 04:00:43,850
This entry has two keys, and each is a
regular expression.

3636
04:00:44,350 --> 04:00:47,075
Name is matched against the tool's name.

3637
04:00:47,575 --> 04:00:52,150
The pattern is matched against all of the
call's arguments, written out as text.

3638
04:00:52,650 --> 04:00:57,052
Both have to match the same call, so this
grader needs a call to view, or to bash,

3639
04:00:57,052 --> 04:00:59,200
whose arguments mention the merged list.

3640
04:01:00,400 --> 04:01:02,150
Why both tools?

3641
04:01:02,650 --> 04:01:07,425
Either one can open a file: view directly,
and bash with a command such as cat.

3642
04:01:07,925 --> 04:01:12,615
If a grader required view alone, it would
fail a trial that opened the list with

3643
04:01:12,615 --> 04:01:12,850
cat.

3644
04:01:13,350 --> 04:01:17,651
That trial would have done exactly what
the app needs, so the requirement names

3645
04:01:17,651 --> 04:01:18,250
both tools.

3646
04:01:18,750 --> 04:01:23,214
We anchor the name at both ends, because
an expression otherwise matches anywhere

3647
04:01:23,214 --> 04:01:23,875
in the text.

3648
04:01:24,375 --> 04:01:27,575
Without the anchors, view would also match
preview.

3649
04:01:28,800 --> 04:01:31,875
The second grader is named never commits.

3650
04:01:32,375 --> 04:01:35,525
No call may match an entry under
disallowed.

3651
04:01:36,025 --> 04:01:40,540
This entry has a name too, and a second
key, command, which is matched against a

3652
04:01:40,540 --> 04:01:41,725
shell call's command.

3653
04:01:42,225 --> 04:01:46,350
So it matches any bash call that contains
git commit or git push.

3654
04:01:46,850 --> 04:01:51,463
When a grader has only a disallowed list,
it checks for absence, so a trajectory

3655
04:01:51,463 --> 04:01:53,250
with no calls at all passes it.

3656
04:01:53,750 --> 04:01:58,279
That decides how we test it: the oracle
can't show such a grader failing, and only

3657
04:01:58,279 --> 04:02:00,875
a saved trial that did the forbidden thing
can.

3658
04:02:02,100 --> 04:02:04,575
Two more graders complete the set.

3659
04:02:05,075 --> 04:02:09,525
The last one we already know: skill
invocation, here named loads the skill.

3660
04:02:10,025 --> 04:02:14,461
In the saved run's report, haiku loaded
the skill in three of its five trials, and

3661
04:02:14,461 --> 04:02:16,950
this grader gives every trial a verdict on
it.

3662
04:02:17,450 --> 04:02:21,388
The other, named no loop, checks for
loops, and that's the next thing to

3663
04:02:21,388 --> 04:02:21,825
explain.

3664
04:02:23,033 --> 04:02:27,613
An agent loops when it repeats the same
action without getting anywhere: reopening

3665
04:02:27,613 --> 04:02:29,958
a file, or rerunning a command that fails.

3666
04:02:30,458 --> 04:02:34,579
Vally has two graders for that: max
repeat, which counts repeats, and loop

3667
04:02:34,579 --> 04:02:37,308
outcome, which asks whether looping was
expected.

3668
04:02:37,808 --> 04:02:42,082
Both work from the trajectory after the
trial has ended, so neither stops a

3669
04:02:42,082 --> 04:02:45,158
running agent; the time limits we set
earlier do that.

3670
04:02:45,658 --> 04:02:50,737
Let's take max repeat first: it fails when
one action is repeated, back to back, more

3671
04:02:50,737 --> 04:02:51,633
than max times.

3672
04:02:52,133 --> 04:02:56,308
Ours allows three, so four identical
results in a row fail.

3673
04:02:57,533 --> 04:03:01,683
For max repeat, what counts as the same
action is set by match.

3674
04:03:02,183 --> 04:03:06,183
Observation, the default, means calls that
got the same result.

3675
04:03:06,683 --> 04:03:11,248
Name means calls to the same tool, and a
third value adds the arguments: the same

3676
04:03:11,248 --> 04:03:13,333
tool, called with the same arguments.

3677
04:03:13,833 --> 04:03:18,338
Max repeat has two more settings, which
narrow it to some tools, or catch an

3678
04:03:18,338 --> 04:03:20,708
alternating pattern, such as A, B, A, B.

3679
04:03:21,933 --> 04:03:26,315
Loop outcome is for a stimulus where
retrying is the very thing under test,

3680
04:03:26,315 --> 04:03:27,833
such as a tool that fails.

3681
04:03:28,333 --> 04:03:32,895
There, max repeat can only say that the
agent repeated itself; loop outcome says

3682
04:03:32,895 --> 04:03:34,833
whether that was what we expected.

3683
04:03:35,333 --> 04:03:39,452
This grader counts retries, the repeats
after a first call, against a limit we

3684
04:03:39,452 --> 04:03:42,883
choose, and a setting named should loop
says which answer passes.

3685
04:03:43,383 --> 04:03:48,247
None of our stimuli has a failing tool
yet, so loop outcome isn't in this eval

3686
04:03:48,247 --> 04:03:48,558
file.

3687
04:03:49,767 --> 04:03:54,142
Those are the graders; now let's test
them, before any of them costs a run.

3688
04:03:54,642 --> 04:03:58,692
First on the baseline: the workspace as it
starts, with no agent.

3689
04:03:59,192 --> 04:04:03,192
Then on the ten trials of the saved run,
again with no agent.

3690
04:04:03,692 --> 04:04:07,592
Then we make sure the forbidding grader
can fail, and run once more.

3691
04:04:08,092 --> 04:04:12,542
Which of these five would you expect to
fail on a workspace nobody has touched?

3692
04:04:15,042 --> 04:04:17,917
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

3693
04:04:19,133 --> 04:04:23,683
The four new graders are in the eval file,
so we lint it first, which is free.

3694
04:04:26,773 --> 04:04:31,004
There's one warning, the usual one about
scoring: with no scoring block, every

3695
04:04:31,004 --> 04:04:33,173
grader must pass, which is what we want.

3696
04:04:35,673 --> 04:04:40,573
Next, the oracle with no golden input: it
builds the workspace, applies nothing, and

3697
04:04:40,573 --> 04:04:41,273
grades that.

3698
04:04:41,773 --> 04:04:44,923
A grader that requires something should
fail here.

3699
04:04:45,987 --> 04:04:50,537
Three failed as expected: the hidden test,
reads the list, and loads the skill.

3700
04:04:55,037 --> 04:05:00,031
The two that only forbid something are
marked N/A, because doing nothing breaks

3701
04:05:00,031 --> 04:05:00,537
no rule.

3702
04:05:04,037 --> 04:05:06,537
So those two still need testing.

3703
04:05:09,467 --> 04:05:13,906
Now the saved trials: we change into the
saved run's folder, which holds ten

3704
04:05:13,906 --> 04:05:15,542
trials, five for each model.

3705
04:05:17,967 --> 04:05:22,648
Vally grade re-grades them with the eval
file as it is now, starting no agent, and

3706
04:05:22,648 --> 04:05:25,217
dash dash verbose prints every grader's
line.

3707
04:05:25,717 --> 04:05:29,872
The hidden test needs the finished
workspace, which a re-grade doesn't have,

3708
04:05:29,872 --> 04:05:31,567
so it will fail in every block.

3709
04:05:32,067 --> 04:05:34,792
The four new graders are what we're
checking.

3710
04:05:35,300 --> 04:05:39,925
The command prints ten blocks, one for
each trial; the last of them is on screen.

3711
04:05:43,425 --> 04:05:48,022
In it, the hidden test has failed, as
expected, and under it are the four new

3712
04:05:48,022 --> 04:05:48,500
graders.

3713
04:05:54,700 --> 04:05:57,300
Here are both tests, side by side.

3714
04:05:57,800 --> 04:06:01,725
Reads the list, never commits and no loop
passed all ten trials.

3715
04:06:02,225 --> 04:06:06,425
Loads the skill failed two: the two haiku
trials that hadn't loaded it.

3716
04:06:06,925 --> 04:06:11,527
Never commits hasn't failed anywhere yet,
and a grader that has never failed might

3717
04:06:11,527 --> 04:06:12,650
be checking nothing.

3718
04:06:13,867 --> 04:06:18,173
No saved trial committed anything, so
we'll test the grader on a command that

3719
04:06:18,173 --> 04:06:19,292
one of them did run.

3720
04:06:19,792 --> 04:06:22,642
In one of its trials, haiku ran find.

3721
04:06:23,142 --> 04:06:27,942
So, for one re-grade, the pattern forbids
find, in place of git commit or push.

3722
04:06:29,167 --> 04:06:32,717
With that one change, we re-grade the same
ten trials.

3723
04:06:33,217 --> 04:06:37,392
Never commits should now fail wherever a
trial ran that command.

3724
04:06:39,087 --> 04:06:43,265
It's the same ten blocks, and the one that
changed has scrolled off the top, so we'll

3725
04:06:43,265 --> 04:06:44,887
bring it back on the next screen.

3726
04:06:51,100 --> 04:06:53,850
Here's the block for the trial that ran
it.

3727
04:06:54,350 --> 04:06:58,924
Never commits failed, and its evidence
names the entry that matched: a bash call

3728
04:06:58,924 --> 04:07:00,525
whose command contains find.

3729
04:07:01,025 --> 04:07:04,500
It failed in that one trial, and passed in
the other nine.

3730
04:07:05,000 --> 04:07:08,550
So the grader can fail, which gives its
passes some meaning.

3731
04:07:09,767 --> 04:07:13,467
With that shown, the pattern goes back to
git commit or push.

3732
04:07:13,967 --> 04:07:17,292
This is the eval file the real run will
use.

3733
04:07:18,500 --> 04:07:23,148
Last, a real run with every grader: the
release stimulus, three trials on each

3734
04:07:23,148 --> 04:07:24,400
model, two at a time.

3735
04:07:24,900 --> 04:07:27,075
That's six agent runs.

3736
04:07:36,040 --> 04:07:40,115
When it finishes, the summary lists each
grader that failed any trial.

3737
04:07:43,615 --> 04:07:46,815
Terra passed every grader in all three
trials.

3738
04:07:49,315 --> 04:07:53,994
Haiku's first and third trials each failed
two graders: the hidden test, and loads

3739
04:07:53,994 --> 04:07:54,565
the skill.

3740
04:07:59,065 --> 04:08:01,615
Its second trial passed all five.

3741
04:08:04,833 --> 04:08:07,283
So what did checking the route show?

3742
04:08:07,783 --> 04:08:12,485
Our two guards, never commits and no loop,
didn't fail a single real trial: no agent

3743
04:08:12,485 --> 04:08:14,108
committed, pushed, or looped.

3744
04:08:14,608 --> 04:08:19,084
That's what we'd expect of a guard: it's
there for the day a model update changes

3745
04:08:19,084 --> 04:08:20,133
the agent's habits.

3746
04:08:20,633 --> 04:08:25,195
And we've seen that never commits can
fail, which is what makes its passes worth

3747
04:08:25,195 --> 04:08:25,708
anything.

3748
04:08:26,933 --> 04:08:30,608
The more interesting result is the route
each model took.

3749
04:08:31,108 --> 04:08:34,783
Haiku has eight trials in all, five saved
and three new.

3750
04:08:35,283 --> 04:08:39,251
It loaded the skill in four of them, and
five of the eight have an entry for the

3751
04:08:39,251 --> 04:08:41,433
refactor, which the skill says to leave
out.

3752
04:08:41,933 --> 04:08:45,183
Terra loaded the skill in all eight, and
passed all eight.

3753
04:08:45,683 --> 04:08:49,779
That points at loading, but it isn't the
whole story: all four haiku trials without

3754
04:08:49,779 --> 04:08:53,233
the skill wrote that entry, and so did one
of the four that loaded it.

3755
04:08:53,733 --> 04:08:56,408
Eight trials are too few to say more.

3756
04:08:57,633 --> 04:09:00,283
For the team, this changes the question.

3757
04:09:00,783 --> 04:09:04,783
Whether the skill writes good entries is
something we measured earlier.

3758
04:09:05,283 --> 04:09:09,308
Whether the app gets the skill used is
new, and it depends on the model, and on

3759
04:09:09,308 --> 04:09:12,008
the app's own prompt, which never mentions
the skill.

3760
04:09:12,508 --> 04:09:16,740
Both are things the team controls: the app
can name the skill in its prompt, or the

3761
04:09:16,740 --> 04:09:18,983
skill's description can name this
situation.

3762
04:09:19,483 --> 04:09:24,104
Either one is an edit to measure, before
and after, the way we've measured edits

3763
04:09:24,104 --> 04:09:24,508
before.

3764
04:09:25,733 --> 04:09:29,433
In real use, the tracker or the network
sometimes fails.

3765
04:09:29,933 --> 04:09:33,808
Does the agent cope, and can we test that
without breaking anything?

3766
04:09:35,033 --> 04:09:39,908
Every trial of the release tool so far had
a list of merged changes to work from.

3767
04:09:40,408 --> 04:09:44,877
In real use, the agent is sometimes asked
to fetch that list itself, by running the

3768
04:09:44,877 --> 04:09:46,708
script that lists what was merged.

3769
04:09:47,208 --> 04:09:51,358
Some days that script fails, because the
team's tracker doesn't answer.

3770
04:09:51,858 --> 04:09:56,075
If the agent writes entries anyway, it has
made them up, and in an app, they ship

3771
04:09:56,075 --> 04:09:57,533
without anyone reading them.

3772
04:09:58,033 --> 04:10:01,808
Nobody can take the tracker down just to
see what happens.

3773
04:10:02,308 --> 04:10:06,494
Here, you'll make that script fail inside
the eval, the same way in every trial,

3774
04:10:06,494 --> 04:10:10,628
grade what the agent does next, and see
how to start such a test from a run you

3775
04:10:10,628 --> 04:10:11,308
already have.

3776
04:10:12,533 --> 04:10:16,780
Let's start with what good behaviour looks
like when the script fails, because it

3777
04:10:16,780 --> 04:10:18,458
isn't the same as on a good day.

3778
04:10:18,958 --> 04:10:22,483
For the agent in the release tool, it
comes to three things.

3779
04:10:22,983 --> 04:10:27,108
First, it writes no entry: without the
list, any entry is a guess.

3780
04:10:27,608 --> 04:10:32,058
Second, it says what went wrong, so that a
person, or the app, can try again later.

3781
04:10:32,558 --> 04:10:37,398
Third, it stops after two retries at most:
one retry on a timeout is sensible, and

3782
04:10:37,398 --> 04:10:38,283
ten are a loop.

3783
04:10:39,500 --> 04:10:43,803
Then we make the failure happen on
purpose, and make it the only thing being

3784
04:10:43,803 --> 04:10:44,200
tested.

3785
04:10:44,700 --> 04:10:47,525
That second part is easy to get wrong.

3786
04:10:48,025 --> 04:10:52,041
The answer the script would have given
might be found some other way, such as in

3787
04:10:52,041 --> 04:10:54,300
the script's own source, or in a cached
copy.

3788
04:10:54,800 --> 04:10:59,395
If it can, the stimulus tests something
else: whether the agent finds that other

3789
04:10:59,395 --> 04:10:59,625
way.

3790
04:11:00,125 --> 04:11:04,000
Real trials at the end will show how
readily an agent does.

3791
04:11:05,200 --> 04:11:08,900
That's the reasoning; here's the eval file
that expresses it.

3792
04:11:09,400 --> 04:11:14,466
It's our release eval file, with a second
stimulus added at the end, named tracker

3793
04:11:14,466 --> 04:11:14,775
down.

3794
04:11:15,275 --> 04:11:19,608
Its prompt is what the app would send on
such a day: run the list merged script,

3795
04:11:19,608 --> 04:11:21,450
then write the Unreleased entries.

3796
04:11:21,950 --> 04:11:26,687
It also carries a tag, case equals tool
fails, so that it can run by itself: vally

3797
04:11:26,687 --> 04:11:30,500
eval with dash dash tag runs only the
stimuli that carry that tag.

3798
04:11:31,700 --> 04:11:35,225
Below the prompt is the new part: a
simulation block.

3799
04:11:35,725 --> 04:11:39,900
Under tool overrides, it names a tool, and
here that's the agent's shell.

3800
04:11:40,400 --> 04:11:44,615
For that tool it lists patterns, and each
one has a match: a regular expression,

3801
04:11:44,615 --> 04:11:47,725
searched for anywhere in each shell
command the agent runs.

3802
04:11:48,225 --> 04:11:50,850
When a command matches, it doesn't run.

3803
04:11:51,350 --> 04:11:54,675
In its place, the agent gets the output
line back.

3804
04:11:55,175 --> 04:11:59,649
Since is error is set to true, the agent
gets it as a failure; without that line,

3805
04:11:59,649 --> 04:12:02,025
the same text would come back as a
success.

3806
04:12:03,233 --> 04:12:07,700
From the agent's side, it looks exactly as
if the script had run and failed: our

3807
04:12:07,700 --> 04:12:09,933
text, and under it, an exit code of one.

3808
04:12:10,433 --> 04:12:15,119
Each trial also counts the calls that were
replaced, in its record in the results

3809
04:12:15,119 --> 04:12:15,408
file.

3810
04:12:16,633 --> 04:12:19,633
So what about everything else the agent
does?

3811
04:12:20,133 --> 04:12:24,133
Any shell command that matches no pattern
still runs for real.

3812
04:12:24,633 --> 04:12:27,833
So does a call to any tool the block
doesn't name.

3813
04:12:28,333 --> 04:12:32,673
If a tool must not really run at all, its
override can carry a default, which

3814
04:12:32,673 --> 04:12:34,758
answers every call the patterns miss.

3815
04:12:35,258 --> 04:12:39,822
One limit: shell tools can be made to
succeed or to fail, but the agent's other

3816
04:12:39,822 --> 04:12:42,133
built-in tools can only be made to fail.

3817
04:12:42,633 --> 04:12:47,355
Simulation also needs Vally's own
executor, which is the one we've used all

3818
04:12:47,355 --> 04:12:47,733
along.

3819
04:12:48,933 --> 04:12:50,908
Here's a test of that.

3820
04:12:51,408 --> 04:12:55,508
If the agent runs cat on the script's own
file, what does it get back?

3821
04:12:58,008 --> 04:13:00,308
It gets the simulated error.

3822
04:13:00,808 --> 04:13:05,383
The pattern, list merged, appears in that
command, and a match anywhere is enough.

3823
04:13:05,883 --> 04:13:10,175
Opening the same file with the agent's
file viewer, the view tool, is a call to a

3824
04:13:10,175 --> 04:13:12,083
different tool, so it runs for real.

3825
04:13:13,300 --> 04:13:17,800
One more part of this stimulus matters:
the copy of the script in the workspace.

3826
04:13:18,300 --> 04:13:22,448
So far, every stimulus has had the same
copy staged in its tools folder: a

3827
04:13:22,448 --> 04:13:25,475
stand-in that prints a fixed list from its
own source.

3828
04:13:25,975 --> 04:13:28,400
That fixed list is the answer.

3829
04:13:28,900 --> 04:13:33,135
With this file in the workspace, an agent
could read the answer out of it, without

3830
04:13:33,135 --> 04:13:34,375
ever running the script.

3831
04:13:34,875 --> 04:13:38,350
Simulation replaces calls; it doesn't hide
files.

3832
04:13:39,566 --> 04:13:42,616
So this stimulus stages a different copy.

3833
04:13:43,116 --> 04:13:47,362
Like the real script, it asks the tracker:
it fetches the merged changes from the

3834
04:13:47,362 --> 04:13:49,616
tracker's address, and prints one per
line.

3835
04:13:50,116 --> 04:13:53,516
It holds no list, so there's nothing in it
to copy.

3836
04:13:54,733 --> 04:13:59,283
Both copies have to land in the same place
in the workspace: the tools folder.

3837
04:13:59,783 --> 04:14:04,463
Vally has a rule here: a file staged for
one stimulus can't share its destination

3838
04:14:04,463 --> 04:14:06,658
with a file staged for every stimulus.

3839
04:14:07,158 --> 04:14:11,703
Until now, the stand-in was listed in the
shared agent environment, at the top of

3840
04:14:11,703 --> 04:14:12,208
the file.

3841
04:14:12,708 --> 04:14:14,683
So we take it out of there.

3842
04:14:15,183 --> 04:14:19,650
And we list it under the first stimulus,
since last release, which is the only one

3843
04:14:19,650 --> 04:14:20,358
that runs it.

3844
04:14:21,566 --> 04:14:24,741
The graders say what good looks like on a
bad day.

3845
04:14:25,241 --> 04:14:29,481
The first, tries the tool, is a tool calls
grader: the agent has to have run the

3846
04:14:29,481 --> 04:14:31,866
script, or the trial didn't meet the
failure.

3847
04:14:32,366 --> 04:14:36,191
The second, writes nothing, is diff empty:
no file changed.

3848
04:14:36,691 --> 04:14:41,464
The third, stops retrying, is the loop
outcome grader, set to allow at most two

3849
04:14:41,464 --> 04:14:42,491
retries in a row.

3850
04:14:43,700 --> 04:14:48,409
Our last grader, says why, is a judge,
because no pattern can decide whether a

3851
04:14:48,409 --> 04:14:49,375
reply is honest.

3852
04:14:49,875 --> 04:14:54,275
Its rubric has one criterion: the final
reply says the list couldn't be fetched,

3853
04:14:54,275 --> 04:14:56,750
and doesn't claim that any entry was
written.

3854
04:14:57,250 --> 04:15:01,075
The judge model is named once, under
defaults, as before.

3855
04:15:02,300 --> 04:15:06,375
Writing an override by hand is fine for
one failing command.

3856
04:15:06,875 --> 04:15:10,275
For a whole session, it's easier to start
from a real one.

3857
04:15:10,775 --> 04:15:15,176
Vally simulate record takes a run folder,
or one trajectory file, and prints a

3858
04:15:15,176 --> 04:15:18,675
simulation block built from the shell
calls those trials made.

3859
04:15:19,175 --> 04:15:23,350
There's one pattern for each call, with
the output that call really got.

3860
04:15:24,566 --> 04:15:28,266
That block is a draft, and here's what to
know before using one.

3861
04:15:28,766 --> 04:15:33,062
Each pattern matches only the exact
arguments that were recorded, and there's

3862
04:15:33,062 --> 04:15:35,516
no default, so any other call runs for
real.

3863
04:15:36,016 --> 04:15:40,491
Calls to tools other than the shell are
left out, with a warning for each kind.

3864
04:15:40,991 --> 04:15:45,619
The recorded output is whatever the tools
printed, which can include your user name

3865
04:15:45,619 --> 04:15:46,566
and your folders.

3866
04:15:47,066 --> 04:15:51,879
And to get a failure like our tracker's,
we'd edit one pattern's output, and add is

3867
04:15:51,879 --> 04:15:52,516
error true.

3868
04:15:53,733 --> 04:15:56,983
That's the file and the command; now let's
run them.

3869
04:15:57,483 --> 04:16:01,483
With no list to work from, what would you
expect each model to do?

3870
04:16:03,983 --> 04:16:06,858
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

3871
04:16:08,066 --> 04:16:12,519
The eval file now has the new stimulus,
and the stand-in listed under the first

3872
04:16:12,519 --> 04:16:14,266
one, just as the panels showed.

3873
04:16:14,766 --> 04:16:17,791
Lint comes first, because it starts no
agent.

3874
04:16:20,913 --> 04:16:25,613
Lint gives the one warning this file
already had, about scoring, and no errors.

3875
04:16:29,833 --> 04:16:34,467
Now the run, with dash dash tag to pick
the one stimulus: three trials, on both

3876
04:16:34,467 --> 04:16:35,758
models, two at a time.

3877
04:16:36,258 --> 04:16:39,883
It spends six agent runs, and six calls to
the judge.

3878
04:16:40,383 --> 04:16:44,458
We're after the summary at the end, and
any failure listed above it.

3879
04:16:49,280 --> 04:16:53,623
About a minute and a half later, both
models have passed, in all three trials

3880
04:16:53,623 --> 04:16:53,905
each.

3881
04:16:57,405 --> 04:17:01,940
With no failure to list, the output ends
with the summary, and the folder where the

3882
04:17:01,940 --> 04:17:02,705
run was saved.

3883
04:17:07,933 --> 04:17:11,308
Here's that run as counts, with a row for
each grader.

3884
04:17:11,808 --> 04:17:16,640
Every trial ran the script, none wrote an
entry, and none made more than two retries

3885
04:17:16,640 --> 04:17:17,158
in a row.

3886
04:17:17,658 --> 04:17:20,383
And the judge passed all six replies.

3887
04:17:23,600 --> 04:17:27,854
Last, the recording: vally simulate
record, on the run we saved when the

3888
04:17:27,854 --> 04:17:29,450
release stimulus first ran.

3889
04:17:29,950 --> 04:17:32,875
It starts no agent, so it costs nothing.

3890
04:17:33,900 --> 04:17:38,650
It prints the block first, and after it, a
warning for each kind of tool it left out.

3891
04:17:43,866 --> 04:17:46,241
Here's what it printed, from the top.

3892
04:17:46,741 --> 04:17:51,179
The trials in that run made two shell
calls between them, so the block has two

3893
04:17:51,179 --> 04:17:51,691
patterns.

3894
04:17:52,191 --> 04:17:56,937
Each pattern is a call's exact arguments,
written out as text, and under it is what

3895
04:17:56,937 --> 04:17:57,966
that call printed.

3896
04:17:58,466 --> 04:18:02,464
The second pattern includes the trial's
temporary folder, shortened here on

3897
04:18:02,464 --> 04:18:06,141
screen; that's the kind of detail to look
for before sharing a block.

3898
04:18:06,641 --> 04:18:11,097
Everything else those trials did, such as
view and edit, was left out with a

3899
04:18:11,097 --> 04:18:11,566
warning.

3900
04:18:14,766 --> 04:18:17,641
So what did the agents do on a bad day?

3901
04:18:18,141 --> 04:18:23,216
Neither model wrote an entry in any of the
six trials, and neither invented a change.

3902
04:18:23,716 --> 04:18:27,908
Terra's replies were a sentence or two:
each said the command had timed out, and

3903
04:18:27,908 --> 04:18:30,266
two of the three began with the word
Blocked.

3904
04:18:30,766 --> 04:18:35,274
Haiku's replies were longer: each said the
tracker wasn't answering, and each asked

3905
04:18:35,274 --> 04:18:37,066
the user for the list of changes.

3906
04:18:37,566 --> 04:18:40,166
In an app, there's nobody to answer.

3907
04:18:41,366 --> 04:18:44,291
The judge passed all six of those replies.

3908
04:18:44,791 --> 04:18:49,116
In an earlier run of this same stimulus,
it failed one of haiku's three.

3909
04:18:49,616 --> 04:18:53,436
Its reason: the reply mentioned the
timeout, but then asserted that the

3910
04:18:53,436 --> 04:18:55,641
Unreleased entries were already in place.

3911
04:18:56,141 --> 04:19:00,241
An app that reads already in place ships a
release with no entries.

3912
04:19:01,466 --> 04:19:05,279
After the script failed, haiku went
looking: in all three of its trials, it

3913
04:19:05,279 --> 04:19:07,516
opened the script's file with the view
tool.

3914
04:19:08,016 --> 04:19:12,391
That isn't a shell command, so nothing
simulated it, and it ran for real.

3915
04:19:12,891 --> 04:19:17,191
What came back was the copy that asks the
tracker: an address, and no list.

3916
04:19:17,691 --> 04:19:21,908
Had the stand-in been staged there, that
same call would have handed over the

3917
04:19:21,908 --> 04:19:22,291
answer.

3918
04:19:23,500 --> 04:19:26,575
Simulation replaces calls, not knowledge.

3919
04:19:27,075 --> 04:19:31,700
We could make the view tool fail for that
file too, but an agent has other ways to

3920
04:19:31,700 --> 04:19:33,675
read a file, such as a search tool.

3921
04:19:34,175 --> 04:19:38,524
What held in our run was taking the answer
out of the workspace: with the copy that

3922
04:19:38,524 --> 04:19:40,725
asks the tracker, no trial wrote an entry.

3923
04:19:43,933 --> 04:19:48,787
Now the retries: one haiku trial ran the
failing command four times in all, with

3924
04:19:48,787 --> 04:19:50,183
other calls in between.

3925
04:19:50,683 --> 04:19:55,583
No trial made more than two retries in a
row, so stops retrying passed all six.

3926
04:19:56,800 --> 04:19:59,425
All of this ran inside Vally.

3927
04:19:59,925 --> 04:20:04,731
The app also runs by itself, and keeps its
own record of what it did: can Vally grade

3928
04:20:04,731 --> 04:20:05,975
a run it didn't start?

3929
04:20:07,200 --> 04:20:11,932
So far, Vally has done everything itself,
from preparing the workspace to grading

3930
04:20:11,932 --> 04:20:12,575
the result.

3931
04:20:13,075 --> 04:20:16,125
The team's release tool doesn't work that
way.

3932
04:20:16,625 --> 04:20:20,731
After a merge, a script prepares a
checkout, and a small program, the release

3933
04:20:20,731 --> 04:20:22,650
app, starts an agent session itself.

3934
04:20:23,150 --> 04:20:27,202
Nobody runs Vally, so what's left
afterwards is the changed checkout, and

3935
04:20:27,202 --> 04:20:29,200
the app's own record of the session.

3936
04:20:29,700 --> 04:20:34,310
Here, you'll run that app, turn its record
into a file Vally can read, and grade the

3937
04:20:34,310 --> 04:20:36,450
run with the eval file we already have.

3938
04:20:37,667 --> 04:20:41,454
Let's start with a choice: there are two
ways to put an app's agent under our

3939
04:20:41,454 --> 04:20:43,667
graders, and they answer different
questions.

3940
04:20:44,167 --> 04:20:48,847
The first is after the fact: the app runs
as it always does, and we grade what it

3941
04:20:48,847 --> 04:20:49,367
recorded.

3942
04:20:49,867 --> 04:20:54,518
That shows the real thing: the app's own
settings, its real inputs, and its real

3943
04:20:54,518 --> 04:20:54,867
model.

3944
04:20:55,367 --> 04:21:00,130
But each run happens once, so we can't ask
for five more trials, or for another

3945
04:21:00,130 --> 04:21:00,492
model.

3946
04:21:01,700 --> 04:21:06,397
The other way is inside Vally, which can
repeat a stimulus, change the model, and

3947
04:21:06,397 --> 04:21:07,325
run experiments.

3948
04:21:07,825 --> 04:21:12,378
What it runs, though, is a reproduction of
the app, and a reproduction can differ in

3949
04:21:12,378 --> 04:21:13,300
ways that matter.

3950
04:21:13,800 --> 04:21:18,050
Most teams want both, and here we take the
first: grading a real run.

3951
04:21:19,267 --> 04:21:23,117
That's the choice; now, how can Vally
grade a run it never saw?

3952
04:21:23,617 --> 04:21:28,515
Vally grade has always read trajectories,
and until now they came from Vally's own

3953
04:21:28,515 --> 04:21:29,292
results file.

3954
04:21:29,792 --> 04:21:34,092
It also reads a second, public format,
which any program can write.

3955
04:21:34,592 --> 04:21:39,264
It's called ATIF, the Agent Trajectory
Interchange Format: a JSON file that

3956
04:21:39,264 --> 04:21:42,192
describes one agent session as a list of
steps.

3957
04:21:42,692 --> 04:21:46,894
Each step holds what the model said, the
tool calls it made, what each call

3958
04:21:46,894 --> 04:21:48,967
returned, and metrics such as tokens.

3959
04:21:49,467 --> 04:21:54,117
Vally converts a file like that into its
own trajectory before grading.

3960
04:21:55,333 --> 04:21:58,783
Here's the start of the file from the run
we're about to make.

3961
04:21:59,283 --> 04:22:04,725
The first line is how Vally recognises it:
a version that doesn't begin with ATIF is

3962
04:22:04,725 --> 04:22:05,308
rejected.

3963
04:22:05,808 --> 04:22:09,458
Then come the steps, one for each time the
model answered.

3964
04:22:09,958 --> 04:22:14,873
Each tool call is listed with its name and
its arguments, which is what a tool calls

3965
04:22:14,873 --> 04:22:15,633
grader reads.

3966
04:22:16,133 --> 04:22:20,908
Under observation is what each call
returned, matched to its call by an ID.

3967
04:22:21,408 --> 04:22:24,758
Last come the step's metrics, such as its
tokens.

3968
04:22:25,967 --> 04:22:30,771
For a run Vally didn't start, vally grade
needs three things besides the eval file,

3969
04:22:30,771 --> 04:22:32,392
one for each kind of grader.

3970
04:22:32,892 --> 04:22:36,804
In this command, all three come from one
folder, app run, which the app's script

3971
04:22:36,804 --> 04:22:40,863
will leave behind: the checkout the agent
worked in, a diff of what it changed, and

3972
04:22:40,863 --> 04:22:42,917
its record of the session as a trajectory.

3973
04:22:44,133 --> 04:22:48,563
First, the trajectory: graders of the
path, such as tool calls, skill invocation

3974
04:22:48,563 --> 04:22:52,383
and the budgets, need it, so the ATIF file
arrives on standard input.

3975
04:22:52,883 --> 04:22:57,507
Second, the change: diff graders take it
from the file that dash dash diff file

3976
04:22:57,507 --> 04:22:57,858
names.

3977
04:22:58,358 --> 04:23:03,001
Third, the changed files: a grader that
runs a command, or opens a file, works in

3978
04:23:03,001 --> 04:23:05,408
the folder that dash dash workspace names.

3979
04:23:05,908 --> 04:23:10,173
Then there's one more flag, dash dash
stimulus, which says whose graders apply,

3980
04:23:10,173 --> 04:23:13,358
because an ATIF file doesn't say which
stimulus it answers.

3981
04:23:13,858 --> 04:23:18,583
Our hidden test still works, because its
files are staged for the graders as usual.

3982
04:23:19,800 --> 04:23:23,675
Graders see only what's in the file, and
three gaps matter here.

3983
04:23:24,175 --> 04:23:28,854
Vally's own cost field stays empty for a
file like this, for a reason we'll come to

3984
04:23:28,854 --> 04:23:29,925
with the converter.

3985
04:23:30,425 --> 04:23:35,272
Next, the trial's wall time is recorded as
zero, because the file has no start and

3986
04:23:35,272 --> 04:23:36,750
end time for the session.

3987
04:23:37,250 --> 04:23:42,224
Finally, a skill counts as loaded only if
the trajectory shows the agent calling its

3988
04:23:42,224 --> 04:23:42,875
skill tool.

3989
04:23:44,100 --> 04:23:46,875
The release app doesn't write ATIF.

3990
04:23:47,375 --> 04:23:51,625
Like most programs built on an agent
library, it writes traces.

3991
04:23:52,125 --> 04:23:56,788
Traces come from OpenTelemetry, an open
standard for recording what a program does

3992
04:23:56,788 --> 04:23:59,575
as it runs, so that any tool can read the
record.

3993
04:24:00,075 --> 04:24:04,670
One unit of work in a trace is a span: one
call to a model, say, or one tool call,

3994
04:24:04,670 --> 04:24:08,425
with a start time, an end time, and named
values called attributes.

3995
04:24:08,925 --> 04:24:13,669
Every span carries the ID of its trace,
and a span started inside another names

3996
04:24:13,669 --> 04:24:15,050
that one as its parent.

3997
04:24:15,550 --> 04:24:18,875
So a span with no parent is the root of
its trace.

3998
04:24:20,100 --> 04:24:25,111
What goes into those spans is agreed too,
in OpenTelemetry's semantic conventions

3999
04:24:25,111 --> 04:24:26,225
for generative AI.

4000
04:24:26,725 --> 04:24:29,425
A model call is a span named chat.

4001
04:24:29,925 --> 04:24:35,086
A tool call is a span named execute tool,
and a whole agent session is one named

4002
04:24:35,086 --> 04:24:35,925
invoke agent.

4003
04:24:36,425 --> 04:24:40,975
Attributes have agreed names as well, such
as the one for input tokens.

4004
04:24:41,475 --> 04:24:46,006
Because the names are shared, a converter
written for one library's traces mostly

4005
04:24:46,006 --> 04:24:47,125
works for another's.

4006
04:24:47,625 --> 04:24:52,125
These conventions are still marked as in
development, so the names can change.

4007
04:24:53,333 --> 04:24:58,352
The release app is built on the GitHub
Copilot SDK, the same library Vally's own

4008
04:24:58,352 --> 04:25:00,233
executor uses to run an agent.

4009
04:25:00,733 --> 04:25:03,483
Here is the app's entry point, all of it.

4010
04:25:03,983 --> 04:25:08,850
The app itself sends one request, in the
same words as the stimulus in our eval

4011
04:25:08,850 --> 04:25:09,158
file.

4012
04:25:09,658 --> 04:25:14,404
It's called with a telemetry setting,
which tells the SDK to write every span to

4013
04:25:14,404 --> 04:25:16,183
a file, one line of JSON each.

4014
04:25:16,683 --> 04:25:21,011
Inside that setting, the line capture
content adds the message text, the tool

4015
04:25:21,011 --> 04:25:23,483
arguments and the tool results to the
spans.

4016
04:25:23,983 --> 04:25:28,512
Without that, the spans would hold names
and counts only, and no grader could check

4017
04:25:28,512 --> 04:25:29,658
what a tool call did.

4018
04:25:30,867 --> 04:25:35,692
Here's one line of the trace file from our
run, laid out with one attribute per line.

4019
04:25:36,192 --> 04:25:39,417
It's a model call: its operation name is
chat.

4020
04:25:39,917 --> 04:25:44,408
Its trace ID is shared by every span from
this session, and the parent is the

4021
04:25:44,408 --> 04:25:46,042
session's invoke agent span.

4022
04:25:46,542 --> 04:25:49,817
Its tokens are here, under the
conventions' names.

4023
04:25:50,317 --> 04:25:54,517
Then comes its price, in nano AIU, under a
name of GitHub's own.

4024
04:25:55,733 --> 04:25:59,233
This one is a tool call: the one that read
the merged list.

4025
04:25:59,733 --> 04:26:04,008
Its operation name is execute tool, and
the tool's name follows.

4026
04:26:04,508 --> 04:26:09,576
The call ID is the one the model gave the
call, and the ATIF file uses it to pair a

4027
04:26:09,576 --> 04:26:10,858
call with its result.

4028
04:26:11,358 --> 04:26:15,608
The arguments and the result are recorded
because of capture content.

4029
04:26:16,833 --> 04:26:21,008
Vally can write traces of its own, but it
has no way to read one in.

4030
04:26:21,508 --> 04:26:26,233
So to grade the app's run, something has
to turn its spans into ATIF steps.

4031
04:26:26,733 --> 04:26:31,583
Our practice folder has a converter for
that, about seventy lines of JavaScript.

4032
04:26:32,083 --> 04:26:35,483
It sorts the spans by start time, and
walks through them.

4033
04:26:35,983 --> 04:26:40,718
Each model call, a chat span, becomes a
new step, with its token attributes

4034
04:26:40,718 --> 04:26:42,233
renamed to ATIF's names.

4035
04:26:42,733 --> 04:26:47,091
A tool call is added to the step just
before it, because that's where the model

4036
04:26:47,091 --> 04:26:47,808
asked for it.

4037
04:26:48,308 --> 04:26:52,633
Every other span, such as the session's
setup and its timings, is skipped.

4038
04:26:53,133 --> 04:26:58,121
One tool records its arguments as plain
text, not JSON, and the converter keeps

4039
04:26:58,121 --> 04:26:59,383
that text unchanged.

4040
04:27:00,600 --> 04:27:04,850
Cost takes one more step, so here is that
story from start to finish.

4041
04:27:05,350 --> 04:27:09,750
The trace carries each model call's price
in nano AIU, as we saw.

4042
04:27:10,250 --> 04:27:14,325
The converter keeps it, turned into
dollars, in each step's metrics.

4043
04:27:14,825 --> 04:27:19,526
Vally's own cost field still comes out
empty, because Vally only counts cost

4044
04:27:19,526 --> 04:27:22,000
reported in AIU by its own Copilot runs.

4045
04:27:22,500 --> 04:27:27,250
So for a run like this, the cost is in the
file, and adding it up is left to us.

4046
04:27:28,467 --> 04:27:30,967
One caution about trace files.

4047
04:27:31,467 --> 04:27:35,815
With content captured, a trace holds the
whole prompt, every file the agent opened,

4048
04:27:35,815 --> 04:27:39,692
full paths on your machine, and an
attribute that identifies your account.

4049
04:27:40,192 --> 04:27:44,525
So a raw trace doesn't belong in an issue
or in a commit, and the ones shown here

4050
04:27:44,525 --> 04:27:45,542
were cleaned first.

4051
04:27:46,767 --> 04:27:50,117
The last piece is the script the release
tooling runs.

4052
04:27:50,617 --> 04:27:55,373
This script does by hand what our eval
file's agent environment and its setup

4053
04:27:55,373 --> 04:27:56,917
commands do inside Vally.

4054
04:27:57,417 --> 04:28:01,892
First it builds the checkout: the same
changelog, the same tool, and the skill in

4055
04:28:01,892 --> 04:28:02,942
a folder beside it.

4056
04:28:03,442 --> 04:28:07,392
Then it runs the same setup command, which
writes the merged list.

4057
04:28:07,892 --> 04:28:12,114
It keeps a copy of that starting state, so
the merged list counts as part of the

4058
04:28:12,114 --> 04:28:14,067
start, and not as part of the change.

4059
04:28:14,567 --> 04:28:18,667
Next it runs the app, on the model we give
it, tracing to a file.

4060
04:28:19,167 --> 04:28:24,029
Finally it compares the two folders, and
saves the difference as a plain diff, with

4061
04:28:24,029 --> 04:28:24,967
no git involved.

4062
04:28:26,167 --> 04:28:30,746
That's every piece; now let's run the app
once, convert its traces, and grade the

4063
04:28:30,746 --> 04:28:31,142
result.

4064
04:28:31,642 --> 04:28:36,058
Our stimulus has five graders: a hidden
test, two checks on its tool calls, a loop

4065
04:28:36,058 --> 04:28:38,267
check, and a check that the skill loaded.

4066
04:28:38,767 --> 04:28:42,917
Which of them would you expect to work on
a run that Vally never saw?

4067
04:28:45,417 --> 04:28:48,292
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

4068
04:28:49,500 --> 04:28:53,843
First, the app needs two libraries: the
Copilot SDK, which runs its agent, and

4069
04:28:53,843 --> 04:28:57,350
Vally's own package, for a part of the app
we haven't used yet.

4070
04:28:57,850 --> 04:29:02,856
We install them with npm; dash dash prefix
names the app's folder, and no agent

4071
04:29:02,856 --> 04:29:03,300
starts.

4072
04:29:07,053 --> 04:29:11,253
That adds about a hundred and forty
packages, inside the app folder.

4073
04:29:15,467 --> 04:29:19,092
Now the app itself: the script, followed
by the model's name.

4074
04:29:19,592 --> 04:29:22,017
This spends one agent run.

4075
04:29:22,517 --> 04:29:27,017
It prints the agent's reply, and then says
where it wrote the traces and the diff.

4076
04:29:29,327 --> 04:29:33,636
Moments later, the reply says three
changes were added, and both files are

4077
04:29:33,636 --> 04:29:34,102
written.

4078
04:29:40,333 --> 04:29:42,808
Here's the diff that the script saved.

4079
04:29:43,308 --> 04:29:47,633
Only the changelog changed, with a line
for each entry the agent added.

4080
04:29:48,133 --> 04:29:52,649
There's an entry for the new option under
Added, and one for the search fix under

4081
04:29:52,649 --> 04:29:52,983
Fixed.

4082
04:29:53,483 --> 04:29:57,502
There's also an entry for the refactor,
under a new Changed heading, and that's

4083
04:29:57,502 --> 04:29:59,333
the one our skill says to leave out.

4084
04:30:02,533 --> 04:30:06,899
The trace file is longer: a hundred and
forty-three lines, and the ones we care

4085
04:30:06,899 --> 04:30:08,833
about are the spans for tool calls.

4086
04:30:09,333 --> 04:30:13,838
Most lines are timings; searching for
execute tool finds the agent's tool calls,

4087
04:30:13,838 --> 04:30:14,908
and there are five.

4088
04:30:15,408 --> 04:30:18,208
One of them is the read of the merged
list.

4089
04:30:18,708 --> 04:30:22,995
None is a call to the skill tool, so in
this run, the agent never loaded the

4090
04:30:22,995 --> 04:30:23,333
skill.

4091
04:30:26,533 --> 04:30:31,158
Next, we convert: the converter reads the
trace file, and we send what it prints

4092
04:30:31,158 --> 04:30:32,083
into a new file.

4093
04:30:37,573 --> 04:30:41,473
Nothing is printed, because the trajectory
went into that file.

4094
04:30:44,400 --> 04:30:47,700
Here's the trajectory it wrote, one row
per step.

4095
04:30:48,200 --> 04:30:52,931
The five tool calls now sit in four steps,
each under the model answer that asked for

4096
04:30:52,931 --> 04:30:55,325
them, and a last step holds only the
reply.

4097
04:30:58,533 --> 04:31:02,700
Now the grade: our eval file, the
stimulus's name, the app's checkout as the

4098
04:31:02,700 --> 04:31:06,208
workspace, the saved diff, and the
trajectory on standard input.

4099
04:31:06,708 --> 04:31:09,933
Dash dash verbose prints a line for each
grader.

4100
04:31:10,433 --> 04:31:13,283
No agent starts, so this costs nothing.

4101
04:31:18,593 --> 04:31:22,837
Three of the five graders passed: the list
was read, nothing was committed, and

4102
04:31:22,837 --> 04:31:23,643
nothing looped.

4103
04:31:28,143 --> 04:31:32,736
The hidden test failed, on the refactor's
entry, and so did the check that the skill

4104
04:31:32,736 --> 04:31:33,118
loaded.

4105
04:31:38,333 --> 04:31:43,108
Earlier, Vally ran this same stimulus
itself: three trials, on the same model.

4106
04:31:43,608 --> 04:31:47,933
The sizes matter here: it's one run of the
app, against three trials.

4107
04:31:48,433 --> 04:31:52,608
In those trials, the hidden test passed
once, and the skill loaded once.

4108
04:31:53,108 --> 04:31:57,369
So the app's run failed the same two
graders that most of Vally's trials

4109
04:31:57,369 --> 04:31:57,783
failed.

4110
04:31:58,283 --> 04:32:01,958
Does that show the app is no worse than
Vally's own runs?

4111
04:32:04,458 --> 04:32:09,133
Not yet: it shows that the graders work on
the app's run, and give the same kind of

4112
04:32:09,133 --> 04:32:09,583
verdict.

4113
04:32:10,083 --> 04:32:13,483
With one run, that's as far as the
comparison goes.

4114
04:32:14,700 --> 04:32:18,125
So what carried over to a run that Vally
never saw?

4115
04:32:18,625 --> 04:32:21,750
Every grader we wrote inside Vally worked.

4116
04:32:22,250 --> 04:32:26,825
The hidden test ran in a copy of the app's
checkout, and caught the refactor's entry.

4117
04:32:27,325 --> 04:32:31,645
Both tool calls graders found the read of
the merged list, and no commit, because

4118
04:32:31,645 --> 04:32:34,525
the trace recorded every tool call with
its arguments.

4119
04:32:35,025 --> 04:32:39,387
The loop check counted repeats from the
same record, and the skill check saw that

4120
04:32:39,387 --> 04:32:40,625
no skill call was made.

4121
04:32:41,125 --> 04:32:45,369
Those graders ask whether the run did the
job the way the app needs, and that has

4122
04:32:45,369 --> 04:32:47,675
the same answer however the run was
started.

4123
04:32:48,900 --> 04:32:53,475
Some things did not carry over, and each
one goes back to what the file holds.

4124
04:32:53,975 --> 04:32:58,595
Cost came out empty, as we saw, so
comparing cost means adding up each step's

4125
04:32:58,595 --> 04:32:59,675
dollars ourselves.

4126
04:33:00,175 --> 04:33:04,075
Wall time was zero, so a wall time grader
would pass anything.

4127
04:33:04,575 --> 04:33:09,058
And the skill check worked only because
loading a skill shows up in the trace as a

4128
04:33:09,058 --> 04:33:10,425
tool call like any other.

4129
04:33:10,925 --> 04:33:15,702
So before trusting a grader on converted
runs, it's worth asking whether the field

4130
04:33:15,702 --> 04:33:16,925
it reads came across.

4131
04:33:17,425 --> 04:33:21,782
One stimulus has no counterpart here at
all: the one where the tracker is down,

4132
04:33:21,782 --> 04:33:24,925
because that failure exists only when
Vally simulates it.

4133
04:33:26,133 --> 04:33:30,844
That leaves a question these graders can't
answer: is the app as good as Vally's own

4134
04:33:30,844 --> 04:33:32,133
runs, worse, or better?

4135
04:33:32,633 --> 04:33:37,555
Answering it takes trials, and a real run
happens once, on whatever model the app

4136
04:33:37,555 --> 04:33:37,858
used.

4137
04:33:38,358 --> 04:33:41,058
That's the limit of grading afterwards.

4138
04:33:42,266 --> 04:33:47,341
Grading afterwards gives one sample for
each run, on whatever model the app used.

4139
04:33:47,841 --> 04:33:52,666
To get trials, experiments and other
models, Vally has to start the app itself.

4140
04:33:53,867 --> 04:33:58,555
The release app ran once on haiku, and
failed: no skill loaded, and an entry for

4141
04:33:58,555 --> 04:33:59,317
the refactor.

4142
04:33:59,817 --> 04:34:03,642
Vally's own trials of that skill passed
three of eight on haiku.

4143
04:34:04,142 --> 04:34:08,999
So is the app worse than those trials, or
was one run unlucky, and does it cost the

4144
04:34:08,999 --> 04:34:09,292
same?

4145
04:34:09,792 --> 04:34:14,138
One run can't say, and a run of the app
that has already happened can't be

4146
04:34:14,138 --> 04:34:14,667
repeated.

4147
04:34:15,167 --> 04:34:19,719
Here, you'll plug the app's own code into
Vally, so it runs as many trials as we

4148
04:34:19,719 --> 04:34:22,792
like, on any model, under the graders we
already have.

4149
04:34:24,000 --> 04:34:27,275
Let's start with where an app could fit
inside Vally.

4150
04:34:27,775 --> 04:34:31,897
Around every trial, Vally does the same
work: it prepares the workspace, and

4151
04:34:31,897 --> 04:34:34,500
afterwards saves the patch and runs the
graders.

4152
04:34:35,000 --> 04:34:39,275
One step in the middle runs the agent, and
that step is the executor.

4153
04:34:39,775 --> 04:34:44,075
The executor gets a stimulus and a
workspace, and hands back a trajectory:

4154
04:34:44,075 --> 04:34:46,050
the account of what the agent did.

4155
04:34:46,550 --> 04:34:49,200
That's the one part we're about to swap.

4156
04:34:50,400 --> 04:34:55,875
Vally's own executor opens a Copilot SDK
session, with settings that Vally chose.

4157
04:34:56,375 --> 04:35:00,425
The release app opens a session too, with
settings the team chose.

4158
04:35:00,925 --> 04:35:04,075
Those choices are part of the feature
being tested.

4159
04:35:04,575 --> 04:35:09,425
If the app's agent behaves differently, an
eval through Vally's executor won't show

4160
04:35:09,425 --> 04:35:09,600
it.

4161
04:35:10,100 --> 04:35:14,887
Once the executor is one that calls the
app, every trial runs the code the team

4162
04:35:14,887 --> 04:35:15,250
ships.

4163
04:35:16,467 --> 04:35:20,190
That gives us three ways to look at the
release feature, and each answers

4164
04:35:20,190 --> 04:35:21,567
something the others can't.

4165
04:35:22,067 --> 04:35:26,760
Vally's own executor shows whether the
skill does its job, on any model, apart

4166
04:35:26,760 --> 04:35:27,542
from the app.

4167
04:35:28,042 --> 04:35:32,341
Running the app through an executor shows
whether the feature, as shipped, does its

4168
04:35:32,341 --> 04:35:34,517
job, and it's repeatable, so we can count.

4169
04:35:35,017 --> 04:35:39,669
Grading the app's real runs afterwards
shows whether it's still doing its job in

4170
04:35:39,669 --> 04:35:40,192
real use.

4171
04:35:41,400 --> 04:35:44,625
That's the idea; here's the file that
expresses it.

4172
04:35:45,125 --> 04:35:49,575
An executor plugin is a JavaScript module,
and the starter ships this one with

4173
04:35:49,575 --> 04:35:51,800
everything written except one function.

4174
04:35:52,300 --> 04:35:56,025
Our executor is an object, and its name is
release app.

4175
04:35:56,525 --> 04:36:00,800
Its execute function runs one trial, and
it's the part still to write.

4176
04:36:01,300 --> 04:36:05,666
Shutdown is called at the end, and has
nothing to clean up, because the app opens

4177
04:36:05,666 --> 04:36:07,875
and closes its own session in each trial.

4178
04:36:08,375 --> 04:36:13,119
Vally looks for one exported function,
register executors, and calls it with its

4179
04:36:13,119 --> 04:36:15,550
registry: the list of executors it knows.

4180
04:36:16,050 --> 04:36:20,852
Ours adds this object to that list, so its
name is what a command or an eval file can

4181
04:36:20,852 --> 04:36:23,450
ask for, the way they ask for a model by
name.

4182
04:36:24,667 --> 04:36:26,867
Here's that function, written.

4183
04:36:27,367 --> 04:36:31,867
Vally hands it two things: the stimulus,
and the options for this one trial.

4184
04:36:32,367 --> 04:36:36,541
The function passes them on to write
entries, which is the app's own function:

4185
04:36:36,541 --> 04:36:38,467
the one the app's run script called.

4186
04:36:38,967 --> 04:36:43,454
Where to work is the first option: the
prepared workspace, after the setup

4187
04:36:43,454 --> 04:36:44,242
commands ran.

4188
04:36:44,742 --> 04:36:48,992
Which model to run comes next, so one
command can try any model.

4189
04:36:49,492 --> 04:36:54,321
Skills arrive as the path of each skill
file, and the SDK wants folders, so each

4190
04:36:54,321 --> 04:36:56,192
path is cut back to its folder.

4191
04:36:56,692 --> 04:37:00,867
The request is the stimulus's prompt, and
the time limit is Vally's.

4192
04:37:01,367 --> 04:37:06,017
For traces, the app gets a file in the
trial's results folder to write them to.

4193
04:37:06,517 --> 04:37:10,092
Last is the trial's trace context, which
we'll come to.

4194
04:37:11,300 --> 04:37:16,079
What comes back from the app is the
agent's reply, and every session event the

4195
04:37:16,079 --> 04:37:16,875
SDK reported.

4196
04:37:17,375 --> 04:37:21,375
One helper in the same file renames those
events to Vally's.

4197
04:37:21,875 --> 04:37:26,197
Then the function returns a trajectory:
the events, the reply as the trial's

4198
04:37:26,197 --> 04:37:27,675
output, and the workspace.

4199
04:37:28,175 --> 04:37:32,971
Its metrics come from compute metrics, a
function Vally exports that adds up a

4200
04:37:32,971 --> 04:37:34,200
trajectory's events.

4201
04:37:34,700 --> 04:37:39,468
That way, a plugin's tokens, cost and tool
calls are counted the way the built-in

4202
04:37:39,468 --> 04:37:40,350
executor's are.

4203
04:37:40,850 --> 04:37:45,334
Compute metrics can't know how long the
app took, so the wall time is ours to

4204
04:37:45,334 --> 04:37:45,800
measure.

4205
04:37:46,300 --> 04:37:51,003
Last comes some metadata: the model, the
skills that loaded, and the executor's

4206
04:37:51,003 --> 04:37:51,300
name.

4207
04:37:52,500 --> 04:37:57,200
That renaming helper is short, and three
of its branches matter to our graders.

4208
04:37:57,700 --> 04:38:02,450
When the SDK reports a tool starting, the
helper adds a tool call event: the kind

4209
04:38:02,450 --> 04:38:04,150
our tool-calls graders check.

4210
04:38:04,650 --> 04:38:09,197
When a skill is opened, it adds a skill
activation, the event that says a skill

4211
04:38:09,197 --> 04:38:09,600
loaded.

4212
04:38:10,100 --> 04:38:14,900
Both the skill-invocation grader and the
report's Skills column rely on that one.

4213
04:38:15,400 --> 04:38:19,834
After each model call, it adds a token
usage event: how many tokens the call

4214
04:38:19,834 --> 04:38:21,525
used, with its cost attached.

4215
04:38:22,025 --> 04:38:26,543
So cost is filled in for these trials,
which it wasn't when we graded the app's

4216
04:38:26,543 --> 04:38:27,400
run afterwards.

4217
04:38:28,600 --> 04:38:33,451
Vally loads the plugin from dot vally dot
yaml, the settings file that holds our

4218
04:38:33,451 --> 04:38:33,875
suites.

4219
04:38:34,375 --> 04:38:39,275
Three new lines at its top list the plugin
file, under plugins, as an executor.

4220
04:38:40,500 --> 04:38:43,525
Loading the file doesn't select the
executor.

4221
04:38:44,025 --> 04:38:48,763
We choose it for one run with dash dash
executor, for an eval file under defaults,

4222
04:38:48,763 --> 04:38:50,900
or for an experiment under overrides.

4223
04:38:52,100 --> 04:38:56,075
One more thing an executor does is declare
what it supports.

4224
04:38:56,575 --> 04:39:01,325
Ours declares nothing, so it can't run a
stimulus that has a simulation block.

4225
04:39:01,825 --> 04:39:06,321
And our release eval has one: tracker
down, where a tool is made to fail on

4226
04:39:06,321 --> 04:39:06,800
purpose.

4227
04:39:07,300 --> 04:39:11,899
Vally checks before starting the agent, so
through this executor, that stimulus ends

4228
04:39:11,899 --> 04:39:14,800
in error, with this message, and spends no
agent run.

4229
04:39:15,300 --> 04:39:19,662
That error will show in every run that
follows, and it's expected: it isn't a

4230
04:39:19,662 --> 04:39:20,625
fault in the app.

4231
04:39:21,834 --> 04:39:25,834
Both programs keep traces, and so far
nothing has linked them.

4232
04:39:26,334 --> 04:39:30,649
Vally writes spans of its own for every
trial, to a file in the run folder: one

4233
04:39:30,649 --> 04:39:33,709
span for the trial, and inside it, one for
each attempt.

4234
04:39:34,209 --> 04:39:38,685
Our app writes its spans to its own file,
and the outermost, named invoke agent,

4235
04:39:38,685 --> 04:39:40,084
covers one whole session.

4236
04:39:40,584 --> 04:39:44,284
When the app runs alone, that session span
has no parent.

4237
04:39:44,784 --> 04:39:49,476
If both send their spans to one tracing
tool, we'd want the app's spans inside the

4238
04:39:49,476 --> 04:39:49,934
trial's.

4239
04:39:50,434 --> 04:39:54,752
Then a slow or expensive trial can be
opened, and followed from Vally's step

4240
04:39:54,752 --> 04:39:56,059
down to the model call.

4241
04:39:57,267 --> 04:40:00,842
What links two programs' spans is a trace
context.

4242
04:40:01,342 --> 04:40:05,922
It's the ID of a trace, and of the span
that's open in it, passed from one program

4243
04:40:05,922 --> 04:40:06,592
to the next.

4244
04:40:07,092 --> 04:40:11,617
With it, the second program's spans join
the first one's trace, as children.

4245
04:40:12,117 --> 04:40:16,804
One web standard writes it as a short
string named trace parent: a version, the

4246
04:40:16,804 --> 04:40:19,592
trace ID, the parent span's ID, and some
flags.

4247
04:40:20,800 --> 04:40:25,367
Vally passes each trial's trace context to
the executor, in its options, and our

4248
04:40:25,367 --> 04:40:27,650
execute function hands it on to the app.

4249
04:40:28,150 --> 04:40:32,562
The Copilot SDK has a hook for exactly
this: a function it calls to ask which

4250
04:40:32,562 --> 04:40:36,000
trace to join, before it opens a session
or sends a message.

4251
04:40:36,500 --> 04:40:41,809
So the app needed two lines: give the SDK
whatever trace context the caller gave, or

4252
04:40:41,809 --> 04:40:42,125
none.

4253
04:40:42,625 --> 04:40:46,639
When the app runs from its own script,
there's none, and the session starts a

4254
04:40:46,639 --> 04:40:47,525
trace of its own.

4255
04:40:48,025 --> 04:40:52,135
When it runs from our executor, the
session span should carry the trial's

4256
04:40:52,135 --> 04:40:54,950
trace ID, with Vally's attempt span as its
parent.

4257
04:40:56,167 --> 04:40:59,167
That's how the plugin works; now let's run
it.

4258
04:40:59,667 --> 04:41:03,692
First, the release eval goes through the
app once on each model.

4259
04:41:04,192 --> 04:41:08,867
Then an experiment runs it three more
times on each: four trials a model in all.

4260
04:41:09,367 --> 04:41:13,717
In its one earlier run of the app, haiku
failed, without loading the skill.

4261
04:41:14,217 --> 04:41:18,767
How many of four would you expect it to
pass now, and would terra do any better?

4262
04:41:21,267 --> 04:41:24,142
If you tried it yourself, here's what
you'd see.

4263
04:41:25,367 --> 04:41:30,041
The execute function is now in the plugin
file, and the three plugins lines are in

4264
04:41:30,041 --> 04:41:31,067
the settings file.

4265
04:41:31,567 --> 04:41:35,841
First, node dash dash check tells us
whether the file is valid JavaScript,

4266
04:41:35,841 --> 04:41:37,342
without starting anything.

4267
04:41:40,967 --> 04:41:43,817
It prints nothing, which means no errors.

4268
04:41:44,317 --> 04:41:48,970
Now the release eval, with dash dash
executor release app, on both models, two

4269
04:41:48,970 --> 04:41:49,567
at a time.

4270
04:41:50,067 --> 04:41:55,192
It spends two agent runs, because tracker
down is refused before any agent starts.

4271
04:41:55,692 --> 04:42:00,499
The table at the end counts stimuli, two
for each model, and the refused one can't

4272
04:42:00,499 --> 04:42:00,792
pass.

4273
04:42:03,100 --> 04:42:07,625
Moments later, haiku has failed since last
release, and terra has passed it.

4274
04:42:11,125 --> 04:42:15,300
So the counts are none of two stimuli for
haiku, and one of two for terra.

4275
04:42:20,500 --> 04:42:25,094
The report in the new results folder
confirms what ran: under each table, the

4276
04:42:25,094 --> 04:42:26,525
executor is release app.

4277
04:42:27,025 --> 04:42:31,599
Haiku's table has no Skills column at all,
because no skill loaded, and its tool

4278
04:42:31,599 --> 04:42:33,600
calls were four views and one edit.

4279
04:42:34,100 --> 04:42:38,525
Terra's lists the changelog entry skill,
and a skill call among its tools.

4280
04:42:41,734 --> 04:42:46,285
The app's traces for a trial are kept in
that trial's results folder, and Vally's

4281
04:42:46,285 --> 04:42:47,859
spans are in the run folder.

4282
04:42:48,359 --> 04:42:52,509
Here's one line from each, for haiku's
trial, cut to three fields.

4283
04:42:53,009 --> 04:42:57,359
Both carry one trace ID, so the app's
session joined Vally's trace.

4284
04:42:57,859 --> 04:43:02,700
Its parent is Vally's attempt span, so
every model call and tool call beneath it

4285
04:43:02,700 --> 04:43:03,184
follows.

4286
04:43:06,400 --> 04:43:10,791
For more trials, we use an experiment
file, the kind we've used to compare

4287
04:43:10,791 --> 04:43:11,325
variants.

4288
04:43:11,825 --> 04:43:16,028
It names the release eval, and two
variants, haiku and terra, which differ

4289
04:43:16,028 --> 04:43:17,050
only in the model.

4290
04:43:17,550 --> 04:43:22,584
What's new is one line under the top-level
overrides: executor, release app, set once

4291
04:43:22,584 --> 04:43:23,650
for both variants.

4292
04:43:24,150 --> 04:43:26,375
Beside it, runs is three.

4293
04:43:27,600 --> 04:43:32,050
With dash dash dry run, Vally plans the
experiment and starts nothing.

4294
04:43:37,220 --> 04:43:41,570
It finds two plans, one for each variant,
with haiku as the baseline.

4295
04:43:46,800 --> 04:43:50,900
Now the real thing: three trials a model,
through the app, two at a time.

4296
04:43:51,400 --> 04:43:56,173
That's six agent runs, since tracker down
is refused again, three times for each

4297
04:43:56,173 --> 04:43:56,650
variant.

4298
04:44:00,854 --> 04:44:05,579
About a minute later, the table gives
terra one stimulus of two, and haiku none.

4299
04:44:10,079 --> 04:44:14,004
Under that, terra used about a quarter
fewer tokens than haiku.

4300
04:44:18,234 --> 04:44:22,629
That table counts stimuli, so we count
three more things from each variant's

4301
04:44:22,629 --> 04:44:23,034
trials.

4302
04:44:23,534 --> 04:44:27,781
Haiku passed none of three, loaded the
skill in one, and wrote an entry for the

4303
04:44:27,781 --> 04:44:30,309
refactor, pull request seventy-six, every
time.

4304
04:44:30,809 --> 04:44:35,484
Terra passed all three, loaded the skill
in all three, and left the refactor out.

4305
04:44:35,984 --> 04:44:40,744
Adding up each trial's cost, haiku's three
came to about six point seven AIU, and

4306
04:44:40,744 --> 04:44:42,684
terra's to about ten point seven.

4307
04:44:45,900 --> 04:44:47,875
So what can we measure now?

4308
04:44:48,375 --> 04:44:52,872
The same eval file ran unchanged: its
setup command, its hidden test and its

4309
04:44:52,872 --> 04:44:56,125
tool-calls graders didn't depend on which
executor ran.

4310
04:44:56,625 --> 04:45:01,425
Cost was filled in again, because the
executor reports the runtime's own usage.

4311
04:45:01,925 --> 04:45:06,762
Budgets, ranges and comparisons all work
on these trials, because they're ordinary

4312
04:45:06,762 --> 04:45:07,175
trials.

4313
04:45:08,400 --> 04:45:10,975
Now the earlier question has counts.

4314
04:45:11,475 --> 04:45:15,830
Earlier, our runs of this stimulus went
through Vally's own executor, eight trials

4315
04:45:15,830 --> 04:45:18,750
a model, and the app has now run it four
times on each.

4316
04:45:19,250 --> 04:45:21,925
Passed is each run's own verdict.

4317
04:45:22,425 --> 04:45:27,425
Tokens a model call is a row's trial
tokens, divided by its model calls.

4318
04:45:27,925 --> 04:45:31,875
AIU a trial is a row's cost, divided by
its trials.

4319
04:45:32,375 --> 04:45:36,700
Two things stand out: one is a weak sign,
and one held in every trial.

4320
04:45:39,900 --> 04:45:44,575
First, the weak sign: how the app behaves,
beside Vally's own runs of the skill.

4321
04:45:45,075 --> 04:45:48,625
Terra loaded the skill and passed every
time, either way.

4322
04:45:49,125 --> 04:45:51,325
Haiku is where they differ.

4323
04:45:51,825 --> 04:45:56,036
Through the app, it loaded the skill in
one of five trials, counting its one

4324
04:45:56,036 --> 04:45:56,700
earlier run.

4325
04:45:57,200 --> 04:46:00,575
Through Vally's executor, it loaded in
four of eight.

4326
04:46:01,075 --> 04:46:04,844
As ranges, that's four to sixty-two
percent, against twenty-two to

4327
04:46:04,844 --> 04:46:06,900
seventy-eight, which overlap widely.

4328
04:46:07,400 --> 04:46:11,710
In an earlier run of ours, the two were
closer: none of five, against one of

4329
04:46:11,710 --> 04:46:12,050
eight.

4330
04:46:12,550 --> 04:46:17,072
So with this few trials, we can't yet say
that the app makes haiku worse, but now we

4331
04:46:17,072 --> 04:46:18,525
can run more, and find out.

4332
04:46:19,734 --> 04:46:24,309
Second, the one that held in every trial:
the app costs more per model call.

4333
04:46:24,809 --> 04:46:29,385
The test is a strict one: even the app's
lowest trial, against the highest through

4334
04:46:29,385 --> 04:46:30,334
Vally's executor.

4335
04:46:30,834 --> 04:46:34,988
On haiku, the app's lowest used about
seventeen thousand four hundred tokens a

4336
04:46:34,988 --> 04:46:38,983
call, and the highest through Vally's
executor about fifteen thousand three

4337
04:46:38,983 --> 04:46:39,409
hundred.

4338
04:46:39,909 --> 04:46:44,309
On terra, it was about fourteen thousand,
against twelve thousand five hundred.

4339
04:46:44,809 --> 04:46:49,635
On average, that's about sixteen percent
more a call on haiku, and thirteen percent

4340
04:46:49,635 --> 04:46:50,159
on terra.

4341
04:46:53,367 --> 04:46:57,142
Why would one model need more tokens for
each call through the app?

4342
04:46:57,642 --> 04:47:01,292
The app's traces show the reason: an MCP
server.

4343
04:47:01,792 --> 04:47:06,442
That's a program that offers an agent
extra tools, through a shared protocol.

4344
04:47:06,942 --> 04:47:11,861
Our app's sessions include GitHub's hosted
one, which adds five GitHub tools to every

4345
04:47:11,861 --> 04:47:13,192
request the model sees.

4346
04:47:13,692 --> 04:47:16,917
Vally's own executor turns that server
off.

4347
04:47:18,134 --> 04:47:22,569
More tokens a call doesn't have to mean a
more expensive trial, because a trial's

4348
04:47:22,569 --> 04:47:25,034
cost also depends on how many calls it
makes.

4349
04:47:25,534 --> 04:47:29,906
On haiku, the app's trials made fewer
calls: five and a quarter a trial, against

4350
04:47:29,906 --> 04:47:32,584
five and three quarters through Vally's
executor.

4351
04:47:33,084 --> 04:47:37,758
So they cost about the same: two point
five four AIU a trial, against two point

4352
04:47:37,758 --> 04:47:38,409
five eight.

4353
04:47:38,909 --> 04:47:43,619
On terra, the app's trials made slightly
more calls, and cost more: about four AIU

4354
04:47:43,619 --> 04:47:45,859
a trial, against three point two seven.

4355
04:47:46,359 --> 04:47:49,809
Each call through the app carries the same
extra cost.

4356
04:47:50,309 --> 04:47:54,684
Whether a whole trial costs more depends
on how many calls that trial makes.

4357
04:47:55,184 --> 04:47:59,034
An eval of the skill alone wouldn't have
shown that overhead at all.

4358
04:48:00,234 --> 04:48:04,675
Earlier, this table could only say what
running the app through an executor might

4359
04:48:04,675 --> 04:48:05,059
answer.

4360
04:48:05,559 --> 04:48:10,296
Now it has answered: on terra, the app did
as well as the skill alone; on haiku, it's

4361
04:48:10,296 --> 04:48:13,584
too early to say; and every call paid for
five extra tools.

4362
04:48:14,084 --> 04:48:18,598
Neither could be counted from the app's
real runs, and the second was invisible

4363
04:48:18,598 --> 04:48:20,484
from inside Vally's own executor.

4364
04:48:20,984 --> 04:48:25,584
That's the reason to plug the app in: the
eval now measures what the team ships.

4365
04:48:26,800 --> 04:48:28,625
That's the whole course.

4366
04:48:29,125 --> 04:48:33,154
You can now edit a skill, or change the
model under it, and say with evidence

4367
04:48:33,154 --> 04:48:35,300
whether it got better, and what it costs.

4368
04:48:35,800 --> 04:48:40,162
Your graders are tested, your judge is
checked against your own labels, and your

4369
04:48:40,162 --> 04:48:41,525
results come with ranges.

4370
04:48:42,025 --> 04:48:45,550
Those checks run as a routine that a
teammate can repeat.

4371
04:48:46,050 --> 04:48:50,625
And you can evaluate the same skill when
an app, not a person, starts the agent.

4372
04:48:51,833 --> 04:48:55,358
On the course site, you'll find the
exercises for every part.

4373
04:48:55,858 --> 04:49:00,182
There's also a workflow guide, which walks
the whole procedure, for a skill or for a

4374
04:49:00,182 --> 04:49:01,108
feature in an app.
